HT_DATA.load("detail", {"ZK3O402wf1c":{"wy":"It is HN's default recommendation for learning linear algebra, praised for Strang's teaching and for building real understanding useful in machine learning.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend the MIT 18.06 open course on linear algebra - the lectures are first rate.","i":2992134,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":19812266,"author":"olooney","month":"2019-05","story":"Self Studying the MIT Applied Math Curriculum","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deepened understanding of key concepts like SVD","quote":"recommend it highly. I was looking for refresher but I gained a deeper understanding of several important concepts","text":"I did Strang's linear algebra course[0] (link goes to Youtube playlist of lectures) several years after graduating and recommend it highly. I was looking for refresher but I gained a deeper understanding of several important concepts; in particular it's fair to say I barely understood, or perhaps even misunderstood, SVD until Strang. If you're not sure if you need something like that, I suggest doing something like testing yourself on this video[1] or the MIT problem sets[2] it's easy to tell yourself that you \"know\" linear algebra when it would be closer to the truth to say that you used to know linear algebra, but can't answer even basic questions today. After Strang, Golub's book on Matrix Computations is also really incredible.[3] [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":19939788,"author":"SantalBlush","month":"2019-05","story":"Gilbert Strang's New Course on Linear Algebra+ for ML Now Online","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Motivates each topic and shares his train of thought","quote":"Love Gilbert Strang's original linear algebra lectures on YouTube [1].","text":"Love Gilbert Strang's original linear algebra lectures on YouTube [1]. They were a huge help while I took the course. In my opinion, he takes great care to motivate each topic and express his train of thought when working through problems. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19176596,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2019-02","story":"Ask HN: Linear algebra textbooks","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Awesome course from a great teacher","quote":"are awesome. What a great teacher.","text":"Gilbert Strang's Linear Algebra course from MIT, available on youtube (3 million views!), and his textbook of the same name are awesome. What a great teacher. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9056266,"author":"raz32dust","month":"2015-02","story":"How to Machine Learn","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Beautiful lectures that deepen appreciation of linear algebra","quote":"Absolute must-watch if you are into machine learning or related areas.","text":"Useful list. I would recommend adding one more resource for linear algebra/machine learning: I absolutely enjoyed learning Linear Algebra from these beautiful lectures by Prof. Gilbert Strang (MIT): [VIDEO] Seriously, I gained a new found appreciation for Linear algebra after going through these lectures. You should go over some of these lectures even if you already know linear algebra - it might give you insights you never had before (it did, for me). Absolute must-watch if you are into machine learning or related areas."},{"id":2992134,"author":"palebluedot","month":"2011-09","story":"Surprises from numerical linear algebra (2010)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"First-rate MIT linear algebra lectures, good refresher","quote":"I highly recommend the MIT 18.06 open course on linear algebra - the lectures are first rate.","text":"I highly recommend the MIT 18.06 open course on linear algebra - the lectures are first rate. I've been going through this as a refresher, in prep for the Stanford machine learning class. Link to the course: [LINK] Link to the YouTube videos of the lectures: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22675704,"author":"webartifex","month":"2020-03","story":"Show HN: Intro to Python and Programming for non-CS majors","stance":"rec","why":"Baseline course for anyone claiming to be a data scientist","quote":"make sure they know all the contents from here (Gilbert Strang's Linear Algebra course)","text":"It depends on who wants to hire you. For the top jobs, this is for sure not enough. I always tell people to please make sure they know all the contents from here [VIDEO] (Gilbert Strang's Linear Algebra course) before they make any claims about being a data scientist. I bet I can teach a monkey to open a CSV in pandas and call .fit() in sklearn. But do the people really understand the underlying assumptions. Most self-proclaimed data scientists don't I am sure. I still have a hard time calling myself a data scientist. And I am three years into a relevant PhD. The more I study, the less I feel I truly know."},{"id":18658967,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Linear algebra for Machine learning","stance":"rec","why":"Useful Strang lectures for learning linear algebra","quote":"And then there are the Gilbert Strange lectures[4] on Youtube.","text":"Does your degree program not include at least one Linear Algebra class? Or are you just trying to get a head start? In either case, you might find this course[1] and accompanying videos[2] useful. Also, of course, is 3blue1brown[3] on Youtube. And then there are the Gilbert Strange lectures[4] on Youtube. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16751673,"author":"theCricketer","month":"2018-04","story":"Book: Mathematics for Machine Learning","stance":"rec","why":"Part of a curated list of math courses for ML","quote":"","text":"I've mentioned this on HN before but I think its still relevant to people interested in learning ML who feel they are behind on the math. If, like me, you can't sit thru lots of pages of mathematics text and instead prefer that a human explains it to you via videos that you can replay, here is a list of courses that take you from basic algebra and pre-calculus math all the way to the concepts you need to understand the principles behind the most advanced ML algorithms. All explained by very energetic people who are experts in their fields, and starting from the very basics. This covers calculus, linear algebra, probability, statistics, convex optimization and a math for ML course thrown in for the HN audience: (The first two are \"MOOCs\" recorded in the 1970s! probably the first ever recorded MOOC, even before the internet, and the lecturer is absolute gold) Calculus Revisited: Single Variable Calculus | MIT [LINK] . Calculus Revisited: Multivariable Calculus | MIT [LINK] . Complex Variables, Differential Equations, and Linear Algebra | MIT [LINK] . Linear Algebra | MIT - [VIDEO] . Introduction to Linear Dynamical Systems |Stanford [LINK] Probability | Harvard [LINK] . Intermediate Statistics | CMU [LINK] . Convex Optimization I | Stanford [LINK] Math Background for ML | CMU [LINK] ."},{"id":14581962,"author":"theCricketer","month":"2017-06","story":"Georgia Tech's free math textbook collective","stance":"rec","why":"Part of a curated math lecture curriculum","quote":"","text":"If you find it easier to keep at it and learn from lecture videos instead of from textbooks, here's a math curriculum of lecture videos I've curated. This covers calculus, linear algebra, probability, statistics, convex optimization and a math for ML course thrown in for the HN audience: Calculus Revisited: Single Variable Calculus | MIT [LINK] Calculus Revisited: Multivariable Calculus | MIT [LINK] Complex Variables, Differential Equations, and Linear Algebra | MIT [LINK] Linear Algebra | MIT - [VIDEO] Introduction to Linear Dynamical Systems |Stanford [LINK] Probability | Harvard [LINK] Intermediate Statistics | CMU [LINK] Convex Optimization I | Stanford [LINK] Math Background for ML | CMU [LINK]"},{"id":13638544,"author":"blennon","month":"2017-02","story":"Big Picture of Calculus (2010) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Highly recommended course for learning linear algebra","quote":"I highly recommend Gilbert Strang's book and course from MIT","text":"For anyone who wants to learn linear algebra, I highly recommend Gilbert Strang's book and course from MIT: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":11295179,"author":"SixSigma","month":"2016-03","story":"Ask HN: What's the most useful online course you have watched?","stance":"rec","why":"Most useful online course, strong content and delivery","quote":"Gilbert Strang's linear algebra","text":"Gilbert Strang's linear algebra [VIDEO] Walter Lewin's Classical Mechanics [LINK] for the content and the delivery"},{"id":7690181,"author":"SixSigma","month":"2014-05","story":"Is CSS Turing Complete?","stance":"rec","why":"Pleasure-reading style linear algebra lectures without exam pressure","quote":"I can heartily recommend MIT's open coursewear videos featruring Gilbert Strang","text":"Ah but the beauty is that you can read for pleasure knowing that you won't have an exam at the end and trying to work out what your professor thinks is important. If you want to enjoy linear algebra without the pressure of doing any, I can heartily recommend MIT's open coursewear videos featruring Gilbert Strang [VIDEO] In fact all the MIT stuff I have tried is fun to watch"},{"id":2060420,"author":"jackfoxy","month":"2011-01","story":"Johnny Chung Lee to donate $5k for Khan-style linear algebra lectures","stance":"rec","why":"Series of 35 excellent MIT linear algebra lectures","quote":"Gilbert Strang of MIT has a series of 35 excellent lectures on Linear Algebra","text":"I'm not sure how to pass the clearly understandable Khan academy style test, but Gilbert Strang of MIT has a series of 35 excellent lectures on Linear Algebra, starting here [VIDEO]"},{"id":16753527,"author":"thomanq","month":"2018-04","story":"Book: Mathematics for Machine Learning","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Links as per the original comment [0] Calculus Revisited: Single Variable Calculus | MIT [LINK] Calculus Revisited: Multivariable Calculus | MIT [LINK] Complex Variables, Differential Equations, and Linear Algebra | MIT [LINK] Linear Algebra | MIT - [VIDEO] Introduction to Linear Dynamical Systems |Stanford [LINK] Probability | Harvard [LINK] Intermediate Statistics | CMU [LINK] Convex Optimization I | Stanford [LINK] Math Background for ML | CMU [LINK] [0] [LINK]"},{"id":7363132,"author":"baby","month":"2014-03","story":"LHC Physics Center bans Powerpoint, switches to whiteboard-only forums","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Gilbert Strang comes to my mind[1]","text":"I don't understand this. Most of my teachers use blackboards and it's really annoying to follow a presentation like that, you have to wait for the person to write, you have no slides later on to support your notes, and since you have no slides online you have to write everything they write, so you can't even listen properly to the talk. And some stuff are just clearer on slides... I don't really see a lot of benefits in whiteboard-only lectures. Combination of whiteboard and slides are best. I can still think of some great people who don't use slides but it's rare and a few people do it well (Gilbert Strang comes to my mind[1]). [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["h2cs8QLnxlU",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["443UNeGrFoM",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["XvDZLjaCJuw",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1],["q7K0vRUKXKc",1]]},"qv6UVOQ0F44":{"wy":"The go-to accessible introduction to neuroevolution and NEAT, praised for crediting prior research.","q":{"x":"his brief videos give a really nice introduction to the topic that should be accessible to most people","i":9792946,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":44279661,"author":"andai","month":"2025-06","story":"Self-Adapting Language Models","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite introduction to NEAT.","quote":"Here is my favorite introduction to NEAT","text":"Here is my favorite introduction to NEAT: SethBling's MarI/O - Machine Learning for Video Games [VIDEO]"},{"id":41438839,"author":"dberst","month":"2024-09","story":"Synchronizing Pong to music with constrained optimization","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent video on machine learning with Super Mario World.","quote":"There also SethBling's excellent video on YouTube about machine learning specifically with Super Mario World","text":"I'm a novice at machine learning, but Open AI made a python library for reinforcement learning in video games, and a fork of it is still actively maintained [1]. It's been a few years, but I remember being able to get it up and running in a day or two (maybe a weekend). It used the Retroarch emulator, which is compatible with a huge number of emulators and consoles. [LINK] There also SethBling's excellent video on YouTube about machine learning specifically with Super Mario World: [VIDEO] I encourage you to give it a try! I feel that video games are a bit underrated by current AI buzz, I think there's lots of potential for a machine to learn a skill through playing a game. And lots of potential for a game being selected or even created with the goal of teaching a specific skill. However at that point maybe it's better to forego the audio and visuals and speak to the machine in text or with pure data. On the other hand, I have seen a video about convolutional neural networks that feed on each pixel of an image. So perhaps training with sound data, or with the pixels of a spectrogram, could have some positive results. And certainly I would be amused to see a game played in time with music, or possibly even dancing with a songs melody, harmony, and story as well. Anything that's ever been created by humans, existed first in the imagination of a human brain. And you've got one of those. A mental vision pursued and brought from the mind into physical reality is a beautiful thing, "},{"id":16744062,"author":"deepnet","month":"2018-04","story":"The differences between tinkering and research (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Video explains NEAT and credits prior research thoroughly.","quote":"Seth's [Show More] Links on the MarI/O video[1] are very very comprehensive and (IMHO) indeed a great example of scholarship !","text":"OP, Togelius, is quite wrong in 1 point at least. tl;dr Seth Bling directly references NEAT and his links[Show More [1]] link to 2 Togelius papers[13][14] among many others ! To wit, in OP, Togelius mentions Seth Bling's MarI/O[1] as lacking scholarship because he didn't mention Togelius: \" seen as research, it is atrocious because of the complete lack of scholarship. The guy didn't even know he was reinventing the wheel, and didn't care to look it up. \" Rather disdaignfully (sadly/ ironically/ poigniantly) Togelius refers to Seth not by name but only as \" some guy \". Yet Seth Bling[2] in his short video, mentions and explains NEAT and directly links Ken Stanley's and Risto Miikkulainen's foundational NEAT paper[10] at U.texas which Togelius builds on [hint: click SHOW MORE under Seth's video] And much more, i.e.: ---- the innovative toolchain Seth uses: Bizhawk to script SNES emulators in Lua [12]! ---- Seth links the wikipedia page on Neuroevolution[11] which directly references 2 of Togelius's own papers[13][14] Seth's [Show More] Links on the MarI/O video[1] are very very comprehensive and (IMHO) indeed a great example of scholarship ! Togelius is a top flight scholar, and publishes in the field of AI game players and level generators[15] so I hope he will welcome some random tinkerer pointing out his error. [full disclosure: I am a Togelius fan] To give Togelius his due he does at least link to Seth's MarI/O video. Perhaps Togelius failed to click / read the Show More li"},{"id":37010025,"author":"cookiengineer","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: Is genetic programming still actively researched?","stance":"rec","why":"Nice intro to the NEAT concept.","quote":"There was a nice intro video into the underlying base concept which is called NEAT","text":"The genetic programming scene kind of evolved into NEAT, HyperNEAT and ES-HyperNEAT as a meta learning concept. Connections between layers/nodes are serialized as genes of agents with phenotypes and dominant/recessive markers, and an observing CPPN learns to categorize agents into different traits to find more efficient breeding mechanisms. It's a strong concept, and AFAIK it's still used a lot in the robotics world where you have to guarantee behaviors and have to be able to reproduce behaviors due to safety regulations. There was a nice intro video into the underlying base concept which is called NEAT by a youtuber named SethBling [2] In my case I'm using this as a decentralized learning approach, because peer to peer networks can benefit heavily from genetic AIs that can communicate, adapt and learn from themselves. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35757475,"author":"circuit10","month":"2023-04","story":"The future of generative AI is niche, not generalized","stance":"rec","why":"Good introduction to how computers learn.","quote":"This is a good introduction to the concept of how a computer could learn","text":"If a human brain doesn’t use maths, what does it use? It’s Magic? > \"training\", more accurately described as references Uh, no, that's not at all how the training process works. The training process very roughly mirrors evolution/natural selection except more more direct and faster. This is a good introduction to the concept of how a computer could learn: [VIDEO] This is a more technical look at how exactly it works in more modern AIs: [LINK] (it's a series) > If it encounters a problem it hasn't been programmed for, it cannot reason a new solution. But an LLM can? It's not as good at it as a human but it can Relevant: [LINK] (if you don’t want to click it it says “referring to AI models as \"just math\" or \"matrix multiplication\" is as uselessly reductive as referring to tigers as \"just biology\" or \"biochemical reactions\"”) > By your logic, video game characters must use reasoning, when no, they don't. It's maths. “By your logic, if tigers are dangerous, then bananas must be too, when no, they aren’t. It’s biology.”"},{"id":30956620,"author":"intunderflow","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: Which algorithms based on biological ideas do you know?","stance":"rec","why":"Inspired commenter to implement NEAT as a teenager.","quote":"after watching a Youtube video of an implementation of it","text":"Neuroevolution of Augmenting Topologies is a classic genetic algorithm, as a teenager it was my first genetic algorithm I implemented after watching a Youtube video of an implementation of it - [VIDEO]"},{"id":13253990,"author":"new299","month":"2016-12","story":"Whence your reward function?","stance":"rec","why":"Neat demo of an AI learning Super Mario.","quote":"This video of a Super Mario playing AI was pretty neat","text":"This video of a Super Mario playing AI was pretty neat: [VIDEO] It may, or may not be on a useful path to solving harder AI problems. I don't know. But it's a good way of demonstrating value (and publishing papers)."},{"id":12918380,"author":"notgood","month":"2016-11","story":"NeuroEvolution – Flappy Bird","stance":"rec","why":"Educational video on ML playing Mario.","quote":"(great educational video)","text":"You mean this script? Or machine learning in general? If the later then it has already been done: [VIDEO] (great educational video)"},{"id":11281087,"author":"slpsys","month":"2016-03","story":"Ask HN: What are some examples of beautiful software?","stance":"rec","why":"Helps visualize how a neural net learns to play Mario.","quote":"There's a great video that made the rounds last year about building a neural net that plays Super Mario World","text":"There is an objective, but it may not be exactly as you reason about it. There's a great video that made the rounds last year about building a neural net that plays Super Mario World, that may help visualize what's going on - [VIDEO] There's also a great snippet in the currently-ongoing AlphaGo videos that explains that when AlphaGo plays in ways that you may not expect, it's because it's strictly worried about _winning_ (even by the slimmest margin) with the greatest probability, and not necessarily by winning handily, like a human might."},{"id":9792946,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2015-06","story":"Hacking Super Mario World to Warp to the Credits [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Brief accessible introduction to neuroevolution playing Mario.","quote":"his brief videos give a really nice introduction to the topic that should be accessible to most people","text":"I believe it's been posted before, but sethbling (of vanilla-minecraft-hacks fame) pulled off the impossible and did the credit warp on an actual SNES, live[1]. As this video very-nicely explains, this required incredible amounts of pixel-perfect accuracy. Lining up the line of koopa shells on exact x-offsets and very narrow heights mid jump (!) to write the landing-sled - using a controller, realtime - is utterly insane. As a side note sethbling's latest project is teaching[2] (using neuro-evolution) his machine learning bot to play SMW[2], SMB1[3], and Mario Kart (original)[4]. While he machine learning is simple by today's standards, his brief videos give a really nice introduction to the topic that should be accessible to most people. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":49069250,"author":"iwhalen","month":"2026-07","story":"Show HN: Watch 14-Byte AI \"brains\" attempt to solve a 2D maze (Its hard)","stance":"reference","why":"Cool example of small NEAT network on hard task.","quote":"A cool example of a small network for a hard task using NEAT is MarI/O","text":"If your goal is to get the network as small as possible, you could try using NEAT[1]. A cool example of a small network for a hard task using NEAT is MarI/O[2]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23553975,"author":"charleskinbote","month":"2020-06","story":"Mutant garden (cartesian genetic programming)","stance":"reference","why":"Uses the same NEAT algorithm as Picbreeder.","quote":"It runs on the same evolutionary algorithm behind MarI/O [2], called NEAT [3].","text":"This is very similar to Picbreeder [1], which allows you to evolve art in a very similar manner, with evolutionary algorithms. It runs on the same evolutionary algorithm behind MarI/O [2], called NEAT [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":17489459,"author":"mockingbirdy","month":"2018-07","story":"Logic Theorist","stance":"reference","why":"Example of AI learning Mario by trial and reward.","quote":"","text":"If you watch videos of AIs learning to run [1] or playing Mario [2], you see that there's definitely \"try random shit until your reward function gives you positive feedback\" as a method for training AIs. The difficult part is developing the reward function. There's a lot of intelligence in our hormone-based incentive system - that we feel pain, that we feel sad, etc. Many of those emotions are pre-programmed. If we find a way to design reinforcement systems with the right goals, we can do it like mother nature did it for several billion years. But it's still a ton of computational power required. If we look at nature as a massively parallelized computational system that defined \"just live\" as the reward (not because it \"wants\" it, but because it just emerged), it shows us how much power we would need if we would try to build a completely random process that gains intelligence. I think your proposed method works (\"just assemble random solutions and test them\") because we've already seen it in nature. But it takes a lot of time and energy, regardless of our computational power. I think we're faster when we find the right boundaries and reward functions instead of randomly trying stuff. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16741395,"author":"sloreti","month":"2018-04","story":"The differences between tinkering and research (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Video credits previous work, rebutting the complaint.","quote":"the video discusses previous work starting at 4:40","text":"Also the video discusses previous work starting at 4:40, so I'm confused what OP is complaining about unless the MarI/O guy re-uploaded with this attribution. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16761645,"author":"1wu","month":"2018-04","story":"The differences between tinkering and research (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Seth's concrete tinkering project in debate about research.","quote":"","text":"Thanks for the commentary togelius [1, 2] and deepnet [above]. Anyone interested in adding a soupçon of scholarship to Seth's project? Words do mean different things to different people, in different contexts.. There is a recent body of literature that explores the modern \"maker\" [3] movement. However, \"maker\" as a term may not have been a good fit for the OP's argument [1], which contrasted (academic) researchers with so-called \"tinkerers\". An alternative term for \"tinkerer\" might be \"bricoleur\", a loanword from French. (Roughly, it still means one who tinkers: [LINK] but has other meanings depending on the academic lens.) Given that we are discussing AIs that play, in the context of education, we can also go back to Seymour Papert's work on [LINK] . Originally known for work on _Perceptrons_ with Marvin Minsky, AI researcher Papert later adapted theories from education towards the vision of \"learning-by-making\" and the (young) bricoleur [4]. This can approach can be seen in the evolution from 1960s graphical [turtle] [LINK] to Lego [LINK] to modern day efforts to encourage coding-for-kids [5,6,7]. One of Papert's later collaborators, Sherry Turkel, discusses bricolage as it applies to programming -- [LINK] . When it comes to early education, Turkel argues for epistemological pluralism [8] and cites anthropologist Levi-Strauss in comparing analytic science with a \"science of the concrete\". We can appreciate both Seth Bling's concreteness [9] and Togelius's original papers fo"},{"id":16616651,"author":"f00_","month":"2018-03","story":"The Best Books on the Philosophy of Mind","stance":"reference","why":"Example of NEAT in practice.","quote":"","text":"connectionism is an approach to the philosophy of mind, the most successful approach as evidenced by recent deep learning hype the only book in the OP I thought was even mildly relevant was the one by Dennet, and it's generally acknowledged that it's pretty bad. A more interesting but flawed approach, that resonates as someone who loves Dawkins and Hitchens (atheists and advocates of evolution), is Gerald Edelman's Neural Darwinism (1978). There is even modern research (that is real, working code) at the University of Texas that could be considered a successor to Edelman: NeuroEvolution of Augmenting Topologies (NEAT) [VIDEO]"},{"id":15633068,"author":"flor1s","month":"2017-11","story":"MariFlow – Self-Driving Mario Kart With Recurrent Neural Network [YouTube]","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier video by the same creator, compared in newer one.","quote":"This is from the same guy who made the MarI/O video","text":"This is from the same guy who made the MarI/O video ( [VIDEO] - using Neuroevolution). In the video he also compares MariFlow vs MarI/O."},{"id":13176048,"author":"halflings","month":"2016-12","story":"AI Programmer’s Bookshelf","stance":"reference","why":"Popular video of learned behavior applied to Mario.","quote":"There was a popular video showing this applied on Mario","text":"It's true that game AI is surprisingly under-developped compared to what you see in academia on other topics... but it is not always that basic. What you describe in your football example is often called \"Behavior Tree\", and there are ways to learn those using machine learning (usually by reinforcement learning, using neural networks and/or genetic programming). There was a popular video showing this applied on Mario [1] ... and a paper published 7 years before that video doing the same thing [2] (more context about this in [3]). I remember seeing something on Gamasutra saying that similar methods are used in some AAA games. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":11913794,"author":"rawnlq","month":"2016-06","story":"Japan student held for making Puzzle and Dragons hack","stance":"reference","why":"Example of ML research on a game.","quote":"","text":"If someone did this in the US, can they get in trouble? When exactly does \"hacking\" a game become illegal? What about people doing it for research like the machine learning mario guy: [VIDEO] . Or tool-assisted speedruns? Or the starcraft broodwar ai? Is it only when you breach the TOS or copyrights? Just curious since it never even occurred to me that such things are punishable."},{"id":9960852,"author":"nl","month":"2015-07","story":"FANN.js – Fast Artificial Neural Network Compiled with Emscripten","stance":"reference","why":"Example of neural net demos that appear intelligent.","quote":"","text":"Jason Weston (Facebook) etc's work on Memory Nets[1] Anything to do with transfer learning (the 2013 Zero-Shot Learning Through Cross-Modal Transfer [2] paper is a good place to start) The increasing amount of demos around using NNs to generate \"things\" that look kinda-almost \"intelligent\". I can't point at a paper, but Andrej Karpathy demo of generating Shakespere-like writing, \"Wikipedia\" pages and \"C\" code in The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Recurrent Neural Networks [3] is the kind of thing I'm talking about. The beginnings of work around goal-seeking. The (now Google) DeepMind Atari demo[4] and Marl/O[5] Finally, the work being done on making this stuff usable by programmers (Torch etc). [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["tcdVC4e6EV4",2],["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"u6XAPnuFjJc":{"wy":"HN uses it in discussions of pay, management, and motivation as the standard short explanation of autonomy, mastery and purpose.","q":{"x":"the \"Drive: The surprising truth about what motivates us\" video is a great rebuttal of this idea.","i":1709767,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":7398595,"author":"btilly","month":"2014-03","story":"Why good managers are so rare","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Autonomy, mastery, purpose; spot on about motivation.","quote":"To see why it matters, go watch","text":"The previous manager was very good at \"leading through inspiration\". He made people feel good about themselves, feel like they were learning, and feel like they were making a difference both for their corner of the world and the whole organization. The new manager was good at keeping track of things and micromanaging. But the most inspiring line she could come up with was, \"You still have a job.\" (I wish I was kidding.) She also had an unfortunate habit of lying badly and often, with a resulting destruction of trust. But that aspect I'm too bitter about at the moment to want to discuss. To see why it matters, go watch [VIDEO] . I believe that its message is not complete (for instance I've noticed that correlations between stress and mental performance is highly dependent upon the mental task you are doing), but what it says about motivation is spot on. The key factors are Autonomy, Mastery and Purpose. Good leaders can give people the sense of all three while still getting them to do what the organization needs them to do. Micromanagement makes it clear what the organization wants, but destroys motivation."},{"id":2093913,"author":"MrDunham","month":"2011-01","story":"Inequality in Equalland","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Pay versus performance beyond menial labor.","quote":"Highly worth a watch.","text":"There's a fantastic video on What Motivates Us. Link is [video] [VIDEO] . One of the things they showed an inverse correlation on amount someone gets paid and what they produce when it's anything above menial labor. Highly worth a watch."},{"id":30072092,"author":"Kim_Bruning","month":"2022-01","story":"Basic income would not reduce people’s willingness to work","stance":"rec","why":"Dan Pink on what motivates us.","quote":"have a video about drive/what motivates us by Dan Pink for RSA.","text":"I'm still around thinking about my next answer. In the mean time, have a video about drive/what motivates us by Dan Pink for RSA. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7256706,"author":"balls187","month":"2014-02","story":"By The Time You Give Them a Raise, They’re Already Out The Door","stance":"rec","why":"Good points that money motivates less past a point.","quote":"The following video makes some pretty good points along that line","text":"For the record, I 100% believe in paying people what they are worth. Determining that worth isn't an exact science. I also believe that after a certain point, money has less appeal to people--that is, people are no longer motivated by the money. By asking someone what they want, I'd like them to tell me what it is they need to be happy, so I can offer it to them. The following video makes some pretty good points along that line: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7004637,"author":"jtchang","month":"2014-01","story":"Twitter SVP Chris Fry Breaks Down How His Engineering Org Works","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on the three motivators.","quote":"Good video about this from RSA Animate if you haven't seen it yet","text":"Chris mentions the 3 things that motivate people. Good video about this from RSA Animate if you haven't seen it yet: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3378525,"author":"danberger","month":"2011-12","story":"Bingo Card Creator (etc) Year In Review 2011","stance":"rec","why":"10 minute video on what drives people.","quote":"I recommend watching this [1] 10m video out about what drives us to understand this a little more.","text":"He is obviously not motivated by money and most people aren't when you dig deeper. Many geeks want to achieve mastery, succumb new challenges, etc. I recommend watching this [1] 10m video out about what drives us to understand this a little more. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2808031,"author":"szany","month":"2011-07","story":"When you're in a team that I lead, there are 3 things that I'd like to ask you","stance":"rec","why":"Pink's 10-minute talk on autonomy-based leadership.","quote":"Dan Pink gave a good 10-minute talk about this sort of thing","text":"My instantaneous gut reaction is discomfort. These are all good things, but the tone puts me off. As someone who appreciates autonomy, I would rather be given an objective with advice on how to attain it than rules to follow. Even if the content is the same, it shows more respect. In this case, the objective is simply situation awareness ( [LINK] ) for both parties. The advice is to ask, debrief, and warn. Dan Pink gave a good 10-minute talk about this sort of thing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2829109,"author":"tayip9","month":"2011-07","story":"Ask HN: Is passion a fair thing to ask for from an employee?","stance":"rec","why":"Passion can come from incentives beyond salary.","quote":"Passion can be driven by incentives outside of salary.","text":"This might fit in with Dan Pink's talk on 'Drive': [VIDEO] Passion can be driven by incentives outside of salary."},{"id":2621699,"author":"sgentle","month":"2011-06","story":"Ask HN: No intrinisic motivation to build stuff","stance":"rec","why":"Short, well-done summary of Pink's Drive.","quote":"actually very well summarised here","text":"The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People by Steven Covey is a pretty seminal personal management book. It's my top recommendation in terms of \"foundational\" material. It's not got much in the way of practical tools, but the ideas are applicable everywhere. I would recommend Drive by Dan Pink if you're interested in learning more about creative motivation. It's pretty short, and actually very well summarised here: [VIDEO] Peopleware by Tom DeMarco and Timothy Lister is very good programmer-specific management material. They cite a lot of work benchmarking the productivity of different workplaces, so it's very \"x is good, x is bad\". Jim Collins' Good to Great is an examination of businesses that succeed vs fail, and the attributes that get them there, it's essentially an extended summary of a longitudinal study he and his team did of businesses that outperformed the stock market by a high factor over 10 years. It's more leadership than management, but still very useful. One that I haven't read yet (damn my stupidly big reading list) but recommend on reputation alone is First, Break All The Rules by Marcus Buckingham and Curt Coffman. Here's a summary: [LINK] I share your frustration about blah-blah blog posts. With the exception of First, Break All The Rules (which I haven't read yet) and 7 Habits (which I forgive because it's so damn good), the books I mention are based on actual research which is cited in the book, not just some dude going \"I think this because I know stuff\". "},{"id":1709767,"author":"hasenj","month":"2010-09","story":"Ask HN: What's the big deal with startups anyways...","stance":"rec","why":"Rebuttal of the idea using Drive.","quote":"the \"Drive: The surprising truth about what motivates us\" video is a great rebuttal of this idea.","text":"Exactly, the \"Drive: The surprising truth about what motivates us\" video is a great rebuttal of this idea. [VIDEO]"},{"id":1447515,"author":"dreadlabs","month":"2010-06","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite TED Talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Daniel Pink on workplace motivation, good for employers.","quote":"Really good for employers.","text":"I'm note sure if its my favourite but its one i find really interesting. Daniel Pink's talk on Drive, what makes people motivated to perform in the workplace. Really good for employers. There is a TED talk on it and i've seen it but i actually like the RSA animate one better which is essentially the same talk. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29950800,"author":"chrisweekly","month":"2022-01","story":"How to Be Useless","stance":"reference","why":"Mastery, autonomy, purpose as job satisfaction pillars.","quote":"Mastery, Autonomy, and Purpose were the three job satisfaction pillars in a popular RSAnimate whiteboard presentation video","text":"Mastery, Autonomy, and Purpose were the three job satisfaction pillars in a popular RSAnimate whiteboard presentation video a few years ago... and it resonated with me (while feeling incomplete, given it omits social / interpersonal factors). EDIT: Found the video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27005514,"author":"jokoon","month":"2021-05","story":"Horses and Sparrows: The myth of 'trickle down' economics","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as counter to a claim.","quote":"","text":"you said > hiring good people would be a roll of the dice and I'd even say that our whole economy wouldn't be able to function. > That's just a well accepted fact Says who? [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":12808848,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2016-10","story":"Google’s former happiness guru's three-second brain exercise for finding joy","stance":"reference","why":"Source for autonomy, mastery, purpose.","quote":"Happiness is feeling useful/having purpose (autonomy, mastery, purpose) [1]","text":"Happiness is feeling useful/having purpose (autonomy, mastery, purpose) [1], meeting your basic needs (~$75K/year research shows [2]), and spending time with friends and loved ones [3] [4]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":11926998,"author":"milesf","month":"2016-06","story":"Why Team Happiness Can Be the Wrong Thing to Aim for [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Autonomy, mastery, purpose lead to happiness.","quote":"Provide an environment that give workers autonomy, mastery, and purpose","text":"Provide an environment that give workers autonomy, mastery, and purpose (see Daniel Pink's work title Drive [VIDEO] ). You provide those three things and a sane group of people, happiness will follow."},{"id":10894820,"author":"eloff","month":"2016-01","story":"Open Guide to Equity Compensation","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on whether pay changes motivation.","quote":"","text":"Typically movie stars negotiate a tiny % of gross revenue in their contract. They used to negotiate for % of profit, but studios got very good at fiddling with the numbers for the film until a successful movie had nearly no profit. I don't see why that can't be done for software engineers. That is essentially dividends though, and it's obvious why growth companies don't want to do that. But it's also not clear that stock options have the intended motivating effect (it's not even clear that increases in salary or some kind of profit sharing would have the intended effect either[1]) I think it's clear that stock options are sub-optimal for the intended purpose, but it's not obvious to me how to fix that. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7908458,"author":"Ygg2","month":"2014-06","story":"The Highest-Paid CEOs Are The Worst Performers, New Study Says","stance":"reference","why":"Supports autonomy, mastery and purpose over money.","quote":"People give more performance if you give them more autonomy, mastery and purpose.","text":"I'd argue it's not. People that are paid most to do a job that isn't repetitive manual labor have worst performance scores, time and time again. So unless CEOs are paid to manually bolt things or throws ball into a hoop, they probably aren't doing well because, in part, they are given money. Money introduces fear (i.e. fear that you won't make enough money or lose your position with lots of money) and that leads to tunnel vision. People give more performance if you give them more autonomy, mastery and purpose. [VIDEO]"},{"id":5190527,"author":"Pkeod","month":"2013-02","story":"Netflix's Culture Document (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] High enough salary + great benefits to the point where needs are met are ideal. \"Rockstars\" may be able to negotiate with companies who want to hire those kinds of people, but if you are hiring only the best people and not the best people for your company then your culture will suffer. I like the way Valve does things. No rockstar culture like WallSt or Hollywood has. Everyone gets even footing and credit. You can earn more if your peers agree that you deserve it - people there are not chasing rewards - they are doing what they love well and being rewarded for it. It seems to work very well for them and other co-op like companies."},{"id":3886285,"author":"rguzman","month":"2012-04","story":"Show HN: iDoneThis+AwesomenessReminders, Get Work Done, Get Awesomeness","stance":"reference","why":"Research on progress and appreciation as motivators.","quote":"current research suggests that the feeling of making constant progress and being appreciated at work are the most important motivator for people.","text":"current research suggests that the feeling of making constant progress and being appreciated at work are the most important motivator for people. [VIDEO]"},{"id":1424520,"author":"thunk","month":"2010-06","story":"Intrinsic motivation doesn't exist","stance":"reference","why":"Counterpoint to claim that money motivates.","quote":"Ok, well then what about this [1] video from this [2] submission?","text":"> “There is no reason that money can't be an effective motivator, or that grades can't motivate students in school,” he said. Ok, well then what about this [1] video from this [2] submission? This makes me think about something else entirely: It should be easier to draw connections between submissions like these. It should be easier to link repetitive discussions into a cohesive whole. It should be easier to build something grand from the time we invest in social news communities. As it is, it often feels like a waste of time. Someone please build this. My time's spoken for. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] Edit: It'd be somewhere around the intersection of Stack Overflow, Wikipedia and HN"}],"r":[["agZ0xISi40E",1],["vGAqYNFQdZ4",1],["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["d8RRE2rDw4k",1],["ovJcsL7vyrk",1],["EYPapE-3FRw",1],["0xS68sl2D70",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["AF8d72mA41M",1],["A9UmdY0E8hU",1],["-4EDhdAHrOg",1],["f84n5oFoZBc",1],["I4flQ8XdvJM",1],["yFVXsjVdvmY",1]]},"IOiZatlZtGU":{"wy":"HN regards it as an entertaining and eye-opening introduction to why logic and functional programming are connected, and it is a frequent pick in favorite-tech-talk threads.","q":{"x":"best ~40 minutes I've ever spent.","i":13494586,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":17755557,"author":"jgautsch","month":"2018-08","story":"Curry–Howard correspondence","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Introduces Curry-Howard correspondence with history and follow-on ideas","quote":"it's one of my favorite talks. Well worth a watch if you haven't seen it","text":"Professor Philip Wadler has a fantastic talk titled \"Propositions as Types\" that introduces the Curry-Howard correspondence, some history behind it and ideas following it. In fact it's one of my favorite talks. Well worth a watch if you haven't seen it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13494586,"author":"imode","month":"2017-01","story":"Proofs are Programs – 19th Century Logic and 21st Century Computing (2000) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Opened eyes to the utility of logic in everyday programming","quote":"best ~40 minutes I've ever spent.","text":"curry-howard and Wadler's \"Propositions as Types\" talk at StrangeLoop* opened my eyes pretty early on to the sweet, sweet utility of logic in my every day programming. it sounds odd to start with but I never realized why these things mattered prior to viewing this talk. props to wadler. best ~40 minutes I've ever spent. * [VIDEO]"},{"id":12639103,"author":"runT1ME","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changes how you view everyday programming tasks","quote":"I think this can really really change how we look at everyday programming tasks","text":"Propositions as Types: [VIDEO] I think this can really really change how we look at everyday programming tasks everywhere from the type of tooling we choose to how we approach problems."},{"id":10563473,"author":"jcox92","month":"2015-11","story":"Propositions as Types (2014) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite talk of the conference","quote":"Probably my favorite talk at Strange Loop this year.","text":"+1 Probably my favorite talk at Strange Loop this year. It's worth watching just for the minute or so starting here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40791648,"author":"mrkeen","month":"2024-06","story":"A reckless introduction to Hindley-Milner type inference (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining talk on mapping between type systems and logic","quote":"This is nice and genuinely entertaining","text":"This is nice and genuinely entertaining [VIDEO] (Propositions as types by phil wadler). Its true focus is the 1-1 mapping between type systems and logic systems, but it's well worth the watch. This is good reading - it demystifies the funny typing rules in their mathematical form. [LINK] (Neither of these tell you how to implement the checker yourself though)"},{"id":18219338,"author":"pmiri","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth look at functional languages paralleling mathematical logic","quote":"A really in depth look into why functional languages stand out as a programming paradigm.","text":"Propositions as Types by Philip Wadler A really in depth look into why functional languages stand out as a programming paradigm. The short of it being that they parallel perfectly (almost proof for proof!) with mathematical logic. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16839619,"author":"yawaramin","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Favorite tech talk, also introduced Lambdaman","quote":"that was the talk that introduced Lambdaman to the world.","text":"Philip Wadler's 'Propositions as Types': [VIDEO] Bonus: that was the talk that introduced Lambdaman to the world."},{"id":12132719,"author":"mietek","month":"2016-07","story":"Very Long Proofs","stance":"rec","why":"Authority on proofs being programs, in video form","quote":"If you like watching videos, try","text":"If you’re using the right language, proofs are programs; it’s as simple as that. “Compressing” a proof, then, amounts to doing what good programmers are already doing — noticing patterns and eliminating redundancy. I’ll refer you to an authority on the subject, Phil Wadler. If you like watching videos, try < [VIDEO] . If you’d rather read an entertaining paper, try < [LINK] ."},{"id":10562409,"author":"chrislloyd","month":"2015-11","story":"Propositions as Types (2014) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining talk on the same subject as the paper","quote":"Philip gave a extremely entertaining talk on the same subject at Strange Loop this year","text":"If you're finding the paper too dense, Philip gave a extremely entertaining talk on the same subject at Strange Loop this year: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48252356,"author":"pyrale","month":"2026-05","story":"- -dangerously-skip-reading-code – olano.dev","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for programs as proofs in any language","quote":"","text":"That's any programming language, really [1]. Any website contains millions of \"proofs\", not all of them are useful. Choosing what needs to be proven is hard. And the spectrum of languages/type systems and their usability as either is more explored nowadays than it used to be. If you don't likue coq, you can look for agda. If agda is too far for you, you can look for Haskell. If that's still impractical, there's rust or f#, etc... The tradeoff you have between \"convenient for expressing proofs\" and \"convenient for programming\" has many options. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37171970,"author":"vishnugupta","month":"2023-08","story":"λ Calculus [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Linked within a historical background on lambda calculus","quote":"","text":"A little bit of historical background. There was a concerted effort in the late 19th, early 20th century (perhaps earlier too) to mechanise computation i.e., reducing it to a pure symbolic manipulation. There were obvious benefits, a famous one being Enigma Machine that was used to (successfully I think) break the German code during WW-2. On the philosophical side a parallel and overlapping effort was going on to figure out using mathematics to represent all possible truths , again as symbolic manipulation system. Bertrand Russel's magnum opus Principia Mathematica [1] was one such famous work towards it. Kurt Gödel then made a breakthrough when he proved that such a system is impossible; i.e., a system can either capture all the truths or it can be consistent but not both[2]. Put differently, any mathematical system capable of representing all the truths will necessarily contain contradictions within it. Now coming to your question. Lambda calculus emerged in this milieu. Alonzo Church[3] invented one such system to mechanise computation, named ƛ-calculus. Using this system one can mechanically compute any function purely through symbolic manipulation. Later on Turing, Church's student I think, invented a totally different system named Turing Machine with the same purpose. Later on it was proved (by Church and Turing I think, but I'm not 100% sure) that ƛ-Calculus and Turing Machine are equivalent, Church-Turing thesis[4]. All these work, and more, laid the theoretical found"},{"id":34466358,"author":"peter_d_sherman","month":"2023-01","story":"Idris: A Language for Type-Driven Development","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for evaluation corresponding to proof simplification","quote":"Evaluation corresponds exactly to simplification of proofs","text":"You might wish to have a look at \"Propositions as Types\" by Philip Wadler (thanks rbonvall, for the link!) -- at 21:30 (1290 seconds) into the video: [VIDEO] \" Evaluation corresponds exactly to simplification of proofs ...\" (The rest of the video, both before and after this statement, contain more context...) That being said, I agree with you that languages which are used primarily for theorem proving (AKA, \"proof assistants\") -- are usually not as applicable to as broad a range of programming paradigms and problems as most general purpose computer programming languages are..."},{"id":32633656,"author":"richardjam73","month":"2022-08","story":"Presentation: Four programming languages from forty years ago (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Related video on mathematics and computer science","quote":"A semi related video about the relationship between mathematics and computer science","text":"A semi related video about the relationship between mathematics and computer science [VIDEO] \"Propositions as Types\" by Philip Wadler"},{"id":29938898,"author":"peoplefromibiza","month":"2022-01","story":"Ask HN: Should I publish my research code?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Wadler's remark on computer science naming","quote":"computer science has two problems: computer and science","text":"notoriously Philip Wadler says that computer science has two problems: computer and science. It's not about computers and \"You don't put science on your name if you're a real science\" He prefers the name informatics. source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27171694,"author":"namelosw","month":"2021-05","story":"Shirts of Peter Norvig","stance":"reference","why":"Wadler tore his shirt to reveal a lambda man suit","quote":"all of a sudden he tore his shirt","text":"My favorite is Philip Wadler - during a Strange Loop talk[0], all of a sudden he tore his shirt, showing his \"lambda man\" suit underneath. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20869095,"author":"platz","month":"2019-09","story":"Timeline for Logic, λ-Calculus, and Programming Language Theory (2012) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Wadler comments on the history of logic and computation","quote":"Philip Wadler showcases and comments on some of this history in his conference talk","text":"Philip Wadler showcases and comments on some of this history in his conference talk \"Propositions as Types\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":19203421,"author":"BucketSort","month":"2019-02","story":"Metamath","stance":"reference","why":"Example of themes in functional programming community","quote":"Maybe not the best video or intro to the themes though.","text":"Proof assistants are like that. Check out Coq or Agda. If you aren't aware of it already, your spirit is calling for the stuff they talk about a lot in the functional programming community. See [VIDEO] for example. Maybe not the best video or intro to the themes though. Wadler also just put out a book about programming language foundations in Agda - [LINK] . [LINK] being one of the profound ideas here."},{"id":17947125,"author":"craigsmansion","month":"2018-09","story":"Lisp for the Web (2008)","stance":"reference","why":"Source for lambda calculus being discovered not invented","quote":"Lambda calculus is a discovery, and should we ever encounter an alien space-faring species, we could read their programs","text":"I know it seems like one of those Lispers' idiosyncrasies--\"discovered\" instead of \"invented\". I first heard it explained by Philip Wadler, and upon reflection it was one of those \"enlightenment\" moments that Lispers always go on about: Lambda calculus is a discovery, and should we ever encounter an alien space-faring species, we could read their programs in spite of their alien processing architectures (given that they use a functional language, but let's face it, they have the tech to cross space, they know what they are doing, i.e. they're in the functional camp) \"Propositions as Types\" by Philip Wadler, Strange Loop. ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":15577847,"author":"sitkack","month":"2017-10","story":"The Ergonomics of Type Checking (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"One entry in a list of type system references","quote":"","text":"Came here to mention MyPy and optional typing, the preferable term Gradual Typing means something distinctly different. [LINK] Dropbox employs the creator of MyPy but it isn't a creation of dropbox. Type Systems as Macros [LINK] Pluggable Type Systems [LINK] Propositions as Types [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13260351,"author":"rbonvall","month":"2016-12","story":"An Introduction to the Lambda Calculus (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Wadler on lambda calculus as the omniversal language","quote":"claims that aliens will probably have discovered it","text":"The lambda calculus teaches you how to build computation from a very simple evaluation rule called \"beta reduction\" which is just a fancy name for \"search and replace\" with function parameters. If you can find a system that can rewrite terms in such a fashion, you can compute whatever you want using the tricks you learn in lambda calculus. For example, C++ templates were not meant to be Turing-complete, but they work by rewriting terms [1], so you can encode lambda calculus in it, and run arbitrary programs at compile time . Philip Wadler does a better job than me at getting excited by lambda calculus. He calls it \"the omniversal programming language\", and claims that aliens will probably have discovered it [2]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["QHnLmvDxGTY",1],["Nsjsiz2A9mg",1],["XZ3w_jec1v8",1],["0fpDlAEQio4",1],["E8T_4zqWmq8",1],["Dk306ZkNOuc",1],["TS1lpKBMkgg",1],["FihU5JxmnBg",1]]},"f6kdp27TYZs":{"wy":"The canonical recommendation for learning Go concurrency, praised as entertaining and clarifying when to use channels versus mutexes.","q":{"x":"Great talk by Rob Pike","i":4218574,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":6417758,"author":"canthonytucci","month":"2013-09","story":"Less is exponentially more (2012)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of the commenter's favorite Go talks.","quote":"Go Concurrency Patterns (One of my favorites)","text":"For those who don't know who Rob Pike is, he's pretty awesome, helped create UTF-8 among other things, and now works on Go. [LINK] He has given some really great Go talks which are available on youtube, about an hour each: Origins of Go Concurrency Style - [LINK] Lexical Scanning in Go - [LINK] Go Concurrency Patterns (One of my favorites) - [VIDEO]"},{"id":4218574,"author":"mseepgood","month":"2012-07","story":"Having a Go - first time experience with Golang","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining goroutine demo with gophers passing messages.","quote":"Great talk by Rob Pike","text":"Great talk by Rob Pike: [VIDEO] \"Go Concurrency Patterns\" with cute little gophers playing Chinese whispers with megaphones"},{"id":46050865,"author":"pbohun","month":"2025-11","story":"A million ways to die from a data race in Go","stance":"rec","why":"Useful concurrency patterns built on the CSP model.","quote":"There's also a nice talk Rob Pike gave that illustrated some very useful concurrency patterns","text":"Not quite. Erlang uses the Actor model which delivers messages asynchronously to named processes. In Go, messages are passed between goroutines via channels, which provide a synchronization mechanism (when un-buffered). The ability to synchronize allow one to setup a \"rhythm\" to computation that the Actor model is explicitly not designed to do. Also, note that a process must know its consumer in the Actor model, but goroutines do not need to know their consumer in the CSP model. Channels can even be passed around to other goroutines! Each have their own pros and cons. You can see some of the legends who invented different methods of concurrency here: [LINK] There's also a nice talk Rob Pike gave that illustrated some very useful concurrency patterns that can be built using the CSP model: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31899203,"author":"gunapologist99","month":"2022-06","story":"What's New in Python 3.11?","stance":"rec","why":"Use cases and patterns for Go channels with goroutines.","quote":"Here's a great video that talks about use cases and the patterns in using them","text":"This is true, that's exactly what exceptions are. One could argue (I would) that this is a very powerful part of Python, but the indeterminate nature of the exception (where is it coming from, how is it defined, what should I provide to my client) makes it easier to catch exceptions as they percolate upward, but more difficult to correctly know how to handle them (especially as the permutations approach infinity as underlying libraries get upgraded). Golang actually has a back-channel communication path that is somewhat similar as a second channel of communications. (Actually they're called channels!) They're a first-class and extremely powerful feature of the language. You can even run a for loop over them, block or not block while waiting for an incoming message, etc. Here's a great video that talks about use cases and the patterns in using them, and they pair great with goroutines (and you don't have to sprinkle async before every function, either): [VIDEO]"},{"id":17778281,"author":"staunch","month":"2018-08","story":"Ask HN: Concurrency in Go vs. C/C++","stance":"rec","why":"One of Rob Pike's interesting talks on Go concurrency.","quote":"Rob Pike did a number of interesting talks on concurrency in Go.","text":"Rob Pike did a number of interesting talks on concurrency in Go. If you search \"Go concurrency\" on YouTube there are more good talks. Go Concurrency Patterns [VIDEO] Concurrency Is Not Parallelism [LINK]"},{"id":7925704,"author":"srcreigh","month":"2014-06","story":"Go support for Android","stance":"rec","why":"Among great Go resources alongside the Go Tour.","quote":"the various talks by Rob Pike [2] [3] / Sameer Ajmani [4] are great resources","text":"In my experience with Android apps, I've almost never had to use the NDK. When I did have to use the NDK, it wasn't because I wanted to code in C/C++---it was because there were strong, open-source libraries written in C/C++ that I wanted to use in an app (Though, I have no experience making games on Android). If you want to check out Go, then the Go Tour [1] and the various talks by Rob Pike [2] [3] / Sameer Ajmani [4] are great resources. The Go Tour stands on its own as an interest-building resource; the several programming exercises it has you do are fun and challenging. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK]"},{"id":7395708,"author":"aray","month":"2014-03","story":"Go Concurrency Patterns: Pipelines and cancellation","stance":"rec","why":"Video presentation of the same patterns for watchers.","quote":"And for those that would rather watch a video presentation","text":"Great explanation in document form of these patterns. Also very succinct and easy to reference. For those that would rather read this in presentation form: [LINK] And for those that would rather watch a video presentation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6072965,"author":"danneu","month":"2013-07","story":"ClojureScript Core.async Todos","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested as the starting point for learning Go's strengths.","quote":"Start with Rob Pike's","text":"You're responding to: > Clojurescript is the browser language I always > wanted. JS seems like such a hack. You're getting downvoted for reading into someone's enthusiastic personal endorsement and then ending it with the tiring \"Tell me something good about <thing I refuse to read or understand on my own time but I'll partake in discussion anyways>...\". Start with Rob Pike's \"Go Concurrency Patterns\" ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":5634733,"author":"mseepgood","month":"2013-04","story":"Why Node and Scala will dry up: Go will drink their milkshake","stance":"rec","why":"Preferred as the original source for the concurrency patterns.","quote":"So I prefer the original","text":"I'm a fan of Go, but this is not a very good talk. The first half is unnecessary trolling, and the second half is based on some code snippets taken from Rob Pike's \"Go Concurrency Patterns\", but in this case badly presented. So I prefer the original: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4197854,"author":"luriel","month":"2012-07","story":"Golang at CloudFlare","stance":"rec","why":"Rob Pike on concurrency patterns in Go.","quote":"And Rob Pike's latest talk about concurrency patterns at Google IO last week","text":"In one word: channels. They might be theoretically equivalent to some degree, but in practice they are quite different, and I find reasoning about both concurrency and parallelism using goroutines and channels much more natural. Also see this talk by Rob Pike on the origins of Go's concurrency model here: [LINK] See also this article by Russ Cox about CSP: [LINK] And Rob Pike's latest talk about concurrency patterns at Google IO last week: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28194643,"author":"gravypod","month":"2021-08","story":"Loom: Project Loom Early-Access Builds","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how language-level multiplexing yields cleaner programs.","quote":"See this for more info on how this could lead to cleaner programs","text":"All implementations of in-process thread multiplexing is sort of like a garbage collector. You have a resource, you manage the allocation of a resource to something that consume it, etc. A lot of house keeping and some overhead code that needs to execute before any work is done (each time). In my mind, it makes sense to have a single, well tested, and abstracted implementation that all other systems can use. You can see a need for this at least in the Android space: RxJava, Kotlin coroutines, etc. Also, in a user-managed thread multiplexing system you can't guarantee \"fair\" scheduling. If you have some DOS bugs in your server you can lock up an entire serving thread. A language-level multiplexing system will not have these same kinds of edge cases. There are many places this sort of thing becomes helpful. We could also see concurrent processing utilities that could be very helpful. See this for more info on how this could lead to cleaner programs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26543504,"author":"cratermoon","month":"2021-03","story":"The ecosystem of the Go programming language","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Go's channel-based concurrency without locks or callbacks.","quote":"No locks, no condition variables, no callbacks[2].","text":"Go was built as a language to make communicating sequential processes[1] first-class citizens. Thus the ubiquity of channels and goroutines. No locks, no condition variables, no callbacks[2]. Writing a bunch of code using locks and callbacks in Go goes against the grain of the language. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13575205,"author":"enneff","month":"2017-02","story":"The State of Go","stance":"reference","why":"Example of the present tool running code from slides.","quote":"Can any of those tools execute code from within the slides?","text":"Reveal wasn't a thing when I wrote present. I probably would have used it as a front end, had it existed. Tools like Keynote and Google Slides were exactly the kind of thing we were trying to improve upon. They're great for making pretty slides, but a total PITA for authoring and editing technical content. Can any of those tools execute code from within the slides? [VIDEO] [LINK] And do they let us store our code snippets in actual testable (or at least buildable) source files?"},{"id":5622890,"author":"NyxWulf","month":"2013-04","story":"The Beauty of Concurrency in Go","stance":"reference","why":"Video accompanying the slides on select and channels.","quote":"Video for the slides","text":"Observing other channels, or seeing when a channel does fire is what the select structure is for. It is one of the features that makes channels and goroutines very nice in go. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Video for the slides: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5365401,"author":"shurcooL","month":"2013-03","story":"How We Went from 30 Servers to 2: Go","stance":"reference","why":"Part of a set of materials to learn Go quickly.","quote":"","text":"> \"We also weren't sure if we would be able hire top talent if we chose Go, but we soon found out that we could get top talent because we chose Go.\" I feel[1] that a smart/talented C/C++/anything developer can go from someone who has never seen or heard of golang to a proficient and productive Go developer in a matter of a few weeks, maybe even _days_, if not less. That's how long it takes to go through the following materials (and fav some for later reference) and play with the language a bit. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] - use as reference [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] And a some more similar things that you can mostly get to from golang.org site. The beauty of how concise the language and even its website are, is that you can literally just go through everything there one thing after another. [1] This is my personal opinion based on playing with go the last few weeks/months. I'd love to verify this theory. It's not yet the primary language in which I do things in (I use C++11 atm), but for all my side tasks[2] it proved to be indispensable. And I found it very easy to pick up. I can't wait until I start doing all my work in Go, that will be a true test of its productivity efficiency. [2] [LINK]"},{"id":5073676,"author":"Ezra","month":"2013-01","story":"Go concurrency patterns","stance":"reference","why":"Video of Pike giving the talk with executable slides.","quote":"Video of rob giving the talk","text":"Video of rob giving the talk: [VIDEO] this makes use of the excellent \"Golang Present Tool\", which makes most of the code on the slides executable.[1] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":5075543,"author":"biot","month":"2013-01","story":"Go concurrency patterns","stance":"reference","why":"Q&A answers question about goroutine cleanup.","quote":"Rob Pike has a Q&A at the end of his video where he answers","text":"Rob Pike has a Q&A at the end of his video where he answers (what sounds like) the exact same question you ask: [VIDEO] Essentially, it gets garbage collected without you having to worry about it unless you're doing something fairly special."},{"id":4517076,"author":"mseepgood","month":"2012-09","story":"Will Go be the new go-to programming language?","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among Go talks for goroutines and channels.","quote":"Go Concurrency Patterns Rob Pike, Google I/O 2012 Goroutines, Channels","text":"The \"Talks and Videos\" page ( [LINK] ) is outdated as well. Here's my personal list: Why Learn Go? [LINK] Short interview with Rob Pike, OSCON 2012 Get Started with Go [LINK] Andrew Gerrand A Tour of Go [LINK] Russ Cox Go Concurrency Patterns [VIDEO] Rob Pike, Google I/O 2012 Goroutines, Channels Meet the Go Team [LINK] Panel (Pike, Thompson, Griesemer, Gerrand, Fitzpatrick, Simons), Google I/O 2012 Computing Map Tiles with Go on App Engine [LINK] Chris Broadfoot, Andrew Gerrand, Google I/O 2012 Go in Production [LINK] Gustavo Niemeyer, Google I/O 2012 Go: Code that grows with grace [LINK] (Contains some audio glitches) Andrew Gerrand Interview with Rob Pike [LINK] Rob Pike, GOTO Conference Lexical Scanning in Go [LINK] Rob Pike Cuddle, A Go AppEngine Demo [LINK] Andrew Gerrand Building Integrated Apps on Google's Cloud Platform [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, Google Developer Day Japan 2011 Real World Go [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, I/O BootCamp 2011 Go: it's that simple?! [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, OSDC 2011 Practical Go Programming [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, FOSDEM 2011 Writing Web Apps in Go [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, Rob Pike, Google I/O 2011 Go Programming [LINK] Rob Pike, Google I/O 2010 Another Go at Language Design [LINK] Rob Pike, Stanford University Public Static Void [LINK] Rob Pike, OSCON 2010 The Go Programming Language [LINK] Rob Pike, Google Tech Talk"},{"id":4571202,"author":"ralph","month":"2012-09","story":"Go Bloviations","stance":"reference","why":"Pike's point that channels join large program parts, mutexes guard data.","quote":"Rob Pike, in his search engine example at Google's I/O 2012 IIRC, made the explicit point that mutexes are there","text":"Agreed, Rob Pike, in his search engine example at Google's I/O 2012 IIRC, made the explicit point that mutexes are there and that channels are for joining large concurrent parts of the program together; guarding a data structure seems too small even though it's a common (toy?) example. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HxaD_trXwRE",2],["YdtE3aIUhbU",1],["3DtUzH3zoFo",1],["dX649lnKAU0",1],["tfAnxaWiSeE",1],["Jib2AmRb_rk",1],["ydyztGZnbNs",1],["fPrvnlvnu-k",1],["UF8uR6Z6KLc",1]]},"nJPERZDfyWc":{"wy":"HN invokes it constantly in debates over originality, copyright and AI training, and calls it the best explanation of creativity as remixing.","q":{"x":"The best explanation of creativity that I have found is a video series by Kirby Ferguson called Everything Is a Remix","i":29788601,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":41039329,"author":"indiv0","month":"2024-07","story":"Copying is the way design works (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Video essay on remixing, going further to attack patents and IP law","quote":"Reminds me of one of my favourite video essays","text":"Reminds me of one of my favourite video essays -- \"Everything is a Remix\" [0]. The video and this article cover the same ideas albeit with different examples. Which is funny on a meta level -- the article could be called a remix of the video. The video (if I recall correctly) goes a bit further, attacking patents/IP law as anti-creative. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29788601,"author":"combatentropy","month":"2022-01","story":"Copyright doesn't need 95 years to get the job done","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best explanation of creativity as combining existing things","quote":"The best explanation of creativity that I have found is a video series by Kirby Ferguson called Everything Is a Remix","text":"I find that most people don't understand creativity. If you believe an artist or inventor is some mystic sage who creates works ex nihilo , then you misunderstand creativity and attribute to it far too much. And therefore you might grant an artist a period of copyright that is longer than is really deserved or needed. Through much of my life, I have been accused of being \"creative\". In elementary school my class recognized me as the best at drawing. In fifth grade I wrote a book that was checked out so many times from the school library that it filled up the check-out card front and back. In high school, I made dozens of little movies with my friends. After college I was a graphic designer, and both of my employers expressed awe at my work. The best explanation of creativity that I have found is a video series by Kirby Ferguson called Everything Is a Remix, [VIDEO] It shows what I always knew in the back of my head: that nothing I ever made was from staring at a blank sheet of paper. It was just me ripping off all my favorite things I had seen before. Everyone tries their hand at art, and often it obviously is a copy. My secret was that I would take a little of this, a little of that, copy 5 different things and mix them all together, rather than making a straight copy of just one thing. I wasn't trying to hide anything, it was just how my mind naturally worked. I thought one thing from one movie was cool, so I took part of that. I thought something else from a drawing was co"},{"id":28692263,"author":"WallyFunk","month":"2021-09","story":"Ask HN: How can I get better at original thinking and being creative?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains that creativity is remixing, answering how to be more original","quote":"Watch Everything is a Remix","text":"Watch Everything is a Remix: [VIDEO] I've been prolific over the years in programming and art & code, and about 99% of it was remixes of others work. I'm not ashamed of that - many artists draw inspiration from multiple sources. The trick is to put your own spin on it, not directly rip off someone else's work. Many artists will never tell you they borrowed from various sources, because they think that would come across as unoriginal and merely copying others work, but have a few beers with an artist and suddenly they start sharing their secrets..."},{"id":49319517,"author":"alexpotato","month":"2026-08","story":"Patterns and problems in emerging multi-agent systems","stance":"rec","why":"Shows creative work is largely combining existing items","quote":"I would highly recommend the \"Everything is a Remix\" series on YouTube","text":"I would highly recommend the \"Everything is a Remix\" series on YouTube to show how a lot of \"creative\" work is really just combining different items. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39398416,"author":"rmbyrro","month":"2024-02","story":"Sora: Creating video from text","stance":"rec","why":"Broadened the commenter's view on originality and copying","quote":"Watching \"Everything is a Remix\" [1] helped me broaden my perspective.","text":"I used to have the same view. Watching \"Everything is a Remix\" [1] helped me broaden my perspective. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18029204,"author":"mtgx","month":"2018-09","story":"Senate passes copyright bill to end 140-year protection for old songs","stance":"rec","why":"Shows no creator makes anything from scratch, supporting finite copyright","quote":"I highly suggest you watch this video","text":"> it's to allow the creator to extract all the possible value from their work That may be why governments of the world keep passing copyright laws now and how they view copyright. But copyright was established as a \"balance\" between making it profitable for the author to create the work (read: not to extract billions of dollars out of a work) in order to benefit the public . The primary goal has always been to benefit the public, not the author. Also, one of the reasons why copyright should be finite and not have too long of a term is because no author has ever created anything - anything at all - from scratch . They themselves have built upon the work of others . This is why copyright is meant to be a system where one creates something upon something else, and then someone new can create something new upon that previous work, and so on. Copyright was meant to be a kickstarting mechanism for authors, not a rent-seeking one, which is how it's used today. If you still don't see this what I'm trying to say from my comment, I highly suggest you watch this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15662397,"author":"guiambros","month":"2017-11","story":"Tesla’s Dangerous Sprint into the Future","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary arguing winners are not unique; ideas emerge from many players","quote":"Worth watching.","text":"This discussion reminds me of the \"Everything is a Remix\" [1] documentary. We tend to look at the winners to explain history, even though in many cases multiple players were working on the same theories at the same time, and the likely outcome would have been exactly the same if the winners have not existed. Worth watching. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47563437,"author":"ghywertelling","month":"2026-03","story":"Miasma: A tool to trap AI web scrapers in an endless poison pit","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"We should promote remix culture rather than hamper it.","text":"If copying content from harddrive to another is theft, then so is DNA copying itself. Everything is a Remix culture. We should promote remix culture rather than hamper it. Everything is a Remix (Original Series) [VIDEO]"},{"id":45714233,"author":"haunter","month":"2025-10","story":"Books by People – Defending Organic Literature in an AI World","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> some piece of work was human created Are thoughts and ideas creations? Or you just mean the literal typewriting? How do you prove an idea is original and you have been in a vacuum not influenced _by anything at all_? If anything The Hunger Games is the perfect example that you can get away with anything you want, and that was almost 20 years ago. Everything is a remix [VIDEO] or if you hate your life [LINK]"},{"id":43646122,"author":"timschmidt","month":"2025-04","story":"MusiCoT, a chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting technique for music generation [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Whatever helps you sleep at night. You just described everything: [VIDEO] The entire evolutionary process which resulted in us and all of human invention can be summed as copy w/ blur; fight; repeat."},{"id":42473065,"author":"Minocula","month":"2024-12","story":"20 Years of Social Future // NoSocial Past [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This video is a remix of all kinds of other videos in the same Content - Net Neutrality. Everything is a Remix Remastered [VIDEO] Open Source Documentary on Net Neutrality [LINK] EVOLUTION - Linux Commercial [LINK] …and many more! This is a nonprofit video with one simple goal: to make you think."},{"id":41393935,"author":"ranger_danger","month":"2024-08","story":"What Is Post-Quantum Cryptography? – NIST","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> he didn't invent or spearhead the whole thing Many of the bigger inventions in history were popularized by someone other than the original inventor, because everything is a remix: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35603938,"author":"thot_experiment","month":"2023-04","story":"I used Stable Diffusion and Dreambooth to create an art portrait of my dog","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Do not start with me with this shit. Of course artists need to eat. I'm on the fucking internet essentially begging because I struggle so much with capitalism and the idea that almost all forms of employment force me to restrict my intellectual property, or assign it to someone else. Every time I'm forced to do it because the bank account is low I can feel it grinding down my soul. It is antisocial to prevent people from building upon my ideas (or any ideas). We should take every step we can to strengthen the commons. (see \"Everything is a Remix\"[0]) I dropped out of fucking high school and was homeless couch surfing in LA for years trying to break into steady VFX work, I don't think for a lack of skill/blood/sweat/tears[1]. I'm well aware artists need to eat. The problem isn't technological progress, (jfc this is all open source!!! stability is doing god's work by undermining the value of ghouls who try to restrict access to these models for personal profit). It's certainly not that copyright is too weak in preventing people form building off the aggregate styles and work of artists. I learned style by fucking copying the masters[2]!! This is how art is supposed to work. The problem it's the disgusting economic/political system that has twisted copyrights and patents into a device to enable rent seeking for those who can afford to purchase/commission them rather than protecting the innovation and creativity that they were meant to. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":34218997,"author":"theCrowing","month":"2023-01","story":"Against Automated Plagiarism","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The 2012 video \"Everything is a remix\" couldn't be truer today than it was 12 years ago.","text":"The 2012 video \"Everything is a remix\" couldn't be truer today than it was 12 years ago. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33232454,"author":"youssefabdelm","month":"2022-10","story":"An Interview with Jerry Seinfeld (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Starting point for an argument that all creation builds on influences","quote":"","text":"Everything is a Remix: [VIDEO] \"A child never writes his own alphabet. A seed does not grow, it needs soil, water, and radiant energy. A sailboat does not sail, it's shoved by the wind. Nothing in nature is self-activating.\" - Jacque Fresco We claim to be self-made, to be \"born that way\", yet we all have accents. But not just accents of speech, accents of action (the surfer learning to skate will skate with a 'surfing accent': [LINK] ), accents of thought (many of us are familiar with the archetypal poor person in a difficult environment which imprints a 'roof could fall in at any moment' mentality, who then becomes rich yet remains in that frugal mindset), accents permeating every atom of behavior we exhibit. Why are we drawing a line around speech and saying \"accents only develop here... everywhere else, we can be totally original or totally genetically talented!\" The mind is fashioned by what it feeds on."},{"id":33246193,"author":"youssefabdelm","month":"2022-10","story":"GitHub Copi­lot inves­ti­ga­tion","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Creation requires influence","text":"Couldn't agree more... It's very depressing that this post is popular, wouldn't want Copilot shut down over some drama queen lawyers that have no connection to the reality of software development and ALL creative fields. Creation requires influence: [VIDEO] The entire fucking concept of intellectual property and copyright is flawed from the get go. The issue people are wrestling with beneath the surface is not copyright but the monetary system itself which incentivizes this \"chisel off one another\" behavior and \"MINE!\" behavior because otherwise how will you survive if you can't monetize your actions?, but intelligent socioeconomic alternatives exist: [LINK] People are trying to solve this problem in an ass-backwards way. Either move to universal basic income or a resource-based economy and make all ideas 'free', 'copyable' and 'remixable' since it doesn't matter either way you have access to some resources (in UBI) or all resources for free (in resource based economy) and don't need to monetize anything since you have access to everything...instead people are content with making life shittier. \"We stand on the shoulders of giants\" said Newton, but oh no.. this piece of paper called 'the law' knows better!"},{"id":31361343,"author":"freeflight","month":"2022-05","story":"DeepMind: A Generalist Agent","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Arguments like this always make me think of Everything is a Remix [0] or how allegedly the most sincerest form of flattery is imitation. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24714489,"author":"ChuckMcM","month":"2020-10","story":"XB-1 Supersonic Rollout","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Lots of differences with the x-59, no wing canards, one engine on the x-59 vs 3 on the XB-1, the XB-1 seems much shorter. On the plus side the X-59 was developed by Lockheed and one of their advisors, Dr. Johnson was a CTO at Lockheed so there could be some cross pollination there. That said, its kind of picking nits. If everything's a remix[1] is true, that is even MORE true in aviation. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["rufnWLVQcKg",1],["wfYbgdo8e-8",1],["X9RYuvPCQUA",1],["8dhp_20j0Ys",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["pKcmNGvaDUs",1],["wqu_IpkOYBg",1],["DkS1pkKpILY",1],["rHdS_4GsKmg",1],["cIQ9IXSUzuM",1],["WTYet-qf1jo",1],["tk862BbjWx4",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["lr4MmmWQtZM",1],["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["mw2z9lV3W1g",1],["v3pK0dccQ38",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["1gEz__sMVaY",1]]},"-HFwok9SlQQ":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it when arguing that generational labels are marketing inventions used to belittle groups.","q":{"x":"Adam (from Adam Ruins Everything) does a really good talk at a marketing conference on why Millennials don't exist.","i":15843702,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":38029972,"author":"justin_oaks","month":"2023-10","story":"Gen Z wants less sex in TV and movies","stance":"rec","why":"Humorous but serious argument that lumping people into generations is useless or harmful.","quote":"Adam Conover did a great talk about this [0].","text":"Adam Conover did a great talk about this [0]. It was entitled \"Millenials Don't Exist\". In it he humorously but also seriously asserts that lumping people together as generations is useless at best and harmful at worst. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16968874,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2018-05","story":"Americans Are a Lonely Lot, and Young People Bear the Heaviest Burden","stance":"rec","why":"Generational titles are invented to sell books and belittle people.","quote":"I like to refer to Adam ruins everything on Millennials","text":"I like to refer to Adam ruins everything on Millennials: [VIDEO] TL;DW Generational titles (Boomers, Gen X, Y, whatever) are invented by authors to sell books. They never bring people up and are always used to tear people down. Everything said about Millennials has been said about every generation ever. You can take a Time magazine cover from the 50s about the entitled generation and slap a modern photo on it and it's the same damn thing. It's lazy. Millennials are the most diverse of any generation. That's about all you can really say, other than we're the most in debt and most likely to take unpaid internships compared to any previous generation. It's a straw man and it needs to die."},{"id":15843702,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-12","story":"Why We Blame Millennials for Everything","stance":"rec","why":"Why millennials don't exist: generational labels exist to tear groups down.","quote":"Adam (from Adam Ruins Everything) does a really good talk at a marketing conference on why Millennials don't exist.","text":"Adam (from Adam Ruins Everything) does a really good talk at a marketing conference on why Millennials don't exist. Generational titles are never used to bring people up, but always tear a group of people down. Time magazine covers about Baby Boomers from decades ago are almost identical to those calling Millennials entitled today: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14703086,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-07","story":"I'm not a Woman in Tech","stance":"rec","why":"Generational terms are invented by authors and condescend to people.","quote":"He talks about how generational terms are just made up by authors who want to get rich","text":"It kinda makes me thing of the \"Millennials Don't Exist\" talk by Adam Conover. He talks about how generational terms are just made up by authors who want to get rich and they're almost always condescending (those entitled baby boomers, those lazy millennials, those gen x-ers) and how people don't liked to be talked down to: [VIDEO] Are millennials attached do their cellphones? Yes .. so are mothers, fathers, grandparents and some dogs. It's literally the most compact and powerful communication and computational device we've ever had. When you break down all the labels, all you really have is people. Talk to them like people."},{"id":14537934,"author":"sr2","month":"2017-06","story":"Decades","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the term millennial as a meaningless label.","quote":"Here's an interesting talk by Adam Conover debunking the term millennial","text":"I still see the term 'millennial fashion' and 'the noughties' been thrown around, but these are meaningless terms. Here's an interesting talk by Adam Conover debunking the term millennial: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13066312,"author":"zerognowl","month":"2016-11","story":"3 Out of 4 Millennials Have Received Financial Help from Parents After College","stance":"rec","why":"Talk arguing the millennial category is not real.","quote":"Millennials don't exist, according to Adam Conover","text":"Millennials don't exist, according to Adam Conover: [VIDEO] I'm careful using the word \"Millennial\" these days and increasingly skeptical of online articles with the word \"Millennial\" in the title. Thanks to shows like Adam Ruins Everything[1], I am more informed on a variety of topics. (I learn visually and prefer to watch his videos instead of read an article). [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":12033224,"author":"fattire","month":"2016-07","story":"I’m a millennial and my generation sucks","stance":"rec","why":"Argues generational labels are meaningless pile-ons.","quote":"this bears watching again...","text":"Agreed. My favorite quote was \"In some ways I love being a millennial, because it’s so much easier to be better than the rest of our generation\" I can't tell if this statement supports or contradicts the rest of the article, but regardless, this bears watching again... [VIDEO] The generational pile-on goes back and is pretty meaningless."},{"id":18128906,"author":"AngryAnt","month":"2018-10","story":"Boomers Retire, Knowledge Goes with Them","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Needs a \"Boomer: \" prefix or \"says boomer\" postfix. \"I’m surprised to see online videos at the bottom of the list with just 16%. I suspect that this percentage is higher among millennials and lower among baby boomers. I prefer to read about how to do something as opposed to watching a video — that is, unless I’m trying to take something apart to fix it. Having a video is often better than looking at photos or drawings.\" I guess that the responsible thing is to let this article fade into irrelevance, but I felt like sharing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15920004,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-12","story":"Nearly 5M Americans in Default on Student Loans","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"The modern theory of the perpetuation of debt has drenched the earth with blood, and crushed its inhabitants under burdens ever accumulating.\" -Thomas Jefferson I know I mention this book a lot on here, but _Debt: The First 5,000 Years_ is the most amazing book I've ever read on the history of debt, money and slavery. Debt relief has to come. Every great civilization throughout history has had to forgive debts. Current student debt is not even remotely payable. Millennials are the most in debt out of any generation, the lowest paid and most likely to take unpaid internships[1]. They put up with so much more that no previous generation would have even stood for. \"A debt is just the perversion of a promise. It is a promise corrupted by both math and violence. If freedom (real freedom) is the ability to make friends, then it is also, necessarily, the ability to make real promises. What sorts of promises might genuinely free men and women make to one another? At this point we can’t even say. It's more a question of how we can get to a place that will allow us to find out. And the first step in that journey, in turn, is to accept that in the largest scheme of things, just as no one has the right to tell us our true value, no one has the right to tell us what we truly owe.\" Graber, Debt: The First 5,000 Years [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14567158,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-06","story":"A Sociology of the Smartphone","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There's a lot wrong with this article. Do people actually use their phones for transit? I have transit cards from like 10 cities, and transit cards don't run out of batteries. Cards/cash don't run out of batteries either, and you can use them to refill your transit card, and I don't see those getting replaced any time soon. (Germans still use cash for like everything, which feels weird in an EU country). And we still have plenty of phone booths ... if you need to take a piss. I'm pretty sure that's the only thing they're used for in London. Seriously don't touch those; they're disgusting. Do people actually meet legit partners and friends from apps? I've never met anyone off Tinder (I'm not all that cute really), dating websites are a wasteland and all the people I've had meaningful relationships I've met at things I like to do. Sure we have meetups, but they really just facilitate what we use to do with flyers/posters before. Meeting people still takes work. No one meets of Internet chat rooms like in the AOL/Seinfeld era. The Facebook generation of social networks wants you to only connect with people you already know. Smart phones, cell phones, affordable laptops, the Internet, television, radio, magazines, news and print media ... Every advancement is huge. Every advancement initially supplied free and open speech. Printing presses pre-civil war were less than $10k USD to setup (in today's money). Papers crossed the country and spread news and opinions. Large media, adver"},{"id":13421128,"author":"2xlbuds","month":"2017-01","story":"How much does employee turnover really cost?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he makes a ton of assertions mostly backed by often-repeated cliches rather than data","text":"I've seen this talk before, and he makes a ton of assertions mostly backed by often-repeated cliches rather than data (e.g. participation trophies). This is a really good talk I've seen posted in response to this very video before, so I'll post it again. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["KNWh6Kw3ejQ",1],["uI1eRy0uBI8",1],["fwjwePe-HmA",1],["pnv5iKB2hl4",1],["LrObZ_HZZUc",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["niKblgZupOc",1],["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["Eco2s3-0zsQ",1]]},"7vfqkvwW2fs":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an insightful explanation of why modern film scores sound alike, with lessons beyond Marvel.","q":{"x":"You don't have to have seen any Marvel movies, the insights are universal.","i":30778503,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":48440515,"author":"testdelacc1","month":"2026-06","story":"I design with Claude more than Figma now","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Directors score to filler music, making all film music sound alike","quote":"from 5:50 on but the whole video is a masterpiece.","text":"There was this video which showed film directors doing something similar. They film their scene with “filler” music previously used in some other film. Then they hand the scene to the music director and ask a score for it, which forces the director to make something very similar to the filler music. It then makes all film music sound similar. This was only possible due to the “productivity boost” of digital editing pipelines, which allowed directors to edit immediately after filming. [VIDEO] from 5:50 on but the whole video is a masterpiece."},{"id":30778503,"author":"open-source-ux","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: Why are some soundtracks of movies so powerful?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why some scores are memorable and others forgettable, with universal insights","quote":"You don't have to have seen any Marvel movies, the insights are universal.","text":"The YouTube channel Every Frame a Painting has an excellent video about music in films. Why are some scores so memorable while others are easily forgotten? The Marvel Symphonic Universe (2016): [VIDEO] You don't have to have seen any Marvel movies, the insights are universal. (Also worth watching the follow-up video: Hollywood Scores & Soundtracks: What Do They Sound Like? [LINK] )"},{"id":31752057,"author":"Litost","month":"2022-06","story":"Game Design Mimetics (Or, What Happened to Game Design?)","stance":"rec","why":"Compares how memorable Marvel music is versus Star Wars and Bond","quote":"there's an interesting video here comparing Marvel with Star Wars and James Bond","text":"This statement might be more poignant than you think, there's an interesting video here comparing Marvel with Star Wars and James Bond in terms of how memorable any of the music is, because it's almost all cookie cutter: [VIDEO] I think all the points here have value, I've definitely played some really good games recently including some that were mentioned above, but I definitely think there's a general risk averseness because AAA game studios are too big to experiment so a lot of games are rehashed sequels or incremental \"improvements\" (as in the movie industry or whatever's in the music charts). I'd put some of the blame of this on the outcome bias, things are judged on whether the outcome was favourable (e.g. profit) not whether it was the right decision (e.g. let's try something different with novelty, but riskier). As Julia Galef covers in one of her podcasts (will have to find the link), the exec that tries something risky might get a promotion if it succeeds and get sacked if it doesn't as opposed to getting promoted from suggesting something novel regardless of whether it worked. See also \"No one ever got fired for choosing IBM\". How many films that get critical reception now, got poor/indifferent reviews initially because they were just too experimental/novel for the time, e.g. Original Blade Runner. What will people think in 10, 20, 50 years about Marvel films."},{"id":31440374,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2022-05","story":"Vangelis has died","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Marvel movie music is not memorable","quote":"Every Frame A Painting explains it really well in this video on why you can't remember any music from Marvel movies","text":"That's more due to how movie scores are produced these days. Every Frame A Painting explains it really well in this video on why you can't remember any music from Marvel movies: [VIDEO] (well, up until they made that video, at least)"},{"id":30333561,"author":"jstx1","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Why are memorable melodies less common nowadays in movie soundtracks?","stance":"rec","why":"Video essay on temp music and unnoticeable film scores","quote":"Every Frame a Painting has a great video essay on this","text":"Temp music and the idea that music shouldn’t be noticeable in a film. Every Frame a Painting has a great video essay on this - [VIDEO]"},{"id":17135021,"author":"lentil_soup","month":"2018-05","story":"Filmstro: Royalty-Free Music","stance":"rec","why":"Covers Marvel music and the general use of temp music","quote":"I recommend this video about the Marvel Universe music and the general use of \"temp music\".","text":"In that vein, I recommend this video about the Marvel Universe music and the general use of \"temp music\". It touches a lot of the points you mention: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32646069,"author":"bsder","month":"2022-08","story":"Why DALL-E will not steal my job as an illustrator","stance":"reference","why":"Temp music analogy for genericization of AI art","quote":"It will most likely cause a \"genericization\" like \"temp music\" caused in Hollywood movie music.","text":"It will most likely cause a \"genericization\" like \"temp music\" caused in Hollywood movie music. See \"Every Frame a Painting\" for \"The Marvel Symphonic Universe\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":31889030,"author":"Terretta","month":"2022-06","story":"‘Blade Runner’ at 40","stance":"reference","why":"Takes down Marvel scores as safe and forgettable","quote":"Nobody can hum from Marvel movies because they play it safe (aka boring)!","text":"> comprehend why Blade runner even has the possibility of being boring then I'm likely too stupid to be communicating with you Step away from the sugar drinks and try a digital detox. :-) People paid to see Vangelis sound and light shows, when there wasn’t even a movie rolling. Surely with a movie attached, the sound and light isn’t that dull? [LINK] Buried in that thread is a pushback to your premise about MCU, a video that actually excerpts from Inception while taking down those “MCU scores” (pun intended) in particular . Nobody can hum from Marvel movies because they play it safe (aka boring)! [VIDEO] I appreciated a quote from that discussion, tying back to your hypothesis: “People do not remember safe choices. Only bold, original music.” Blade Runner wasn’t safe, though they almost ruined it with the release voice overs trying to make it safer… Interestingly if you go down the rabbit hole, a reply video to that one points out the Star Wars theme everyone remembers was “copied to hell” from countless old movies before it. That response goes on to say the problem is, roughly, digital production sameness. > there is absolute truth to the statement when someone says that movie is in general quite slow and boring Well, no. Boredom comes from within. When you have too many thoughts, or too few, to focus, boredom happens. Blade Runner is an experience, the pacing makes room to take it in. If you don’t open your mind to take it in, if your mind wanders off, or loses the plot, yo"},{"id":30779123,"author":"dangom","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: Why are some soundtracks of movies so powerful?","stance":"reference","why":"Overview of soundtrack design by major filmmakers","quote":"Also see [2] for a birds eye view on soundtrack design by major movie makers.","text":"Read about the concept of Leitmotif [1], that some claim was introduced by Wagner but seems to have already been present in the early 17th century. Leitmotif represents the idea that you can imply dramatic progression through music. The idea is that by using short melodies and simple chord progressions you can bind together elements of a story. Also see [2] for a birds eye view on soundtrack design by major movie makers. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30188508,"author":"SAI_Peregrinus","month":"2022-02","story":"Over-reliance on CGI in movies","stance":"reference","why":"Critiques Marvel film music, cited as one flaw among several","quote":"the music[2] (though they improved since the linked video came out, directly in response to it)","text":"I think we're sort of aggressively agreeing with each other. It's not the CGI (or lack thereof) that makes the modern superhero films bad (except as a sort of demoscene-with-infinite-budget visual spectacle where they're great). It's the writing. To a lesser extent the cinematography is poor, which makes it hard to really feel what's going on. There's lots of unnecessary camera movement, frenetic jump cuts during fights, and all the colors are dull and muted. Every Frame a Painting has a good video on Kurosawa's use of movement in his films, and actually uses The Avengers as a contrasting example and how the cinematography & direction makes the scene in question feel meaningless.[1] So I agree that it's not the CGI that's the problem. It's the writing, the direction, the cinematography, the editing, the music[2] (though they improved since the linked video came out, directly in response to it), etc. The films are bad in many ways, but the CGI isn't really one of them. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20681653,"author":"zwkrt","month":"2019-08","story":"Where did the melody go? (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Why big budget movies lack recognizable melodies","quote":"has a video talking about how big budget movies tend not to have recognizable melodies","text":"Every Frame a Painting has a video talking about how big budget movies tend not to have recognizable melodies, as well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16789789,"author":"tinyrick2","month":"2018-04","story":"“Close to tears, he left at the intermission”: how Kubrick upset Clarke (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Video essay on temp music usage in movies","quote":"talked about the usage of temp music in movies on one of its video","text":"The YouTube channel Every Frame a Painting talked about the usage of temp music in movies on one of its video, titled Marvel Symphonic Universe: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14380917,"author":"buzzybee","month":"2017-05","story":"Making a Neural Synthesizer Instrument","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how temp tracks lead to similar-sounding film scores","quote":"offered a take on how the tendency is to work very closely to a temp track and then ask for something identical","text":"It's not purely the sampling that's doing it to Hollywood: In some fashion, it's the heavily-derisked blockbuster formula to blame, and technology comes along for the ride. Tony Zhou's Every Frame A Painting [0] offered a take on how the tendency is to work very closely to a temp track and then ask for something identical, which of course can only get you increasingly similar sounds. Dan Golding responded to this by adding some nuance, noting that temp tracks have always been in use, so the answer has to be a little more complicated, and he points back to the technology. [1] I would say that the technology is just a piece of the puzzle; you can order in a different type of sound and get it, whether or not you're using a computer-heavy approach. That's aptly demonstrated by the variety seen in indie games, for example. This is a problem that movies have made for themselves by being focused on fitting everything to a formula. The occasional film does slip through that has a great score that draws on something bigger than other films(for one example: Scott Pilgrim vs the World). [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}],"r":[["jILgxeNBK_8",1],["xguam0TKMw8",1],["OqEeIG8aPPk",1],["A4UMyTnlaMY",1],["h7apO7q16V0",1],["yiw6_JakZFc",1],["sD0NjbwqlYw",1],["bBC-nXj3Ng4",1],["6_yU9eJ0NxA",1],["FACK2knC08E",1],["wO2lWmgEK1Y",1],["iuumnjJWFO4",1],["LsGZ_2RuJ2A",1],["Unzc731iCUY",1],["H2tuKiiznsY",1],["otAcmD6XEEE",1],["3-son3EJTrU",1],["6y8XgGhXkTQ",1],["WwslBPj8GgI",1],["IAF8DjrQSSk",1]]},"w3WYdYyjek4":{"wy":"HN commenters, including TigerBeetle people, recommend it as the explanation of a methodology for building defect-free, high-assurance software.","q":{"x":"The TigerStyle video is well worth watching for anyone who wants to build defect-free software systems","i":40851012,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40851012,"author":"samatman","month":"2024-07","story":"A write-ahead log is not a universal part of durability","stance":"strong_rec","why":"TigerStyle philosophy for building defect-free software","quote":"The TigerStyle video is well worth watching for anyone who wants to build defect-free software systems","text":"I second this recommendation. I'm impressed by what TigerBeetle has accomplished, and would love to see more of the industry approach software engineering with their philosophy and rigour. The TigerStyle video is well worth watching for anyone who wants to build defect-free software systems, or recognizes that this is the correct thing to aim for. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45783444,"author":"jorangreef","month":"2025-11","story":"Tigerbeetle Simulator","stance":"rec","why":"Full background for the deterministic simulation visualization","quote":"Here’s the full background context and explanation from our talk, TigerStyle, for which we developed this sim","text":"Joran from TigerBeetle here! This is a “walking sim” real visualization of our Deterministic Simulation Testing harness, the VOPR. Typically we do DST headless in the terminal but this is a visualization, more or less the same thing but with a gaming engine on top. It’s running real TigerBeetle code, but with simulated network/storage and fault injection, and multiple replicas in a cluster, all compiled to WASM and running in the browser, and you can interact with the simulation to poke it and watch the consensus recover. Here’s the full background context and explanation from our talk, TigerStyle, for which we developed this sim: [VIDEO] See also a shorter explanation: [LINK]"},{"id":45705246,"author":"jorangreef","month":"2025-10","story":"TigerBeetle and Synadia pledge $512k to the Zig Software Foundation","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on TigerStyle as a methodology","quote":"For more on TigerStyle as a methodology, hope you enjoy this talk","text":"You're right! In the past, distributed databases written without Deterministic Simulation Testing (DST) would typically take around ~10 years (~5 years for the consensus protocol and ~5 years for the storage engine). And then not without bugs. However, and we write about this also in the post, but TigerStyle and DST enabled us to ship TigerBeetle to production in 3.5 years, in less time, to a higher standard, and to be the first distributed database with an explicit storage fault model (Kyle Kingsbury added new storage fault injectors to Jepsen) solving Protocol-Aware Recovery. Our Jepsen audit is here (also linked in the post): [LINK] For more on TigerStyle as a methodology, hope you enjoy this talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44603752,"author":"jorangreef","month":"2025-07","story":"NIH is cheaper than the wrong dependency","stance":"rec","why":"TigerStyle ships safer software in less time","quote":"TigerStyle is not only about shipping safer software, but shipping it in less time","text":"We apply this pretty much fully at TigerBeetle [1]. Reason being that TigerStyle is not only about shipping safer software, but shipping it in less time [2], factoring in the Total Cost of Ownership to end users. In other words, not only optimizing our development time, but also all the time spent in production with subsequent fixes. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44603827,"author":"jorangreef","month":"2025-07","story":"NIH is cheaper than the wrong dependency","stance":"rec","why":"Background motivation behind TigerStyle","quote":"Some of the background motivation behind TigerStyle","text":"Appreciated your post, Lewis! From the beginning, TigerStyle was intended not only for TigerBeetle as infrastructure, but for software (up and down the stack) in general. Nobody wants buggier, slower software. And so TigerStyle as a methodology was codified primarily to address this problem: How to write “higher quality software in less time”? [1] Before designing TigerBeetle, I was finding the principles more and more valuable, regardless of whether I was doing frontend or backend work, and so brought them into TigerBeetle. Of course, software higher up is likely to use some dependencies, but there's always a cost, and this principle (“do more with less”), at least in my experience, tends to prove valuable when applied. [1] Some of the background motivation behind TigerStyle: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44606975,"author":"jorangreef","month":"2025-07","story":"NIH is cheaper than the wrong dependency","stance":"rec","why":"TigerStyle enabled shipping TigerBeetle with high quality","quote":"without TigerStyle [0], there's no way we could have shipped TigerBeetle from 0 to production in 3.5 years","text":"> How does TigerBeetle's existence support the argument that rolling your own dependencies is better than using off-the-shelf tools? At least from my own experience, without TigerStyle [0], there's no way we could have shipped TigerBeetle from 0 to production in 3.5 years, with the same quality, and to the same tolerances: [LINK] Whereas, for example, if we'd used off-the-shelf dependencies such as Raft or RocksDB, it would have been a nightmare, and taken longer, just to fix them up to solve our storage fault model and implement Protocol-Aware Recovery correctly. Even then, we'd have been stuck with a systems language of the last 30 years (C/C++), when we could have invested in a language for the next 30 and with a more powerful toolchain. Of course, we'd also have been without Deterministic Simulation Testing, because most software dependencies are typically not designed for DST. But finally (and to your point about sustainability), without TigerStyle, we wouldn't have had the company, or the investment, team, and customers we have today, or be as far along financially. Not saying that you need to share our engineering philosophy. But it works for us. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43646566,"author":"jorangreef","month":"2025-04","story":"We Designed TigerBeetle's Docs from Scratch","stance":"rec","why":"Explains TigerBeetle's engineering methodology","quote":"To understand our engineering methodology, and why we prefer to paint than sculpt, see TigerStyle","text":"Joran from TigerBeetle here! We didn't design our docs because it was \"a fun thing\" (as suggested) but rather because we simply care deeply about the experience of developers reading our docs. For example, concerning performance and offline use, which were further reasons we gave in the post. We have a high bar for taking on dependencies. We don't take on dependencies automatically without justification. It's just not a philosophy that we share, to assume or to insist that everything needs to be a dependency. (The discussion on CSP in our post was also not given as motivation, but as an example of the thought process that went into this. Again, as per the post, it's a matter of defense-in-depth. We have plans for how our docs will be used in future, that you may not be aware of, and security is important.) Finally, we're happy with the result, the project was small and didn't take long. We're used to \"painting\" things like this fairly quickly. It's just easier for us than trying to \"sculpt\" off the shelf dependencies. That's not to suggest that everyone needs to paint like we do at TigerBeetle, but it's equally true that not everyone needs to sculpt either. [1] [1] To understand our engineering methodology, and why we prefer to paint than sculpt, see TigerStyle: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41526836,"author":"samatman","month":"2024-09","story":"Why Haskell?","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point for understanding Zig in high-assurance systems","quote":"If you'd like to understand Zig (and what I mean) better, this video is a good start","text":"> Zig will be easy to understand and use but too unsafe to use for important applications This is an outside perspective on Zig, and I have to say, not an informed one. If you'd like to understand Zig (and what I mean) better, this video is a good start: [VIDEO] Zig is, right now, being used for high-assurance systems where correctness is a terminal value, and it provides many tools and affordances to assist in doing so. It isn't a good choice for projects which give lip-service to correctness, but for ones which actually mean it, and are willing and able to put in the effort and expense to achieve it, it's an excellent choice. I'm willing to gloss that domain as \"important applications\", but perhaps you meant something different by that term."},{"id":43332761,"author":"agentultra","month":"2025-03","story":"Formal Verification of Zero-Downtime Database Migration in PlusCal","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as sign developers value up-front design","quote":"And I think more software developers are starting to realize this [0]","text":"I recall learning TLA+ around 2015/2016 to solve a hard bug in an OpenStack deployment. I was miffed that I was some 17 years into my career and had only learned about it then. It was so useful! Since then I've used it for similar purposes as this. I try to share with my team when I do this kind of work. But often folks are highly resistant to it so I use it on my own when I'm working on a hard project. Spending the time up-front to work out the design is often more cost effective in terms of time and money than iterating in production towards a, \"more correct design.\" Getting it right first sounds hard, and it's challenging, but it's worth it for the kinds of projects where mistakes are costly, difficult to diagnose and to fix. And I think more software developers are starting to realize this [0] (even if they're using different methods). [0]: [VIDEO] Update : grammar/spelling"},{"id":36510721,"author":"jorangreef","month":"2023-06","story":"Fintech Engineering Mistakes","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Hey, Joran from TigerBeetle here! You raise a fair question. Coincidentally, one of the reasons we picked Zig was for how readable it was, and strikingly so, even for high level programmers who might not understand systems programming or C. Because Zig reads like TypeScript, and we were working in payment switches where the majority of programmers could read that. This particular switch, in fact, had this same business requirement, that programmers should be able to at least read the systems language. But generally, our experience has been that people who understand C will understand how to maintain Zig [0]. Zig's toolchain is also more accessible, and across all platforms. Zig's compiler is already being used by Uber for hermetic builds. It's also easy to learn. You can pick up Zig in a week and be comfortable in a month. Zig has a simple grammar. I love how, when we have someone join the team, we never have a discussion about how to learn Zig, as if it's a difficult language to master (like C++!). Rather, there's excitement around learning the language, even ahead of starting at work, and within a day or two they're committing. We made this decision for TigerBeetle in July 2020, and didn't take it lightly. We had already followed Zig's progress for 2 years by that point, and many factors were considered [1][2][3]. C was the other contender, given that we had to handle memory allocation failure. The crux of the decision, then, was whether to invest in a systems language of t"}],"r":[["NRoUC9P1PmA",1],["zeLToGnjIUM",1],["mwrA5IRGpfU",1],["oCZMoY3q2uM",1]]},"CaCcOwJPytQ":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends this series as a clear, step-by-step way to really understand Kalman filtering.","q":{"x":"It's really worth watching even just the first couple videos to get a deeper understanding of this very useful technique.","i":29475414,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29475414,"author":"michelpp","month":"2021-12","story":"How a Kalman filter works, in pictures","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Walks from rationale through algebraic example to full matrix solution","quote":"It's really worth watching even just the first couple videos to get a deeper understanding of this very useful technique.","text":"Michel van Biezen has an excellent video series on Kalman filters: [VIDEO] He walks you though all the way from the rationale, to a simple algebraic example, to the full blown Linear Algebraic Matrix solution. It's really worth watching even just the first couple videos to get a deeper understanding of this very useful technique."},{"id":39348769,"author":"cocostation","month":"2024-02","story":"Kalman Filter Explained Simply","stance":"rec","why":"Clearer Kalman filter explanation than the original post","quote":"I really like this set of videos for explaining the KF.","text":"I really like this set of videos for explaining the KF. I 'got it' more than I did with the material on the original post. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37880932,"author":"dpflan","month":"2023-10","story":"Kalman Filter Tutorial: Kalman Filter from the Ground Up","stance":"rec","why":"Step-by-step Kalman filter video playlist","quote":"Here is another tutorial on Kalman Filters, step-by-step video playlist","text":"Here is another tutorial on Kalman Filters, step-by-step video playlist -- [VIDEO] Once you get the intuition, Kalman filters are really interesting. As are particle filters -- those are fun to work with and visualize."},{"id":16578812,"author":"SatvikBeri","month":"2018-03","story":"Quick explanation of how a Kalman filter works","stance":"rec","why":"55 short videos giving a detailed Kalman filter deep dive","quote":"I found this sequence of videos really helpful","text":"Great explanation! If you want a very detailed deep dive into Kalman Filters, I found this sequence of videos really helpful: [VIDEO] It's 55 short videos, each one going over a specific topic or example."},{"id":13450939,"author":"JamesBarney","month":"2017-01","story":"How a Kalman filter works, in pictures (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"55-lecture series explaining Kalman filtering","quote":"It's a great set of 55 video lectures on Kalman filtering.","text":"For anyone who read this blog post and thought \"Wow that's interesting, but I don't understand half of this. I need a whole youtube series to explain this to me.\" Well we're in luck. [VIDEO] . It's a great set of 55 video lectures on Kalman filtering. (I'm only on number 5 but so far they've been great)"},{"id":42752190,"author":"dpflan","month":"2025-01","story":"Kalman Filter Tutorial","stance":"reference","why":"Lectures used while learning Kalman filters","quote":"Anyone else watch Michael van Biezem (with the bow tie) lectures on Kalman Filters while learning this topic?","text":"Anyone else watch Michael van Biezem (with the bow tie) lectures on Kalman Filters while learning this topic? - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xnez6tloNSQ",1],["o9pEzgHorH0",1],["04ERSb06dOg",1],["Lu56xVlZ40M",1]]},"Y6U728AZnV0":{"wy":"HN cites it whenever people question whether more exercise is always better, especially extreme endurance running.","q":{"x":"I think about this TED Talk from 2012 a lot..","i":45240098,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45240098,"author":"browningstreet","month":"2025-09","story":"Why are so many young, fit, non-smoking women getting lung cancer?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Data-driven talk that changed the commenter's fitness goals.","quote":"I think about this TED Talk from 2012 a lot..","text":"I think about this TED Talk from 2012 a lot.. and he presents some data. It changed some of my goals, and I’m a bit more multi-sport than I used to be. Run for your life! At a comfortable pace, and not too far: James O'Keefe at TED×UMKC [VIDEO]"},{"id":42100811,"author":"browningstreet","month":"2024-11","story":"Five minutes of exercise a day could lower blood pressure","stance":"rec","why":"Longevity stats for runners; recommends running a little, not a lot.","quote":"He recommends running a little, not a lot.","text":"This guy looked at longevity stats for runners. He recommends running a little, not a lot. 2012 TEDx Talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33641221,"author":"twawaaay","month":"2022-11","story":"Aerobic exercise can reduce the risk of metastatic cancer in mice: study","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how intense daily running can scar heart tissue.","quote":"I think watching this Ted might be helpful","text":"I believe running your heart at such high intensity every day isn't particularly healthy. It causes scarring of the heart tissue and possible problems as you age. I think watching this Ted might be helpful: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24880997,"author":"andrewstuart","month":"2020-10","story":"How to Live with Dying","stance":"rec","why":"Cardiologist and runner on heart scarring from long-distance exercise.","quote":"overexercise can damage the heart by James OKeefe, a cardiologist and lifetime obsessive runner","text":"On the topic of how overexercise can damage the heart by James OKeefe, a cardiologist and lifetime obsessive runner: Notably he points out that they tested people blood after doing long distance events and found that many of them had chemicals usually found in heart attack victims, indicating heart damage. Basically he says that the long term overexercise/heart damage means the heart muscle constantly has microtears which heal up and the healed tissue is scar tissue which is hard and not like normal heart tissue - much harder and less elastic. So people who overtrain end up with a heart much older than their physical age. [VIDEO] Transcript: [LINK] Another video: [LINK] And from another author on extreme exercise and the heart: [LINK]"},{"id":17948967,"author":"andrewstuart","month":"2018-09","story":"Extreme Athleticism Is the New Midlife Crisis","stance":"rec","why":"Cardiac researcher explains how extreme exercise can damage the heart.","quote":"Here's an interesting video if you are an extreme athlete","text":"Here's an interesting video if you are an extreme athlete: it is a cardiac researcher and lifelong exerciser who identifies that such exercise patterns damage the heart: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49329680,"author":"andrewstuart","month":"2026-08","story":"The Mysterious Syndrome Destroying Endurance Athletes","stance":"reference","why":"Source for claim that ultra training damages the heart.","quote":"","text":"It’s well known ultra training destroys your heart. In any sport. They tested the blood of marathon runners and found heart attack markers in many of them because the heart muscle is being damaged and healing and scarring and hardening the muscle leaving young ultra trainers with the heart of an 80 year old. The best modern advice is don’t overtrain. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40747085,"author":"ngcc_hk","month":"2024-06","story":"How to live with dying (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Quoted older comment citing the talk on overexercise.","quote":"","text":"Big issue of doing running. ——- read the old HN below and if not at least these “andrewstuart on Oct 24, 2020 | prev | next [–] On the topic of how overexercise can damage the heart by James OKeefe, a cardiologist and lifetime obsessive runner: Notably he points out that they tested people blood after doing long distance events and found that many of them had chemicals usually found in heart attack victims, indicating heart damage. Basically he says that the long term overexercise/heart damage means the heart muscle constantly has microtears which heal up and the healed tissue is scar tissue which is hard and not like normal heart tissue - much harder and less elastic. So people who overtrain end up with a heart much older than their physical age. [VIDEO] Transcript: [LINK] ... Another video: [LINK] And from another author on extreme exercise and the heart: [LINK] ... DoreenMichele on Oct 24, 202… about walking “"},{"id":35885671,"author":"gregwebs","month":"2023-05","story":"The World’s Oldest Ultramarathon Runner Is Racing Against Death","stance":"reference","why":"Warns that running too far or fast may be hazardous.","quote":"","text":"Great story. Think twice though before adopting mind over matter approach. The Born to Run hero died at ~60 while doing a 12 mile training run of massive heart damage. Running too far or even with too fast a pace may be hazardous for the average individual according to scientists studying this subject [VIDEO]"},{"id":30607784,"author":"anonymouse94759","month":"2022-03","story":"I am the healthiest person I know, and I got cancer","stance":"reference","why":"Argues long-distance running is not healthy for the heart.","quote":"Running long distances regularly isn't healthy","text":">My uncle was a professional national level runner Running long distances regularly isn't healthy: [VIDEO] Multiple studies have found a U-shaped curve of CAC scores as a function of regular running distance/intensity: [LINK] Conclusion A significantly higher rate of coronary artery calcification existed in long-term marathon, ultramarathon, and extreme runners than in submarathon runners. Marathoners and ultramarathoners also had a higher incidence of calcification, as well as higher average plaque burden, as compared to a standard database. Marathoners and ultramarathoners also had above-average coronary calcium scores as compared to a national database. Other forms of endurance exercise (swimming, cycling) have similar effects if taken to excess . Sprinting short distances is great, and walking seems to have no upperbound on how much you can safely do of it (the Hadza do it all day and have childlike levels of LDL and minimal CAC)."},{"id":27100722,"author":"ljhsiung","month":"2021-05","story":"The Animal Is Tired","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for moderate running advice.","quote":"","text":"Numerous studies show that exercise provides U-shaped benefits to health [1], [2]. Specific to cardiovascular disease, repeat marathon runners [3] have higher calcium buildup than those who do not run in marathons, which as Robin might say, is one of those cases where the animal is driven too hard. While you have a point for extreme exertion, I'm not sure what you mean by \"lazy\". Current general recommendations are 150 minutes a week, or <30 minutes a day [4], which definitely falls underneath moderate exercise. I don't think \"no one\" is ignoring these recommendations (or, at least, they agree with them-- few people follow them :^)). As for your grandpa's longevity-- your genetics is just a risk modifier, not a nullifier. Wearing a seatbelt doesn't prevent your death, it just reduces your odds of it during a car crash. So, at least, I hope you still wear your seatbelt, because the time cost of this activity is so miniscule compared to the time reward. As I like to say, \"Run for your life! At a comfortable pace, and not too far\" [5] EDIT: I should add-- I ran competitively a few years ago. My \"animal\" is no longer as fierce, and thus am a bit more moderate these days. But there is a large emotional aspect and validation to pushing yourself that goes beyond physical health. As with any sport-- running, swimming, football, basketball-- the physical toll is worth it. This, though, is what I would classify under Robin's description of a \"tired animal\" and is likely what Bolt, MJ, "},{"id":11064330,"author":"Matrixik","month":"2016-02","story":"Dead Men Write No Code","stance":"reference","why":"Talk arguing moderate running is better than extreme amounts.","quote":"some of something is great does not mean more of it is better","text":"As always some of something is great does not mean more of it is better: \"Run for your life! At a comfortable pace, and not too far: James O'Keefe at TEDxUMKC\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wE5G1kTndI4",1],["dCSIXLIzhzk",1]]},"g1ib43q3uXQ":{"wy":"Cited in threads about teaching kids and beginners to code as research-based evidence that explicit instruction beats pure discovery.","q":{"x":"The talk is on YouTube and I gotta say, it's very insightful.","i":23030606,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23030606,"author":"kashfi","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: As a programmer, how should I teach coding to my kid?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Keynote on effective ways to teach programming.","quote":"The talk is on YouTube and I gotta say, it's very insightful.","text":"Also, I attended Strange Loop conference last September and there was a keynote speaker who discussed effective ways of teaching programming to kids. The talk is on YouTube and I gotta say, it's very insightful. \"How to teach programming (and other things)?\" by Felienne Hermans Link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24339299,"author":"greggman3","month":"2020-09","story":"How to Learn Everything: The MasterClass Diaries","stance":"rec","why":"Research suggests telling learners how to do things.","quote":"I think she makes a good point.","text":"According to this person there is plenty of research saying you should tell them how to do it [VIDEO] I think she makes a good point. We don't hand people a guitar and say \"figure it out on your own\". Cooking shows don't generally show the final dish and say \"if you want to make this yourself figure it out on your own\". You might say computer programming is different but I suspect for most people here you were taught things like sorting algorithms, database queries, lexers and parsing algorithms, etc. You were not made to figure them on your own. Sure maybe you were self motivated and looked up the topics by yourself but likely you mostly learned by looking at existing code and over time figured out how to see what it was doing and how to modify it or apply the same ideas."},{"id":24068756,"author":"greggman3","month":"2020-08","story":"The Hacker Way: How I taught my nephew to program","stance":"rec","why":"Research says self-directed study fits most students poorly.","quote":"Self directed study doesn't fit most students according to the research presented in the video.","text":"I was self directed as apparently are many software engineers so I've always assumed that the best way to teach would be to let kids direct themselves and they can ask questions if they get stuck. Well, this video claims I'm wrong. Self directed study doesn't fit most students according to the research presented in the video. [VIDEO] you can jump to 11:00 if you want to jump to the part about learning."},{"id":23053225,"author":"chrisdone","month":"2020-05","story":"History of Logo","stance":"rec","why":"Challenges exploration-only pedagogy in programming education.","quote":"It's a worthwhile talk.","text":"There's a StrangeLoop talk about teaching programming by Felienne Hermans ( [VIDEO] ) that claims that all fields have an \"exploration vs instruction\" debate on how to teach their field, but this debate is apparently absent from the field of programming for the most part, wherein the \"mess around and figure it out\" exploration style of LOGO and Papert (\"every time you teach something, you deprive a [student] of the pleasure and benefit of discovery\") is the predominant unquestioned wisdom. Felienne wants more pedagogy fights. It's a worthwhile talk. I love LOGO and I think our generation of programmers are very sympathetic Papert's philosophy due to most of us being self-taught to some degree (you can see the fond nostalgia already on display in the comments here), but Felienne made me think about it more critically."},{"id":21736710,"author":"noelwelsh","month":"2019-12","story":"Learning to code vs. learning to automate","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks myth that only self-taught programmers succeed.","quote":"This talk addresses that misconception","text":"I'm struggling to parse \"essentially motivationally self-taught\" but if you mean to say something like \"only self-taught programmers will successfully learn to program\" that's a myth that isn't backed up by the evidence and serves to exclude people from programming. This talk addresses that misconception: [VIDEO] The paper discusses \"constructionism\" (which, loosely put, is the idea you give someone a toy and let them figure it out): [LINK] There is more out there but that's all I have time to find right now."},{"id":48407038,"author":"socalgal2","month":"2026-06","story":"Why I'm Skeptical About Efforts to Revolutionize Schooling","stance":"reference","why":"Data suggests forcing students to figure it out is not best.","quote":"which claims data shows students being forced to \"figure it out\" is not the best way to learn.","text":"There's also this: [VIDEO] which claims data shows students being forced to \"figure it out\" is not the best way to learn. Most HNer disagree with this."},{"id":29053501,"author":"greggman3","month":"2021-10","story":"How to help a programming student get unstuck","stance":"reference","why":"Studies claim giving the answer is best teaching.","quote":"this talk claims that studies show giving the answer is the best way to teach","text":"I think there is a difference between \"figure it out yourself\" and asking questions of the student. In any case, this talk claims that studies show giving the answer is the best way to teach (don't shoot the messenger) [VIDEO]"},{"id":24135629,"author":"greggman3","month":"2020-08","story":"That coworker who never stops refactoring","stance":"reference","why":"Research favoring explicit explanation over figuring things out alone.","quote":"Extensive research says your intuition that figuring out on your own is better than having someone explain it to you is wrong","text":"Extensive research says your intuition that figuring out on your own is better than having someone explain it to you is wrong [VIDEO]"},{"id":23065917,"author":"greggman3","month":"2020-05","story":"What Makes a Good Teacher? (2001)","stance":"reference","why":"Research contradicting stepwise-guidance teaching claim.","quote":"This sounds like it doesn't match this research which someone posted yesterday","text":"> they guide you to take the very first step, nothing more. Then just the second step, nothing more This sounds like it doesn't match this research which someone posted yesterday [VIDEO]"}]},"iD9xk3SDSYc":{"wy":"HN holds it up as a showcase of what a stock Amiga 500 can do and as one of the best demos ever in technique and storytelling.","q":{"x":"I feel like this is one of the best demos of all time from both a technical point of view plus storytelling.","i":48030099,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48030099,"author":"kw3b","month":"2026-05","story":"Write some software, give it away for free","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of the best demos ever technically and in storytelling.","quote":"I feel like this is one of the best demos of all time from both a technical point of view plus storytelling.","text":"This is awesome as hell, haven't seen that one yet. I love that cracktros/demos are still a thing. A cracktro a day keeps the slop away. If you like that one, you'd probably dig this. I feel like this is one of the best demos of all time from both a technical point of view plus storytelling. Dropped back in 2019. Warms my heart. The Black Lotus - Eon: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39498681,"author":"dansalvato","month":"2024-02","story":"Coding the anime \"woosh\" screen on Amiga","stance":"rec","why":"Demoscene favorites running on a stock Amiga 500.","quote":"The demoscene has gotten insanely good since around 2017.","text":"I use the same trick today! I have some \"benchmark\" macros set up which forcibly override the background color when I want to see where in the frame my operations are taking place. > Didn't know there were people still trying to squeeze more out of that chipset nowadays. You will be amazed. The demoscene has gotten insanely good since around 2017. Here are some of my favorites that all run on a stock Amiga 500 (with the trapdoor memory): [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":37860335,"author":"dansalvato","month":"2023-10","story":"Grind – A first person shooter for Amiga 500","stance":"rec","why":"Favorite recent demos running on a stock A500.","quote":"Here are some of my personal favorites from recent years.","text":"Certainly! Here are some of my personal favorites from recent years. These all run on a stock A500 (with the ubiquitous trapdoor memory expansion). [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":25246849,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2020-11","story":"Little Things That Made Amiga Great","stance":"rec","why":"Favorite Amiga demo by The Black Lotus.","quote":"Two Amiga demos that I like from The Black Lotus (TBL)","text":"The Amiga has a very impressive demoscene... Two Amiga demos that I like from The Black Lotus (TBL): - Eon (2019): [VIDEO] (4:16 is my favorite part) - Starstruck (2006): [LINK]"},{"id":20690596,"author":"unixhero","month":"2019-08","story":"We Made Amiga, They Fucked It Up (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what Amigas can do in 2019.","quote":"This is what Amigas can do in 2019","text":"Vampire V4 SA (coming 2019/Q3) ------ There is a new Amiga being developed. It is an accelerator board with a brand new 68080 processor. The thing is, it being FPGA, it actually doesn't need to be hosted in an actual Amiga any more. Therefore they are planning to make a standalone, essentially new Amiga. The Apollo 68080 Accelerator - New Amiga: [LINK] Also. This is what Amigas can do in 2019: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["rxOKEsBx4NU",1],["rOzrJjdZDRQ",1],["vYLEuQrvND0",1],["mII9NZ8MMVM",1],["ZUvGfuLlZus",1],["vgT4Y30DkaA",1],["RPdB_zdyMbM",1],["3Fx5Q8xGU8k",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["Xe5AE-qYan8",1],["8pBPZMUcsh0",1],["P4VBqTViEx4",1],["yFIa-Mc2KSk",1],["bpAi70WWBlw",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1],["PdO3IP0Pro8",1],["Tml94je2edk",1]]},"YpuX-5E7xoU":{"wy":"HN people cite it in threads on vehicle size and safety as a clear explanation tying grille height to deaths.","q":{"x":"This guy ties grill height directly to fatalities.","i":39919737,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":47936758,"author":"stickfigure","month":"2026-04","story":"UAE Leaves OPEC and OPEC+","stance":"rec","why":"Explains tall grilles are an aesthetic choice that safety standards could fix.","quote":"Tall grilles are a purely aesthetic choice.","text":"It isn't about weight, it's way stupider than that: [VIDEO] Tall grilles are a purely aesthetic choice. We could create safety standards for pedestrian impacts and end this inane trend. And still drive trucks!"},{"id":41462090,"author":"mech975","month":"2024-09","story":"What to do about America's killer cars","stance":"rec","why":"Offers a good view on other aspects of vehicle size trends.","quote":"offers a good view on some other aspects of the situation.","text":"CAFE standards were written in such a way as to encourage large vehicle footprints to work around the requirements of the standards. Motorcycling youtuber fortnine offers a good view on some other aspects of the situation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41310487,"author":"Workaccount2","month":"2024-08","story":"A road safety plan that will lead to cars communicating with each other","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why tall truck fronts kill pedestrians and motorcyclists.","quote":"Thank you Ryan from Fortnine.","text":"People just love trucks that are practically purpose built to kill pedestrians and motorcyclists. [VIDEO] Thank you Ryan from Fortnine."},{"id":39919737,"author":"kungfooey","month":"2024-04","story":"Plunging Pickup Truck Sales Threaten Detroit's Profit Engine","stance":"rec","why":"Ties grille height directly to fatalities.","quote":"This guy ties grill height directly to fatalities.","text":"This guy ties grill height directly to fatalities. They really are a menace, but it's by design. Quoth one designer, \"I want it to look like a fist coming through the air.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":39642993,"author":"fnimick","month":"2024-03","story":"A 7k-Pound Car Smashed Through a Guardrail. That's Bad News for All of Us","stance":"rec","why":"Shows a minor grille change could cut deaths and injuries.","quote":"it would take only a minor change to have a huge impact and reduce deaths and injuries","text":"Not only that, it would take only a minor change to have a huge impact and reduce deaths and injuries: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48649252,"author":"genxy","month":"2026-06","story":"The Deadly Rise of Giant Trucks and S.U.V.s","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Grille Trend that Kills 509 People per Year [VIDEO] video discussed previously [LINK] These Stupid Trucks are Literally Killing Us [LINK] The original designer wanted the truck to feel like you were driving a fist, punching through the air. So we are killing people so that the driver can have an aggressive aesthetic. And the design has spread like a contagious meme, even BMWs have it now."},{"id":48652909,"author":"brikym","month":"2026-06","story":"The Deadly Rise of Giant Trucks and S.U.V.s","stance":"reference","why":"Shows engine bays of tall trucks are mostly empty.","quote":"it's possible to stand inside the mostly empty engine bays of these things as FortNine showed","text":"These are the epitome of individualism. It's a Fuck you I'm safe . They're even designed to look like fists. Here is a quote from General Motors truck designer Karan Moorjani: “I remember wanting it to make it feel very locomotive… my first week in Detroit I was driving through downtown and seeing the fist of Joe Louis, and remember thinking that’s what this truck should look like – a massive fist moving through the air.” ... we spent a lot of time making sure that when you stand in front of this thing it looks like it’s going to come get you. It’s got that pissed-off feel, but not in a boyish way, still looking mature. It just had to have that imposing look,” explained the GM designer.\" The design of these things is deliberately antisocial. The huge hood is not even needed since it's possible to stand inside the mostly empty engine bays of these things as FortNine showed: [VIDEO] But it's not just the hood heights wrong with modern vehicles. Multiple design trends are hostile to other road users and reduce safety equality if you will. - Headlight hues are white and blinding - Headlight positioning has increased with the hood height which tends to blind people. - The pillars in vehicles are really thick and raked which is safer for the occupants but also means visibility is reduced. Great, so now people in bigger, newer, safer vehicles are more likely to hit pedestrians and people in older, smaller vehicles. - Windows are more tinted and often smaller meaning vehicles are opa"},{"id":45921822,"author":"bryanlarsen","month":"2025-11","story":"Tesla Is Recalling Cybertrucks Again","stance":"reference","why":"Lowering hood height 10 inches makes truck far less likely to kill a child.","quote":"lowering the hood height by 10 inches will make the truck 81% less likely to kill a kid in a collision.","text":"Pickups are about 10 inches taller now than they used to be. That seems like a lot to me. According to the youtube video that a different commenter linked ( [VIDEO] ), lowering the hood height by 10 inches will make the truck 81% less likely to kill a kid in a collision."},{"id":44935200,"author":"YeGoblynQueenne","month":"2025-08","story":"'Safety Today Is a Luxury,' Giorgetto Giugiaro Says After His Crash","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"And yet, observational data suggests that driving a huge car makes it harder for you to get injured an easier for everyone else. For example: \"First off, the vitriol against large vehicles is misplaced. The larger the vehicle, the safer for its occupants\". [VIDEO] \"American roads are deadliest than ever for pedestrians, cyclists, and, most of all, motorcyclists\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":42203613,"author":"david-gpu","month":"2024-11","story":"How do cars do in out-of-sample crash testing? (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows tall blunt hoods increase fatalities.","quote":"it is known that tall blunt hoods increase fatalities","text":"I wish car testing was less about the safety of the people inside of the car and more about the safety of the people outside of it, like pedestrians, cyclists and other vehicles. After all, people buying a car already have a strong incentive to purchase something that is safe for themselves, but how many people put any thought into reducing the risk that the force upon others? For context, pedestrian and cycling deaths have increased for the past decade in North America [0] and it is known that tall blunt hoods increase fatalities [1]. Yet, nothing is being done about it in NA as far as I know. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41491504,"author":"sitkack","month":"2024-09","story":"NHTSA Proposes New Vehicle Safety Standard to Better Protect Pedestrians","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Grille Trend that Kills 509 People per Year [VIDEO] I thought that pedestrian safety was already part of mandatory crash worthiness testing."}],"r":[["SBvclVcesEE",1],["31CcclqEiZw",1]]},"Cym4TZwTCNU":{"wy":"HN points people to it as the clearest account of why Datomic is designed the way it is and of the problems with conventional database architecture.","q":{"x":"Rich Hickey's talk on the design of Datomic is also something every programmer should see at least once","i":22807290,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22807290,"author":"slowmovintarget","month":"2020-04","story":"The Impedance Mismatch Is Our Fault (2012)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Rich Hickey on the design of Datomic.","quote":"Rich Hickey's talk on the design of Datomic is also something every programmer should see at least once","text":"Rich Hickey's talk on the design of Datomic is also something every programmer should see at least once: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17808857,"author":"pgt","month":"2018-08","story":"Idempotence: What is it and why should I care?","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces the fact-based database Datomic by Rich Hickey.","quote":"You will enjoy the fact-based database Datomic, which is the reason Rich Hickey made Clojure","text":"You will enjoy the fact-based database Datomic, which is the reason Rich Hickey made Clojure: [VIDEO] Datalog is a much, much, much query language better than SQL: [LINK]"},{"id":10189644,"author":"shotgun","month":"2015-09","story":"Ask HN: CTM gave CS the “kernel language”. Is there a “kernel database”?","stance":"rec","why":"Hickey explains traditional database architecture and the reasons behind Datomic.","quote":"I feel you might enjoy presentations by Rich Hickey wherein he describes traditional database architectures","text":"Although tangential to your question, I feel you might enjoy presentations by Rich Hickey wherein he describes traditional database architectures and presents the whys and wherefores behind Datomic. For example: [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":4600489,"author":"MoosePlissken","month":"2012-10","story":"Rethinking caching in web apps","stance":"rec","why":"Rich Hickey on the design thinking behind Datomic.","quote":"It's long but I thought it was worthwhile.","text":"We recently started using Solr in a similar manner where I work. We don't go as far as to pack an index in with our code, but each page serving instance has an embedded Solr server that it uses to pull a lot of fairly static data. Those indexes are updated nightly from a master Solr server which imports its data from a normal relational database. So far it has worked really well, but I don't think it really offers many advantages scalability wise over other NoSQL solutions. Like most other solutions it trades fast writes/consistent reads for fast reads, which is fine in many cases. Around the time we were adding Solr though, I heard about Datomic ( [LINK] ). I haven't had time to really investigate it, but it seems to me that Datomic provides fast reads in a manner very similar to the Solr configuration you described. Live versions of the database's index are deployed to agents that live on your app servers, but unlike the Solr configuration these agents receive updates to the index in real time. At a glance at least, it seems to offer all of the advantages of the Solr solution while still allowing relatively fast updates to the data. This is an interesting talk on the topic by Rich Hickey (who created Datomic): [VIDEO] . It's long but I thought it was worthwhile."}],"r":[["R3xX37RGJKE",1],["d6PyyatqJSE",1],["7oWip00iXbo",1],["5hVmeOCJjOU",1],["esQyYGezS7c",1],["xt1KNDmOYqA",1]]},"EOwxxsPaogY":{"wy":"Recommended as an entertaining overview of Harley's decline and as a case study in selling a lifestyle rather than a product.","q":{"x":"This very entertaining 11 minute video from Fortnine (still one of the best channels on YouTube)","i":26920503,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26920503,"author":"IgorPartola","month":"2021-04","story":"Volkswagen launches its first all-electric SUV, the ID.4","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining 11 minute explanation of how Harley ended up in decline","quote":"This very entertaining 11 minute video from Fortnine (still one of the best channels on YouTube)","text":"You are correct. This very entertaining 11 minute video from Fortnine (still one of the best channels on YouTube) does a great job explaining exactly how HD wound up where they are today: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44767969,"author":"ubermonkey","month":"2025-08","story":"Why leather is best motorcycle protection [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Part of a Harley trilogy focused on their demographic problem","quote":"which is more about their demographic problem.","text":"It's nuts, right? I mean, I ride, but I know plenty of folks who are subscribed to the F9 Youtube channel who don't just because so much of the content they do scratches nerdy itches. Some of my favorites, in case anyone's interested: * Ryan's periodic \"check out this weird bike from the past\" clips are always great, but I really LOVE the one about the Honda Rune: [LINK] * Their \"trilogy\" of sorts about Harley, and the hows and whys of their odd and honestly self-defeating decisions, are all great, but my favorite is the one that compares an Indian Scout to its then-corresponding Harley model; the Indian made 43% more power, and it's all down to Harley's genuflection at \"tradition\". [LINK] The other two are \"why millions ride HD\" ( [LINK] ), which kinda lays the background for the tradition at work in the aforementioned video; and \"How HD killed itself\" ( [VIDEO] ), which is more about their demographic problem. * They did a \"how to deal with dealers\" video that is really a thinly veiled tribute to a particularly excellent and longstanding dealership, which is just lovely: [LINK] * And my personal favorite is the FANTASTIC film homage built into Ryan's vid about the Ducati Desert X, which he ended up buying: [LINK]"},{"id":27501796,"author":"noir_lord","month":"2021-06","story":"The Rise and Fall of Planet Hollywood","stance":"rec","why":"Fantastic overview of Harley's narrow niche and aging customers","quote":"Fantastic overview here","text":"Harley Davidson is on the ropes long term though. They made the mistake of pitching their entire product at a very singular niche and the people in that niche are now getting older and in many cases too old to ride. Fantastic overview here [VIDEO] Simply put HD make nothing that appeal to rides my age (41) and younger."},{"id":25749159,"author":"volta87","month":"2021-01","story":"Airbus hit as new US tariffs come into force","stance":"rec","why":"Summary of Harley-Davidson's decline","quote":"I recommend for a summary.","text":"That's because the company started dying longer ago. Their stock price today is around 30$. In the early 2000s it was around 90$. Also, like the other comment mentioned, HD has been playing a lot of financial tricks over the last 20 years to prevent the stock price from declining (e.g. buy backs to artificially inflate stock price, take a look at the number of outstanding shares: [LINK] , a reduction from 280 million to 150 million in the 2005-2019 time period). EDIT: I recommend [VIDEO] for a summary."},{"id":23974309,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-07","story":"Remington Arms Declares Bankruptcy","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates selling a lifestyle rather than a product","quote":"FortNine had a video made the rounds a little while ago","text":"> I've always got the impression from their marketing material that the Europeans are selling a product, whereas the Americans are always selling a lifestyle. A lot of people say the same thing about Harley-Davidson motorcycles. FortNine had a video made the rounds a little while ago: * [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LZgyVadkgmI",1],["oTpA5jt1g60",1],["QpDn4-Na5co",1],["eqQBubilSXU",1],["wM8Xli2KTzI",1]]},"GcJxRqTs5nk":{"wy":"Commenters call it a must-watch, funny and entertaining demonstration that morality has natural roots in animals.","q":{"x":"Here is one of his excellent must-watch Ted talks -","i":39179549,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39179549,"author":"rramadass","month":"2024-01","story":"Hell is other people: performance management at Big Tech","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent talk on cooperation and fairness in primates.","quote":"Here is one of his excellent must-watch Ted talks -","text":"I disagree because we are not doing it based on the Science we already know now; we can do much better. From behavioural psychology/social biology/game theory(Prisoner's dilemma) etc. we know Humans naturally have a in-group vs. out-group social structure. Within a group, cooperation with trust and fairness rules and competition is generally absent or if it exists is playful. The research by Frans de Waal ( [LINK] ) on Primate Social Behaviour (Cooperation/Fairness/Empathy/Reciprocity/etc.) is path-breaking. Here is one of his excellent must-watch Ted talks - [VIDEO] Machiavellian competition comes in only for \"status\"/\"reward\" within a group and is not the norm. Competition is more prevalent between groups. Thus if you look at a person in a organizational hierarchy, there are three immediate groups ; a) the people you report to (out group), b) your peers (in group, cooperation predominating, with maybe a little bit of playful competition), c) the people that report to you (in group, cooperation but with trust/fairness predominating). For everybody else we don't know and hence almost always respond with tit-for-tat principle. Finally there is also a need to understand what drives self-motivation. This is the natural order for Social Homo Sapiens as we understand from current knowledge. But none of this is being considered when measuring Performance/Productivity. For example, all Leaders/Managers almost always place Trust above everything else, Peers emphasize Fairness and the"},{"id":28056026,"author":"BeefWellington","month":"2021-08","story":"Goose flying upside down is simply showing off, say experts","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Funny, entertaining examples of animal behavior.","quote":"This is one of my favorite ted talks, on animal behaviour","text":"This is one of my favorite ted talks, on animal behaviour: [VIDEO] The examples of animals doing things the way they do are both funny and entertaining."},{"id":19689492,"author":"acqq","month":"2019-04","story":"Life of Brian at 40: an assertion of individual freedom that still resonates","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains best how morality is natural even in capuchin monkeys.","quote":"This is the video that explains the best how the \"morality\" is natural even in the capuchin monkeys","text":"This is the video that explains the best how the \"morality\" is natural even in the capuchin monkeys: [LINK] So, no to those that claim that, humans aren't special, and no, the good sides of morality don't come from religion, especially not from the modern ones with a \"jealous\" god (killing homosexuals, or punishing women when not wearsing something, however, if that is considered \"morality\", indeed provably does come from religion). The whole talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21124458,"author":"marci","month":"2019-10","story":"Great apes appear to have “theory of mind”","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for empathy in mammals kept by humans.","quote":"","text":"Would you call torture human guidance? [LINK] It's an extreme situation that created this, but I think if you take species in which empathy has been observed, and put them in a secure environment, where they are all well fed, I don't think it's crazy to imagine that they too will have the luxury of inter species empathy, without any human involve in that regard. In general, \"That's actually why people keeps mammals in the home and not turtles or snake or something like that who don't have that kind of empathy.\" (Moral Behaviors in animals | Franz de Waal [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["y-uuk4Pr2i8",1],["meiU6TxysCg",1],["-ga41edXw3A",1],["Q7jcBGLIpus",1],["36GT2zI8lVA",1],["PLyEO0jNt6M",1],["n8Dd5aVXLCc",1],["144uOfr4SYA",1],["x5Efg42-Qn0",1],["r1ZLGqL1FMo",1],["ffkIQrq-m34",1],["pQ0CvjAJXz4",1],["yErKTVdETpw",1],["f7CW7S0zxv4",1],["A-fxij3zM7g",1],["bLk1cjZ4ll0",1]]},"p5_tpq5ejFQ":{"wy":"Cited whenever threads discuss recovering hidden images or non-line-of-sight imaging, as a clear demonstration of light transport.","q":{"x":"Skip ahead to 4:19 for a succinct demo.","i":7715543,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7715543,"author":"uptown","month":"2014-05","story":"How taking pictures through windows  can inadvertently reveal your location","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Succinct SIGGRAPH 2005 dual photography demo.","quote":"Skip ahead to 4:19 for a succinct demo.","text":"Dual Photography has always been the optical \"trick\" that's blown me away. Here's the demo video from Siggraph in 2005: [VIDEO] Skip ahead to 4:19 for a succinct demo."},{"id":23423715,"author":"saeranv","month":"2020-06","story":"In a photo of a black hole, a possible key to mysteries","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates how light carries information, rendering from the light's view.","quote":"A very cool demonstration of how light conveys information is in dual photography","text":"> Does anyone assert that any photon carries information about what it interacted with in the past? Does a photon have any information content other than its energy/wavelength? So \"each one carries a bit of the image of whatever it hits\" is total (ok, be nice now) poetic blarney. An analogy to how we perceive things seems appropriate. The light from some source (i.e the sun) hits an object, the light rays are then reflected/transmitted/refracted through the surface based on various material properties. Your eye, acting like a pinhole camera, captures all the light rays from that surface, and then projects it onto the sensor reception surface (I think the retina?) creating the 'image' that we see. So the specific way the light rays interacts with the surface is what creates the 'information content'. For example, we perceive a blue-colored surface because the surface properties (i.e roughness) will specifically reflect wavelengths associated with blue. A specular surface will reflect light back to the eye, creating a 'shiny' surface. A very cool demonstration of how light conveys information is in dual photography: [VIDEO] And the original paper: [LINK] . They shine a light source onto an object, take a picture, and then by processing the light rays in the photo, are able to generate the same scene but from the perspective of the light source. It's really astounding when you see it. They can do this reconstruction because the flow of light rays from the light source to the obj"},{"id":22263376,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2020-02","story":"TV Backlight Compensation","stance":"rec","why":"Clear demonstration of swapping lights and cameras in a scene.","quote":"I recommend watchign the video from the paper for good explains and demonstrates the technique.","text":"It sounds like \"Dual Photography\". [LINK] \"We present a novel photographic technique called dual photography, which exploits Helmholtz reciprocity to interchange the lights and cameras in a scene. With a video projector providing structured illumination, reciprocity permits us to generate pictures from the viewpoint of the projector, even though no camera was present at that location.\" I recommend watchign the video from the paper for good explains and demonstrates the technique. It's very impressive work. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6972866,"author":"evanb","month":"2013-12","story":"Reflected hidden faces in photographs revealed in pupil","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation including the hidden playing card reveal.","quote":"The video [2] gives a good explanation","text":"This reminds me of dual photography [1], in which you trade many projectors and one camera for one projector and many cameras using time reversal symmetry. The video [2] gives a good explanation, and the revelation of the playing card is what strikes me as similar to revealing reflected hidden faces. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48152383,"author":"janfoeh","month":"2026-05","story":"Image-blaster: Creates 3D environments, SFX, and meshes from a single image","stance":"reference","why":"Computationally switching camera and light source in a static scene.","quote":"I've never forgotten this SIGGRAPH demo from almost twenty years ago","text":"I've never forgotten this SIGGRAPH demo from almost twenty years ago, in which the authors effectively switch camera and light source computationally (... in a static scene) [1] Ever since then, I have viewed scenes such as the \"lingerie store scene\" from Enemy of the State [2] with a little bit less eye rolling... [1] - [VIDEO] [2] - [LINK]"},{"id":26559986,"author":"rzzzt","month":"2021-03","story":"Non–line-of-sight imaging over 1.43km [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Photographing a playing card facing away.","quote":"","text":"I was immediately reminded of the Dual Photography paper (well, I had to look it up first; I only remembered the \"photographing a playing card that is facing away\" demo at the end): - [LINK] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":19013580,"author":"rzzzt","month":"2019-01","story":"Shadows used to peer around corners","stance":"reference","why":"Reversing light path between projector and camera.","quote":"Reminds me of this work","text":"Reminds me of this work, aiming to \"reverse\" the light path between a projector and a camera/photosensor: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15532447,"author":"evanb","month":"2017-10","story":"Turning Corners into Cameras: Principles and Methods","stance":"reference","why":"Recovering hidden images from diffuse light transfer.","quote":"where one can recover hidden images from diffuse-diffuse light transfer.","text":"This reminds me of dual photography, where one can recover hidden images from diffuse-diffuse light transfer. video: [VIDEO] page: [LINK]"},{"id":8871805,"author":"evanb","month":"2015-01","story":"An Algorithm That Unscrambles Fractured Images","stance":"reference","why":"Dual photography with known sources in transfer matrix.","quote":"the video there didn't work for me any more, here's the youtube link","text":"This reminds me of dual photography: [LINK] in the sense that I imagine unscrambling the glittered image will be significantly easier if you can plug in known sources to the transfer matrix. Edit: the video there didn't work for me any more, here's the youtube link [VIDEO]"},{"id":8132692,"author":"uptown","month":"2014-08","story":"Extracting audio from visual information","stance":"reference","why":"Constructing new views from light transport data.","quote":"All the data you need to construct a new view is available if you know where and how to look","text":"Reminds me a little of \"Dual Photography\" as presented at Siggraph in 2005. All the data you need to construct a new view is available if you know where and how to look: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"9LfmrkyP81M":{"wy":"HN points to it in discussions of programming as a creative craft and of how to write clear software.","q":{"x":"I really like this[1] talk from DHH on the subject","i":17282485,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":26191137,"author":"shaicoleman","month":"2021-02","story":"Ask HN: What is your advice to someone who is starting out in software industry?","stance":"rec","why":"DHH keynote on writing clear software through iterative drafts","quote":"There a good talk on this topic: Writing Software by David Heinemeier Hansson","text":"Software is read orders of magnitude more often than it is written. Therefore, the most important thing is to optimise your code for clarity. Software is an interative process. Sometimes it can take 5+ draft versions before you produce high quality code. There a good talk on this topic: Writing Software by David Heinemeier Hansson [1] If you're short on time, you can skip to the highlight here: [2] 1. [VIDEO] 2. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17282485,"author":"mosselman","month":"2018-06","story":"Why Crystal Is My Next Language","stance":"rec","why":"Programming as creative work, not science; thought provoking","quote":"I really like this[1] talk from DHH on the subject","text":"I really like this[1] talk from DHH on the subject, where he talks about the misconception of many programmers that we are doing science rather than something creative. While laced with hyperbolic statements it is at the very least thought provoking on some of the things that we do as developers. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15641735,"author":"mosselman","month":"2017-11","story":"Sick of Ruby, dynamic typing, side effects, and object-orientation (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Programming as creative craft rather than science","quote":"DHH has a great keynote on this phenomenon","text":"I don't really see how this would be much more different from language to language. The being 'sick of' part, not so much the specific irritations. Every languages has its issues and from all the languages I have tried I have found Ruby to be the most pleasant to work with, but I am sure this is different from person to person. One of the struggles that the writer seems to have is that programming applications is not science, but he seems to think it is. DHH has a great keynote on this phenomenon: \"Writing Software by David Heinemeier Hansson\" [VIDEO] As mentioned in the comments of the article you will run into issues with any programming language. One of the tricks is to not hold on so tightly to 'rules'. Like (take from the article) 'break functionality into lots of small objects'. This is horrible advice if applied to all situations. You should only do this when it makes sense. I see so many programmers breaking up everything in useless classes and methods/functions/whatever just because someone wrote a blog post about doing so. This just makes complicated code look simple at a glance, but when you want to find out what the code really does you have to jump up and down through files looking at what each function does, you have to open split screens between abstract classes that have some dedicated piece of code in a class that had to exist in order to satisfy the 'break things up' rule, etc. Long story short: just relax, write code (in whatever language you like, for what"},{"id":8815820,"author":"luhgor","month":"2014-12","story":"Software engineers should write","stance":"rec","why":"Coins software writer, prioritizing code clarity over technical superiority","quote":"Very opinionated, but recommended nonetheless","text":"Many people might have already seen it, but I thought it'd make sense to mention DHH's talk at RailsConf either way. In his talk, he is coining the term \"software writer\", which goes one step further and motivates a style of programming which prioritizes clarity of code over alleged technical superiority. Very opinionated, but recommended nonetheless: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25109239,"author":"raihansaputra","month":"2020-11","story":"Ask HN: How correct are geohot's opinions on CRUD developers?","stance":"reference","why":"DHH prefers information systems over computer science","quote":"An interesting take on this and the wider topic of how to write software is DHH's keynote from 2014[0].","text":"Half true, I guess? I'm definitely in the camp of the CRUD developers. I do accept that I don't do lower level work (even making plugins/affecting how the framework works) in my day to day job. And it's fine. Evidently these skills are worth paying for and create value (monetary or otherwise) in the world. An interesting take on this and the wider topic of how to write software is DHH's keynote from 2014[0]. He states that he himself is more interested in the 'Information System' parts instead of the 'Computer Science' part. I think there's space for more specialized titles to differentiate these jobs/skillsets. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23369227,"author":"frou_dh","month":"2020-05","story":"Programming is boring art","stance":"reference","why":"Reaches the same conclusion that programmers are writers","quote":"Maybe relevant talk from DHH coming to the same conclusion","text":"> This brought me back to the time my SO tried to explain an 84 year old lady she was assisting what is it that I do for a living. The consensus was that I'm a writer. Maybe relevant talk from DHH coming to the same conclusion: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["7kGnfXXIKZM",1],["7nJgHBbEgsE",1],["y3qfeoqErtY",1],["rQmqVVmMB3k",1],["u4ZoJKF_VuA",1]]},"R3xX37RGJKE":{"wy":"Recommended in incremental computation and reactive UI threads as the best conceptual overview of Incremental and incr_dom.","q":{"x":"Any discussion about incremental UI should include Yaron Minsky's talk about Jane Street's OCaml framework, incr_dom from 2016","i":21683385,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21683385,"author":"pgt","month":"2019-12","story":"Moxie: Incremental Declarative UI in Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Yaron Minsky on Jane Street's incremental OCaml UI framework incr_dom.","quote":"Any discussion about incremental UI should include Yaron Minsky's talk about Jane Street's OCaml framework, incr_dom from 2016","text":"Any discussion about incremental UI should include Yaron Minsky's talk about Jane Street's OCaml framework, incr_dom from 2016: [VIDEO] Incremental is essentially an implementation of Adapton from 2014: [LINK] There is a big upset coming in the UX world as we converge toward a generalized implementation of the \"diff & patch\" pattern which underlies Git, React, compiler optimization, scene rendering, and query optimization. Two sleeper startups in this space that are going to make a lot of money are Frank McSherry's Materialize Inc. and Nathan Marz's Red Planet Labs. incr_dom source: [LINK] Differential Dataflow: [LINK] Declarative Dataflow Client: [LINK] Crux adapter for Declarative Dataflow: [LINK]"},{"id":21618700,"author":"75dvtwin","month":"2019-11","story":"Towards a unified theory of reactive UI","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on incremental data-driven UIs with parallels to reactive UI theory.","quote":"Perhaps if you could review the above (if you have not done already), additional ideas/data points might help","text":"Thank you for sharing the write up. I think the model you are describing, has parallels with Yaron Minksy's Data Driven UIs, incrementally [VIDEO] Perhaps if you could review the above (if you have not done already), additional ideas/data points might help with your formalization effort."},{"id":18750109,"author":"chubot","month":"2018-12","story":"Adapton: Programming Language Abstractions for Incremental Computation","stance":"rec","why":"Jane Street's Incremental library with a live JS table demo.","quote":"This talk has a pretty impressive live JS table demo!","text":"From what I can tell, incremental computation is starting to be used in practice: Jane St has an OCaml library called Incremental: \"Data Driven UIs, Incrementally\" by Yaron Minsky at Strange Loop 2018: [VIDEO] This talk has a pretty impressive live JS table demo! It looks faster and nicer than most JS UIs I've seen. ----- And the Skip language was developed Facebook. Apparently they started to use it for some production stuff, but somehow they decided against it and spun it out: [LINK] [LINK] Julien Verlaguet - Reflex: Reactive Programming at Facebook -- [LINK] Reflex was an earlier name or earlier incarnation of Skip."},{"id":18312480,"author":"nateabele","month":"2018-10","story":"Incremental – Library for incremental computations","stance":"rec","why":"Better conceptual overview of Incremental than the docs talk.","quote":"provides a better conceptual overview than the talk linked in the docs. Pretty interesting stuff.","text":"The author just gave a talk at Strange Loop [0] that provides a better conceptual overview than the talk linked in the docs. Pretty interesting stuff. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31299177,"author":"infogulch","month":"2022-05","story":"Xilem: An Architecture for UI in Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the incr_dom approach to model-level incrementalization.","quote":"This somehow seems convergent with the model-level incrementalization approach that incr_dom [1] and its successor bonsai [2] are using.","text":"Very interesting! This somehow seems convergent with the model-level incrementalization approach that incr_dom [1] and its successor bonsai [2] are using. Have you had a chance to compare these? [1]: [LINK] | [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":18270009,"author":"espeed","month":"2018-10","story":"Type-Driven Program Synthesis [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Type-driven development, meta languages, specs, provably correct code, and automatic code generation were some of the big overlapping themes at Strange Loop [1] this year. All the talks posted online this week [2], and I've posted links to some of the related videos below [3]. It's a good indicator that the time has come for an idea when multiple lines of research and projects from disparate domains begin converging on the same idea from different directions. Several facets of the puzzle I've been working the last few years have converged towards this area so it's good to see that it's not just me, maybe that means we're all on the right track. But as 'pron and those who have been working in this area have pointed out [4], this part of the puzzle is not yet solved, and one of the primary open issues is how to scale it to the system level (beyond provably correct programs to provably correct systems). One of the questions I've been pondering is how can we combine provably correct software specs with hardware specs identified for any given system to find the optimal data structures and algorithms given the constraints of that architecture. Does something similar already exist? What's the best software currently out there for simulating ideal data flow for a given hardware configuration and a given set of system constraints? [1] Strange Loop [LINK] [2] Strange Loop 2018 talks (80 videos) [LINK] [3] Related videos... * Vellvm - Verifying the LLVM, by Steve Zdancewic [LINK] * All "}],"r":[["xguam0TKMw8",1],["lHLpKzUxjGk",1],["YR5WdGrpoug",1],["DJtef410XaM",1],["KINIAgRpkDA",1],["CuD7SCqHB7k",1],["TgmA48fILq8",1],["4fFDFbi3toc",1],["7HKbjYqqPPQ",1],["gFEE3w7F0ww",1],["IeSu_odkI5I",1],["hFSPHfZQpIQ",1],["ds77e3aO9nA",1],["Cym4TZwTCNU",1],["d6PyyatqJSE",1]]},"Y99Wj-NStok":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a gripping entry point into speedrunning and as a model of a well-made history video.","q":{"x":"I didn't realize I cared about speed-running until I saw this YouTube video from Summoning Salt","i":17693549,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18109269,"author":"nayuki","month":"2018-10","story":"Mario Kart Wii: The History of the Ultra Shortcut [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains the Choco Mountain shortcut history.","quote":"this YouTuber made a fantastic video explaining the Mario Kart 64 Choco Mountain shortcut","text":"Along the same topic, this YouTuber made a fantastic video explaining the Mario Kart 64 Choco Mountain shortcut: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18078553,"author":"msla","month":"2018-09","story":"'Super Mario Bros.' speedrunner hits nearly inhuman 4:55 world record","stance":"strong_rec","why":"History of Choco Mountain records with livestream captures and context.","quote":"Summoning Salt makes great videos.","text":"Summoning Salt makes great videos. The SMB1 video has already been linked, but I also love his video on the Choco Mountain track in Mario Kart 64. It has some great livestream captures which show records being broken, and contextualizes them well enough to give them some pathos: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17693549,"author":"mcast","month":"2018-08","story":"Ask HN: What are some niche communities you enjoy?","stance":"rec","why":"Video that got the commenter interested in speedrunning.","quote":"I didn't realize I cared about speed-running until I saw this YouTube video from Summoning Salt","text":"I didn't realize I cared about speed-running until I saw this YouTube video from Summoning Salt about N64's Mario Kart level: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["cZYNADOHhVY",1],["1Jwo5qc78QU",1],["dnHdqPBrtH8",1],["r1ZLGqL1FMo",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["P7GKK3liv8M",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["43XKfuptnik",1],["9W74aeuqsiU",1],["kdz6FeQLuHQ",1],["d4EgbgTm0Bg",1],["dqQw05Mr63E",1],["-opBifFfsMY",1],["AcS3NOQnsQM",1],["f7MzFfuNOtY",1],["w3_0x6oaDmI",1]]},"jMmUoZh3Hq4":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it to correct misconceptions about US voltage and to explain 240V availability in American homes.","q":{"x":"There's a lot of misconceptions about US electricity. This video does a nice job at pointing out the good and the bad.","i":32301628,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":41483366,"author":"jamesbfb","month":"2024-09","story":"Serving AI from the Basement – 192GB of VRAM Setup","stance":"rec","why":"Great video on US voltage.","quote":"Reminds me of this great Technology Connections video","text":"Huh, what?! Mega TIL moment for me as an Australian with an electric kettle and dryer plugged into whatever power socket I wish! Reminds me of this great Technology Connections video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34334138,"author":"Rimintil","month":"2023-01","story":"A US federal agency is considering a ban on gas stoves","stance":"rec","why":"Explains US 240V split-phase for large appliances.","quote":"Technology Connections (YouTube) explains this fairly well","text":"Single family homes in America are 240v rails for large appliances, like stoves/ovens, furnaces, and dryers (two-phase). Technology Connections (YouTube) explains this fairly well: > [VIDEO]"},{"id":32301628,"author":"coreyp_1","month":"2022-08","story":"A looming copper crunch and why recycling can’t fix it","stance":"rec","why":"Corrects misconceptions about US electricity, good and bad.","quote":"There's a lot of misconceptions about US electricity. This video does a nice job at pointing out the good and the bad.","text":"There's a lot of misconceptions about US electricity. This video does a nice job at pointing out the good and the bad. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29340451,"author":"kruador","month":"2021-11","story":"Museum of Plugs and Sockets","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the breaker panel and how US homes get 240V.","quote":"Relevant video: Technology Connections","text":"Relevant video: Technology Connections, \"The US electrical system is not 120V\", where there is a (long) discussion of how the breaker panel works and how you get 240V. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48052485,"author":"kibwen","month":"2026-05","story":"California leaders report four to six weeks worth of gasoline and diesel supply","stance":"reference","why":"Shows US homes already have 240V.","quote":"Plenty of US home appliances are already 240 volt; this is a solved problem.","text":"> Also our infra not being 240v is hurting us. The rest of the world can just plug in overnight to any regular outlet and it is good enough for almost any commute. US homes don't need any significant accommodations for 240 volt infra. Plenty of US home appliances are already 240 volt; this is a solved problem. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46181520,"author":"andrybak","month":"2025-12","story":"My car charger can boil water really fast [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Covers different voltages in the US electrical system.","quote":"a 2020 video about different voltages in the US electrical systems","text":"A 2022 Technology Connections video explores various ways to boil water: [LINK] And a 2020 video about different voltages in the US electrical systems: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32690151,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2022-09","story":"USB 4 version 2 promises 80 Gbps speeds over existing cables","stance":"reference","why":"US runs on split 240V.","quote":"The US (and CA) runs on 240V as well","text":"The US (and CA) runs on 240V as well, it's just split in half for smaller appliances. See requisite Technology Connections video: * [VIDEO] My stove and clothes dryer are certainly not 120V."},{"id":31854757,"author":"breakingcups","month":"2022-06","story":"A lightning bolt would be worth only about a nickel (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There's probably a large overlap between software people and people who subscribe to Technology Connections","text":"There's probably a large overlap between software people and people who subscribe to Technology Connections: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["SEDuVF7kiPU",1],["s1i-dnAH9Y4",1]]},"ljOoGyCso8s":{"wy":"Recommended as the best look inside a modern PCB fab, cited in threads on hobby PCB vendors, manufacturing in China and automation.","q":{"x":"It's a tour of JLCPCB's factory, where they go through all the PCB manufacturing steps.","i":35308730,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35308730,"author":"elihu","month":"2023-03","story":"Comparing Hobby PCB Vendors","stance":"rec","why":"Tour of JLCPCB's factory covering all PCB manufacturing steps.","quote":"It's a tour of JLCPCB's factory, where they go through all the PCB manufacturing steps.","text":"This is very interesting: [VIDEO] It's a tour of JLCPCB's factory, where they go through all the PCB manufacturing steps. (They just cover the PBC itself, not the assembly service.)"},{"id":25825041,"author":"antoniuschan99","month":"2021-01","story":"Foxconn gets green light for $270M factory in Vietnam","stance":"rec","why":"Inside look at PCB fabs like PCBWay and JLCPCB.","quote":"Strange parts has some cool inside look at the popular pcb manufacturing fabs","text":"Strange parts has some cool inside look at the popular pcb manufacturing fabs such as pcbway and jlcpcb [VIDEO]"},{"id":18430606,"author":"ddeck","month":"2018-11","story":"Etch a circuit board with kitchen supplies","stance":"rec","why":"Factory tour of JLC's PCB facility in China.","quote":"Strange Parts[1] made a great factory tour video of the JLC facility in China","text":"Strange Parts[1] made a great factory tour video of the JLC facility in China for anyone that interested in such things: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":18159157,"author":"est","month":"2018-10","story":"Apple Insiders Say Nobody Knows What’s Going on with Bloomberg's Hack Story","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what QA looks like in a PCB factory","quote":"the whole vid is worth watching","text":"In case anyone want to see how QA looks like in a factory, here's a video [VIDEO] (the whole vid is worth watching)"},{"id":46597668,"author":"nebula8804","month":"2026-01","story":"39c3: In-house electronics manufacturing from scratch: How hard can it be? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of free engineer review in cheap PCB service.","quote":"","text":"Yeah I didn't say that Chinese people didn't want to go down the same route, infact lifestyle influencers have a better shot there (probably 0.1% better). There is so much overproduction of engineers that you are possibly working a customer support role with that degree. Another reason SASS supposedly never took off there, why pay some company when you can just hire from the batch of overproduced STEM graduates and make any software you need. When I can have an experienced engineer correct my garbage designs for free as part of a manufacturing service you know I am benefiting off of a lucky circumstance that will go away one day: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45625703,"author":"nebula8804","month":"2025-10","story":"Jeep wrangler owners waiting for answers week after an update bricked their cars","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for engineers doing customer support enabling cheap hobbyist PCBs.","quote":"","text":"Who in Japan is good at this? I can only imagine the R&D division at maybe Playstation and thats it. China has so many people that EE and CS are a dime a dozen, thats why they have a competitive market thats low cost. People are in a jungle trying to survive. [0]: [VIDEO] The video is (Supposedly) EEs doing customer support roles thus allowing Cheap PCB for hobbyists, the equivalent in the US does not exist. Side Note: I hear this is also why SASS never really took off in China. Why pay someone else for software when you can get a dozen people to make your own cheaper."},{"id":43850351,"author":"nebula8804","month":"2025-04","story":"Port of Los Angeles says shipping volume will plummet 35% next week","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the full JLCPCB process, cited to dispute easy automation.","quote":"You might as well watch the entire process of how JLCPCB works","text":"You might as well watch the entire process of how JLCPCB works and then tell me if all of that can be \"automated\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":35310894,"author":"Ao7bei3s","month":"2023-03","story":"Comparing Hobby PCB Vendors","stance":"reference","why":"Step-by-step view of JLCPCB board cutting with CNC router and vacuum table.","quote":"Here's a step-by-step video about how JLCPCB makes PCB's.","text":"Here's a step-by-step video about how JLCPCB makes PCB's. The link goes directly to that step at 21:09: [VIDEO] It looks like in this case they are using a CNC router to make the cuts, and a vacuum table to keep the PCB's in place (it's a table with a grid of tiny holes that has vacuum inside to suck the PCB's onto the table)."}],"r":[["LS3GQk9ETRU",1],["JtKAScORevQ",1],["jA1C9VFqJKo",1],["thOifuHs6eY",1],["p5Ac7ap_MAY",1],["c12cYAUTXXs",1],["TneLO5TdW_M",1],["kgCUn4fQTsc",1]]},"oUaOucZRlmE":{"wy":"HN treats it as one of the canonical Bret Victor talks, cited whenever discussion turns to better tools and interfaces for thinking.","q":{"x":"Tools let us think previously unthinkable thoughts.","i":10591987,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":36082773,"author":"boomlinde","month":"2023-05","story":"Ask HN: How do you approach a problem you are not sure has a solution?","stance":"rec","why":"Inspiration on representing problems differently","quote":"I recommend watching this talk by Bret Victor","text":"I look for different ways of representing the problem domain or the desired outcome. This will improve your vocabulary/toolbox for reasoning about and subdividing them into more approachable units. For some inspiration, I recommend watching this talk by Bret Victor [VIDEO] (Media for Thinking the Unthinkable)"},{"id":34487952,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Has anyone fully attempted Bret Victor's vision?","stance":"rec","why":"One of Bret Victor's talks, frequently cited on HN","quote":"he gets more mentions than anyone else, usually","text":"For the people asking who is Bret Victor: When \"What are your favourite tech talks of all time?\" is asked on HN, he gets more mentions than anyone else, usually. Some of his talks: Inventing on Principle [LINK] The Future of Programming [LINK] The Humane Representation of Thought [LINK] Media for Thinking the Unthinkable [VIDEO] Seeing Spaces [LINK] Drawing Dynamic Visualizations [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":28368610,"author":"vignesh_warar","month":"2021-08","story":"A visual introduction to Gaussian Belief Propagation","stance":"rec","why":"Related talk on visual thinking media","quote":"It reminds me of a talk given by Bret Victor","text":"What a great article - and the way it's presented is insanely great. It reminds me of a talk given by Bret Victor [VIDEO] (Media for Thinking the Unthinkable)"},{"id":25360403,"author":"magicink81","month":"2020-12","story":"Learnable Programming (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Bret Victor on varied interactive notations","quote":"goes into detail on this, especially from minute 10 to minute 17","text":"You make a great point about notations becoming more abstract for more advanced use cases, indeed this may be a \"natural\" process. However I'd like to add nuance with the observation that Bret may not be suggesting that we reverse the trend from abstract notation back to concrete visualizations. Actually I think he is calling for a rich variety of visual/concrete notations and symbolic/abstract notations that work in concert together to express our dynamic concepts, and extending those with the use of the dynamism of the computing medium to add interactive aspects to the notations (ala \"Magic Ink\"). His talk \"Media for thinking the unthinkable\" goes into detail on this, especially from minute 10 to minute 17: [VIDEO] The essay Magic Ink: INFORMATION SOFTWARE AND THE GRAPHICAL INTERFACE also goes into detail about this topic [LINK]"},{"id":46546255,"author":"snorwick","month":"2026-01","story":"Lights and Shadows","stance":"reference","why":"Bret Victor's work on computer-enabled teaching and learning","quote":"","text":"There is the “tools for thought” space I came across where some work felt compelling for computer enabled teaching and learning. Bret Victor [0], Andy Matuschak [1], and Seymour Papert [2] to point to a few names. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":34525815,"author":"morphle","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Has anyone fully attempted Bret Victor's vision?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I cool a wafer scale with a liquid that boils at 43 C and immersing the wafer in this liquid. The bubbles (cavitation) of the boiling liquid should not damage the surface layers of the wafer, of course. This boiling liquid is further cooled by water and a sonic heat pump moving the heat into a water tank where the stored heat is used for showers or cooking [1]. Given 45 trillion transistors (45x10e12) times 3 femtojoule (3x10e-15) to switch each transistor at 1 Ghz (10e9) you get 1.000.000 joules/sec = 1 megawatt. These are ball-park numbers, back of the envelope calculations. In reality I make full physics simulations and electrical SPICE simulations of the entire wafer on a supercomputer aka on the wafer scale integration FPGA prototypes and the wafer supercomputer itself. The EDA (Electronic Design Automation) software tools we write ourselves in Smalltalk and Ometa, and these also need our own supercomputer to run on. Of course the feedback loops are Bret Victor style visualizers [3][2]. Apple Silicon or this small company demonstrate that only with custom EDA tools can you design ultra-low power transistors to prevent our wafer to melt. The FPGA prototype is a few thousand Cyclone 10 or Polarfire FPGA's with a few terabytes/sec memory bandwidth or a cluster of Mac Studio Ultra's networked together in a Slim Fly network that can double as a neighbourhood solar smart grid [5]. You need a dinosaur egg to design a dinosaur, or is is it the other way around? [6] A TDP (Therma"},{"id":18223107,"author":"mitchtbaum","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"reference","why":"Among Bret Victor talks listed as best technical talks","quote":"","text":"on one level, yes on another, they're down right scary and overwhelming, or at least dumbfounding and perplexing ..thinking specifically about The Humane Representation of Thought [LINK] I had to stop it half way through.. :-/ see also Seeing Spaces [LINK] and Media for Thinking the Unthinkable [VIDEO]"},{"id":10533139,"author":"kragen","month":"2015-11","story":"TensorFlow: open-source library for machine intelligence","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is really significant. At this moment in history, the growth of computer power has made a bunch of important signal-processing and statistical tasks just feasible, so we are seeing things like self-driving cars, superhuman image recognition, and so on. But it's been very difficult to take advantage of the available computational power, because it's in the form of GPUs and clusters. TensorFlow is a library designed to make it easy to do exactly these things, and to scale them with your available computing power, along with libraries of the latest tricks in neural networks, machine learning (which is pretty close to \"statistics\"). As a bonus, it has built-in automatic differentiation so that you can run gradient descent on any algorithm — which means that you can just write a program to evaluate the goodness of a solution and efficiently iterate it to a local maximum. If you do this enough times, hopefully you'll find a global maximum. There are a variety of other numerical optimization algorithms, but gradient descent is very simple and broadly applicable. And it runs in IPython (now Jupyter), which is a really amazingly powerful way to do exploratory software development. If you haven't tried Jupyter/IPython, google up a screencast and take a look. I'm just repeating the stuff that's on the home page in a different form, but this is a really big deal . Most of the most significant software of the next five or ten years is going to be built in TensorFlow or something like"},{"id":10591987,"author":"mkl","month":"2015-11","story":"The Space Doctor’s Big Idea","stance":"reference","why":"Tools enabling new thoughts, with al-Khwarizmi example","quote":"Tools let us think previously unthinkable thoughts.","text":"In principle, yes, but in practice, no. Tools let us think previously unthinkable thoughts. See Bret Victor's Media for Thinking the Unthinkable [1], where he quotes al-Khwarizmi from the ninth century: What is the square which when taken with ten of its roots will give a sum of thirty nine? Now the roots in the problem before us are ten. Therefore take five, which multiplied by itself gives twenty five, and amount you add to thirty nine to give sixty four. Having taken the square root of this which is eight, subtract from this half the roots, five leaving three. The number three represents one root of this square, which itself, of course, is nine. Nine therefore gives the square. He's solving x^2+10x = 39 by completing the square. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["h2cs8QLnxlU",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["443UNeGrFoM",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["XvDZLjaCJuw",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1],["s1i-dnAH9Y4",1]]},"uY-u1qyRM5w":{"wy":"Strongly recommended for its funny, human account of Los Alamos, which one commenter calls almost a stand-up act.","q":{"x":"He gives an incredible account of his time there in the lecture (and almost stand-up-comedy act)","i":36933971,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36933971,"author":"mk_stjames","month":"2023-07","story":"Richard Feynman's 1964 Messenger Lectures at Cornell University","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman's funny, incredible account of his time at Los Alamos.","quote":"He gives an incredible account of his time there in the lecture (and almost stand-up-comedy act)","text":"I spent a fair amount of time during the film playing 'Spot Jack Quaid' in anticipation of Feynman being called out at some point. He is spotted early during the montage when Oppenheimer goes recruiting, and then during various scenes in the background, twice playing bongos (!), but only once is he called by name- Teller calls his name right before the Trinity test for not having goggles or a welding glass to look through, and he notes he is in a truck with a thick windshield that blocks the UV. I smiled so much when that little tidbit happens, as it is a story he prominently tells in his book 'Surely You're Joking Mr Feynman...' He gives an incredible account of his time there in the lecture (and almost stand-up-comedy act) 'Los Alamos from Below' - [VIDEO] So highly entertaining."},{"id":17002164,"author":"gizmodo59","month":"2018-05","story":"How the Manhattan Project’s Nuclear Suburb Stayed Secret","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman's personal anecdotes from working at Los Alamos.","quote":"I highly recommend this talk by Richard Feynman on Los Alamos","text":"I highly recommend this talk by Richard Feynman on Los Alamos: [VIDEO] . His personal anecdote on some interesting details while he was working there."},{"id":34568433,"author":"MarkusWandel","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What happens if a nuclear missile is air-destroyed before detonation?","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining lecture covering the computing effort behind the bomb.","quote":"the whole lecture is a hoot","text":"If the fissile core is anything like the original Trinity test one, it relies on a solid ball of plutonium metal being compacted to reach criticality (modern ones may be hollow). This means the explosion wavefront needs to arrive simultaneously all around the ball. This is hard! A ton of the computational effort legendarily described by Feynman with his IBM tabulator/multiple coloured cards story was, I believe, just to make this work. Link to the relevant part (the whole lecture is a hoot). [VIDEO] Anyway the original bomb had a lot of detonators all around that needed to be fired right. Modern ones have only two, from the little I've seen in published imagery. [LINK] One hopes this is arranged so simultaneous firing of both detonators results in a fizzle, and a precise stagger is needed. Either way though, an external explosive force will just disassemble the thing, not cause it to go off."}],"r":[["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["R7EEoWg6Ekk",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1]]},"3x1b_S6Qp2Q":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a fascinating conversation showing how the Ethereum founder thinks.","q":{"x":"listening to the founder of Ethereum on the Lex Fridman podcast was fascinating.","i":25538212,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25538212,"author":"neillyons","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2020?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fascinating conversation showing how the Ethereum founder thinks.","quote":"listening to the founder of Ethereum on the Lex Fridman podcast was fascinating.","text":"I don't want anything to do with cryptocurrencies but listening to the founder of Ethereum on the Lex Fridman podcast was fascinating. The way that guy thinks is just impressive. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26137833,"author":"mjgs","month":"2021-02","story":"Brendan Eich on the Lex Fridman Podcast [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Wide-ranging, well paced talk with the Ethereum founder from Bitcoin to immortality.","quote":"Fascinating and well paced conversation with the founder of Etherium","text":"I didn’t watch that one. So far I’ve only listened to a few. I mostly listen via the podcast feed. I posted the ones I liked to my linkblog ( [LINK] ): [2020-12-26] Lex Friedman Podcast #80 - Vitalik Buterin - Ethereum, Cryptocurrency, and the Future of Money - Fascinating and well paced conversation with the founder of Etherium covering a wide range of topics including Satoshi Nakamoto, blockchains, proof of work and identity, PKI and digital signatures, Bitcoin, money, the Etherium origin story, smart contracts, software engineering and project governance challenges, proof of stake and consensus algorithms, sharding of storage and computation, Etherium 2.0, games built using smart contracts, Uniswap, AI and crypto, and closes on Immortality [VIDEO] [2021-02-13] Lex Friedman Podcast #159 – Richard Craib: WallStreetBets, Numerai, and the Future of Stock Trading - Looks at the recent WalkStreetBets and Gamestop Saga that caused such a stir in the trading markets, then moves on to talk about hedge funds, shorting, new ways crypto and AI could change the world of finance, aswell as more esoteric topics like running a startup and the meaning of life [LINK] I might have a listen to the cringey one you linked to. I wonder if it was just an off day for him or perhaps he didn’t gel well with the guest."},{"id":24240656,"author":"ZephyrBlu","month":"2020-08","story":"Trust Models","stance":"rec","why":"Lex Fridman's interview with Vitalik Buterin.","quote":"I recently watched Lex Fridman's podcast episode with him and it was really interesting as well","text":"Not related to this topic, but I recently watched Lex Fridman's podcast episode with him and it was really interesting as well: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":27536716,"author":"zakember","month":"2021-06","story":"World Bank rejects El Salvador request for Bitcoin help","stance":"reference","why":"Buterin argues quantum computer overhead outweighs mining speed-up.","quote":"the overhead of running a Quantum computer will be higher than the speed-up advantage you get by using it for mining.","text":"Yes. Vitalik Buterin (Ethereum founder) talks about this in his talk with Lex Fridman. According to him, the overhead of running a Quantum computer will be higher than the speed-up advantage you get by using it for mining. Link to his talk [VIDEO] (43:00)"}],"r":[["lhYGXYeMq_E",1],["JLb5hZLw44s",1],["5tSTk1083VY",1]]},"PDylgzybWAw":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a good overview of predatory lending and the problems with payday loans.","q":{"x":"I think he does a pretty good job in giving an overview of what predatory lending is.","i":8272670,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":9466406,"author":"VikingCoder","month":"2015-04","story":"Want a steady income? There's an app for that","stance":"strong_rec","why":"John Oliver segment on payday loans.","quote":"I highly recommend you watch the John Oliver segment on Payday Loans.","text":"I highly recommend you watch the John Oliver segment on Payday Loans. [VIDEO] ANYTHING that helps get rid of those scum is a huge improvement."},{"id":29777089,"author":"otterley","month":"2022-01","story":"The Case Against Crypto","stance":"rec","why":"Discusses problems with predatory lending.","quote":"Spend some time with John Oliver, discussing just a few of the problems with predatory lending","text":"Spend some time with John Oliver, discussing just a few of the problems with predatory lending: [VIDEO] Usury laws go back to Biblical times: [LINK] - there is plenty of literature to follow from this point. There are still prohibitions on drug use that most agree should still be upheld due to the serious harm they tend to cause (addiction -> crime, serious health decline) relative to their benefits: heroin, fentanyl, cocaine, crystal meth, etc. Go spend some time in some majority Black neighborhoods in the U.S. like deep East Oakland, Detroit, Philadelphia, etc. if you want to get a real sense of how bad the problems are and what the locals think about it. Or spend some time in poor white areas in Appalachia or the midwest U.S."},{"id":8272670,"author":"WestCoastJustin","month":"2014-09","story":"Secret Network Connects Harvard Money to Payday Loans","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of what predatory lending is.","quote":"I think he does a pretty good job in giving an overview of what predatory lending is.","text":"If you are interested in this topic, you should watch: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver: Predatory Lending (HBO) [1]. I think he does a pretty good job in giving an overview of what predatory lending is. Although, he does not talk about Harvard, MIT, or pension funds, this is all new as far as I know. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8273192,"author":"themartorana","month":"2014-09","story":"Secret Network Connects Harvard Money to Payday Loans","stance":"rec","why":"Segment exposing payday loan companies.","quote":"check out Last Week Tonight's bit (John Oliver's show on HBO) on payday loan companies","text":"Even better, check out Last Week Tonight's bit (John Oliver's show on HBO) on payday loan companies [0] - they're utterly appalling. [0] [VIDEO] Edit: spelling"},{"id":11058627,"author":"hobs","month":"2016-02","story":"Uninstalling Facebook app saves up to 15% of iPhone battery life","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The demand for heroin is overwhelming.[0] An argument from popularity is not an argument that something is a net positive for society. Obesity is becoming more popular. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10440183,"author":"justifier","month":"2015-10","story":"People Want Fairness, Not Economic Equality","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"fairness is a horrendous choice of words, but the one this article chose to use there are many unfair side effects of current economics look at taxes.. recently i read a major corporate entity paid 4k pounds in taxes in 2014(o). The top comment says: TIL our small UK startup pays 10-20x more corp tax .. Doing things honestly and by the book is expensive in both money terms and time terms (paying accountants) Big corps .. make me feel like a fool. though these corps are seen as equals in buraecratic lines, the expectations and avenues of utilising those regulations seems unfairly balanced or look at fines.. if i get a parking ticket(i) for 100$, i may be unable to pay it right away with my zero savings and my 12k/yr income, someone making 80k+ a year will just pay the 100$ grievance and be done with it i will have to start budgeting myself to make enough to pay the 100$ fine, but it will take time with bimonthly paychecks and in that time i will be punished with late fees only increasing the financial burden and time needed to accrue the funds even if trying to do the right thing people punished further simply for economic circumstance so though the two violators are seen as equal, the expectations and avenues of how to rectify the violations seems unfairly balanced people making less end up paying more for the same violation due to the bureaucracy maintaining the violations or i could use a payday loan(ii), another damning option these are only two examples of areas where a c"},{"id":9517780,"author":"cyphunk","month":"2015-05","story":"USA Where the Poor Live Dearly","stance":"reference","why":"Documents predatory lenders targeting poor neighborhoods.","quote":"","text":"On point 2, HN flagging things that are critical of the US... I find surprising. On point 1, facts. I haven't found where to dispute the articles facts yet. Where the commenters dispute it's actually an issue of perception. - [1] Predetory Loan and banking services such as ACE that market to predominant poor neighborhoods has a negative impact that is documented in study and pop [2] - [3] the initial argument by power user tptacek is that the author didn't bother to check poor white parts of the country. If you assume this article was about something other than black poverty, okay I understand the argument. But it was about poverty effecting the black community. So I don't see the factual problem. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO] 3. [LINK]"},{"id":8377594,"author":"eevilspock","month":"2014-09","story":"Antisocial – Ello and Facebook","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"John Oliver is funny","text":"> Newspapers have been subsidised by advertising for years (the cost of the paper basically covers distribution costs). That never made them any less useful. Are you kidding? I can come up with better, but John Oliver is funny: [VIDEO] > Yes, I understand \"if you don't pay for it you are the product\". I think that's bunk - or rather, I don't think that makes it any less useful. That's like saying smoking's long term carcinogenic effects don't make it any less enjoyable. By the way, advertising doesn't subsidize anything. Advertisers get their money from us. There is no free lunch. In fact, advertising gives us lunch that is less tasty, costs more, and gives us food poisoning: [LINK]"}],"r":[["KtQ9nt2ZeGM",1],["c12cYAUTXXs",1],["08mwXICY4JM",1],["9XaYdCdwiWU",1],["G4hL5Om4IJ4",1],["j-vJxez9UF8",1],["coGpmA4saEk",1],["0qKp-0h9P18",1],["F9cO3-MLHOM",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["2qv3fcXW1mg",1],["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["uwCwtBxZM7g",1],["U_X5uR7VC4M",1],["pX8BXH3SJn0",1]]},"fDqBeXJ8Zx8":{"wy":"Cited in threads on YouTube algorithms and creator complaints as a data-driven look at the trending tab.","q":{"x":"Here's an excellent video breaking down with extremely compelling data how the \"trending\" tab is very heavily manipulated","i":20698385,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20698385,"author":"khawkins","month":"2019-08","story":"Watch Out Google, YouTubers Are Unionizing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Compelling data showing trending tab favors mainstream media.","quote":"Here's an excellent video breaking down with extremely compelling data how the \"trending\" tab is very heavily manipulated","text":"Here's an excellent video breaking down with extremely compelling data how the \"trending\" tab is very heavily manipulated in favor of mainstream media sources: [VIDEO] What's interesting is that extreme bias in the US is not seen in Canada. Though I'm not sure it's stated in the video, I think that Youtube has been propping up MSM sources so that they can make themselves seem more relevant in the eyes of the MSM. They're doing this so that they can partner with the MSM and expand their YoutubeTV service. They desperately want some sort of service model to make money, and they're willing to destroy the goodwill of the community to achieve it."},{"id":23765735,"author":"symplee","month":"2020-07","story":"Analysis of all YouTube trending videos in US for 2019","stance":"rec","why":"First data-driven look at the YouTube trending tab.","quote":"Another excellent analysis of what Trends on YouTube","text":"Another excellent analysis of what Trends on YouTube [0] \"Is the trending tab rigged against creators? Is late night dominating the charts? Why does it feel your favorite controversial creator never trends. All of that and more, in the first ever data-driven look at the trending tab.\" What 40,000 Videos Tell Us About The Trending Tab: [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20558249,"author":"aphextim","month":"2019-07","story":"YouTube faces creator backlash","stance":"rec","why":"Data-driven look at trending tab bias; alarming findings.","quote":"they are pretty alarming no matter what side of isle you sit","text":"This is a video I recall watching recently that backs up your point. Not sure if anyone has refuted this person's points but they are pretty alarming no matter what side of isle you sit. [VIDEO] (What 40,000 Videos Tell Us About The Trending Tab)"},{"id":20557239,"author":"munmaek","month":"2019-07","story":"YouTube faces creator backlash","stance":"reference","why":"Data analysis of trending tab across 40,000 videos.","quote":"an analysis of 40,000 trending videos [7 months of trending tab videos]","text":"Not only is it manipulated, it's flagrantly obvious. PewDiePie for example trends in many countries except the US [e.g. 45 times in Canada, 42 times in Germany vs 1 time in the US], and it's not just him either but many \"controversial\" channels. Source: an analysis of 40,000 trending videos [7 months of trending tab videos]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20557405,"author":"munmaek","month":"2019-07","story":"YouTube faces creator backlash","stance":"reference","why":"Analysis of seven months of trending tab videos.","quote":"An analysis of 7 months worth of trending tab videos, or roughly ~40k videos that reached the trending tab.","text":"It's obvious youtube is for mainstream media now, with content creators being a leftover wart, minuscule on youtube's radar. Content creators deal with an opaque algorithm that demonetizes videos based on how the wind is blowing, or it would appear. Weeks or months of work can be wiped out by an arbitrary demonetization preventing content creators from getting paid for their video's views. JonTron discusses this [1]. It's very easy to violate the \"advertiser-friendly\" guidelines. They're vague: \"violence\", \"harmful content\", \"controversial or sensitive issues\". Under profanity: \"strong profanity used in video even if bleeped for comedy, documentary, news, or educational purposes \". ??! It has been obvious for a long time that daily content uploads are better for the algorithm than longer, quality videos uploaded less frequently. If you look at the LinusTechTips network of channels, they produce at least 5-7 videos per week. [0]: An analysis of 7 months worth of trending tab videos, or roughly ~40k videos that reached the trending tab. [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"}],"r":[["7lVAFcDX4eM",1],["QyQvfaW54NU",1]]},"gqGEMQveoqg":{"wy":"Recommended as the foundational talk on content-centric and named-data networking, and for its internet history stories.","q":{"x":"There's an old tech talk that got me out of the IP-shaped box a lot of us are trapped in","i":41457145,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41457145,"author":"barrkel","month":"2024-09","story":"How the Hourglass Won","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Data-centric networking idea that breaks out of IP thinking","quote":"There's an old tech talk that got me out of the IP-shaped box a lot of us are trapped in","text":"Consider a society that has left Earth. If light takes minutes to travel between locations, is a chatty protocol what you want? Or do you need a whole host of different application-level solutions to this problem? There's a different lens to look at it. There's an old tech talk that got me out of the IP-shaped box a lot of us are trapped in: [VIDEO] I got Gemini 1.5 Pro to summarize the transcript, which I butchered a bit to fit in a text box: The Problem: The Internet is treated as a binary concept: You're either connected or not, leading to issues with mobile devices and seamless connectivity. The focus on connections (TCP/IP conversations) hinders security: Security is tied to the pipe, not the data, making it difficult to address issues like spam and content integrity. Inefficient use of resources: Broadcasting and multi-point communication are inefficient as the network is unaware of content, leading to redundant data transmission. The Proposed Solution: Data-centric architecture: Data is identified by name, not location. This enables: Trust and integrity based on data itself, not the source. Efficient multi-point communication and broadcasting. Seamless mobility and intermittent connectivity. Improved security against spam, phishing, and content manipulation. Key principles: Data integrity and trust are derived from the data itself through cryptographic signatures and verification. Immutable content with versioning and updates through supersession. Data segmentation for"},{"id":14472179,"author":"pololee","month":"2017-06","story":"Network Protocols","stance":"rec","why":"Internet history stories from congestion control pioneer","quote":"I'd also recommend Van Jacobson's talk You'll learn some good stories about internet history.","text":"Very good reading. I'd also recommend Van Jacobson's talk [VIDEO] You'll learn some good stories about internet history. He has done a lot of work on TCP congestion control, especially the fast retransmission idea by using duplicate ACKs. There is an interesting thing about TCP. A lot of popular TCP implementation use city names, e.g. TCP Reno, TCP Vegas, TCP Westwood. TCP Westwood is a very interesting implementation. It has very intelligent way to estimate bandwidth, (not just based on duplicate ACKs). You may find this paper very interesting. [LINK]"},{"id":4200736,"author":"seiji","month":"2012-07","story":"Wiki inventor Ward Cunningham develops federated wiki","stance":"rec","why":"Van Jacobson's talk on a new approach to networking","quote":"Ye olde, but still worth watching: Van Jacobson's Google Talk from 2006","text":"Ye olde, but still worth watching: Van Jacobson's Google Talk from 2006, \"A New Way to look at Networking\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":28897696,"author":"anaphor","month":"2021-10","story":"HTTP is obsolete – it's time for the distributed, permanent web (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Named-data networking is literally what this is describing","text":"Named-data networking is literally what this is describing, except NDN works as a replacement for TCP, not HTTP. In the long term I'd rather have it work at a lower level and not have to think about it much. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28899216,"author":"xccx","month":"2021-10","story":"HTTP is obsolete – it's time for the distributed, permanent web (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"NDN is the way p2p no huge IP infrastructure between nodes interest packets at narrow waist (like human attention, scarce) sign all packets, more secure, easier to trust|not hash for pointers, no surprises in containers crypto apply more cryptography, less web3 baloney broadcast radio native, no emulation of copper wire data, closer to where you want, find faster, keep content you want w/out intermediation, need to find \"where\" within IP/udp/tcp addresses [LINK] node to node [LINK] more application, less security hassle [LINK] hash name the data [VIDEO] data integrity w/out need to trust foreign server"},{"id":23730124,"author":"pwdisswordfish2","month":"2020-07","story":"Where Am I? NYTimes or Google?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"(Google Tech Talk from Van Jacobsen on CCN many years ago)","text":"In what ways is this different/similar from \"content centric networking\"? [VIDEO] (Google Tech Talk from Van Jacobsen on CCN many years ago)"},{"id":12773690,"author":"th-ai","month":"2016-10","story":"IPFS is the Distributed Web","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Integrity and trust are properties of the data; not of the way that you obtain ​it.","text":"Any bashing on Named Data Networking? [LINK] Rather than the system getting less reliable exponentially as it scales up, it gets more reliable exponentially as it scales up. [VIDEO] Integrity and trust are properties of the data; not of the way that you obtain ​it. [VIDEO] There's no routing. You get rid of all of the routing chatter. You get rid of all of the routing brittleness. [LINK] We wanted to turn around the incentive structure where right now senders have a lot of power and receivers have basically nothing, they have to eat what they're fed. [LINK]"}],"r":[["oCZMoY3q2uM",1]]},"sfGtw2C95Ms":{"wy":"Cited as the short, famous introduction to Jobs To Be Done and competition framing.","q":{"x":"well worth the 5 minute watch","i":41687418,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":25441444,"author":"sarreph","month":"2020-12","story":"The biggest competitor to your digital service? The Mars Bar","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Enlightening milkshake explanation of competition framing","quote":"I would implore you to check-out Clayton Christensen's famous","text":"This is very much in the vein of Jobs to Be Done theory[0]. If you haven't heard of JTBD before, I would implore you to check-out Clayton Christensen's famous \"milkshake\" video here: [VIDEO] It goes into enlightening detail about the same framing of competition as the author here does. [0] - [LINK]"},{"id":41687418,"author":"nomilk","month":"2024-09","story":"Measuring Developers' Jobs-to-be-done","stance":"rec","why":"Famous short Jobs to be Done talk","quote":"well worth the 5 minute watch","text":"Also odd is the expression 'jobs to be done'. At first glance this seemed like an allusion to this famous ' Jobs to be Done ' Clay Christian talk [1] (well worth the 5 minute watch). But AFICT it's just coincidental use of the phrase. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21466517,"author":"andygcook","month":"2019-11","story":"Know Your Customers’ “Jobs to Be Done” (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Good summary of Jobs To Be Done using milkshake example","quote":"This video from Clayton Christensen gives a good summary of Jobs To Be Done using milkshakes at a fast food restaurant as an example","text":"This video from Clayton Christensen gives a good summary of Jobs To Be Done using milkshakes at a fast food restaurant as an example: [VIDEO] Unrelated, but I saw Clayton Christensen speak at a conference years ago. He started out the talk by telling everyone that he had a stroke years earlier and after it, he couldn't speak clearly. For a professor, you can imagine that's not ideal. So he went out and bought Rosetta Stone and taught himself how to talk again. I was blown away and besides a few small stutters during the talk, I barely noticed. That story of grit always stuck with me as a truly remarkable example of how versatile humans can be."}],"r":[["NNnIGh9g6fA",1]]},"v3pK0dccQ38":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it in vitamin D threads as a thorough walkthrough of why deficiency may matter.","q":{"x":"Well worth to watch the whole video","i":24063476,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":16063727,"author":"mtgx","month":"2018-01","story":"High doses of vitamin D rapidly reduce arterial stiffness","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues vitamin D supplementation benefits are underappreciated","quote":"I highly recommend these videos","text":"Proper supplementation (read: not what is currently recommended) of Vitamin D was found to reduce all-mortality causes (including cancer, diabetes, heart attacks, etc) by as much as 50%-70%. To get to that level of 40-50 ng/ml 25(OH)D, you need around 8,000-12,000 IU per day (depending on body mass). It was also found to reduce flu occurences by as much as 70%. This is starting to look like a super important vitamin, even for \"healthy individuals that work outside\" - let alone everyone else that stays all-day indoors. It also seems to have a huge effect on sleeping well, as it's apparently what the body needs to repair itself properly at night. I highly recommend these videos: [VIDEO] [LINK] Also, you should combine it with Vitamin K2, another hugely important vitamin most of us are likely lacking, that works in conjunction with D: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":24063476,"author":"mtgx","month":"2020-08","story":"Vitamin D deficiency and Covid-19 mortality [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"References studies on optimal vitamin D levels","quote":"Well worth to watch the whole video","text":"More studies referenced here, also showing ~55 ng/ml being optimum for maximum health benefits. The current recommendations are way below that. Well worth to watch the whole video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24263274,"author":"mitko","month":"2020-08","story":"Vitamin D, part 2: Shannon's story","stance":"rec","why":"Informative explanation of how the body uses vitamin D","quote":"There are two videos I found particularly informative.","text":"Few months ago I head how vitamin D is associated with COVID-19 outcomes and went into a rabbit hole to find more information about wtf is Vitamin D, and how our bodies use it/need it. There are two videos I found particularly informative. 1. [LINK] Cost/Benefit of Optimal Health with Sunshine Vitamin D William B. Grant, PhD 2015 2. [VIDEO] D is for Debacle - The Crucial Story of Vitamin D and Human Health Ivor Cummins BE(Chem), CEng MIEI 2014"},{"id":15868327,"author":"sitkack","month":"2017-12","story":"The Big Vitamin D Mistake","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on the story of vitamin D and human health","quote":"One of the many great videos on the subject","text":"One of the many great videos on the subject D is for Debacle - The Crucial Story of Vitamin D and Human Health, [VIDEO]"},{"id":16558341,"author":"mtgx","month":"2018-03","story":"Is vitamin D really a cure-all, and how should we get our fix?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in discussion of vitamin D levels","quote":"","text":"According to the study in the other HN link here, they made an error in calculating how much Vitamin D you need. The medical community also seems to think that 20 ng/ml is when the deficiency starts, when in reality it's more like 40. Some studies have shown that you can see benefits upwards of 60 ng/ml. Other research has shown that Vitamin D helps with sleep in the 60-80 ng/ml range. I've seen many people on Reddit say that their blood tests level came out at 5-10. That's crazy. Get your 25(OH)D level checked. Some of the studies that have shown \"no improvement\" from Vitamin D saw that result mainly because they were still prescribing far too little Vitamin D (yet still higher than the RDA). 10,000 IU may be necessary just to maintain your level. However, you also have to keep in mind that you won't be getting D only from the supplement, so throughout the year you should actually increase your Vitamin D level as you get more sun exposure. Meanwhile the 10,000 IU (which by the way is \"only\" 250 micrograms ) will maintain those high levels. Then, in the second year, you may further increase your Vitamin D level as you once again get more exposure in the sunny days. So you should check your Vitamin D at least after a year, to ensure your levels aren't too high. After you're stabilized you should need around 5,000-7000 IU, but it's really dependent on each person's needs, so you'll have to figure out on your own through trial and error. However, you also need to keep in mind th"}],"r":[["EL7Au1tzNxE",1],["1OEu9C51K2A",1],["gzgyksS5pX8",1],["97t7Xj_iBv0",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["n8m7lFQ3njk",1],["7hwO8Q_TyCA",1],["RMWNvwLiXIQ",1],["WTYet-qf1jo",1],["tk862BbjWx4",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["lr4MmmWQtZM",1],["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["nJPERZDfyWc",1],["mw2z9lV3W1g",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["1gEz__sMVaY",1],["amJ-ev8Ial8",1]]},"0wtvQZijPzg":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a good talk on ethics in software engineering, and Cantrill himself cites it as capturing his views.","q":{"x":"Bryan Cantrill (DTrace etc) has a good talk on ethics in software engineering[0].","i":29601593,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29601593,"author":"earleybird","month":"2021-12","story":"CCPA Scam – Human subject research study conducted by Princeton University","stance":"rec","why":"Good ethics talk, including Facebook's 2011 news feed experiments.","quote":"Bryan Cantrill (DTrace etc) has a good talk on ethics in software engineering[0].","text":"Bryan Cantrill (DTrace etc) has a good talk on ethics in software engineering[0]. Skip to 12:29 where he recalls Facebook in 2011 experimenting with users news feeds. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25457030,"author":"Bayart","month":"2020-12","story":"SEC charges Robinhood $65M for misleading customers about revenue sources","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on how engineering thinking conflicts with obeying the law.","quote":"Bryan Cantrill has a talk on YT on ethics in software [1].","text":"Bryan Cantrill has a talk on YT on ethics in software [1]. And as I remember it the take away is that the way we think about problems isn't very conducive to obeying the law, or being good to people in general. And I've got to say the amount of people I see thinking of regulations, social norms or morals as « cruft » is pretty alarming. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21863478,"author":"bitofhope","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2019?","stance":"rec","why":"Cantrill talk on ethical dilemmas in software.","quote":"Highly recommend both.","text":"This year's Cantrill talks include one on Ethical Dilemmas [0] and another on the \"Moore's Law\" paper [1]. Highly recommend both. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":43360837,"author":"Voultapher","month":"2025-03","story":"Ex-Facebook director's new book paints brutal image of Mark Zuckerberg","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill on social media being born flawed.","quote":"Brian Cantrill talks about how social media was born crocked [1]","text":"It was also born by the guy - Zuck and arguably in a similar spirit - who initially created a hot or not webpage to rank female college classmates by \"fuckability\". No clue how any of this would come as a surprise if you know his history. Brian Cantrill talks about how social media was born crocked [1] while referring to the eerily similar friendster backstory. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42952958,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2025-02","story":"Oracle justified its JavaScript trademark with Node.js–now it wants that ignored","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker lists his own talk among his criticisms of moral decay in tech.","quote":"I am using that metaphor to condemn Ellison, not to condone or accept him.","text":"I am glad to see that many people are being correcting you, but just to put a sharp point on it: I am using that metaphor to condemn Ellison, not to condone or accept him. Unfortunately, since that talk in 2011, what was an aberration (albeit a broadly unspoken one) has since -- tragically -- become the norm. I refuse to accept this, however: I will continue to speak my truth about the moral decay in tech, and why we must hold ourselves to a higher standard.[0][1][2][3][4] And I obviously agree with your indignation that poor corporate behavior should not be the status quo! [0] Principles of Technology Leadership , [LINK] (2017) [1] Andreessen's Corollary: Ethical Dilemmas in Software Engineering , [VIDEO] (2019) [2] (Anti-)application Video for YC120 , [LINK] (2019) [3] Coming Of Age , [LINK] (2022) [4] Intelligence is not Enough , [LINK] (2023)"},{"id":42585442,"author":"Voultapher","month":"2025-01","story":"I am rich and have no idea what to do","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source on move-fast attitude causing bad results.","quote":"","text":"> Within 2 minutes of talking to the final interviewer for DOGE, he asked me if I wanted to join. I said “yes”. Then he said “cool” and I was in multiple Signal groups. [...] So I reached out to some people and got in. After 8 calls with people who all talked fast and sounded very autistic smart, I was added to a number of Signal groups and immediately put to work. [...] The next 4 weeks of my life consisted of 100s of calls recruiting the smartest people I’ve ever talked to, working on various projects I’m definitely not able to talk about, and learning how completely dysfunctional the government was. It was a blast. While I can attest that current day governments in general - some more, some less and I assume past governments as well - can be quite inefficient. I sometimes fantasize about making parts of it more lean and efficient because how hard could it be, right? And with the \"right\" kind of result evaluation it will not be that hard to score better than it does right now. But having silicon valley techbros apply their move fast and break stuff attitude to things that aren't tech startups has repeatedly resulted in awful results [1]. So I must say reading this passage is quite revealing in ways I think the author did not intend it to be. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35131092,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2023-03","story":"Signature Bank Closed by FDIC","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker cites his own talk as evidence of outspokenness.","quote":"","text":"Yes, you have brought this up before.[0] And as I have said before[1] -- and will presumably say for the next 26 years as well -- I very much regret that quip. (The irony is that that was dredged up in part because people very strongly disagreed with my handling of the Noordhuis incident a decade ago.) But I will stand by my assertion that I have been outspoken about my disagreements with what I have found to be problematic in modern Silicon Valley, e.g. [2][3][4]. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":25817404,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2021-01","story":"Parler's home page is back online, but app still not in stores","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker cites own talk as capturing his views.","quote":"that talk captures my feelings on the matter","text":"Yes, it was actually Gab -- and I talked about it in a talk on ethics on software engineering.[1] As you indicate, that talk captures my feelings on the matter, so I'll let it speak for itself... [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["dCuZkaaou0Q",1],["fE2KDzZaxvE",1],["GnBbhXBDmwU",1],["EESYomdoeCs",1],["TM9h89Vo_Qo",1]]},"5kj5ApnhPAE":{"wy":"HN uses it as a concise statement of the thinking behind Go's design.","q":{"x":"gave this short speech at OSCON 2010 about what's wrong with programming languages, their thinking, and their recent direction","i":7859879,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":9776957,"author":"mtrn","month":"2015-06","story":"The Unix Philosophy","stance":"rec","why":"Rob Pike spends 12 minutes on what he sees as wrong in software.","quote":"12 minutes where Pike talks about a few things, that seem to be wrong in our software world","text":"> I wonder what rob pike has to say about OOP or java, I wish I could listen to it. 12 minutes where Pike talks about a few things, that seem to be wrong in our software world: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7859879,"author":"dottrap","month":"2014-06","story":"Why is there so much hate for .NET?","stance":"rec","why":"Short speech on what's wrong with C++/Java/C# and Go's response","quote":"gave this short speech at OSCON 2010 about what's wrong with programming languages, their thinking, and their recent direction","text":"\"Public Static Void\" Rob Pike (Bell Labs, Plan 9, Google Go) gave this short speech at OSCON 2010 about what's wrong with programming languages, their thinking, and their recent direction, with a lot of focus on C++/Java/C#, and how Go was a response to that. [VIDEO] Apple Swift seems to share much of the thinking, and the huge enthusiasm for Swift this week suggests developers seem to at least subconsciously recognize there is a problem."},{"id":4806930,"author":"james4k","month":"2012-11","story":"Go is PHP for the Backend","stance":"rec","why":"Short talk on why Go was made","quote":"Rob Pike argues exactly that in this short talk on why they made Go","text":">PHP is horribly inconsistent, breaks all the rules about programming language design, and is infuriating. Some say that rules are made to be broken. In the context of Go, Rob Pike argues exactly that in this short talk on why they made Go: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36899710,"author":"Yasuraka","month":"2023-07","story":"Austral Programming Language","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on lightness and verbosity of languages, paired with a quote.","quote":"Reminds me of this talk","text":"Reminds me of this talk: [VIDEO] And the quote to go along with it: \"I'm always delighted by the light touch and stillness of early programming languages. Not much text; a lot gets done. Old programs read like quiet conversations between a well-spoken research worker and a well-studied mechanical colleague, not as a debate with a compiler. Who'd have guessed sophistication bought such noise?\""},{"id":30444276,"author":"mustache_kimono","month":"2022-02","story":"“Sustainability with Rust” post misleading about Go","stance":"reference","why":"Pike presents Go as a simpler systems language in contrast to Java.","quote":"I actually just watched this video yesterday","text":"Rob Pike actually defined it exactly this way when it was introduced -- a simpler systems language, in contrast to Java. It's funny -- I actually just watched this video yesterday: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23712962,"author":"bokwoon","month":"2020-07","story":"Go is boring, and that’s fantastic","stance":"reference","why":"Go talk saying patterns demonstrate weakness in a language.","quote":"patterns are a demonstration of weakness in a language","text":"In Go you use the pattern of prefixing enum constants with the enum category to indicate that the enums are related to each other (e.g. StatusOk, StatusNotFound instead of status.OK, status.NotFound). Which is funny because today I came across this quote that \"patterns are a demonstration of weakness in a language\" in a Go talk [VIDEO] . I wish enums will eventually be implemented, generics are coming after all."},{"id":14963454,"author":"andrewstuart2","month":"2017-08","story":"Context should go away for Go 2","stance":"reference","why":"Pike describes what lightness in a language means, largely about verbosity.","quote":"where he probably describes pretty well what \"lightness\" means to him","text":"I guess I'm thinking mostly about the talk Rob Pike gave called \"public static void\" [1], where he probably describes pretty well what \"lightness\" means to him, or at least what it meant to him 7 years ago. It seems largely about verbosity. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4517076,"author":"mseepgood","month":"2012-09","story":"Will Go be the new go-to programming language?","stance":"reference","why":"One entry in a list of Go videos","quote":"","text":"The \"Talks and Videos\" page ( [LINK] ) is outdated as well. Here's my personal list: Why Learn Go? [LINK] Short interview with Rob Pike, OSCON 2012 Get Started with Go [LINK] Andrew Gerrand A Tour of Go [LINK] Russ Cox Go Concurrency Patterns [LINK] Rob Pike, Google I/O 2012 Goroutines, Channels Meet the Go Team [LINK] Panel (Pike, Thompson, Griesemer, Gerrand, Fitzpatrick, Simons), Google I/O 2012 Computing Map Tiles with Go on App Engine [LINK] Chris Broadfoot, Andrew Gerrand, Google I/O 2012 Go in Production [LINK] Gustavo Niemeyer, Google I/O 2012 Go: Code that grows with grace [LINK] (Contains some audio glitches) Andrew Gerrand Interview with Rob Pike [LINK] Rob Pike, GOTO Conference Lexical Scanning in Go [LINK] Rob Pike Cuddle, A Go AppEngine Demo [LINK] Andrew Gerrand Building Integrated Apps on Google's Cloud Platform [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, Google Developer Day Japan 2011 Real World Go [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, I/O BootCamp 2011 Go: it's that simple?! [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, OSDC 2011 Practical Go Programming [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, FOSDEM 2011 Writing Web Apps in Go [LINK] Andrew Gerrand, Rob Pike, Google I/O 2011 Go Programming [LINK] Rob Pike, Google I/O 2010 Another Go at Language Design [LINK] Rob Pike, Stanford University Public Static Void [VIDEO] Rob Pike, OSCON 2010 The Go Programming Language [LINK] Rob Pike, Google Tech Talk"}],"r":[["d3kfEeMZ65c",1],["5PmHRSeA2c8",1],["sln-gJaURzk",1]]},"PriwCi6SzLo":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an informative review of the academic publishing business and its monopoly-like structure.","q":{"x":"I found this discussion really informative","i":27087271,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29223093,"author":"TaXaZ","month":"2021-11","story":"Journal of Functional Programming moving to open access","stance":"rec","why":"Good review of publishing industry monopoly scheme.","quote":"You can find a good review of this scheme from below YouTube video[3].","text":"The devils are in the details. This does not still justify current model and the steep price they are paid (by taxpayers, FYI a typical university pay millions of dollar to these institutions). Consider openreview by ICLR[1] as an counter example for your claim. The maintenance, quality control, software development is not as steep as current gatekeepers advertise yet their review quality is much higher (network/transparency effect). Not to mention the profit margin of scientific publication is 3x of the Apple (38% for Elsevier) and it should be because the science workers, works free for these institutions (fighting for credit). The current business model as a whole is a legacy institution based on earlier monopoly by a charlatan named Maxwell [2]. He basically lured scientist by shiny hotels+extra packages to build the initial reputation and then monopolize the entire industry for decades. It's interesting how the model works by rip off the taxpayers twice (by publishing and access) while still peer-review process is free (from money, not credit). You can find a good review of this scheme from below YouTube video[3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29288371,"author":"TaXaZ","month":"2021-11","story":"Sci-Hub: Researchers File Intervention Application to Fight ISP Blocking","stance":"rec","why":"Good review of the publishing industry scheme.","quote":"You can find a good review of this scheme from below YouTube video[3].","text":"The current scientific publication model is only benefits the gatekeepers in publishing industry. It directly goes to the packet of publishing industry by ripping off the tax-payers twice with a steep price. Nowadays research institution pay high fees both for paywall and open-access. No matter you access or publish you some how give tax-payers money to these publishing industries. It has been shown the steep price is also un-justified as peer-review are done freely (for credit, not money) by science worker themselves and there are plenty of open-review and open-science projects which prove the price is not justified. It might worth it to repeat part of my comment in [1]: > The current business model as a whole is a legacy institution based on earlier monopoly by a charlatan named Maxwell [2]. He basically lured scientist by shiny hotels+extra packages to build the initial reputation and then monopolize the entire industry for decades. You can find a good review of this scheme from below YouTube video[3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27087271,"author":"haxiomic","month":"2021-05","story":"FBI subpoenaed Apple for data of Sci-Hub founder","stance":"rec","why":"Background on how science publishing ended up this way.","quote":"I found this discussion really informative","text":"Science Publisher Elsevier's reported profit margin is 38%, compared to Apple's 21% – there's lot of cash at stake for them and a big warchest to defend it For anyone looking to understand the background on the science publishing industry, how it ended up this way and why they will fight Sci-Hub so aggressively, I found this discussion really informative: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24884718,"author":"d0mine","month":"2020-10","story":"YouTube-dl is now part of GitHub/dmca.git","stance":"rec","why":"Recent video on academic publishing business.","quote":"(recent video on the topic that I liked)","text":"> That pirate bay and scihub are still around, that their authors are walking around freely Let's not equate these sites. scihub gives people what they are likely already paid for. Academic publishing business is a fraud. [VIDEO] (recent video on the topic that I liked)"},{"id":29223253,"author":"TaXaZ","month":"2021-11","story":"Journal of Functional Programming moving to open access","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that publishing system favors gatekeepers.","quote":"You may check this video[1]","text":"The funny thing is taxpayers ripped twice, on publishing and on access to the science and the profit goes to publication industry (not universities, research institution). If you summarize the cash flow, the publishing industry charges twice the taxpayers in addition to the science worker and research institutions costs. If you want to make your article open access, then you pay extra fees and nowadays, the university funds ripped off twice: they still bear the cost of pay-wall publishers and open access. Long story short, the whole system is rigged in favor of gatekeepers. You may check this video[1] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PC2JOQ7z5L0",1],["N1pIYI5JQLE",1],["iph500cPK28",1],["UDtlvZGmHYk",1],["rzQEIRRJ2T0",1],["0g9BcZvQbXU",1]]},"ZCmnQR3_qWg":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a good overview of the NIST candidates and the state of post-quantum crypto.","q":{"x":"This talk by djb and Tanja Lange is a good overview of the NIST candidates and the current state of pqcrypto","i":19151197,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20001419,"author":"est31","month":"2019-05","story":"How to factor 2048 bit RSA integers in 8 hours using 20M noisy qubits","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on quantum-proof algorithms and the NIST competition.","quote":"You can find an awesome talk on the topic here","text":"AES-256 is believed to be quantum-resistant. The only known quantum attack on AES-256 is utilizing Grover's algorithm, which is an universal approach. As for assymetric crypto, the ones we have now are susceptible to quantum attacks. There are a bunch of proposals for quantum-proof algorithms but nothing officially standardized yet. There is a NIST competition going on right now that is trying to find a new suite of assymetric cryptographic algorithms [1]. You can find an awesome talk on the topic here [2]. If quantum computers are possible, we should switch to quantum proof algorithms now rather than later, because then we'd reduce the traffic that can be decrypted at a later point in time. It's quite dangerous right now considering that basically everything is protected by quantum-non-resistant ciphers. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19535847,"author":"shittyadmin","month":"2019-03","story":"Cryptography of SSH (2006)","stance":"rec","why":"Details on progress of post-quantum algorithm research.","quote":"I'd advise checking out some of the recent DJB and Tanja Lange talks for further detail","text":"They're using a mixture of Streamlined NTRU Prime 4591^761 and X25519. [LINK] Note that this is still considered \"experimental\" and probably won't be enabled by default. I'd advise checking out some of the recent DJB and Tanja Lange talks for further detail on how research on these algorithms is progressing: [VIDEO] There's also the older but still relevant PQCHacks: [LINK] Haven't seen any talk of post quantum signatures in changelogs yet at least, but research is happening rapidly in this field and OpenSSH has really upped their game in recent years."},{"id":19151197,"author":"sigil","month":"2019-02","story":"NIST Reveals 26 Algorithms Advancing to the Post-Quantum Crypto ‘Semifinals’","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of NIST candidates and state of post-quantum crypto.","quote":"This talk by djb and Tanja Lange is a good overview of the NIST candidates and the current state of pqcrypto","text":"This talk by djb and Tanja Lange is a good overview of the NIST candidates and the current state of pqcrypto: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21010635,"author":"cyphar","month":"2019-09","story":"IBM will soon launch a 53-qubit quantum computer","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for broken post-quantum proposals.","quote":"","text":"PQ-Crypto is still a very young research area. There is no widespread (or even niche) use of PQ-Crypto algorithms, no consensus on how safe any of the proposed algorithms are (and many proposals were found to be exceptionally broken -- on classical hardware -- within weeks of their submission[1]). I'm not really sure it's fair to say that just because there are some on-paper algorithms (which have no usage outside academia at the moment ) that might solve the problem that it's false to say that \"all modern PK crypto is broken with quantum attackers\". But if you prefer, you could imagine that I said \"all modern PK crypto [that is actually in use today] is broken\". Because, at the end of the day, that's all that really matters. [As an aside, for RSA the security is provided by the hardness of factorisation which also breaks under QC attackers, not discrete log (it's discrete log for DH, and EC discrete log for ECDH).] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19894946,"author":"cyphar","month":"2019-05","story":"Signal, a fast, secure and simple messenger endorsed by Snowden","stance":"reference","why":"Notes djb and colleagues give talks on post-quantum crypto.","quote":"","text":"The problem is that the largest QCs that we know of in the public are so ridiculously weak that I find it incredibly improbable that any government is anywhere close to having large enough QCs to break anything. The largest QCs are on the order of tens of qubits, but in order to run Shor's algorithm (to break RSA, or the discrete-log equivalent to break Diffie-Hellman) you need hundreds or thousands of stable qubits -- and the current generation of qubits are unstable. You can make stable qubits out of unstable ones by using error correction, but that requires you to have ~1000 unstable qubits to make one stable qubit. So there's at least 5-6 orders of magnitude separating the current state-of-the-art and when classic public-key crypto will be broken. > Does signal use the quantum-proof variety of encryption? No, there is currently no trusted post-quantum encryption (though the real issue is finding a replacement for public-key crypto because that seems to be the weakest link where quantum computers appear to have the most power). It's being worked on but is a very new field. In addition most post-quantum systems require keys that are kilobytes or even gigabytes in size (elliptic curve keys are tens of bytes). djb (a very well-known cryptographer) and his colleagues give quite a few talks about this[1]. Funnily enough it appears that elliptic curves (which are harder for classic computers to break, and can be easier for classic computers to compute safely) are actually easier"},{"id":19894991,"author":"cyphar","month":"2019-05","story":"Signal, a fast, secure and simple messenger endorsed by Snowden","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to clarify what Google's post-quantum experiment actually tested.","quote":"This was gone through in the Bernstein and Lange talk this year at 35C3","text":"> Google and others have experimented with it for https and it worked fine To be clear, Google didn't experiment with actually using post-quantum crypto. Their experiment was to figure out what was the largest key sizes that browsers and networks would accept before you start degrading connections. Their \"post-quantum keys\" were just padding bytes. This was gone through in the Bernstein and Lange talk this year at 35C3[1]. This is a good thing, given the history of the security of post-quantum candidates[2]. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["gbYXBJOFgeI",1],["bnKIVX968PQ",1],["snAhsXyO3Ck",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["8xDK2LgSeyk",1],["pRlh8LX4kQI",1]]},"aY985qzn7oI":{"wy":"Frequently linked as an in-depth, well-presented skeptical take whenever Loeb's alien claims come up.","q":{"x":"I was surprised about myself that I watched the entire thing and I guess it's because of the high quality of the presentation","i":36662982,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37280851,"author":"DemocracyFTW2","month":"2023-08","story":"Astrophysicist Avi Loeb: ‘UFOs should be the subject of mainstream inquiry'","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Required viewing on Loeb","quote":"Required viewing","text":"Yeah, that guy again. Required viewing: acollierastro harvard & aliens & crackpots: a disambiguation of Avi Loeb [VIDEO]"},{"id":36662982,"author":"DemocracyFTW2","month":"2023-07","story":"Professor believes alien fragments found in 2014 Papua New Guinea meteor","stance":"strong_rec","why":"High-quality, deadpan takedown of Loeb's unsupported alien claims","quote":"I was surprised about myself that I watched the entire thing and I guess it's because of the high quality of the presentation","text":"posted here a few days ago: [ harvard & aliens & crackpots: a disambiguation of Avi Loeb ]( [VIDEO] ) by Angela Collier. It's 1h6m long but I was surprised about myself that I watched the entire thing and I guess it's because of the high quality of the presentation and the occasional deadpan remark that keep you awake. Talk about Avi Loeb specifically start at 24 minutes. The most damning part of her takedown is probably when she shows zoom footage of a physicists' video conference and other snippets where Loeb is literally foaming with expletives directed mostly at fellow physicists, y'know, the Establishment who just wouldn't follow his by-now predictable but wholly unsupported jump from \"I found mineral spherules on the bottom of the ocean\" to \"these must come from that deep space meteorite that I lately discovered\" to \"It's aliens!\". Collier is such a sound antidote to all that flaky and faky science coverage we're getting. For all the clear words that she finds she still leaves viewers with an open question: \"So is Loeb a grifter? I don't think so. Is he a crackpot? Future will tell.\""},{"id":37293634,"author":"hikingsimulator","month":"2023-08","story":"A Harvard Professor Became the World’s Leading Alien Hunter","stance":"rec","why":"Small science youtuber covers Loeb and crackpots in science","quote":"a small youtuber who does science communication","text":"Since it's on topic, I'd like to share a small youtuber who does science communication. She covered Loeb and the topics of crackpots in science [1]. Her other video on string theory and how it damaged science communication is really good too. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37295787,"author":"adolph","month":"2023-08","story":"A Harvard Professor Became the World’s Leading Alien Hunter","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Loeb's interaction with Tarter, with a fair-minded vlogger","quote":"The entire video is worth listening to and the vlogger is refreshingly fair minded.","text":"Thanks to hikingsimulator's link below, here is Loeb interacting with Tarter [0]. The entire video is worth listening to and the vlogger is refreshingly fair minded. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37318556,"author":"Eji1700","month":"2023-08","story":"Discovery of Spherules of Likely Extrasolar Composition in the Pacific Ocean [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Accurate, in-depth breakdown of why Loeb's paper count is not impressive","quote":"If you want a very accurate and in depth breakdown of why, i suggest you watch this","text":"> He's written \"over 800 scientific papers\". Is that... a good thing in his field? No...it really isn't. If you want a very accurate and in depth breakdown of why, i suggest you watch this: [VIDEO] If you don't want to do the whole thing, just jump to his lovely little interaction with Jill Tarter. Then look at all the comments trying to somehow give this snake oil salesman credibility. He's a publicity chaser who's past his prime and cashing in on his reputation and abusing his position. The fact harvard is profiting from it frankly is a disgrace."},{"id":37328727,"author":"DemocracyFTW2","month":"2023-08","story":"Discovery of Spherules of Likely Extrasolar Composition in the Pacific Ocean [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Very detailed, lengthy but entertaining video on Loeb","quote":"that very detailed (and sort of lengthy, but entertaining) video","text":"OK so maybe I should've just linked e.g. to that very detailed (and sort of lengthy, but entertaining) video by acollierastro \"harvard & aliens & crackpots: a disambiguation of Avi Loeb\" ( [VIDEO] ) which is definitely not flamewar-ish. Guess I've gotten somewhat thin-skinned given the volume of faux / overblown science news of late. For those reading this, there's yet another post here ( [LINK] ) that links to a harvard.edu article that gives more details on why the collectors of the spherules believe they have reason to assume that (1) they are really from the suspected meteorite, (2) the material's composition doesn't resemble materials known from other meteorites. Many questions remain though; for those interested in spherules from outer space, there's yet another earlier post ( [LINK] ) that links to a video that shows how excrutiating it is to collect and sort out teeny meteorites from the environment. Having seen that and a clip about the expedition's method of collecting (lowering a strong magnet to the seafloor) I wonder how often they lifted the thing up to collect material because otherwise they could not produce statistics about abundance in relation to the asteroid's flight path."},{"id":36654197,"author":"maxbond","month":"2023-07","story":"Physicist who found spherical meteor fragments claims they are alien","stance":"rec","why":"Video essay adding interesting context on Loeb, though long","quote":"this video adds interesting context","text":"This isn't the first time Loeb has shouted \"aliens!\" Previously he said Ouamuamua was aliens based on a hunch, and after the news got their hands on it and declared aliens to the public, astronomers were understandably peeved with him and asked him not to shout \"aliens!\" unilaterally based on a hunch. Loeb didn't take it well. There's an amazing clip where he rants about how he is the only one serious about finding extraterrestrial intelligence... To a room full of career SETI astronomers. Here's a video essay about him. (People who have a strong preference for direct/to the point videos would probably be better served seeking out another source, I feel like this video adds interesting context but I know a lot of you would feel it's fluff.) [VIDEO]"},{"id":38483438,"author":"DemocracyFTW2","month":"2023-12","story":"Harvard astronomer's \"alien spherules\" are industrial pollutants","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Fun link dump: * harvard & aliens & crackpots: a disambiguation of Avi Loeb by acollierastro (2022) [VIDEO] * Critique of arXiv submission 2308.15623, \"Discovery of Spherules of Likely Extrasolar Composition in the Pacific Ocean Site of the CNEOS 2014-01-08 (IM1) Bolide\", by A. Loeb et al by Steve Desch, Alan Jackson [LINK] * The IM1 Spherules from the Pacific Ocean Have Extrasolar Composition by Avi Loeb (August 2023) [LINK] * Reconstructing the First Interstellar Meteor, IM1 by Avi Loeb (September 2023) [LINK]"},{"id":37315521,"author":"ftxbro","month":"2023-08","story":"Metallic spheres on Pacific floor are interstellar, Harvard professor finds","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Loeb's background from an astronomer's perspective","quote":"","text":"Oh it's Avi Loeb, head of the astronomy department at Harvard. Here's a youtube about what his deal is, by an astronomer who studied astronomy through postdoc level and then left for industry and doing some youtubes [VIDEO]"},{"id":37317503,"author":"ftxbro","month":"2023-08","story":"Discovery of Spherules of Likely Extrasolar Composition in the Pacific Ocean [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Loeb's background from an astronomer's perspective","quote":"","text":"Oh it's Avi Loeb, head of the astronomy department at Harvard. Here's a youtube about what his deal is, by an astronomer who studied astronomy through postdoc level and then left for industry and doing some youtubes [VIDEO]"},{"id":37317622,"author":"maxbond","month":"2023-08","story":"Discovery of Spherules of Likely Extrasolar Composition in the Pacific Ocean [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Hour-long essay on Loeb's reputation and thin papers","quote":"he doesn't seem to have a good reputation within the SETI community","text":"Here's a video essay from an astrophysicist about Avi Loeb (though she's transitioned to the private sector, I believe software engineering but I may be mistaken): [VIDEO] [1 hour] The video predates the spherecule stuff and focuses on his assertion 'Oumuamua was \"most likely\" debris from an extraterrestrial spacecraft. Tl;Dw: he doesn't seem to have a good reputation within the SETI community, and Dr. Collie's personal opinion is that many of these papers are insubstantial; he often publishes a couple paragraphs proposing a thought experiment or cool idea, more blog posts than research."},{"id":36661929,"author":"throwaway202351","month":"2023-07","story":"Harvard professor Avi Loeb believes he's found fragments of alien technology","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of this youtube vid I saw about him recently, titled \"harvard & aliens & crackpots: a disambiguation of Avi Loeb.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":35237487,"author":"7thaccount","month":"2023-03","story":"Could UFOs be alien probes? A new 2023 Harvard study explores it [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"A physicist goes into depth on the papers in question","quote":"Here's a physicist going into some depth on what I referred to","text":"I watched his chat with SETI in full and it was unprofessional to say the least. I stand by that. Here's an article talking about it: [LINK] As to the part on the papers, I was open that it was just hearsay and asked for someone with better background to weigh in. Here's a physicist going into some depth on what I referred to: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44785273,"author":"Teever","month":"2025-08","story":"Is the interstellar object 3I/ATLAS alien technology? [pdf]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"You should be skeptical of anything he writes after watching this","text":"It really isn't. One of the authors (Abraham Loeb) is well known for writing salami-sliced papers that have tenuous and non-testable premises. You should be skeptical of anything he writes after watching this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39760407,"author":"nick238","month":"2024-03","story":"The humiliating truth behind Harvard astronomer's alien spherules","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I liked the time when Avi yelled at the director of SETI, Jill Tarter","text":"I liked the time when Avi yelled at the director of SETI, Jill Tarter (inspiration for the fictional Ellie Arroway from the movie Contact), whose life's work has been searching for ETs for not searching for ETs in the right way. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38413481,"author":"jonathrg","month":"2023-11","story":"Superficial Toxicity Is the Enemy of Curiosity-Driven Innovation in Science","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here's a nice expos","text":"Important context: this is Avi Loeb, the guy who insisted that Oumuamua might be aliens. Here's a nice exposé on him and his shoddy work [VIDEO]"},{"id":37698494,"author":"Supermancho","month":"2023-09","story":"What if Planet 9 is a Primordial Black Hole? (2019)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"His skype rant to SETI leadership about how nobody is taking the idea of contact or evidence of alien civilizations seriously, is really sad.","text":"The man produces garbage papers. He characterizes the work as useful stepping stones for other scientists to do the rest of the obvious followup work, which he can't be bothered with. He's too busy churning out thought experiments. [VIDEO] His skype rant to SETI leadership about how nobody is taking the idea of contact or evidence of alien civilizations seriously, is really sad."},{"id":37324450,"author":"mcpackieh","month":"2023-08","story":"Discovery of Spherules of Likely Extrasolar Composition in the Pacific Ocean [pdf]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Avi Loeb loudly publicly berated a leader of SETI for not being willing to consider the existence of aliens","text":"For context, Avi Loeb loudly publicly berated a leader of SETI for not being willing to consider the existence of aliens, evidently unaware of who she even was and what sort of work she was involved with: [VIDEO] Avi Loeb is the malicious crank who uses theatrical grandstanding to make himself seem like a victim, alienating people who should be his natural allies if he were calm and sincere."}],"r":[["lC4YLMLar5I",1],["eMmNy11Mn7g",1],["jQIHqkudgNY",1],["mPn2AdMH7UQ",1],["klaJqofCsu4",1],["YdOXS_9_P4U",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["y7h4OtFDnYE",1],["MvgN5gCuLac",1]]},"ez4DgdurRPg":{"wy":"Recommended as a vivid visual demonstration of how unreliable p-values are.","q":{"x":"absolutely fantastic and well worth watching","i":9110898,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9110898,"author":"po","month":"2015-02","story":"Psychology Journal Bans Significance Testing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Dance of the p values demonstration on significance testing","quote":"absolutely fantastic and well worth watching","text":"The audio is a bit quiet but the 'dance of the p values' video [1] linked to by the article is absolutely fantastic and well worth watching. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":3308528,"author":"knowledgesale","month":"2011-12","story":"Ask HN: What interesting non-intuitive things do you know?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visual demonstration of how unstable p-values are across samples","quote":"The [3] video pretty much sums it up and by all means is worth a watch.","text":"The p-value [1] criterion is often used to test a proposed hypothesis in medical and social science research papers. When you stumble upon something along the lines of \"studies that shown that eating broccoli makes people happy\" in your everyday life, it comes down to the p-value calculation being small enough <0.05 for the before and after gathered datasets. The p-value method is practically a standard for scientific reporting in some fields. It also has some drastic shortcomings, including, e,g., dramatic instability for tests with only little data [2]. Naturally, people realize that and try to use additional tools and criteria when available. However, scientists are pretty brutally incentivized to publish positive results and, as a result, more often than it should be, too much weight is put on the single p-value criterion. With issues like this in mind, in my opinion, it makes sense to be somewhat skeptical when seeing reports in the news that \"A effects B\" and definitely not to rush with the conclusions. Trivial, I know. The [3] video pretty much sums it up and by all means is worth a watch. ------------------- [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7432519,"author":"lomnakkus","month":"2014-03","story":"Gravity waves from Big Bang detected","stance":"reference","why":"Shows p-values are a sketchy measure of truth","quote":"p-values are pretty sketchy as a measure of \"is this likely to be true?","text":"I can't claim to understand much of the statistics, but according to [1], p-values are pretty sketchy as a measure of \"is this likely to be true?\" [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AxFzf6yIfcc",1]]},"hFL6qRIJZ_Y":{"wy":"HN people offer it as practical focus and ADHD advice from a credentialed neuroscientist, though some treat it as only a reference.","q":{"x":"He's pretty well credentialed though, so I trust on average the advice is good","i":34490164,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39460290,"author":"StuffMaster","month":"2024-02","story":"Stop postponing things by embracing the mess","stance":"rec","why":"Huberman on improving focus","quote":"+1 for Andrew Huberman","text":"+1 for Andrew Huberman Controlling Your Dopamine For Motivation, Focus & Satisfaction: [LINK] ADHD & How Anyone Can Improve Their Focus: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34490164,"author":"maerF0x0","month":"2023-01","story":"How do ADHD people cope on here?","stance":"rec","why":"Advice on ADHD and focus from a credentialed source","quote":"He's pretty well credentialed though, so I trust on average the advice is good","text":"Stayfocusd chrome extension for sites that are trouble during working hours. I've started to implement the advice here: [VIDEO] albeit too early to tell. He's pretty well credentialed though, so I trust on average the advice is good (but specifically for each individual YMMV) One of his best points is dont act like or prescribe yourself ADHD solutions until you have a true diagnosis by an expert. Losing focus and distractions is commonplace for all people today, not just ADHD folks."},{"id":30473626,"author":"vedran","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Any scientifically proven techniques to boost concentration?","stance":"rec","why":"Huberman on improving focus and ADHD","quote":"A few more from Dr. Huberman","text":"A few more from Dr. Huberman: ADHD & How Anyone Can Improve Their Focus - [VIDEO] How to Focus to Change Your Brain - [LINK]"},{"id":37618212,"author":"corobo","month":"2023-09","story":"Show HN: Summary Cat, a YouTube Video Summary Generator","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Just used this to clear out my watch later list without having to watch anything. Nice! Only note I have at this time is that it seemed to time out or hang or something on a long video (>2h) -- I'm guessing that there might be limitations to how much transcript you can chuck into GPT, it might be worth throwing an error of some sort in that scenario rather than the forever load E: Seen you've asked for an example to the other person mentioning this. In my case it was this video [VIDEO]"},{"id":34033114,"author":"abrichr","month":"2022-12","story":"Show HN: Factual AI Q&A – Answers based on Huberman Lab transcripts","stance":"reference","why":"Brain areas desynchronized in ADHD","quote":"these are three brain areas that normally are synchronized in their activities","text":"> What are the parts of the brain that become de-synchronized in the ADHD brain? >> The default mode network and the task networks become de-synchronized in the ADHD brain. > What are the three parts of the brain that become de-synchronized in the ADHD brain? >> The default mode network, the task networks, and the dopamine circuits. From [VIDEO] : > An area called the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex ... the posterior cingulate cortex, and ... the lateral parietal lobe ... these are three brain areas that normally are synchronized in their activities ... that's how it is in a typical person. In a person with ADHD ... these brain areas are not playing well with each other. I wonder if part of the problem might be the usage of text to speech. Did you consider scraping the transcriptions instead? e.g. with [LINK]"}],"r":[["dngqR9gcDDw",1],["kZA9Hnp3aV4",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["iHzzSao6ypE",1],["QmOF0crdyRU",1]]},"kfM-yu0iQBk":{"wy":"Recommended as a good first-steps shader tutorial that also teaches broad graphics knowledge.","q":{"x":"I liked this tutorial series as a \"first steps\" by Freya Holmér","i":30364881,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":43668943,"author":"FjordWarden","month":"2025-04","story":"Shadertoys Ported to Rust GPU","stance":"rec","why":"Freya has many videos on shaders.","quote":"Freya[2] also has a good number of videos on it.","text":"I've always enjoyed watching The Art of Code[1], Freya[2] also has a good number of videos on it. But that is just vertex shaders for things like Shadertoys. There is much more to computer graphics or GPGPU than this though, Im still learning about that myself X). [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30364881,"author":"honkycat","month":"2022-02","story":"The harsh truth of video games programming","stance":"rec","why":"First-steps shader tutorial series.","quote":"I liked this tutorial series as a \"first steps\" by Freya Holmér","text":"For procedural animation: I have yet to find the path to learning this skill. It is very opaque, and I think you just need to attack it with your brain and learn it. For shader and graphics programming: I liked this tutorial series as a \"first steps\" by Freya Holmér: [VIDEO] Here is another list: [LINK] The Book of Shaders is a popular suggestion, but it appears to be abandoned: [LINK] For general Unreal? Tom Looman's $300 Unreal C++ class, available on his website. It is pricey but really excellent. I also discovered this AWESOME list of learning paths for all gamedev concepts: [LINK]"},{"id":28659968,"author":"high_byte","month":"2021-09","story":"Visualize 3D hyperbolic honeycombs and sphere packings","stance":"rec","why":"Good tutorial with broad graphics information.","quote":"this is a good tutorial but also lots of good info about graphics in general","text":"[LINK] and a good sandbox is [LINK] and you should consider unity too, this is a good tutorial but also lots of good info about graphics in general: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["eKtsY7hYTPg",1]]},"uWUHkCgslNE":{"wy":"Cited as a demo of GPU-powered noise suppression that works remarkably well, in threads on mic muting and speech enhancement.","q":{"x":"It's really pretty impressive, here's a demo","i":25645169,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28318592,"author":"Sohcahtoa82","month":"2021-08","story":"Discord has won over gamers. Now it wants everybody else","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Filters background noise on incoming and outgoing voice.","quote":"NVIDIA Broadcast (Previously called RTX Voice) is a godsend for those scenarios.","text":"> someone else's background or breathing noise NVIDIA Broadcast (Previously called RTX Voice) is a godsend for those scenarios. If you haven't seen it, it's worth checking out. It is absolutely magical. [VIDEO] Sadly, AFAIK, it does require an NVIDIA GPU, but it doesn't need an RTX card. I used it on my GTX 1070 and it worked wonderfully. What's really amazing about it is that you can use it to filter incoming voice audio, so if you have someone in your channel that has tons of background noise or breathing, you can filter it out on your own side."},{"id":25689483,"author":"Sohcahtoa82","month":"2021-01","story":"Mute Your Microphone While Typing with Hushboard","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Noise removal that works like magic.","quote":"Seriously, nVidia's noise removal is absolute magic, as far as I'm concerned.","text":"If you have an RTX card, get nVidia Broadcast. If you have a non-RTX card (like a GTX 1070), get RTX voice. Despite the name, it does not require an RTX card. Seriously, nVidia's noise removal is absolute magic, as far as I'm concerned. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25645169,"author":"wheybags","month":"2021-01","story":"Show HN: Unclack – a macOS app that mutes your microphone while you type","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of GPU neural denoising of voice calls.","quote":"It's really pretty impressive, here's a demo","text":"A solution like rtx voice seems a better way to go tbh. For those who don't know, it's an nvidia thing that uses a GPU accelerated (ofc, it's nvidia) neural network to denoise voice calls, from all kinds of noise, not just typing. It's really pretty impressive, here's a demo: [VIDEO] I wish it wasn't proprietary though (and worse, hardware specific). And windows only of course."},{"id":40509230,"author":"i5heu","month":"2024-05","story":"AI headphones let wearer listen to a single person in a crowd by looking at them","stance":"reference","why":"Related Nvidia voice isolation tech.","quote":"This remembers me of NVIDIA RTX Voice [0].","text":"This remembers me of NVIDIA RTX Voice [0]. Although not made to isolate single persons, this is quite impressive. I hope that this single person isolation will find it's way to consumer noise-cancelling headphones [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34051372,"author":"jacooper","month":"2022-12","story":"Enhance Speech from Adobe – Free AI filter for cleaning up spoken audio","stance":"reference","why":"Similar Nvidia tool running live on recording.","quote":"Nvidia has a similar thing called RTX audio","text":"Nvidia has a similar thing called RTX audio, but its even more impressive and runs live on recording [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["6RhtiPefVzM",1],["thnXzUFJnfQ",1]]},"xMzojQFyMo0":{"wy":"Recommended as a glimpse of Papert's vision of computers as tools for learning, shared especially after his death.","q":{"x":"I firmly believe that we'd all be better off if THIS guy had been the one to introduce computers to the common man","i":8602145,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":13492724,"author":"kickscondor","month":"2017-01","story":"Thinking about Thinking about Seymour: Livestream [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Papert on keeping computer instruction somatic and physical.","quote":"One really powerful idea he had was ensuring that we stay \"somatic\" with our computer instruction","text":"If you haven't been exposed to Papert - check out some of his old videos from the 70s and 80s. [LINK] - One really powerful idea he had was ensuring that we stay \"somatic\" with our computer instruction - playing with abstract concepts in a physical way. Watch the video - he explains it better than I can. [VIDEO] - One from the 1970s - perhaps I'm wrong, but it seems like some of these computers they're using don't even have monitors, just control the turtle. [LINK] - I also like this discussion - it reminds me that school is a prison for children, unless I work to elevate it beyond that. I just kind of watch these periodically to remind me of the wonder that computing was back then."},{"id":12203048,"author":"pfooti","month":"2016-08","story":"Seymour Papert has died","stance":"rec","why":"Early LOGO video pushing programming for all.","quote":"is a great video from the early days of LOGO","text":"Wow, RIP - he was a big inspiration to me professionally both in terms of the tech he helped build and in terms of the learning theory of which he was a proponent. (I also work at the intersection of education, learning sciences, and technology). [0] is a great video from the early days of LOGO, and he's pushing notions of programming for all that felt new and revolutionary in the 2000s. Mindstorms [1] is a great book if you're interested in his ideas about learning. 0: [VIDEO] 1: [LINK]"},{"id":8602145,"author":"daveloyall","month":"2014-11","story":"Microsoft takes .NET open source and cross-platform","stance":"rec","why":"Papert's vision of computers for learning.","quote":"I firmly believe that we'd all be better off if THIS guy had been the one to introduce computers to the common man","text":"[LINK] [LINK] > I think you have it backwards. It's the new vision of computers (walled gardens, all the interesting logic hidden away in data centers, content consumption appliances instead of real computers) that is pale and sickly. Yes, these new trends are worrisome. But it's not the first time! I firmly believe that we'd all be better off if THIS guy had been the one to introduce computers to the common man: [VIDEO] [LINK] To this day, Microsoft is putting computers in schools--but not LOGO, not free software, not unix, not lisp, not crates of disposable m68ks and bread boards... Nope, they are putting products in front of new generations eyeballs. Data can trump anecdotes, and it should when a culture believes they are doing the right thing but things are still going poorly and nobody understands why--at those times, by all means, collect some data and analyze it! But when \"everybody knows\" we're doing something wrong, and things are going poorly, then for the love of god use some common sense and stop doing it! The whole planet got 0wned by MS before many of us here were born--certainly before most of us were competent. So this is what we've got, this is where we are. Big picture: Where do we go from here? Immediate situation: don't become part of a new generation of MS fanboys. We don't need that."}],"r":[["bOf4EMN6-XA",2],["UBc7qBS1Ujo",1]]},"45mMioJ5szc":{"wy":"HN invokes it as the classic illustration of learning through repeated failure and iterating, often in startup threads.","q":{"x":"which everyone should see","i":2661524,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2661524,"author":"edanm","month":"2011-06","story":"The end of Facebook","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jordan's Failure commercial.","quote":"which everyone should see","text":"(Even more off-topic) You really reminded me of Jordan's \"Failure\" commercial, which everyone should see: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11498282,"author":"jathu","month":"2016-04","story":"Every shot Kobe Bryant ever took","stance":"rec","why":"Jordan lists missed shots and lost games behind his success.","quote":"Amazing commercial (like most Nike commercials).","text":"Reminds me of the Jordan commercial [1] where he mentions he lost more than 9000 shots in his career, lost almost 300 games and missed 26 game winning shots. Amazing commercial (like most Nike commercials). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11586324,"author":"ronilan","month":"2016-04","story":"CV of Failures [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Points to the original commercial rather than a remake.","quote":"You failed to link to the original commercial and instead linked to a remake","text":"You failed to link to the original commercial and instead linked to a remake :) ( [VIDEO] Uploaded on Aug 25, 2006)"},{"id":2102692,"author":"whatusername","month":"2011-01","story":"“OK, but there are two rules…”","stance":"reference","why":"Classic sports example of failing and iterating.","quote":"For the classic sports example","text":"It depends on what extreme you take it to. For the classic sports example: [VIDEO] Fail Fast can also be seen as: Perform, get feedback, perform slightly better, get more feedback, iterate until perfect."},{"id":1663081,"author":"jw84","month":"2010-09","story":"How do I write so much, you ask? Well, glad you asked","stance":"reference","why":"Jordan's philosophy of learning through repeated failure.","quote":"This is Michael Jordan's philosophy","text":"Geeky way of saying a million monkeys at a typewriter. This is Michael Jordan's philosophy: [VIDEO] Ira Glass believes that some people start out producing work that isn't as good or match their good taste. It's after a lot of work, trial and error, and mistakes that the disparity between your good taste and your good work match up. Be patient, keep trying, be confidant. I should have joined a football team."},{"id":1567379,"author":"harscoat","month":"2010-08","story":"Ask HN: Do you have any startup related regrets? Epic failures?","stance":"reference","why":"Comment shares startup failure lessons; video not clearly discussed.","quote":"","text":"I failed Zden.com in 2002 (launched in 2000). Regrets turned into positive(?) lessons (I hope one day I can say that). * When you are doomed, you are doomed. Better to start over than try to fix. If there is good chance that the reason why you do not get traction is mono-dimensional (eg. just the UI or (exclusive) just performance...), it seems ok to go on & try to tweak it, but if you it is multidimensional (several reasons) (Technology, management, wrong market assumptions), then pull the plug ASAP. I did not have the guts to cut 3 fingers, so reality took my arm. * Even if others \"co-founders\", I was alone (CEO with masteroftheuniverse equity stake), I had just came back from a 99 boiling BayArea startup, there was a wrong \"guru\" effect: no real co-founder (especially hacker) to tell me \"this is wrong, you are kidding yourself\". Selfcomplacent, mono thinking: doomed. * Focus, focus, focus: you have to make hard choices so that you will NOT do X,Y,Z in order to become better at U. Not only experienced it for Zden, but another company I have worked (could have gone further quicker) for they did not have the balls (at least for 9years) to cut off either distractive product/features, or market sectors that are not going anywhere while another is booming etc. \"Everything for everybody\": doomed * Do not impose new behaviors on users/prospects (or just a super light one maybe) (worst is several at the same time). Users/prospects do not care about you & your service (by definition"},{"id":1640162,"author":"andreyf","month":"2010-08","story":"Steve Jobs explains branding","stance":"reference","why":"Jordan ad about overcoming fear of failure illustrating Nike branding.","quote":"overcoming fear of failure","text":"The strategy behind both Nike's and Apple's branding is much further expounded on in a great film called Art & Copy. I highly recommend it. Nike's message is \"just do it\". It isn't about hanging out with athletes, it's appreciating aspiration and athleticism of all stripes [1]. Nike is a dominant enough clothing brand that they benefit from encouraging general athleticism. Their products are barely present in their branding, if at all. Apple's message is (and has been) to \"think different\" and \"we're here to help you change the world for the better\". Sure, it appeals to the rebellious youth in people, but it also appeals to scientists, artists, and philosophers in search of new paradigms to overturn the status quo. The \"think different\" commercial isn't about their computers. It's a public service announcement sponsored by Apple. 1. Even when Nike puts Michael Jordan in a commercial, it isn't about \"hanging out with him\", but about overcoming fear of failure, and \"just doing it\": [VIDEO] PS: Just searched for \"nike michael jordan commercial\" for more, and found another brilliant piece: [LINK] (again, I'd argue this is about appreciating the beauty of athleticism more so than \"hanging out with Jordan\")."}],"r":[["sqEyWLGvvdw",1],["fNk_zzaMoSs",1]]},"8QiPFmIMxFc":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an inspiring talk on immediate feedback and on living by a principle.","q":{"x":"This gave me a sharp moment of clarity, thank you so much for this!","i":30239526,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30239526,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Inspiring hacker talks and presentations?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gave a sharp moment of clarity on principles","quote":"This gave me a sharp moment of clarity, thank you so much for this!","text":">Brett Victor - Inventing on principle: [VIDEO] This gave me a sharp moment of clarity, thank you so much for this! I'll work on the wording over time, but here's a rough sketch of my principle: My principle is that no person should ever be forced to blindly trust a computer to do the right thing. Computing shouldn't be either blindly trust the black box, or get nothing done. Nobody should have to hand over their wallet to buy an ice cream cone, you can just take the exact change out and pay. Why should you have to give a program access to everything when you just want to edit one text file?"},{"id":30239914,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: How do you deal with getting old and feeling lost?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Helped find a guiding principle to change the world","quote":"I just figured out my pet peeve, the design principle I wish to impart to the world","text":"Is there some pet peeve you have about the world, which you wish to change? If there is, and you can put your finger on it, making it happen could be a very fulfilling thing to do. I just figured out my pet peeve, the design principle I wish to impart to the world... after watching this video recommended in a different thread Bret Victor Inventing on Principle -- [VIDEO] I wish you luck if you find yours."},{"id":30238757,"author":"iwwr","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Inspiring hacker talks and presentations?","stance":"rec","why":"Inspiring hacker talk on living by a principle","quote":"Brett Victor - Inventing on principle","text":"Rich Hickey - Hammock-driven development: [LINK] Brett Victor - Inventing on principle: [VIDEO] Programming is terrible: [LINK] Dave Beazely - Python concurrency from the ground up (applicable to languages in general, with generator and corourine functionality) [LINK] Functional programming, with birds: [LINK] Short and classic: Wat [LINK]"},{"id":22988538,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-04","story":"Maybe visual programming is the answer, maybe not","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on programs responding continuously to changes","quote":"have you ever seen Bret Victor's talks","text":">> They continuously run every time you make any change to them. >Which is very much unlike what a program does. You're saying \"Program that run continuously every time you make any change are very much unlike what a program does?\" That doesn't make any sense to me at all, can you please try to rephrase it? Speaking of program that run continuously, have you ever seen Bret Victor's talks \"The Future of Programming\" and \"Inventing on Principle\", or heard of Doug Engelbart's work? The Future of Programming [LINK] Inventing on Principle [VIDEO] HN discussion: [LINK] \"I'm totally confident that in 40 years we won't be writing code in text files. We've been shown the way [by Doug Engelbart NLS, Grail, Smalltalk, and Plato].\" -Bret Victor Do you still maintain that \"Excel sheets in their widely used form are not instructions or behaviour\", despite the examples and citation I gave you? If so, I'm pretty sure we're not talking about the same Microsoft Excel, or even using the same Wikipedia. Your definition is arbitrarily gerrymandered because you're trying to drag the editor into the definition of the language, while I'm talking about the representation and structure of the language itself, which defines the language, not the tools you use to edit it, which don't define the language. I'll repeat what I already wrote, defining how you can distinguish a non-visual text programming language like C++ from a visual programming language like a spreadsheet or Max/MSP by the number of dimen"},{"id":27842314,"author":"karimf","month":"2021-07","story":"SQLBolt – Interactive lessons and exercises to learn SQL","stance":"reference","why":"Example of principle of immediate feedback","quote":"This might be one of the best example of Bret Victor - Inventing on Principle talk [0].","text":"This might be one of the best example of Bret Victor - Inventing on Principle talk [0]. I wish there are a lot of tutorial or course with the same quality as this one. [0] [VIDEO] . Original link on Vimeo is dead: [LINK] ."}],"r":[["PaypcVFPs48",1],["viJt_DXTfwA",1],["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["36myc8wQhLo",1],["ajGX7odA87k",1],["xMP_AfiNlX4",1],["Th4VlqQyVr4",1],["v4Wy7gRGgeA",1],["RxD6H3ri8RI",1],["4nigRT2KmCE",1],["helScS3coAE",1],["KVN_0qvuhhw",1],["yMjPqr1s-cg",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["whPWKecazgM",1],["pqQSwQLDIK8",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["8Gv0H-vPoDc",1],["wpOxbesRNBc",1],["7hwO8Q_TyCA",1]]},"F3Jd9GI6XqE":{"wy":"HN cites it for the argument that LLMs capture language form but not meaning, and for how the brain's language network works.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend listening to this Lex Friedman podcast episode","i":40587781,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40587781,"author":"photon_lines","month":"2024-06","story":"Simple tasks showing reasoning breakdown in state-of-the-art LLMs","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Covers complexities of modelling language in the human brain","quote":"I highly recommend listening to this Lex Friedman podcast episode","text":"'It's impossible to plan without an internal monologue.' - Sorry, but I disagree with this. I have no 'internal voice' or monologue - whenever I see a problem, my brain actually and fully models it using images. I believe 25% of the population doesn't have the internal monologue which you're referring to and this has been tested and confirmed. I highly recommend listening to this Lex Friedman podcast episode to get a full grasp on the complexities of modelling language and general modelling present in the human brain: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40482733,"author":"hashemian","month":"2024-05","story":"To the brain, reading computer code is not the same as reading language (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains brain language network that ignores code and gibberish","quote":"If you are interested in this topic, I suggest watching the conversation between Edward Gibson and Lex Fridman.","text":"If you are interested in this topic, I suggest watching the conversation between Edward Gibson and Lex Fridman. In the middle of the conversation [1] Edward talks about how there is a \"human language comprehension network\" within human brain, which gets activated only when we read or speak in any human languages, but nothing else. For example for those speaking multiple languages, reading or writing in any of those languages activates the network. But reading gibberish or computer code, neither activate the network. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40210437,"author":"Hanschri","month":"2024-04","story":"ChatGPT provides false information about people, and OpenAI can't correct it","stance":"reference","why":"Source for claim that LLMs convey language form but not meaning","quote":"My knowledge of this comes from Lex Friedman's episode with Edward Gibson","text":"I find that explaining errors and factual mistakes in LLMs or AI in general as hallucinations to be counter-productive. I don't know if the field has landed on a specific definition of what hallucination is, but I mostly associate it with garbled output from tokens like 'SolidGoldMagicKarp'[0] and 'davidjl'[1]. My understanding is that AI models like GPT have been able to convincingly convey the form of language, but not its meaning. It looks and sounds like how a human would communicate, but the AI is unable to imbue meaning into the words and sentences it produces. My knowledge of this comes from Lex Friedman's episode with Edward Gibson [2]. I think that's the fundamental issue with LLMs at the moment. It has managed to mostly exit the uncanny valley because, as far as most people are concerned, the text produced could just as well have been made by a human. I think this lends some credibility to the text produced by the LLM, because more or less all text ever produced has had a human behind it. This is no longer the case and as such there is bound to be a transitional period where we learn how to deal with this new technology. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"}]},"aNAtbYSxzuA":{"wy":"Recommended as an enjoyable look at the hacker mindset and a thoughtful interview worth rewatching.","q":{"x":"I really enjoyed this interview with Larry Wall (of Perl fame)","i":23197573,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23197573,"author":"gilesvangruisen","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: Best resources for non-technical founders to understand hacker mindset?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Larry Wall illustrates the hacker mindset.","quote":"I really enjoyed this interview with Larry Wall (of Perl fame)","text":"I really enjoyed this interview with Larry Wall (of Perl fame), and I come back to it from time to time. He does a great job illustrating the hacker mindset that you mentioned [VIDEO]"},{"id":41207843,"author":"kzisme","month":"2024-08","story":"Larry Wall's Very Own Home Page","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with Larry Wall worth rewatching.","quote":"I do however frequently watch this interview with him","text":"I used to visit his personal site to see updates, but It's been awhile since I last looked. I do however frequently watch this interview with him: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6972082,"author":"hercynium","month":"2013-12","story":"The danger of \"language\" in programming (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Wall discusses how linguistics and programming blurred in his degree.","quote":"He talks about it in more depth in this interview","text":"Indeed, the blurring of this distinction became the means by which Larry Wall earned his college degree! [LINK] He talks about it in more depth in this interview: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6193903,"author":"justinator","month":"2013-08","story":"Vim 7.4 is released","stance":"reference","why":"Larry Wall on designing his own linguistics degree.","quote":"he states that his degree is one that he designed himself","text":"In this interview[1] he states that his degree is one that he designed himself (which his college allowed), and that he used his programming classes and experience as the second of two foreign languages he needed for his Linguistics degree (the first being Classical Greek). He calls this a, \"hack\". He talks about schooling after his undergrad, but doesn't go into what that was. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"xcwJt4bcnXs":{"wy":"Pointed to in debates over stadium subsidies as a persuasive case that cities should not pay for stadiums.","q":{"x":"After watching it, it's pretty clear that cities should not pay for stadiums.","i":30685224,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30685224,"author":"antattack","month":"2022-03","story":"Cities should not pay for new stadiums","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Makes clear cities should not pay for stadiums","quote":"After watching it, it's pretty clear that cities should not pay for stadiums.","text":"John Oliver already settled this in 2015. After watching it, it's pretty clear that cities should not pay for stadiums. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11030118,"author":"joshstrange","month":"2016-02","story":"Why a city should not build a stadium for an NFL team","stance":"rec","why":"Good piece on public funding of stadiums","quote":"John Oliver did a good piece on this as well","text":"John Oliver did a good piece on this as well and how crazy it is that the public pays so much for these things: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15452789,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2017-10","story":"There Are No Houses for Amazonians","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence stadium subsidies are not a net gain","quote":"Because it doesn't work for sports stadiums.","text":"> Hey if it works for sports stadiums why can't it work to create a company town Because it doesn't work for sports stadiums. It works in the sense of corporate welfare, but it is not a net gain for the community. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] It's sad watching cities fall over themselves to give Amazon (a half a trillion dollar company) a handout. Handouts no one can afford . > (but where cities take the hit if the company town fails) As a country [1], states [2], and many municipalities [3], we're very much insolvent as it relates to future obligations. Could we please stop supporting reckless behavior making it worse? [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"}],"r":[["CnMRePtHMZY",1],["ob_GX50Za6c",1],["si9iqF5uTFk",1],["3TFz1xqp3us",1]]},"0Pwj6BuS8Ds":{"wy":"HN commenters send it to people who think vaping is as harmful as smoking, for its graphic comparison.","q":{"x":"Anyone who tries to tell me vaping might be worse I send them to this YouTube video","i":32267237,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":42547368,"author":"rogue7","month":"2024-12","story":"Single cigarette takes 20 minutes off life expectancy, study finds","stance":"rec","why":"Graphically shows different lung impact of vaping and smoking.","quote":"See also this video [1] that shows graphically the different impact it has on the lungs.","text":"Vaping is way less harmful than smoking. See e.g. the study [0] conducted by Public Health England that concludes vaping is at least 95% (!) less harmful than smoking. See also this video [1] that shows graphically the different impact it has on the lungs. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32267237,"author":"mike10921","month":"2022-07","story":"Popcorn Lung: A Dangerous Risk of Flavored E-Cigarettes (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Compares effects of smoking and vaping on lungs.","quote":"Anyone who tries to tell me vaping might be worse I send them to this YouTube video","text":"1000% agree. The whole narrative to non-stop discuss vaping as opposed to smoking is being pushed by the super powerful cigarette companies. Vaping MIGHT be harmful to a person but cigarettes are 100% CONFIRMED to be terrible for humans. Anyone who tries to tell me vaping might be worse I send them to this YouTube video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20943034,"author":"IronWolve","month":"2019-09","story":"Trump Administration Weighs Ban on Flavored E-Cigarettes","stance":"reference","why":"Cotton ball comparison of smoking versus vaping.","quote":"Video showing cotton balls with smoking vs vaping,","text":"Most the people who got sick or died was from THC related vape products, ecigs had nothing to do with it. Also, they didn't ban flavored alcohol or flavored THC products. They really need to stop being a nanny state about adults and stop use the agenda of (Think of the children!) bans. Kids (Young Adults) did start using juul, because gas stations could sell them easily, 7-11 banned those juice flavors awhile ago. The Feds banned an entire market for adults, that has nothing related to the THC vitamin E grease fiasco... Vaping is hell of a lot healthier than smoking, but of course not-smoking is best. But I hate when nanny stats bann something for political reasons, just like they are doing with trans fats (donut scare!) or cities with sugary drink taxes. Video showing cotton balls with smoking vs vaping, [VIDEO]"}]},"9i1WlcCudpU":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as a compact rebuttal to dismissive arguments and as a way to see why people consider AI safety important.","q":{"x":"This video might be interesting to see some arguments for why people think it’s important","i":40402810,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40402810,"author":"circuit10","month":"2024-05","story":"OpenAI created a team to control 'superintelligent' AI – then let it wither","stance":"rec","why":"Lays out arguments for why people consider AI safety important.","quote":"This video might be interesting to see some arguments for why people think it’s important","text":"It’s arguable whether it’s actually improbable. Many people, including (and seemingly especially) experts think it’s likely or even inevitable. This video might be interesting to see some arguments for why people think it’s important: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32533460,"author":"sabellito","month":"2022-08","story":"John Carmack's new AGI company, Keen Technologies, has raised a $20M round","stance":"rec","why":"Rebuts common dismissive reasoning about AI safety.","quote":"The type of reasoning you're displaying is addressed in another video","text":"I don't think you spent enough time checking, he's a person with a PHD precisely in AI safety! The type of reasoning you're displaying is addressed in another video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34938406,"author":"pixl97","month":"2023-02","story":"ChatGPT get-rich-quick schemes are coming for magazines, Amazon, and YouTube","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Robert Miles - 10 Reasons to Ignore AI Safety.","text":">but we should wait and see what happens. Most AI alignment and safety people would disagree with you there. Robert Miles - 10 Reasons to Ignore AI Safety. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aircAruvnKk",1],["JRuNA2eK7w0",1],["hEUO6pjwFOo",1],["3TYT1QfdfsM",1],["qv6UVOQ0F44",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["ZeecOKBus3Q",1]]},"CuD7SCqHB7k":{"wy":"HN repeatedly cites it for Midori lessons: compatibility matters for adoption, and the Windows team stayed sceptical even with Midori running real workloads.","q":{"x":"I summarized this excellent talk here [1], but one of the main points is that compatibility with existing systems is important for adoption.","i":15179941,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":21993526,"author":"shepmaster","month":"2020-01","story":"Blogging about Midori (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Keynote on Midori and Rust's similarities and software quality.","quote":"Midori and Rust have several striking similarities","text":"See also \"Safe Systems Software and the Future of Computing\" by Joe Duffy, the closing keynote at RustConf 2017: [VIDEO] Midori and Rust have several striking similarities, and Microsoft's recent uptick in interest in Rust (and Rust-like languages) bodes well for improved software quality."},{"id":15179941,"author":"chubot","month":"2017-09","story":"Some Were Meant for C: The Endurance of an Unmanageable Language [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Joe Duffy on Midori lessons: compatibility matters, rewrite unit by unit.","quote":"I summarized this excellent talk here [1], but one of the main points is that compatibility with existing systems is important for adoption.","text":"Related: \"Safe Systems Software and the Future of Computing by Joe Duffy\" at RustConf 2017. [VIDEO] I summarized this excellent talk here [1], but one of the main points is that compatibility with existing systems is important for adoption. (They learned that the hard way -- by having their entire project cancelled and almost everything thrown out.) He advocates unit-by-unit rewrites rather than big-bang rewrites, just like Kell does in this conference article. And compatibility with C in Windows should be easier than it is in the Unix world, because the whole OS is architected around a binary protocol AFAIK -- COM. My sense is that Rust may not have thought enough about compatibility early in its life. Only later when they ran into adoption problems did they start talking more about compatibility. Also, it seems Rust competes more with C++ than C, and there seems to be very little attempt to be compatible with C++ (although perhaps that problem is intractable.) Personally I don't think Rust will be a successful C replacement. It will have some adoption, but the Linux kernel will still be running on bajillions of devices 10 years from now, written in C. And in 20 years, something else will come along to replace either C or Linux, but that thing won't involve Rust. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":46002332,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2025-11","story":"New OS aims to provide (some) compatibility with macOS","stance":"reference","why":"Duffy says Windows team was sceptical despite Midori running.","quote":"Joe Duffy also did a few presentations, on one of them (too lazy to search for the exact moment)","text":"Mostly because of internal politics, it was used in production for Bing. > While never reaching commercial release, at one time Midori powered all of Microsoft’s natural language search service for the West Coast and Asia. From [LINK] Enjoy this recording of an internal presentation, while it is still available on YouTube, [LINK] Joe Duffy also did a few presentations, on one of them (too lazy to search for the exact moment), he mentions that even with Midori running in front of them, the Windows team was very sceptical of it, \"RustConf 2017 - Closing Keynote: Safe Systems Software and the Future of Computing by Joe Duffy\" [VIDEO] \"Safe Systems Programming in C# and .NET\" [LINK]"},{"id":44745414,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2025-07","story":".NET 10 Preview 6 brings JIT improvements, one-shot tool execution","stance":"reference","why":"Duffy on Windows team's cold reception of Midori.","quote":"Joe Duffy has some remarks on how even with Midori running Asia Bing infrastructure","text":"I agree with your point of view, this was the second coming of Longhorn, WinRT was after all pushed by the same folks that killed the Longhorn effort, those on Windows team that sided with Steven Sinofsky, using COM in Vista as future foundation for .NET ideas in Longhorn. The Hilo tutorial for Windows 7 remarks regarding C++ are quite telling of their point of view, on which programming language one should be using, [LINK] \"The rich user experience of Windows 7 is best accessed through a powerful, flexible language, and that means C++: by using C++ you can access the raw power of the APIs for Windows 7.\" From [LINK] Joe Duffy has some remarks on how even with Midori running Asia Bing infrastructure, and proving workloads in front of the Windows team, the reception to it was rather cold. [VIDEO] Eventually he left MSR and created Pulumin on top of Go. Meanwhile Apple/NeXT and Google, decided to push languages like Objective-C, Swift, Java, Kotlin, over classical C and C++, proving the point that change is possible if management is willing to support the team, even if that is a very long run. See Apple's Metal Swift bindings, versus Microsoft's Agility SDK for DirectX."},{"id":43479790,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2025-03","story":"Recommendation for non-DOS/Unix open source OS outside x86/X64","stance":"reference","why":"One entry in a list of non-Unix OS resources.","quote":"","text":"Project Oberon, [LINK] The evolution of Oberon based OSes, Ethos, [LINK] Active Oberon which is the Oberon variant I prefer, [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Some screenshots at my article, take it while the site still exists, [LINK] SPIN, done in Modula-3 [LINK] Singularity, [LINK] [LINK] Midori, although no source code, the blog posts, existing talks and internal session at Microsof do provide some nice overview, [LINK] \"The Midori Operating System Overview\" [LINK] \"Safe Systems Programming in C# and .NET\" [LINK] \"Safe Systems Software and the Future of Computing\" [VIDEO] Xerox PARC Mesa, used on the Xerox Star OS [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Xerox PARC Cedar, used on Dorado platforms [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Xerox PARC Smalltalk, original documentation can be taken from [LINK] - Smalltalk-80, Bits of History, Words of Advice - Smalltalk-80: The Language and its Implementation - Smalltalk-80, The Interactive Programming Environment Squeak and Pharos linage, [LINK] [LINK] Xerox PARC Interlisp-D, with Medley [LINK] [LINK] House, written in Haskell [LINK] MirageOS, written in OCaml, partially used by Docker (TCP/IP stack), and Xen Hypervisor [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] AmigaOS, [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Solo in Concurrent Pascal, The solo operating system: A concurrent pascal program [LINK] Lillith in Modula-2 [LINK] [LINK] Now go out and explore, UNIX is not the be all, end all of how an OS is supposed to be, neither is C the ultimate systems programming language."},{"id":34411125,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2023-01","story":"Safe Native Code: The Midori Compiler","stance":"reference","why":"Remarks on WinDev disregarding Midori despite Bing workloads.","quote":"The latter has some remarks on how the WinDev disregarded Midori efforts even with the system proving its value in workloads like Bing[0].","text":"See also from him, Safe Systems Programming in C# and .NET [LINK] RustConf 2017 - Closing Keynote: Safe Systems Software and the Future of Computing [VIDEO] The latter has some remarks on how the WinDev disregarded Midori efforts even with the system proving its value in workloads like Bing[0]. [0] - [LINK]"},{"id":30866910,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2022-03","story":"The wild world of non-C operating systems","stance":"reference","why":"One of Duffy's two talks on Midori.","quote":"Joe Duffy did two talks about the subject","text":"Regarding Midori, besides the blog posts, Joe Duffy did two talks about the subject, \"RustConf 2017 - Closing Keynote: Safe Systems Software and the Future of Computing by Joe Duffy\" [VIDEO] \"Safe Systems Programming in C# and .NET\" [LINK] In one of them, he mentions that even with Midori proving its value to the Windows team, they were quite dimissive of it. It appears to have also been yet another victim of the usual DevDiv vs WinDev politics."},{"id":28817733,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-10","story":"Singularity – Microsoft’s Experimental OS","stance":"reference","why":"Duffy says Windows team stayed sceptical of Midori.","quote":"Here Joe Duffy mentions towards the end that even with Midori running in front of them, the Windows team was sceptical of it.","text":"The usual politics, from .NET vs C++ at Microsoft. Here Joe Duffy mentions towards the end that even with Midori running in front of them, the Windows team was sceptical of it. [VIDEO] Since .NET's introduction, Microsoft seems to lack the same kind of culture that Apple and Google have towards into steering their platforms into safer languages (e.g. how constrained NDK happens to be, or first class bindings to all OS APIs in Swift). It appears that every attempt to do so ends up being sabotaged in some way to assure C++'s reign at Microsoft and Windows subsystems. Note that Windows is the only desktop/mobile OS where the GUI stack is still fully C++ aware, and they even make a point out of it. [LINK] > WinUI is powered by a highly optimized C++ core that delivers blistering performance, long battery life, and responsive interactivity that professional developers demand. Its lower system utilization allows it to run on a wider range of hardware, ensuring your sophisticated workloads run with ease."},{"id":27809296,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-07","story":"A Look into CBL-Mariner, Microsoft’s Internal Linux Distribution","stance":"reference","why":"Duffy hints at why fighting Windows culture failed.","quote":"Joe Duffy does some remarks on his two talks, where he hints at why it was a failure to fight Windows culture","text":"To fully get where I am coming from, you have to go back to when .NET was released. .NET was supposed to be the great reunification of VB, C++ and COM runtimes, then also got a Java touch into the mix and .NET happenend (initially was known as Ext-VOS). [LINK] Hence why CLR is just like WASM + GC if you prefer a modern comparisasion. If you go back into web archives, when Visual Studio.NET was released, it was going to be .NET everywhere, across the whole stack. However a big management mistake happened, .NET was part of DevTools business unit, while C++ was kept under WinDev, up until Satya started to change the culture, it has been pretty much WinDev vs DevTools. So Managed DirectX comes, eventually gets killed, XNA and Silverlight take over Windows Phone 7, get killed by WinRT and DirectXTK and so on. Going back to the originally statement, if you Google for why Longhorn did not work out, you will find many .NET blaming. [LINK] Yet Android, ChromeOS, Midori are examples of what happens when actually everyone works into the same direction bringing an OS into production. Joe Duffy does some remarks on his two talks, where he hints at why it was a failure to fight Windows culture \"Systems Programming in C# \" - [LINK] \"Safe Systems Software and the Future of Computing\" - [VIDEO] Note that for some time the Asian Bing nodes were actually running on top of Midori as production test. A big decision of Vista, was to replicate the .NET design using COM instead (hello WinDev), hence"},{"id":18123905,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-10","story":"A History of .NET Runtimes","stance":"reference","why":"Duffy says WinDev was unreceptive despite Midori running.","quote":"he mentions that even with Midori running in front of them, WinDev guys were still not open to the idea of such kind of system being possible","text":"At RustConf 2017 keynote presented by Joe Duffy regarding Midori at a certain moment, he mentions that even with Midori running in front of them, WinDev guys were still not open to the idea of such kind of system being possible. \"Safe Systems Software and the Future of Computing\" [VIDEO] Sorry a bit lazy to track down the exact moment."},{"id":17068660,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-05","story":"The Evolution of C Programming Practices: A Study of Unix (2016) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited among sources on safer systems languages.","quote":"","text":"First of all, there is no such language as C/C++. C++ although tainted by copy-paste compatibility with C89, does offer the language features for security conscious developers to make use of, and C++17 is quite a pleasure to use. C on the other hand could be nuked for what I care, it has been clear since 1979 that security and C would never go together. Now regarding your assertions. z/OS was initially written in a mix of Assembly and PL/I. C and C++ came later into the picture as the system got a POSIX personality. The way to write libraries exposed to all languages available on z/OS is via the z/OS Language Environment. Chapter 9.12 of z/OS Basics, [LINK] .NET has hardly any pure C code, rather C++. Even so, the C# and VB.NET compilers have been bootstrapped thanks to Rosylin and now C++ is gone from the compiler side. F# was bootstrapped from the early days. Since Roslyn came into production, the .NET team started planning moving parts of the runtime from C++ to C#. \"So, in my view, the primary trend is moving our existing C++ codebase to C#. It makes us so much more efficient and enables a broader set of .NET developers to reason about the base platform more easily and also contribute.\" [LINK] Also the first version of .NET's GC was actually prototyped in Common Lisp, [LINK] Unity introduced HPC# at GDC 2018 and they are now in the process of migrating C++ engine code into HPC#. Both the requirements of HPC# as C# subset for performance critical code, as the experience wi"},{"id":16563559,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-03","story":"D on embedded Linux (ARM)","stance":"reference","why":"Duffy notes Windows team stayed unconvinced despite Midori working.","quote":"Joe Duffy has stated on his Rustconf 2017 presentation [0] that even Midori running on front for their eyes","text":"It has been proven multiple times, at Xerox PARC, ETHZ, DEC Olivetti, Microsoft that garbage collection systems programming languages are doable. However technology adoption always needs to fight against non-believers. Joe Duffy has stated on his Rustconf 2017 presentation [0] that even Midori running on front for their eyes, with some use cases where System C# was even shoulder-with-shoulder with C++ [1], that Windows devteam was not getting it. Nowadays he is an happy Go coder instead of trying to convince them otherwise. D has a big problem with their GC implementation, which provides good arguments for the anti-GC crowd in systems programming. However they have been improving it quite a bit during the latest releases, as well as, making the runtime library more @nogc friendly. As for reaching mainstream, I share your thoughts. It is not only Rust and Go. There is also Swift, C++17 and the ongoing improvements to make Java and C# more machine friendly (memory management and AOT compilation). Still, even if just a small group gets to use it, it is already a victory. Most languages never achieve it. [0] - [VIDEO] [1] - [LINK]"},{"id":16185749,"author":"Promarged","month":"2018-01","story":"Google’s Fuchsia OS on the Pixelbook: It Works It Actually Works","stance":"reference","why":"Duffy describes Midori team quickly writing UI, editors, browsers.","quote":"As Joe Duffy said [0] when they worked on Midori they also wrote UI, editors, browsers and many more applications in months","text":"I don't know why but I'm actually less excited about Fuchsia than the old Singularity/Midori. As Joe Duffy said [0] when they worked on Midori they also wrote UI, editors, browsers and many more applications in months so it's not surprising Google engineers also managed that. [0]: [VIDEO] For me it looks like the classic Not Invented Here syndrome - we already have microkernels, even formally verified ones (seL4) but AFAIK Google didn't want that to \"be more flexible\". Using C for kernel also looks like a poor choice nowadays, unless Google has some magic static analysis (for example Singularity/Midori had custom languages with ownership tracking akin to Rust). Maybe Fuchsia is for bringing microkernel architecture for masses using \"boring technology\"?"},{"id":15910461,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2017-12","story":"Re: Moving from PHP to Go and Back Again","stance":"reference","why":"Duffy's talks on Midori and safe systems languages.","quote":"You only read Joe's blog years ago, yet you missed the talks he gave.","text":"It doesn't matter what Rob Pike says regarding Go and systems programming, because Google, the company that employs him, has decided Go makes sense to write system components of Fucshia in Go. That is a fact, easily validated in Fuchsia's repository. According to you, Rob Pike should call management and let them know it is not a good idea to use Go for writing Fuchsia's TCP/IP stack. You only read Joe's blog years ago, yet you missed the talks he gave. Two years ago is not that long time ago, [LINK] \"My biggest regret is that we didn’t OSS it from the start, where the meritocracy of the Internet could judge its pieces appropriately. As with all big corporations, decisions around the destiny of Midori’s core technology weren’t entirely technology-driven, and sadly, not even entirely business-driven. But therein lies some important lessons too.\" [LINK] \"Over the course of 8 years, we were able to significantly narrow the gap between our version of C# and classical C/C++ systems, to the point where basic code quality, in both size of speed dimensions, was seldom the deciding factor when comparing Midori’s performance to existing workloads. In fact, something counter-intuitive happened. The ability to co-design the language, runtime, frameworks, operating system, and the compiler – making tradeoffs in one area to gain advantages in other areas – gave the compiler far more symbolic information than it ever had before about the program’s semantics and, so, I dare say, was able to e"}],"r":[["xguam0TKMw8",1],["lHLpKzUxjGk",1],["R3xX37RGJKE",1],["YR5WdGrpoug",1],["DJtef410XaM",1],["KINIAgRpkDA",1],["TgmA48fILq8",1],["4fFDFbi3toc",1],["7HKbjYqqPPQ",1],["gFEE3w7F0ww",1],["IeSu_odkI5I",1],["hFSPHfZQpIQ",1],["ds77e3aO9nA",1]]},"fifjjacjLBE":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a candid mea culpa and a kickback demonstration that changed how people use push blocks.","q":{"x":"Pretty good \"mea culpa\" video here from a guy that cut his hand up","i":18522628,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":19082937,"author":"beams_of_light","month":"2019-02","story":"Table Saw Accidents","stance":"rec","why":"Kickback demonstration changed commenter's push-block practice","quote":"This is one of my favorite kickback demonstrations.","text":"This is one of my favorite kickback demonstrations. Since watching this, I have made sure to keep my push block/stick close to the blade & applying pressure toward the fence, instead of closer to the fence & applying pressure toward the blade. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18522628,"author":"tyingq","month":"2018-11","story":"Making transparent wood [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Table saw accident story and safety lessons","quote":"Pretty good \"mea culpa\" video here from a guy that cut his hand up","text":"I moved around a lot as a kid, so I saw a lot of wood shop teachers. All were missing various parts of fingers. Pretty good \"mea culpa\" video here from a guy that cut his hand up: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23809471,"author":"troydavis","month":"2020-07","story":"Complete Guide to Buying Lumber (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Example that experienced woodworkers also get injured","quote":"tons of experienced woodworkers have had blade touches and/or kickback-induced blade touches.","text":"> They are not a problem if a parent teaches young kids how to use them properly. The statistics don’t bear out that training or experience eliminates risk of serious injury. It’s even trivially disprovable with anecdotes - tons of experienced woodworkers have had blade touches and/or kickback-induced blade touches. Here’s 3: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] If anyone is learning advanced shop safety, a few years ago I made the advanced survey that I wished existed: [LINK] . [LINK] has table saw injury stats. In an era of flesh-detecting table saws, using a table saw without it (other than weird special-purpose saws) means underestimating either the probability or the consequences."}],"r":[["LPjzfGChGlE",1],["KrksBdWcZgQ",1],["MCVj5RZOqwY",1]]},"jj8rMwdQf6k":{"wy":"HN cites it as a famous example of someone publicly predicting the housing crash before it happened.","q":{"x":"The best whistle blowing before the bubble popped had to be Peter Schiff","i":5115381,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5115381,"author":"jcven","month":"2013-01","story":"Blowing the Whistle on the Mortgage Bubble","stance":"rec","why":"Schiff warns mortgage bankers they will lose their jobs in 2006.","quote":"The best whistle blowing before the bubble popped had to be Peter Schiff","text":"The best whistle blowing before the bubble popped had to be Peter Schiff telling a huge crowd of mortgage bankers they were about to lose their jobs in Nov 2006: [VIDEO]"},{"id":981787,"author":"iterationx","month":"2009-12","story":"Ask HN: How do you manage money?","stance":"rec","why":"Famous Schiff speech warning mortgage bankers before the crash.","quote":"I recommend Peter Schiff for investing ideas, here's a famous video","text":"I recommend Peter Schiff for investing ideas, here's a famous video, he's got some bestselling books too. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6625325,"author":"7Figures2Commas","month":"2013-10","story":"In Fed and Out, Many Now Think Inflation Helps","stance":"reference","why":"Example of someone who publicly predicted the housing bubble.","quote":"","text":"Interesting comment. If I am to understand it correctly, and follow it to its logical conclusion: 1. Economists have no tools for studying the topics that they're supposed to study, such as asset prices. 2. They are unable to make predictions because doing so would be \"speculative.\" 3. Because we can't know what is going to happen until it happens, we might as well do away with professional economists because they can provide no input of practical (read: forward-looking) use. They can only try to explain why what happened happened. As for the requirement that one have \"special, insider information\" to have predicted the housing bubble, this is simply not true. There were a number of people from a variety of professions who recognized the housing bubble. One, Robert Shiller, to answer my own question, is an economist who also predicted the .com collapse[1]. There were also politicians[2] and market participants who predicted a collapse. Some of the latter, like John Paulson, who made billions betting against subprime, didn't publicly promote their beliefs, but others, like Peter Schiff[3], did. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"}]},"lyu7v7nWzfo":{"wy":"HN cites it whenever threads touch consciousness, perception or predictive-processing, especially for its examples of expectation changing what you perceive.","q":{"x":"This TED talk has a great example where the same sound sounds different to you based on what you expect to hear","i":29255508,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29255508,"author":"guerrilla","month":"2021-11","story":"To be energy-efficient, brains predict their perceptions","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates expectation changing perception; brain as prediction machine","quote":"This TED talk has a great example where the same sound sounds different to you based on what you expect to hear","text":"This TED talk has a great example where the same sound sounds different to you based on what you expect to hear, if you need to prove this to yourself [1]. His thesis is also that the brain is a prediction machine. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20521403,"author":"hcarvalhoalves","month":"2019-07","story":"What If Consciousness Comes First?","stance":"rec","why":"TED talk relevant to questions about consciousness","quote":"If you are intrigued by the question of the original article you might like the TED talk.","text":"I've recently watched this talk titled \"Your brain hallucinates your conscious reality\" [1] by Anil Seth, seems relevant to the topic. If you are intrigued by the question of the original article you might like the TED talk. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46818424,"author":"psychoslave","month":"2026-01","story":"Project Genie: Experimenting with infinite, interactive worlds","stance":"reference","why":"Anil Seth on perception as hallucination and the illusion of self","quote":"","text":"Like, \"Your Brain Hallucinates Your Conscious Reality\" as exposed by Anil Seth[1]? Found that one while searching for something like \"the illusion of the self\" a few years ago. It’s also easy to find this treated in various philosophy/religion through time and space. And anyway as consciousness is eager to project whatever looks like a possible fit, elements of suggesting prior arts can be inferred back as far as traces can be found. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19198884,"author":"sjjshvuiajhz","month":"2019-02","story":"An Honest Living","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on reality as brain-constructed perception","quote":"Your brain hallucinates your conscious reality - Anil Seth - TED","text":"If thoughts like these are making you suffer, there are other ways of thinking. Whether the object you are working on is an engine block or the Chrome debugger, you are working on something designed by humans. If you were working on the engine block, might you still feel that your life wasn’t “real” enough, because you would be surrounded by metal and concrete rather than trees? A set of atoms arranged into a piece of wood and a set of transistors arranged in a particular state are both equally “real”. Consider that your experience of reality consists of a bunch of sensors absorbing random photons or other signals from your environment, stitched together by your brain software into a comprehensible whole. Your brain hallucinates your conscious reality - Anil Seth - TED [VIDEO] Your feeling of “unreality” is really a basic fact of your existence - if you were raised by wolves in the forest and never saw a single human invention, you still might find yourself wondering if you were stuck in a dream. In that way, even the “unreality” you feel is part of nature. Isn’t that cool? If you can’t stop thoughts like that from bothering you on an ongoing basis, you might be suffering from a mild depression. Antidepressants work for many, as does therapy."},{"id":17072651,"author":"yonoataraxia","month":"2018-05","story":"Prefrontal cortex as a meta-reinforcement learning system","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Somewhat interesting: Bananas contain the ingredients necessary to produce dopamine. I can see how this all plays out as a way for some apes to get some bananas. Even social structures, fairness and other things (I love this video - two monkeys getting unequal pay [LINK] ) can be explained by a dopamine system that just likes to give us bananas and sex. And it's incentivized to conserve energy because this is better for survival (also called lazy) - interesting is that humans have some tendency to do stuff that don't necessarily is conserving energy. Maybe this is what makes us successful: Sometimes we invest excessive energy to try new things which happens to let us survive better through innovation (which leads to genetics that encode this behavior). I like it, although it isn't necessarily what I call my meaning of life (I'm a dualist, because materialism is too damn dry). I like bananas, though (and yes, the role of bananas may be exaggerated here). Thought long about it. From a materialistic standpoint there are only quantitative differences between a organism which has a sensor, some type of memory and an actuator and human beings, although they sure look different. Still following the same principles. When I further simplify, we are all just energy (and matter, which is just a form of it) following some first principles hallucinating our consciousnesses trying to evade the eventual entropy that we'll reach nevertheless because this is how the universe works. - - - Cool"},{"id":16009435,"author":"s_gourichon","month":"2017-12","story":"Our Visual Imagination Is Severely Limited","stance":"reference","why":"Anil Seth argues perception is a controlled hallucination","quote":"we're all hallucinating all the time","text":"Reminds me of this: > According to neuroscientist Anil Seth, we're all hallucinating all the time; when we agree about our hallucinations, we call it \"reality.\" [(8) Your brain hallucinates your conscious reality | Anil Seth - YouTube]( [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["Kr3quGh7pJA",1],["gJ5KV3rzuag",1],["8s7NmMl_-yg",1],["RuCnZClWwpQ",1],["KVl5kMXz1vA",1],["S0qjK3TWZE8",1],["gV5obrYaogU",1],["7PoajCqNKpg",1],["o4-YnLpLgtk",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1]]},"lzsZP9o7SlI":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as a researched, eye-opening reference whenever homeschooling oversight comes up.","q":{"x":"John Oliver did an eye opening bit on homeschooling","i":38720671,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38720671,"author":"rbobby","month":"2023-12","story":"Why Is the Press Attacking Home Schoolers?","stance":"rec","why":"Eye-opening bit on lack of homeschooling regulation.","quote":"John Oliver did an eye opening bit on homeschooling","text":"John Oliver did an eye opening bit on homeschooling: [VIDEO] The lack of regulation is concerning. What's truly disturbing is how easily it is for children to fall through the cracks when there are no teachers to report possible abuse. Frightening."},{"id":38720691,"author":"silverbax88","month":"2023-12","story":"Why Is the Press Attacking Home Schoolers?","stance":"rec","why":"Researched breakdown of homeschooling issues.","quote":"John Oliver breaking it down in a lot more researched detail","text":"Here's your chance to get up to speed -> John Oliver breaking it down in a lot more researched detail: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38088313,"author":"nickthegreek","month":"2023-10","story":"Home schooling's rise from fringe to fastest-growing form of education","stance":"rec","why":"John Oliver segment on issues with modern homeschooling.","quote":"John Oliver did a great segment on the issues with home schooling in the modern age","text":"John Oliver did a great segment on the issues with home schooling in the modern age a few weeks ago. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45661052,"author":"ethbr1","month":"2025-10","story":"Today is when the Amazon brain drain sent AWS down the spout","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as starting reference against homeschooling-outperforms claim.","quote":"Ref to start","text":"> And yet home-schooled students widely outperform government-schooled students, both during homeschooling and in college GPAs. I'd love to see your citations on that. Because my impression is that, precisely because of the lack of regulation in many states, homeschooling has bimodal outcomes. Some children turn out better (read: those of wealthy, educated parents with extra time to spend on educating) while some children turn out much worse than even the worst public schools (read: kids of religious/political-indoctrination parents and/or ones of limited socioeconomic means/time). At minimum, it seems pretty reasonable to have homeschooled kids take the same milestone tests as public school kids, in order to objectively measure if their teachers are doing the job well. You know, considering (a) it's a decision children aren't empowered to make for themselves, (b) there are a lot of crazy-as-fuck parents out there, and (c) it's something that will define the rest of kids' lives. \"Oops, my bad\" in the event of poor outcomes won't put Humpty Dumpty back together again. Ref to start: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38090130,"author":"FireBeyond","month":"2023-10","story":"Home schooling's rise from fringe to fastest-growing form of education","stance":"reference","why":"Shows states with no oversight after homeschooling declaration.","quote":"in a startling number of states, a one time declaration that you're homeschooling effectively drops the kids out of the system with zero oversight","text":"Very much so. Watching the Last Week Tonight segment ( [VIDEO] ) on this recently, in a startling number of states, a one time declaration that you're homeschooling effectively drops the kids out of the system with zero oversight from that point on. You get things like a Christian family whose \"curriculum\" for their daughters was: listen to classical music, bible listening 20 minutes/day, handwriting, bible memorization, exercise 15/day (not great so far, but wait for it): clean room 6/week, fold laundry 5/week, clean living room 5/week, clear off bar 6/week, tidy shelves in dining room 3/week, tidy stereo cabinet 2/week, clean brown cupboard in pantry 5/week, clean storeroom 1/week, tidy blue chest, dust piano, clean and vacuum, clean hallway, make bed..."},{"id":38090941,"author":"Justsignedup","month":"2023-10","story":"Home schooling's rise from fringe to fastest-growing form of education","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver's take on homeschooling.","quote":"This is John Oliver's take on it.","text":"Homeschooling _can_ work. But the likelihood of it working well is pretty low. You literally have a teacher wife who is homeschooling the kids. For most people homeschooling = not schooling. Very heavily used by the hassidic community here in nyc to prevent girls from having any education. I linked to [VIDEO] as a top level post, but just to give you some ideas. This is John Oliver's take on it."}]},"okg7uq_HrhQ":{"wy":"In early 2020 HN commenters linked it as a clear explanation of how the virus kills through lung failure and why ventilator choices matter, notably in ventilator design threads.","q":{"x":"explaining how the virus eventually kills by causing failure of the lungs","i":22600394,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22600394,"author":"taf2","month":"2020-03","story":"Low-Cost Arduino-Based Ventilator","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how the virus kills through lung failure","quote":"explaining how the virus eventually kills by causing failure of the lungs","text":"I really like this as DIY - I also found this video [VIDEO] - explaining how the virus eventually kills by causing failure of the lungs. After explaining the functional parts he goes on to explain what seem like some interesting things to consider when designing a ventilator with respect to how they were used during the SARS outbreak. e.g. tidal forces causing iteration and more inflammation, which then cuts off more air flow. So thinking about how you maintain a constant air pressure instead of more pushing force... at least the explanation made sense intuitively but I'm not a doctor and don't know if this video or your Ventilator are safe - but I do like the DIY aspect of it and figure thinking about or sharing ideas can't be bad..."},{"id":22735668,"author":"brigandish","month":"2020-03","story":"E-VENT: Emergency ventilator design toolbox","stance":"rec","why":"ARDS in COVID and why CPAP is inappropriate","quote":"It shows why machines like CPAP aren't appropriate too.","text":"Thanks. I'm not a doctor so I could be wrong (and happy to be corrected) but from my understanding the problem with those techniques in this situation is that they are for clearing mucus from the lungs (in the bronchi and broncioles) not from alveoli, because mucus isn't the type of fluid in the alveoli. Hence, they'd have little effect. This video[1] goes into what happens when you have ARDS caused COVID and how it will be treated. It shows why machines like CPAP aren't appropriate too. [1] [VIDEO] How Coronavirus Kills: Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) & COVID-19 Treatment"},{"id":22200237,"author":"hybrid_cluster","month":"2020-01","story":"WHO declares coronavirus outbreak a public health emergency","stance":"rec","why":"Informative explanation of ARDS and its treatment","quote":"see this informative explanation","text":"One of the main causes of death of the new coronavirus seems to be Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS). This can be treated fairly effectively with ventilation, but such treatment would obviously require a bed, ventilation system, and attentive nurses, at the very least. It's easy to imagine how these requirements may currently not be met for all patients with ARDS symptoms in Wuhan. For more info on ARDS and treatment in the context of the current pandemic, see this informative explanation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23045076,"author":"thebigspacefuck","month":"2020-05","story":"Nvidia Releases Low-Cost, Open-Source Ventilator Design","stance":"reference","why":"ARDS patients need coma induction and repositioning","quote":"My source for this information is this MedCram lecture on Youtube","text":"From what I understood, with ARDS, it was necessary to medically induce a coma/paralysis and, in order to have highest chances of survival, required staff to monitor and posture patients every 8 hours. Even if ventilator supply was not a constraint, would there be other constraints caused by staffing that would limit effectiveness of ventilators? Note: I am NOT a doctor or involved in health or medicine in any way. My source for this information is this MedCram lecture on Youtube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22844885,"author":"DoreenMichele","month":"2020-04","story":"Some doctors moving away from ventilators for virus patients","stance":"reference","why":"How ventilators pressurize lungs to keep alveoli open","quote":"It explains how they pressurize the lungs to try to keep the alveoli open","text":"Someone linked me to this a few days back: [VIDEO] It explains how they pressurize the lungs to try to keep the alveoli open and why that's very problematic, even when it is only short term. It is so problematic, they sedate people on ventilators so they won't pull the tubing out. It sounds just really gruesome and like a brute force method that can't help but cause serious problems for delicate tissues if you use it for more than a fairly short period of time, which is the norm but is not how it is being used for Coronavirus cases. Oxygen delivery is a complicated thing and I think taking a mechanical approach when mechanics may not be the actual problem is likely misguided."},{"id":22225452,"author":"lovehashbrowns","month":"2020-02","story":"Coronavirus Forces World’s Largest Work-from-Home Experiment","stance":"reference","why":"Describes ARDS caused by coronavirus","quote":"Coronavirus can cause ARDS, described here","text":"For coronavirus, it doesn't necessarily have to be the death rate as of this moment that should cause worry. Coronavirus can cause ARDS, described here: [VIDEO] Treating ARDS in a handful of cases isn't so bad. It's treating ARDS in hundreds of thousands of people that's very very bad. Hospitals can quickly become full and hospitals also rely on supply chains for materials, which would also become overwhelmed. I'm not saying panic is the right thing to do, because it isn't, but what I am saying is that the quarantines aren't excessive and there's a reason why governments are paying attention."}],"r":[["FACK2knC08E",1],["d_zt3kGW1NM",1],["ghtpJnrdgbo",1],["O_azyt1JhI0",1]]},"qs2QcycggWU":{"wy":"Recommended for its detailed and clear look at Starlink hardware and beamforming.","q":{"x":"It's a bit long but has some good details!","i":32589413,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34625415,"author":"jasode","month":"2023-02","story":"WiFi: “beamforming” only begins to describe it (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Illustrates constructive and destructive interference used to aim antennas.","quote":"There's a good Youtube video that illustrates the idea of deliberate constructive/destructive interference to \"aim\" antennas.","text":">But wouldn't it be cool if, using software plus magic, you could use multiple antennas to create \"virtual directionality\" and re-aim a \"beam\" automatically as the client device moves around? [...] We're left with the simple (ha ha) matter of making sure that the receiver is in an area of maximum constructive interference at all times. To make a long story short, we do this by adjusting the phase between the otherwise-identical signals coming from the different antennas. There's a good Youtube video that illustrates the idea of deliberate constructive/destructive interference to \"aim\" antennas. Deep link: [VIDEO] The video is about Starklink phased-arrays instead of wifi pointy antennas but it illustrates the same idea."},{"id":32589413,"author":"__sy__","month":"2022-08","story":"Starlink is reducing your monthly service fee","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed explanation of Starlink hardware, beamforming, and bandwidth.","quote":"It's a bit long but has some good details!","text":"While on the topic of Starlink, I wanted to share this cool Youtube video [1] I watched tonight on how it works (PCBA design, beam forming, orbital velocity, up/downlink bandwidth as a result...etc). It's a bit long but has some good details! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38986921,"author":"jacquesm","month":"2024-01","story":"Starlink Successfully Tests Space Direct to Cell Mobile Service","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant section on phased array beam steering.","quote":"This is the relevant bit","text":"The satellite side isn't nearly as power constrained as a cell phone so they don't have to get it that precise. But every dB counts. They hit multiple subscribers at once with the sat side footprint but if an area is nearly empty I don't see why they couldn't keep a particular phone in the centroid (such as in emergency services to uninhabited areas). Likely they will differentiate between populated and less populated areas by allocating more time to a spot to service all of the customers. [LINK] Of course the gain wouldn't be as nice as when both sides can do the phased array trick but cell phones don't have the same kind of antenna capabilities that Starlink's dish has. This is the relevant bit: [VIDEO] I suspect the reason it is text service and not voice is that the phone antenna constraint results in severe bandwidth limitations. And with all that tech working for them I still think it is a small miracle they got this to work."},{"id":38636696,"author":"nomel","month":"2023-12","story":"US agency will not reinstate $900M subsidy for SpaceX Starlink unit","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Starlink routing through satellites and ground stations.","quote":"How Starlink works","text":"How Starlink works: [VIDEO] Its nearest ground station bounced off nearest satellite, or some inter-satellite links, using lasers, to the user. It's optimized to be shortest route, just like routers with the internet. If you stream a movie, that request goes to in a short path from the server to you. You streaming doesn't slow down someone in China, because the data never approaches any equipment in China. Same with Starlink."}],"r":[["B2482h_TNwg",1],["Bd8_vO5zrjo",1],["gDfNRWsMRsU",1],["wh4aWZRtTwU",1],["HSxSgbNQi-g",1],["NlIdzF1_b5M",1],["KRcecxdGxvQ",1],["0MeRN7LE1LQ",1],["069y1MpOkQY",1],["hBpetDxIEMU",1],["y57wwucbXR8",1]]},"OaGp5_Sfbss":{"wy":"HN cites the film as a source on fast fashion exploitation and pollution.","q":{"x":"I stopped shopping those stores since watching it. I recommend it.","i":16533121,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16533121,"author":"spodek","month":"2018-03","story":"The dirty industry of fast fashion is causing an environmental “emergency”","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary on fast fashion's pollution and exploitation; commenter stopped shopping those stores.","quote":"I stopped shopping those stores since watching it. I recommend it.","text":"The documentary The True Cost [VIDEO] covers many of the problems with fast fashion -- the pollution, exploitation, and more, but especially the pollution. I stopped shopping those stores since watching it. I recommend it."},{"id":28161758,"author":"beckman466","month":"2021-08","story":"Adidas ends Reebok era with $2.5B sale to Authentic Brands","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source about Rana Plaza.","quote":"How many people know about Rana Plaza?","text":"> What did Nike do right? Luck and paying the right celebrities for endorsements? Also using sweatshops in the global south to extract superprofits [1]? More and more I'm starting see branding as a way for big companies to slowly condition people to relate emotionally to some spectacle [2] (e.g. an exciting celebrity spokesperson in an advert), distracting them from an often exploitative and unsustainable production process. How many people know about Rana Plaza? [3] It's exciting to see new initiatives like 'Fixing Fashion' though (Alicia Minnaard and Dave Hakkens)[4]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK] - short intro: [LINK]"},{"id":26621017,"author":"beckman466","month":"2021-03","story":"US Is One Step Closer to Having Program to Study Blocking Sun","stance":"reference","why":"Source for clothes made cheaply in global south.","quote":"","text":"> moving forward with a plan to block incoming sunlight could alter rainfall patterns and cause monsoon rains that millions of farmers in India rely on to dry up First, global north capitalist governments and firms force India (and other global south countries) to make their clothes, [1] their iPhones, [2] and more [3], for peanuts. Then they go and fuck up their food security. What a marvellous system. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] , [LINK]"},{"id":25877447,"author":"bergstromm466","month":"2021-01","story":"How hard should I push myself?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Of course in the modern world, calories are too cheap to meter. Our instincts no longer make sense. Maybe for intellectual laborers in the global north. Not for the starving billion who are barely surviving, yet are also producing all the commodities global north capitalists force them to make: often after having been forcefully bribed/expelled from their land, mostly by ruthless global north firms together with corrupt governments. [1] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":25313320,"author":"bergstromm466","month":"2020-12","story":"No One Wants Used Clothes Anymore (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Trailer for documentary on exploitative relationships behind fast fashion.","quote":"The True Cost (2015) documentary trailer","text":"Noone in the elite wants to examine the exploitative capitalist relationships that support fast fashion The True Cost (2015) documentary trailer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23951601,"author":"bergstromm466","month":"2020-07","story":"Apple for the first time makes a top-of-the-line iPhone model in India","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> pretend the positives don't exist Critiquing something is not denying positives. Please point out where I said that? That is not my intention. I would say that critiquing without providing an alternative is about sharing grief [1], than it is about discussing, planning and creating alternatives. I think you've given me the opportunity to reflect on my approach and message. I am for systemic evolution and integration: technology shapes us, and we shape technology, it's a spiral. Promoting intellectual property rights while at the same time talking about 'free markets' is one of the most harmful and backward Doublespeak going on in today's system I think. The systemic components and dynamics that are part of the intellectual property systems are ripe for evolution. I believe we are living with a pre-21st century systemic reality that doesn't account for the new opportunities available to us due to digital technology. The reality is that there is a world out there with people who are suffering at the bottom of the corporate pyramids (the Precariat, e.g. gig workers - who have no job security, no benefits, and not many prospects of a peaceful retirement). Then there are also the hundreds of millions suffering in so called 'developing' countries whose resources are plundered and whose governments compete for industrial-production contracts with our corporatocracy [2][3], creating continuous race-to-the-bottom dynamics that squash workers' rights and create environmental catastro"},{"id":22778020,"author":"thulecitizen","month":"2020-04","story":"Zoom rolled their own encryption scheme, transmit keys through servers in China","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote source for garment workers' starvation wages.","quote":"","text":"> You could even argue that being \"apolitical\" actually means that your political opinion is that the status quo is acceptable I agree with this. I see Cultural Hegemony (domination-justification stories) being very strong in unexpected places. Family members or close friends who indirectly benefit from the artificial scarcity mechanisms (in SV especially Intellectual Property) that make up the various social class realities of rentier capitalism [1]. What seperates me and a mother of three in Bangladesh, who is getting paid starvation wages making the designer clothes I wear [2], is that I was lucky enough to have been born in the global North, in an upper-middle class family. I was able to grow up on the inside of the fort, and benefit from a rich inheritance [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":22152927,"author":"thulecitizen","month":"2020-01","story":"State of Independent SaaS [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as one of several sources on global north exploitation.","quote":"Check out the True Cost (2015)","text":"> Must there always be an issue when the data doesn't suit a worldview? Why compromise a survey 's integrity with your own bias? Hello. Might I remind you of the Global North's historic and continued plundering of the Global South (developing countries)? Or should we forget about all of that? > It costs virtually $0 these days to start a SaaS company Yes, if you have a big cultural inheritance (which was itself plundered and stolen and appropriated from other countries). Oh yes, and you need a big trust fund and upper class parents, or at the least a pedagogical mentor who stimulates you, nourishes your curiosity. By saying this you are showing me the holy ground you are standing on. There are many hands who touch your life every day: feeding you, making your clothes, your electronics, etc. Check out the True Cost (2015): [VIDEO] , or 'Steve Jobs: The Man In The Machine': [LINK] Two key areas one can focus on: 1) imperial intellectual property machine - the work of Ruth Okediji is revealing with this: [LINK] and 2) Corporatist/institutionalized plundering since the colonial era: [LINK] Please would you consider these and try to understand their implications on today's reality?"}],"r":[["GFosQtEqzSE",1],["X8aWBcPVPMo",1],["nUBtKNzoKZ4",1],["3nwwKbM_vJc",1]]},"RGw6fXprV9U":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an informative and entertaining look at how uranium glass is actually made.","q":{"x":"If you're interested in the process and chemistry behind it, it's a very informative and entertaining watch.","i":22371477,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22371477,"author":"Ameo","month":"2020-02","story":"Uranium Glass","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Informative, entertaining NileRed video making uranium glass from scratch.","quote":"If you're interested in the process and chemistry behind it, it's a very informative and entertaining watch.","text":"A really neat video by a Youtuber I follow - NileRed, who creates a lot of interesting chemistry content - was released a few days ago where he produces Uranium glass from scratch: [VIDEO] If you're interested in the process and chemistry behind it, it's a very informative and entertaining watch."},{"id":41207171,"author":"hilbert42","month":"2024-08","story":"DARPA wants to bypass the thermal middleman in nuclear power systems","stance":"reference","why":"NileRed making uranium glass in a home lab, illustrating radiation perspective.","quote":"NileRed (a YouTube channel I like and watch often) making uranium glass in his home lab","text":"Whether it's warranted or not is immaterial given the current situation with regulations and there seems little chance of that changing. Personally, I've a healthy respect for radiation/nuclear materials but I'm not afraid to work with them so long as I know what I'm dealing with—and that's the key point. It'd be a bit pretentious to describe situations where I've been exposed to radiation levels above background except to say they were deemed occupationally safe. That said, I've always avoided such situations when and wherever possible. Let's put my view into perspective: here's NileRed (a YouTube channel I like and watch often) making uranium glass in his home lab: [VIDEO] . Note: I'd never do this despite the low level radiation because of the potential for breathing in uranium dust, albeit a small risk. That said, I nevertheless own several old wine glasses made from uranium glass which I keep not to use for drinking but as a demonstration of how uranium glass fluoresces under UV light. It's very difficult to put information about radiation into proper perspective or in ways that the lay public can properly understand and appreciate, thus the need for tight regulations. Then, as I mentioned, there are the bad actors and of course a small collection of damn fools who are a danger not only to themselves but also to others. No doubt, you're right about cadmium and traces of it in food. That comparison isn't lost on me either. It just so happens at another time I ran a busine"},{"id":33117867,"author":"Schroedingersat","month":"2022-10","story":"BYU profs create new nuclear reactor to produce nuclear energy more safely","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"And you definitely can't just buy it as a private citizen","text":"Good thing no-one who has incentive to build an A bomb or dirty bomb had any U238 shot into their house and/or children or anything. There's absolutely no risk of anyone being able to get their hands on some to make plutonium if there were hundreds of breeder reactors in every country. And you definitely can't just buy it as a private citizen [VIDEO]"},{"id":27252516,"author":"TRcontrarian","month":"2021-05","story":"Collectors who hunt down radioactive glassware","stance":"reference","why":"NileRed made his own uranium glass.","quote":"NileRed even made a video of manufacturing his own uranium glass","text":"I've been to a few antiques stores where they have a blacklight case to show off the fluorescent uranium glass teacups and candlesticks. If you don't have a blacklight with you, it's tough to tell the difference between truly radioactive glass colored with uranium salts, and green-tinted depression glass produced at the same time last century that is neither radioactive nor fluorescent. Depression glass has an interesting story all its own [LINK] ) It actually surprised me that you can buy uranium glass online on ebay or etsy. It's not controlled or anything, and there are some very weird old curios manufactured last century. ( [LINK] ). I bought a tiny little salt cellar this way once the desire to own something radioactive overcame me. NileRed even made a video of manufacturing his own uranium glass [VIDEO]"}]},"_Qd3erAPI9w":{"wy":"HN uses it as a handy reference whenever a thread needs to point at survivorship bias.","q":{"x":"One of my favorite videos is on his alternate channel, 2veritasum.","i":17790410,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17790410,"author":"sillysaurus3","month":"2018-08","story":"The Absurdity of Detecting Gravitational Waves (2017) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A favorite Veritasium video on survivor bias","quote":"One of my favorite videos is on his alternate channel, 2veritasum.","text":"Happy to see Veritasium getting some attention on HN. You may have seen his previous video from 2014, Facebook Fraud: [LINK] One of my favorite videos is on his alternate channel, 2veritasum. Survivor Bias: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31630486,"author":"stevenjgarner","month":"2022-06","story":"I cheated on my Microsoft interview (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for survivor bias in success stories","quote":"Survivor bias [1].","text":"IMHO, this was not cheating. This is a phenomenon called success. If you are fortunate (?) to be around in the entrepreneurial world long enough, you will realize that intelligence, hard work, good time management etc are not adequate. They just qualify as deserving. There are serendipities that separate those who succeed from everybody else who was deserving. Survivor bias [1]. Calling Eli was your serendipity. The opposite is also true: killer asteroids hit the planets of otherwise deserving individuals and initiatives. I was co-founder of a municipal fiber-to-the-home initiative in 1996. We had both a winning technical recipe and also a financial engineering recipe. (Extremely rare even today) We were then fortunate enough to acquire a customer that was a newly minted public company, representing a whopping 65% of our business. Banker's dream, but in actuality a big mistake. Then the double-whammy of fraud by that public company (wiping it out completely before our contract was realized) AND the dot-com crash. Killer asteroids are real. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27127777,"author":"sillysaurusx","month":"2021-05","story":"Diffusion Models Beat GANs on Image Synthesis","stance":"reference","why":"Survivorship bias as explanation for model release patterns","quote":"But it could be survivorship bias","text":"is that actually the case? [are model weights being published less frequently?] It sure feels like it. But it could be survivorship bias: [VIDEO] or regression to the mean: [LINK] . It's possible that StyleGAN and GPT-2 just happened to coincide, and both companies just happened to decide to release their models rather than hoard them, and that the usual case is for companies to rarely (if ever) release weights with their research. But the more interesting question is, why wouldn't they? So onward to your second one: are ethical concerns actually the reasons some models/weights are not released? Or is it about hoarding a potentially valuable commodity? Or some other reason, or a combination of several? As with most things, the answer is likely the mundane, dull truth that big companies tend to have lots of inertia and friction preventing such things from happening. Sources of friction seem to include (but are not limited to): - are we legally responsible for what other people do with the model? (no.) - should we restrict people from using the model commercially? (why bother? but sure, nVidia did that for stylegan. It didn't stop Artbreeder from blatantly ignoring and monetizing it anyway. And while that sounds negative, Artbreeder is one of the coolest ML projects ever, as far as I'm concerned, so the world is better off for ignoring your stupid policy.) - What if people start misusing the model? (So what? I feel like I should just get in the face of whoever is asking this qu"},{"id":17993760,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2018-09","story":"Why most female newscasters have the same hair","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for survivorship bias in perceptions of newscasters","quote":"","text":"> Allowed to be bald?! Allowed to be bald and keep their job . > What are they supposed to do? Straighten it? YES . Again, from the article: >> But dismissing certain styles as “distracting” can also amount to discrimination, especially when it comes to women of color in the industry. “You’ll do better with straight hair,” says Brittany Noble Jones, a digital and broadcast journalist who is black and who relaxed her natural hair for years. For many like her, the expectation isn't just that you'll conform to a certain anchor bob — it includes replacing your hair's natural texture with something else. Also, the business doesn't pay for the chemical relaxers. That gets expensive on a local news anchor salary (2017 avg starting pay: $29,500). > The same standards exist for men. All newscasters look the same. They obviously don't, but I suspect it's difficult for you to see this because comments like this suggests a limited, biased experience possibly due to Survivorship Bias[2]. Yes, all newscasters look very similar! That's exactly the point of the article! You don't see many women , people of color , LGBT people with the hairstyles they consider natural (or, in the some cases, are natural due to genetic differences). Men are allowed some additional variance, but a lot of people - especially women, and doubly so for women of color - are being forced to damage their hair at personal expense to maintain a look that “is stereotypically heteronormative, not overly sexy, and predicta"},{"id":16847000,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2018-04","story":"The Price of Free Is Actually Too High","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on filter bubbles and survivor bias","quote":"","text":"I currently live on SSDI due to unfortunate medical problems that made driving impossible. Three doctors independently looked at a particular lab result and said, \"How are you still alive?\". (Previously I had a decent engineering salary writing embedded firmware) SSDI pays very close to the federal poverty level. Even with the benefit of receiving Medicare, the monthly cost of very-necessary medication plus very frugal living expenses usually leaves me with up to ~$(40 +/- 40) each month in \"discretionary spending\" that I can use to buy anything beyond rent/medication/food. (e.g. clothes, household cleaning/repair/etc, very rarely travel expenses) I'm lucky that my interest has always involved computers, which I can use for free for many different purposes. Well, as long as nothing happens to my old desktop (Core 2 Quad Q9650, nForce 790i, RAID-1 of ancient SATA disks). An extra $300/yr would would mean buying new boots and some new shirts. Paying that would mean dropping on of my medications (maybe fatal in the short term, defiantly fatal within a year or two), or skipping a few months of food . For the record, I'm fine living frugally. I simply suggest that it's important to avoid falling victim to a filter bubble or survivor bias[1], because there are a lot of people - even engineers in Silicon Valley - that have an even worse[2] situation that mine. [1] [VIDEO] [2] I'm fortunate to have the privilege of being male and white . My friends without those privileges have only "},{"id":16464584,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2018-02","story":"EFI Sucks (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Extreme cases distort general impressions","quote":"","text":"> my primary source of linus news comes from articles It's easy to fall into survivorship bias[1] when \"news\" is your only exposure to a topic. You are only seeing the extreme events that were \"newsworthy\". > why should i care to seek out more about one who i find is well known to throw e-mail tantrums Do you want to have an accurate understanding of reality or the distorted view limited to whatever was sensational/scandalous enough to be \"newsworthy\"? When knowledge is derived only from the extreme cases that make headlines, people start to believe inaccurate generalization like \"black people are criminals\", \"Muslims are terrorists\", or \"gamers are antisocial nerds that might shoot up a school\" even though all of these examples refer only to the most extreme members of the group[2]. > but those articles have been my portal to his world If you're going to make accusations about someone, you should probably check if those articles (aka indirect knowledge) are both accurate and representative. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12378283,"author":"ThinkerR","month":"2016-08","story":"Breaking a myth: Data shows you don’t actually need a co-founder","stance":"reference","why":"Veritasium episode on survivor bias","quote":"the survivor bias episode of Veritaseum","text":"Makes me think of the survivor bias episode of Veritaseum [VIDEO] or the french caster ScienceEtonnante on the Simpson Paradox [LINK] [LINK]"}],"r":[["UjZYMI1zB9s",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["VPBH1eW28mo",1],["LLCF7vPanrY",1],["n1QEj09Pe6k",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1]]},"sAz_UvnUeuU":{"wy":"Posted as the obligatory joke in any thread about speech recognition, and recommended in a thread on what makes people laugh.","q":{"x":"I have regularly thrown laughing fits over this.","i":16974717,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16974717,"author":"sdruskat","month":"2018-05","story":"Ask HN: What you watch to laugh?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Scottish sketch comedy that causes laughing fits","quote":"I have regularly thrown laughing fits over this.","text":"- Burnistoun (sample here: [VIDEO] , [LINK] ): I have regularly thrown laughing fits over this. - Hardy Bucks ( [LINK] , [LINK] ) Tech-wise, Silicon Valley I think is funny esp. the earlier series."},{"id":15543450,"author":"pjc50","month":"2017-10","story":"Speech Recognition Is Not Solved","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory \"voice recognition lift in Scotland\" comedy sketch","text":"Obligatory \"voice recognition lift in Scotland\" comedy sketch: [VIDEO] Personally I've had to \"translate\" between a South African and an Ulsterman before, both of whom were speaking English but with extremely different accents."},{"id":12572180,"author":"pjc50","month":"2016-09","story":"A.I. Doesn't Get Black Twitter","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory Burnistoun","text":"Obligatory Burnistoun: [VIDEO] I thought most people would be able to get written Scots dialect, but I did struggle with Walter Scott's Rob Roy where one of the characters speaks it phonetically. There's also a guy doing a newspaper column in Scots in The National, causing quite a bit of controversy. Spoken language is another matter. I've had to interpret between an Ulsterman and an Afrikaaner both of whom were nominally speaking English as a first language."},{"id":11152320,"author":"panglott","month":"2016-02","story":"The Unsuitability of English (2015)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"We will not have a technological Babel fish until we have portable (power-efficient) computers that are both 1. roughly as or more intelligent as a human and 2. understands the world in the same way as a human, including both semantics and pragmatics of such things as humor. This is not anything realistic in anything like the near term. Most of the automated translation software I've used can't even handle something like deixis through a text between two such well-studied and similar languages as English and Spanish. You're talking about a device that could translate between Scots, Tamil, and Huasteca Nahuatl. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10419653,"author":"arethuza","month":"2015-10","story":"Google’s growing problem: 50% of people do zero searches per day on mobile","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Well, I'm Scottish and I don't have a particularly strong accent (at least by the standard of Scottish accents) and my accent appears to be beyond any kind of voice technology (Apple or Android). [VIDEO] [NSFWOS]"}]},"1tQ5XwvjPmA":{"wy":"HN points to it as the primary source for the original Mac demo and the personal post-show Q&A with the team.","q":{"x":"The post-show is my favorite part[0]. It's just so personal. Almost feels like a meetup!","i":26073190,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26073190,"author":"willmeyers","month":"2021-02","story":"Steve Jobs introduces the Macintosh (1984)","stance":"rec","why":"Personal post-show Q&A at the Mac introduction.","quote":"The post-show is my favorite part[0]. It's just so personal. Almost feels like a meetup!","text":"The post-show is my favorite part[0]. It's just so personal. Almost feels like a meetup! [0] 1:06:38 ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":41629413,"author":"breck","month":"2024-09","story":"The Intelligence Age","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs as an example of executives who delivered on grand proclamations.","quote":"And the good ones (like SamA and SJ) deliver.","text":"> Back then, were executives writing such grand proclamations about the future ? Yes. And the good ones (like SamA and SJ) deliver. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29705754,"author":"Someone","month":"2021-12","story":"A checklist for doing live demos","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Jobs demo with likely rigged copy-paste.","quote":"","text":"> 4. Minimize everything else I would say there shouldn’t be anything else. Create a local ‘demo’ account, if needed. That also avoids getting any embarrassing message pop-ups. Also, if your software isn’t that stable and you can get away with it, switch machines all the time. If your demo requires multiple actions foo and bar , demo foo , then switch to a machine where foo was done beforehand to demo how bar works. Look at some of Steve Jobs’ demos and try figuring out how many machines they used to demo some of the prerelease software. Also, don’t be afraid to cheat. For example, when seeing the copy-paste demo in [VIDEO] , I immediately thought “that copy of MacWrite is rigged to always paste that fish”"},{"id":28411858,"author":"uniqueid","month":"2021-09","story":"The Macintosh Way (1990) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"The original Mac demo being discussed.","quote":"here's the demo in question","text":"I didn't notice anything one wouldn't expect, given how different first-hand accounts tend to be. Kawasaki is a marketing guy so his knowledge is weaker than an engineer's in many areas; stronger in a few others. For anyone interested, here's the demo in question: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21066912,"author":"Twisell","month":"2019-09","story":"iPadOS","stance":"reference","why":"1984 Mac demo possibly showing drag-to-trash disk ejection.","quote":"","text":"It was definitely present in MacOS classic can’t tell exactly when contextual menu was added. But on top of that there was actually a third method, select the disk (highlight) and press cmd-E. Which now now that I think of it is just a shortcut for the fourth one, select the disk then select \"Eject\" in the file menu. Actually this last method must be the mother of them all, the drag to trash was also maybe one of the first \"discoverable shortcut\". It’s just that \"drag to the trashcan\" gimmick was present since first Macintosh introduction keynote in 1984 and is still usable as of today so no surprise it’s the first answer on every Q/A. I can’t yet skim through the footage but it might even appear in the 1984 LIVE DEMO (more emphasis needed) of the first Macintosh : [VIDEO]"},{"id":13105336,"author":"i336_","month":"2016-12","story":"Ask HN: Have any side projects from Show HN gone big?","stance":"reference","why":"Used as sample to estimate YouTube encoding storage sizes.","quote":"","text":"I'll answer in reverse order: > Also need to consider how long it will take me to download back those pic once they decide to shutdown the \"free\" service. This is an incredibly good point, both in terms of bandwidth considerations (particularly their ratelimiting) and in terms of products randomly disappearing with limited takeout windows. FWIW, [LINK] UID> will net you takeout archives of your image albums. Incidentally this works with any Google account that doesn't have public photo access turned off, and is rather fun to play with (as is the site: search operator :D) -- > can google backend really handle that, if so for how long? YouTube used to officially report that 300 hours are uploaded per minute, back in 2014. [LINK] says we're likely at 700hr/min now. OK. (Been wanting to do this math for a while, actually...) Let's see. This is all back-of-the-envelope and I wouldn't mind some more concrete numbers to work with! YT reencodes all videos into several formats. I'm looking at [VIDEO] , which is 1:20:58 long. It was uploaded fairly recently so has the full complement of encodings. I see: - 5 DASH audio bitrates: 51k (27.53MB), 66k (31.93MB), and 120k (58.02MB) for clients that can decode OPUS, 89k Vorbis (46.67MB), and 132k M4A (73.16MB) - 6 DASH video sizes in both WebM/MP4 (so 12 total formats): 256x144 (43.09MB / 63.54MB); 426x240 (39.79MB / 140.34MB); 640x360 (71.80MB / 122.65MB); 854x480 (118.37MB / 266.00MB); 1280x720 (234.63MB / 548.81MB); and 1920x1080 (463.04M"}]},"Ma9FrN5COIo":{"wy":"HN points to it as an example of advanced DIY hardware, specifically a camera using an affordable Kodak CCD sensor with global shutter.","q":{"x":"I was hoping to see more advanced projects and I found it in this channel.","i":38760283,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38760283,"author":"graphe","month":"2023-12","story":"The era of American computer magazines has drawn to a close","stance":"rec","why":"Channel with advanced DIY hardware projects","quote":"I was hoping to see more advanced projects and I found it in this channel.","text":"That's unfortunate, as hackaday feels like that too me versus it's older writing style. I was hoping to see more advanced projects and I found it in this channel. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40731163,"author":"jcun4128","month":"2024-06","story":"Show HN: Modular Pi Cam","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a project built around a Kodak sensor","quote":"I have seen some people work on a Kodak sensor","text":"I have seen some people work on a Kodak sensor and also Cine Pi's latest work with the IMX585 sensor is really amazing (4K/8K upscaled). The IMX585 sensor is expensive though compared to the HQ Cam IMX477. Here I'm using the Pi Zero 2 here so can't do 4K I believe (data lanes limit or something, also writing to SD card). Kodak sensor [VIDEO] Recent CinePi work [LINK] I'm not affiliated with CinePi I'm just amazed what you can do when you know what you're doing ha (eg. color grading)"},{"id":38424592,"author":"twobitshifter","month":"2023-11","story":"Apertus – Open-Source Camera","stance":"reference","why":"DIY full-frame Kodak CCD camera with global shutter","quote":"Here’s another project using a more affordable full frame kodak ccd with global shutter","text":"Here’s another project using a more affordable full frame kodak ccd with global shutter [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] Some people like ccd better than cmos for the color range."}]},"NSaRb-iFwPA":{"wy":"HN points to it as an interview with Housel and as the source of his quote that financial decisions happen at the dinner table, not on a spreadsheet.","q":{"x":"Interview with the author","i":29059551,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29059551,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-10","story":"Ask HN: How to start learning about investments?","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with Morgan Housel on money as a tool","quote":"Interview with the author","text":"Besides learning about \"investing\", I would also recommend you take a step back and look at your relationship with money generally. I recommend The Psychology of Money by Morgan Housel as a good start: > Timeless lessons on wealth, greed, and happiness doing well with money isn’t necessarily about what you know. It’s about how you behave. And behavior is hard to teach, even to really smart people. How to manage money, invest it, and make business decisions are typically considered to involve a lot of mathematical calculations, where data and formulae tell us exactly what to do. But in the real world, people don’t make financial decisions on a spreadsheet. They make them at the dinner table, or in a meeting room, where personal history, your unique view of the world, ego, pride, marketing, and odd incentives are scrambled together. In the psychology of money, the author shares 19 short stories exploring the strange ways people think about money and teaches you how to make better sense of one of life’s most important matters. * [LINK] Money is a 'tool' that you should use in your life to accomplish particular goals. Interview with the author: * [VIDEO] * [LINK] Another good book on \"non-financial investing\": > From why we should place more value on social and human capital, we look into why financial planning has a profound impact on how you manage your investments. We touch on direct indexing, the relationship between money and happiness, and the unexpected yet incredible pers"},{"id":45764768,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2025-10","story":"Taking money off the table","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Housel's quote on paying off a mortgage","quote":"But I like to bring up what Morgan Housel, author of the book The Psychology of Money , said on paying down his mortgage","text":"I participate in a personal finance sub-reddit, and there is often a question of whether someone should pay off their mortgage (completely, or make some lump sum payments). The mathematical answer is that if your interest rate is lower than the expected returns of some kind of portfolio you have, than you'll make more money investing. But I like to bring up what Morgan Housel, author of the book The Psychology of Money , said on paying down his mortgage: > It just increased our independence, even if it made no sense on paper. So that's another element of debt that I think goes misunderstood. And a lot of that for both of those points is this idea that people don't make financial decisions on a spreadsheet. They don't make them in Excel. They make financial decisions at the dinner table. That's where they're talking about their goals and their own different personalities and their own unique fears and their own unique skills and whatnot. So that's why I kind of push people to say like, it's okay to make financial decisions that don't make any sense on paper if they work for you, if they check the boxes of your psychology and your goals that makes sense for you. And for me, extreme aversion, what looks like an irrational aversion today, and I would say is an irrational aversion to debt, is what works for me and what makes me happy, so that's why I've done it. * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":40275455,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-05","story":"How I think about debt","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Housel's quote on paying down his mortgage","quote":"people don't make financial decisions on a spreadsheet.","text":"The author of the article, Morgan Housel, is also the author of the book The Psychology of Money . This thoughts on, e.g., paying down his mortgage: > It just increased our independence, even if it made no sense on paper. So that's another element of debt that I think goes misunderstood. And a lot of that for both of those points is this idea that people don't make financial decisions on a spreadsheet. They don't make them in Excel. They make financial decisions at the dinner table. That's where they're talking about their goals and their own different personalities and their own unique fears and their own unique skills and whatnot. So that's why I kind of push people to say like, it's okay to make financial decisions that don't make any sense on paper if they work for you, if they check the boxes of your psychology and your goals that makes sense for you. And for me, extreme aversion, what looks like an irrational aversion today, and I would say is an irrational aversion to debt, is what works for me and what makes me happy, so that's why I've done it. * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":40275503,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-05","story":"How I think about debt","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Housel's quote on paying down his mortgage","quote":"The author on paying down his mortgage","text":"> I have a 30 year mortgage on my house with a 2.75% interest rate. The author on paying down his mortgage: > It just increased our independence, even if it made no sense on paper. So that's another element of debt that I think goes misunderstood. And a lot of that for both of those points is this idea that people don't make financial decisions on a spreadsheet. They don't make them in Excel. They make financial decisions at the dinner table. That's where they're talking about their goals and their own different personalities and their own unique fears and their own unique skills and whatnot. So that's why I kind of push people to say like, it's okay to make financial decisions that don't make any sense on paper if they work for you, if they check the boxes of your psychology and your goals that makes sense for you. And for me, extreme aversion, what looks like an irrational aversion today, and I would say is an irrational aversion to debt, is what works for me and what makes me happy, so that's why I've done it. * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["lBVvnDfW2Xo",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["4j_UjIshzMc",1],["YtfzjehDg74",1],["NRoUC9P1PmA",1]]},"UGttmR2DTY8":{"wy":"HN cites it as a demonstration of how easily bikes get stolen in public.","q":{"x":"First, a white guy. Then, a black guy. Finally, a woman","i":8068831,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8068831,"author":"steveklabnik","month":"2014-07","story":"Show HN: Please Don't Rob Me","stance":"rec","why":"A man attempts to steal his own bike in NYC.","quote":"This guy attempted to 'steal' his own bike in NYC","text":"Two relevant videos. This guy attempted to 'steal' his own bike in NYC: [VIDEO] There's also this one. First, a white guy. Then, a black guy. Finally, a woman: [LINK]"},{"id":39245563,"author":"frenchman_in_ny","month":"2024-02","story":"Paris Vote Targets SUV Drivers with Parking Fees Set to Triple","stance":"reference","why":"Example of what happens to bikes left outdoors in NYC.","quote":"given what happens to cheaper bikes in NYC [1]","text":"I haave had a Babboe City (without an e-assist) [0] in the NYC for ~8 years -- and mostly leave it in storage in the NYC suburbs. It's a heavy beast without the an e-assist, and the few times I've had it in NYC & parked it (during daylight hours), people have left trash in it. I can't fathom leaving a ~$6,000 bike locked outdoors overnight, given what happens to cheaper bikes in NYC [1]. I'd love to use it more, but it's impractical. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6180739,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2013-08","story":"Why is cycling so popular in the Netherlands?","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how easily bikes are stolen.","quote":"Too easy to pick/steal","text":"Agreed. - Spacey or dedicated lanes are a major requirement. And in my fantasies I'd like two kinds of lanes, speedy and casual. Riding around 25kmph for commuting can be dangerous if the lane thins out, people ride/run slow, kids play around. - Also, secured parking spots. I refrain from riding when I don't know where I'm gonna park. Too easy to pick/steal [VIDEO] . Or a <20 bucks tagging device (in 2013 it's probably easy for many manufacturers)"}],"r":[["SKtQgFBRUvQ",1],["vvZA9n3e5pc",1],["3wfNl2L0Gf8",1],["BKP1I7IocYU",1]]},"cjriSNgFHsM":{"wy":"HN commenters use it to show how responsive BeOS was on very modest hardware compared with Windows and Mac of the era.","q":{"x":"Here they play an mp3 and a video that continue to render while you move the windows around, on a 133mhz machine","i":28307209,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28307209,"author":"donatj","month":"2021-08","story":"Haiku has hired an existing contributor to work on Haiku full-time","stance":"rec","why":"Shows BeOS smoothness on 133MHz hardware.","quote":"Here they play an mp3 and a video that continue to render while you move the windows around, on a 133mhz machine","text":"> Doesn't that apply to everyone's OS of choice though? Do people have love deep in their hearts for Windows or even macOS 11? It's hard to explain these days, but back in the late 90s it was just so far ahead of everything else except maybe NeXT and NeXT was entirely out of reach for 99% of the world. The UI was ridiculously smooth and fast and everything worked together like a well oiled machine. - [VIDEO] Here they play an mp3 and a video that continue to render while you move the windows around, on a 133mhz machine without it even putting up a sweat. Clearly that's nothing today but that was unheard of at the time. Beyond that it had amazing features you still don't see on operating systems today like the file system being an actual queryable database. Common metadata like ID3 tags from MP3's were entered into this and queryable. The standard email client stored emails as individual files and just queried the file system. I believe the address book did the same for people. The tabs of the individual windows stack together across apps! There were just so many little wonderful fit-and-finish things like that you don't get these days. - [LINK] I feel like most desktop OS's strive for parity with eachother. BeOS was trying to be better."},{"id":39995184,"author":"ksherlock","month":"2024-04","story":"How might software development have unfolded if CPU speeds were 20x slower?","stance":"reference","why":"BeOS outperformed Windows and Mac on same hardware.","quote":"BeOS was outperforming Windows and Mac on the same hardware.","text":"That wasn't a hardware limitation. BeOS was outperforming Windows and Mac on the same hardware. If JLG hadn't demanded too much money, Apple would have merged with BeOS (and probably be a distant memory by now but that's a separate issue) [VIDEO]"},{"id":11105239,"author":"nickpsecurity","month":"2016-02","story":"What “Worse is Better vs. The Right Thing” is really about (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"He beat me to it as I'm mentally working on a similar essay. Backward compatibility and shipping pressure I already covered a lot in my posts on Schneier's blog elaborating on this. See Steve Lipner's Ethics of Perfection essay for a great take on \"ship first, fix later\" mentality. He had previously done a high-assurance, secure VMM. So, he had been on both sides. [LINK] On backward compatibility, you need to explore lock-in and network effects. These are the strongest drivers of the revenues of the biggest tech firms. Once you get the market with shipping, people will start building on top of and around your solution. They get stuck with it after they do that enough to make it hard to move. Familiarity with language or platform matters here, too. The economics become more monopolistic where you determine just enough additions to keep them from moving. I agree with a commenter there that it needs a OpenVMS tie-in: a great example of Right Thing vs Worse is Better that won in market. While their management was good. ;) It had better security architecture, individual servers went years without reboot, mainframe-like features (eg batch & transactions), cross-language development of apps, clustering in 1980's, more English-like command language, management tech, something like email... the whole kitchen sink all integrated & consistent. Reason was it was a company of engineers making what they themselves would like to use then selling it to others to sustain it. Also mandated qua"}],"r":[["l_aFHrzSBrM",1],["rFejpH_tAHM",1]]},"pcVGqtmd2wM":{"wy":"HN uses it as the go-to example of low-speed vehicle infrastructure in a US suburb, in threads on e-bikes, EVs and autonomous cars.","q":{"x":"Tom Scott did a good video on the city","i":22440441,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22440441,"author":"kingbirdy","month":"2020-02","story":"Citroën introduces a two-seat EV that costs €2,644 and then €19.99 a month","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on Peachtree City's golf cart road network.","quote":"Tom Scott did a good video on the city","text":"Peachtree City, Georgia has an expansive golf cart road network, and is useable by teenagers. Tom Scott did a good video on the city: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32410901,"author":"Tade0","month":"2022-08","story":"Electric Vehicles Are Going to Suck; Here's Why [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a golf-cart oriented town.","quote":"There are even golf-cart oriented towns","text":"> We don't necessarily need simpler cars, we need better electric bikes. Or electric motorbikes/scooters. Or golf carts. Just like so many creatures in nature evolve into crabs, personal transport appears to evolve into golf carts. The Japanese have kei cars, which are just heavy golf carts and there exist cargo bikes, which are just underpowered, three-wheeled golf carts. There are even golf-cart oriented towns: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31663601,"author":"al_borland","month":"2022-06","story":"What If Electric Bikes Were as Cheap as Conventional?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of city built around golf cart paths.","quote":"","text":"My issue isn't needing to pedal, it's the lack of proper bike lanes with physical barriers away from cars. If I could get where I wanted to go without worrying about a car coming into the bike lane, or the bike lane just being a swatch of paint on the road, I would bike much more often. I just moved away from a city that got it's first protected bike lane... but since it was just down one road, and could be walked in under 10 minutes, it wasn't all that useful to me. There were also many spots where cars and trucks still obstructed it. While it's a start and better than nothing, there needs to be a serious investment in infrastructure. I feel like this is a \"build it and they will come\" situation. Peachtree City, GA is an example of that. They built a ton of golf cart paths all over the city, and everyone just takes golf carts everywhere instead of full cars [1]. It looks fantastic. Said city also did some things during COVID to make temporary protected bike lanes with a bunch of construction barriers. It wasn't until they removed it that I found out that was the purpose. I don't think anyone else got the memo either, as very few people used them. It just made traffic horrible, as we lost lanes everywhere, for what looked like a construction project that never started. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30170885,"author":"somethoughts","month":"2022-02","story":"Cruise is opening driverless cars to the public in San Francisco","stance":"reference","why":"Golf cart town as model for autonomous-first community.","quote":"Sort of like this but replace golf carts with autonomous cars.","text":"In addition to going into highly dense cities and inserting autonomous cars into existing driver regulation; an interesting auxiliary strategy could be to partner with a master planned community that was designed from the ground up (physically and regulation wise) to be an autonomous first town where the majority of vehicles were autonomous and the majority of home owners were pro-autonomous cars. The roads and pedestrian crossings could be much clearly marked with RF transceivers, etc. and the inclement weather could be pre-considered. HOA agreement could have a \"I agree to co-exist with autonomous cars\" TOS clause and perhaps a built in monthly subscription. I think a ton of home builders (Lennar, etc.) and senior community developers (Ventas) would be interested if only as a PR concept. I also think a lot of remote Techies/senior citizens would be interested [1]. Sort of like this but replace golf carts with autonomous cars.[2] [1] [LINK] [2] Tom Scott - City of Golf Carts [VIDEO]"},{"id":29189262,"author":"kumarsw","month":"2021-11","story":"Reflections on E-Bikes","stance":"reference","why":"Bonkers example of low-speed vehicle infrastructure in a US suburb.","quote":"For a bonkers example of how it isn't all that hard in moderately wealthy US new development","text":"Something I've been wondering is in what region(s) Ebikes are becoming popular - it seems to be mostly the UK, East Asia, and maybe some larger coastal US cities. Hopefully this will (in the US) lead to cities taking the idea of low-speed vehicles more seriously, which are a good fit for medium-density US cities with room for putting in grade-separated paths. For a bonkers example of how it isn't all that hard in moderately wealthy US new development, see Tom Scott's video \"The City of Golf Carts\" [1] which describes the Atlanta suburb of Peachtree. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["fKy7ljRr0AA",1]]},"rE_M1n-ClOA":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a concrete, working example of large-battery electric shipping with fast automated charging.","q":{"x":"I recently saw this Fully Charged video about a short, but very frequent, ferry crossing that is now fully electric","i":20639622,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20639622,"author":"craz8","month":"2019-08","story":"Battery-Powered Ships Next Up in Battle to Tackle Emissions","stance":"rec","why":"Fully electric short ferry crossing with automated charging.","quote":"I recently saw this Fully Charged video about a short, but very frequent, ferry crossing that is now fully electric","text":"I recently saw this Fully Charged video about a short, but very frequent, ferry crossing that is now fully electric, with automated charging at both ends of the trip The scale here is enormous, and just for a 4km journey [VIDEO]"},{"id":39782688,"author":"Symbiote","month":"2024-03","story":"The Port of San Diego unveils the nation's first all-electric tug boat","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the robot arm coupling charger to the ferry.","quote":"See the chapter \"Robot Arm\" in this video to see it coupling.","text":"The ferry between Helsingør in Denmark and Helsingborg in Sweden has a 10MW charger at each side, to charge for 4-9 minutes depending on the timetable. (It runs back and forth all day and all night, carrying people, cars and trucks.) See the chapter \"Robot Arm\" in this video to see it coupling. 10MW is around the power for an electric freight train, so these kind of numbers aren't uncommon for a port. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":22073412,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2020-01","story":"Hyundai and Uber Unveil Autonomous Pods, EVTOL Aircraft, & Hub Ecosystem at CES","stance":"reference","why":"Example of ferry with large battery and fast charging.","quote":"Here's an electric ferry with a 4.1 MWh battery which can charge at up 6 megawatts.","text":"Different battery chemistries have different C-rates, which is the rate at which a battery can be charged and discharged: [LINK] [LINK] And generally bigger battery packs can charge at higher kilowatts. Here's an electric ferry with a 4.1 MWh battery which can charge at up 6 megawatts. 600 amps at 10,000 volts: [VIDEO] So, like the ferry, these aircraft probably have big batteries that they charge enough to get to the next destination with a buffer for emergencies. The closer you get to 100% state of charge the slower the system will charge in order to protect the battery."}]},"vvr9AMWEU-c":{"wy":"Linked as a nostalgic and clear way to see what the modem handshake is doing, and as an example of sounds people can recognize.","q":{"x":"I went back and watched this youtube video again and it made more sense to me","i":9173962,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9173962,"author":"Fuzzwah","month":"2015-03","story":"The sound of the dialup, pictured and explained (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Spectrogram makes the handshake explanation clearer.","quote":"I went back and watched this youtube video again and it made more sense to me","text":"After reading the explanation I went back and watched this youtube video again and it made more sense to me: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41905641,"author":"jerf","month":"2024-10","story":"Scientists working to decode birdsong","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a sound recognizable as language without understanding it.","quote":"is reasonably recognizable as clearly some sort of language even if we have no (unassisted) human idea what it is saying","text":"However, another major reason is that people have repeatedly gone seeking for language-like or human-language-level behaviors in animals, and repeatedly and consistently failed. It is also worth pointing out that detecting language is a great deal easier than understanding language. Something like [VIDEO] is reasonably recognizable as clearly some sort of language even if we have no (unassisted) human idea what it is saying. We can tell with quite high confidence that most animal sounds are not hiding some deeper layer of information content. Such exceptions as there are, like whalesong, take you back to my first paragraph, though. The idea that language is a uniquely human phenomenon may be \"dogma\", but it is also fairly well-founded in fact. It should also not be that surprising; had another species developed language first, they'd be the ones looking around at their surroundings being surprised they are the only ones with proper language, because they'd probably be the dominant species on the planet. It isn't a \"humanist\" bias, in some sense that humans are super special because they're humans, it's a \"first species to high language\" bias, which happens on this planet to be humans."},{"id":32379751,"author":"tpmx","month":"2022-08","story":"Earbirding – How to Visualize Sounds","stance":"reference","why":"3D visualization of a dial-up handshake.","quote":"Here's a 3d visualization of a dial up modem handshake sound","text":"Here's a 3d visualization of a dial up modem handshake sound apparently made using iZotope Ozone 5 (from 2011): [VIDEO]"},{"id":15637913,"author":"muxator","month":"2017-11","story":"The sound of the dialup, pictured (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"3D spectrogram of the handshake sound for nostalgia.","quote":"3d spectrogram","text":"3d spectrogram: [VIDEO] Animation of the picture illustrated in this article: [LINK] If you live with someone >= 30, turn up the volume and wait for him/her to appear at the door for a nostalgia moment..."},{"id":10936483,"author":"whoopdedo","month":"2016-01","story":"Thanks for Trumpet Winsock","stance":"reference","why":"Example of the Rockwell handshake sweep.","quote":"But I was always fond of the Rockwell sweep[2].","text":"Did you know each manufacturer had a unique sound for their v90 handshake? This one sounds like a Lucent WinModem. (Based on listening to the sounds here[1]. Yay for RealAudio!) The USR/3Com modems were two \"bongs\" (four if the higher speed failed). But I was always fond of the Rockwell sweep[2]. Of course, required reading is HN favorite Oona Räisänen's excellent analysis of a modem handshake. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":9175861,"author":"DiThi","month":"2015-03","story":"The sound of the dialup, pictured and explained (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Has an extra sound pattern at the end.","quote":"you can hear an additional sound pattern at the end that is not explained in the article or the image","text":"In this link [VIDEO] you can hear an additional sound pattern at the end that is not explained in the article or the image, but I remember hearing it every time I connected via modem. Can someone explain what it is?"},{"id":8277956,"author":"draugadrotten","month":"2014-09","story":"Scientists can use their ears instead of their eyes to process data more rapidly","stance":"reference","why":"Nostalgic example of recognizable modem and tape loading sounds.","quote":"it is trivial to recognize the various modem handshakes.","text":"I grew up with the bleeping chirps of software loading from 300bps modems and \"turbo\" C64 tape recorders. I could easily recognize different games from the sound they made when loading, and it is trivial to recognize the various modem handshakes. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":17037875,"author":"Piskvorrr","month":"2018-05","story":"Google Duplex: An AI System for Accomplishing Real World Tasks Over the Phone","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Does this sound look familiar? ;)","text":"Does this sound look familiar? ;) [VIDEO] In other words, if M2M handshake works, switch away from voice."}],"r":[["5wAo54DHDY0",1],["9R5OWh7luL4",1]]},"wTjMqda19wk":{"wy":"Posted in threads on US costs and institutions as a famous fictional rebuttal to American exceptionalism.","q":{"x":"this a great riposte to \"freedom in the US","i":48860234,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48860234,"author":"manarth","month":"2026-07","story":"Why American ambulance rides are so expensive","stance":"rec","why":"Fictional speech rebutting US exceptionalism about freedom.","quote":"this a great riposte to \"freedom in the US","text":"It's from a fictional drama (The Newsroom) but this a great riposte to \"freedom in the US\". You're going to say that we're the only ones in the world that has freedom? Canada has freedom, Japan has freedom, the UK, France, Italy, Germany, Spain, Australia, Belgium has freedom. [VIDEO]"},{"id":49031996,"author":"Ey7NFZ3P0nzAe","month":"2026-07","story":"OpenAI and Anthropic unite against open-weight AI risks to their bottom line","stance":"reference","why":"Speech made 14 years ago, still relevant.","quote":"Reminds me of the speech \"America is not the greatest country in the world\" from the show Newsroom","text":"Reminds me of the speech \"America is not the greatest country in the world\" from the show Newsroom: [VIDEO] It was made 14 years ago and I don't think the US improved on any of those statistics."},{"id":25009188,"author":"mthoms","month":"2020-11","story":"Tunnels in the US cost more than anywhere else","stance":"reference","why":"Clip comparing the US with other democracies.","quote":"This infamous clip from The Newsroom might shed more light","text":"No, it's you who are delusional. The U.S. ranks 45th in press freedoms. There are 25 European countries who rank better. Heck, there are even 5 African countries who rank higher. [LINK] [LINK] This infamous clip from The Newsroom might shed more light on how the U.S. is actually doing in comparison to other democratic countries (all statistics quoted are easily verified if you want to look into it) [VIDEO]"},{"id":16063060,"author":"richardthered","month":"2018-01","story":"Why the U.S. Spends So Much More Than Other Nations on Health Care","stance":"reference","why":"Clip questioning claim that the US is the best country.","quote":"Have you seen this clip of Jeff Daniels in \"The Newsroom\"?","text":"As measured .. how? I understand liking your country. I'm an American, too. I like my country, a lot. But, I think that the phrase \"we are the best in the world\" gets thrown around a lot as a meaningless platitude. It gets used more as a \"my team is the Yankees\"-style of statement. e.g. expressing your preference/allegiance, rather than meaning what it actually says, which is that the US is the 'best' in the world. To be the 'best' at something, you need to define how you're measuring that. Healthiest? Happiest? Longest life span? Because when you look at actual metrics for these types of things, across multiple countries, the US does not rate at the top. Have you seen this clip of Jeff Daniels in \"The Newsroom\"? [VIDEO] (\"America is not the greatest country in the world anymore\")"},{"id":35147081,"author":"HPMOR","month":"2023-03","story":"Ask HN: Has your view of Silicon Valley changed in the last 72 hours?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"(from NewsRoom Intro )","text":"The \"titans\" you speak of are Jerome Powell, and Janet Yellen. Not the schmucks that put Taxis on the internet, or effectively stole billions of dollars from SoftBank to build a rental company. These types of extremely competent people are far and few between in important parts of our society. So idk what the fuck you're talking about Yosemite? (from NewsRoom Intro [VIDEO] )."}]},"xwA4k0E51Oo":{"wy":"Cited as a short, concrete example of consolidated media ownership pushing coordinated messaging through local news.","q":{"x":"adding a link to 2018 reporting on Sinclair from PBS","i":28618157,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28618157,"author":"mvexel","month":"2021-09","story":"Stop Reading News (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"PBS 2018 report on Sinclair-owned local TV, under five minutes.","quote":"adding a link to 2018 reporting on Sinclair from PBS","text":"I could not agree more with this approach. The Economist is excellent, and because they are a weekly publication, their reporting can be more thoughtful and detached from the outrage du jour. They also have excellent podcasts: the daily Intelligence and, for those interested in US politics, the weekly \"Checks and Balance\". Supporting local news has become so much more important here in the U.S., as more and more independent local newspapers are shutting down, even in medium-sized metro areas. In addition, a lot of \"Local\" TV stations are owned by, and puppets of, corporations with political agendas like Sinclair[1]. From the linked article: > About 39% of Americans are within range of a Sinclair-owned TV outlet, most of them unwittingly, because local TV stations typically use the logos of national networks with which they are affiliated, like NBC, CBS and Fox [...] Last year 37% of Americans got their news from local television, according to the Pew Research Centre, compared with 28% who got news from cable networks [...] EDIT adding a link to 2018 reporting on Sinclair from PBS: [VIDEO] (less than 5 minutes) [1] [LINK]"},{"id":48439291,"author":"WarOnPrivacy","month":"2026-06","story":"The architecture of the internet creates risks for democracy","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a footnote for coordinated messaging claim.","quote":"","text":">And, as usual, \"risk/threat to democracy\" is used to mean \"support for parties I don't like\" It can also mean highly influential support for ideologies I don't like - like fascism, authoritarianism and ultranationalism. > many of those complaining now were quite happy to endorse the right of private companies to promote/suppress speech at will A few years ago, bog-standard content moderation was limiting the reach of enormously+reasonably unpopular ideologies like fascism, authoritarianism and ultranationalism. Groups who were profoundly unhappy with these limits would bullhorn complaints of intentional suppression and censorship. With the release of the Twitter Files (which exposed content moderation), it became clear that many folks were unable to differentiate between actual, long-established content moderation methods and actual directed suppression and censorship.¹ This deep misinterpretation seemed to flow from the ignorance of what content moderation looks like at scale. That core misunderstanding was often amplified and made worse when historically-moderated individuals filled in that vacuum with their long standing preconceptions. The upshot are today's efforts to raise the visibility of far-right viewpoints thru coordinated crafted messaging² and thru actual suppression of non-right viewpoints thru new controls over platforms and thru often unaccountable misuse of governmental powers. Our present conditions seem to well reflect and align with the article author's a"},{"id":44761220,"author":"esseph","month":"2025-08","story":"Corporation for Public Broadcasting ceasing operations","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Watch this clip","text":"Public radio and local broadcasting has been gobbled up by right-wing sources, including Sinclair Watch this clip: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23611246,"author":"cmroanirgo","month":"2020-06","story":"I Am Deleting the Blog","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Some examples of \"oligarch news\"","text":"And there's the rub. What is \"quality investigative reporting\" in an objective sense, when most of the MSM outlets are owned by oligarchs, or simply \"toe the line\"? In theory I would gladly support the theory of \"quality investigative reporting\", but the reality is a propagandist machine where opinion pieces replaces actual unbiased, adjective-free objective news. Some examples of \"oligarch news\": [VIDEO] [LINK] As a former developer who worked closely on Thomson Reuters News feed (in the 00's), I've seen how there is almost zero fact checking for the information that appears on news feeds. Instead, news outlets trust the 'upstream' feeds and then quote the reports verbatim. To be fair, there are those who are really awesome at doing research and releasing information that are part of the MSM. Unfortunately, there are plenty others who are not affiliated with MSM news outlets and hence aren't regarded as \"reporters\" per-se. These latter ones are regularly attacked via \"fact checking\" websites as a way to discredit them. An article on the latter is here: [LINK] In short, there's a bunch of information out there and without each and every news report clearly citing original sources, then MSM or not, it must be regarded as suspect. So for \"quality investigative reporting\", the actual reports must rigorously cite objective sources."}]},"-XSC_UG5_kU":{"wy":"HN mentions it mostly as a joke in threads about dongles and USB-C.","m":[{"id":35913747,"author":"OscarTheGrinch","month":"2023-05","story":"Google Announces Pixel Tablet","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Some smartass engineer should have sold Tim Cook on the docks by rebranding them as dongles. Then Apple would have put out ten different coloured dongle-docks for each new device. Related: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15476351,"author":"semi-extrinsic","month":"2017-10","story":"The impossible dream of USB-C","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Apple is very happy to sell you a $$$$ phone/laptop and then $$ dongles to make it actually work with the other things in the world. This is old, but still gold [VIDEO]"},{"id":12884123,"author":"ptaipale","month":"2016-11","story":"Dongle dilemma provokes Apple price cut","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Just in case you didn't see this Youtube video (El Risitas meme) yet, making fun of Apple's decision: [VIDEO] I think the subtitling is very well executed."}]},"BGrfhsxxmdE":{"wy":"Only posted as a joke reply about getting old or out of touch.","m":[{"id":46653352,"author":"alephnerd","month":"2026-01","story":"LWN is currently under the heaviest scraper attack seen yet","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> If I run a big data hungry AI lab consuming training data at 100Gb/s it's much much easier to scrape 10,000 sites at 10Mb/s than DDOS a smaller number of sites with more traffic A little over a decade ago (f*ck I'm old now [0]), I had a similar conversation with an ML Researcher@Nvidia. Their response was \"even if we are overtraining, it's a good problem to have because we can reduce our false negative rate\". Everyone continues to have an incentive to optimize for TP and FP at the expense of FN. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":32515566,"author":"thrown_22","month":"2022-08","story":"To Hell with Facebook (2021)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Just because something is doesn't mean it's facebook. Especially since basically no teens use facebook. Your other example also dates you pretty hard. The surgeon general report of 1964 said conclusively that cigarettes cause lung cancer and other diseases. 1964 is somewhat longer ago than 20 years. In short: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26949555,"author":"JackMorgan","month":"2021-04","story":"And the winner is…who cares?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Finally, a relevant Simpson's","text":"This just sounds like the complaining of every older person since the dawn of time. [LINK] Such complaining disregards the fact that most humans are strongly driven to procreate, and thus present themselves as fit mates with jobs, status, income, and prestige. Humans are also driven to create, and will create beautiful art in the medium that best presents that art. We are also driven to explore, love, and experience life. These things are all alive and well in people who otherwise have and use a mobile phone daily. The empty mental calories of yesteryear were: gossip, tavern attendance, radio, fighting, plays, etc. Those weren't any \"better\" than mobile phones are today. Assuming that civilization is going to collapse because the empty mental calories are a little more accessible is a tad extreme. Finally, a relevant Simpson's: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25155778,"author":"dharmab","month":"2020-11","story":"Roblox S-1","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'm reminded of the \"I used be with it\" joke from The Simpsons","text":"When I was that age it was exactly the same with Flash games. I'm reminded of the \"I used be with it\" joke from The Simpsons: [VIDEO]"}]},"DEP8juRSBRo":{"wy":"Cited as an example of how padding can allow survival of very high-speed impacts.","q":{"x":"jumped from a helicopter at 2,400 feet (730 metres) and steered himself using a wingsuit onto a runway of vertically stacked cardboard boxes.","i":20431423,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":33348750,"author":"fauria","month":"2022-10","story":"I Fell 15,000 Feet and Lived (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Wingsuit landing over cardboard boxes in 2012.","quote":"in 2012 Gary Connery [1] landed a wingsuit over cardboard boxes [2]","text":"Related to this, in 2012 Gary Connery [1] landed a wingsuit over cardboard boxes [2], and in 2016 Luke Aikins [3] jumped from 25000 feet (~7600 metres) without a parachute, landing into a giant net that absorbed the impact [4]. There has been issues of BASE jumpers accidentally landing wingsuits without deploying a parachute too (can't find any reference online at the moment). [1] - [LINK] [2] - [VIDEO] [3] - [LINK] [4] - [LINK]"},{"id":20431423,"author":"oska","month":"2019-07","story":"Unplanned Freefall? Some Survival Tips (2001)","stance":"reference","why":"Wingsuit landing onto stacked cardboard boxes without a parachute.","quote":"jumped from a helicopter at 2,400 feet (730 metres) and steered himself using a wingsuit onto a runway of vertically stacked cardboard boxes.","text":"Stuntman Garry Connery demonstrated the efficacy of what you're describing when he jumped from a helicopter at 2,400 feet (730 metres) and steered himself using a wingsuit onto a runway of vertically stacked cardboard boxes. [1] [1] [VIDEO] > At approximately 200 feet (61 m) over the landing strip, he changed the configuration of his wingsuit so as to decrease the gliding and vertical (falling) components of his velocity to 50 mph (80 km/h) and 15 mph (24 km/h) respectively. [2] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":9597907,"author":"Jach","month":"2015-05","story":"John Nash Has Died","stance":"reference","why":"Example of padding allowing survival of high-speed impact.","quote":"","text":"These are both pretty terrible arguments, due to [LINK] Cars have crumple zones for a reason. This is not to say seat belts aren't good, as they along with air bags contribute to impulse, but there's a huge difference between shooting an egg (or person) at a brick wall at 50 mph while naked, vs. encasing it in sufficient amounts of bubble wrap first. I'll gladly sprint into a tree, or ram a bike into a wall, at such speeds provide I or the tree/wall are sufficiently padded. ( [VIDEO] )"}]},"JDvPIhCd8N4":{"wy":"Cited as a benchmark of what mobile graphics could do in 2010, against which later WebGL games are compared.","q":{"x":"Still waiting to see a WebGL game that matches Infinity Blade","i":37594004,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":46793271,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2026-01","story":"Super Monkey Ball ported to a website","stance":"reference","why":"Infinity Blade as showcase of mobile OpenGL capability","quote":"Infinity Blade was released in 2010 to show what iPhone could do with OpenGL ES 3.0","text":"Fast forward to 2026 and after 15 years, the browser vendors have not yet provided anything remotely similar to RenderDoc, only SpectorJS survives, barely usable. Also this game remains the exception, Infinity Blade was released in 2010 to show what iPhone could do with OpenGL ES 3.0, the base for WebGL 2.0, and yet most Web games are basically Flash like remakes. [LINK] \"Infinity Blade: iPhone Trailer\", 15 years ago [VIDEO]"},{"id":43945847,"author":"corysama","month":"2025-05","story":"WebGL Water (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"Game did real-time water effects on phones years ago","quote":"Everyone forgets what machines are capable of if you actually optimize.","text":"Everyone forgets what machines are capable of if you actually optimize. This game did everything shown here in real time on phones 14 years ago [VIDEO]"},{"id":37594004,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2023-09","story":"Terraria developer bashes Unity, donates $200k to open source alternatives","stance":"reference","why":"Benchmark of graphics WebGL games have not matched","quote":"Still waiting to see a WebGL game that matches Infinity Blade","text":"If they only care about 2011 (WebGL) or 2015 (WebGPU) hardware, sure. Still waiting to see a WebGL game that matches Infinity Blade, released in 2011 alongside iPhone 3G and iPhone 4. Trailer: [VIDEO] Meaning, those graphic API are able to deliver at least PS 3 graphics, yet due to browser support, specially the way browsers blacklist drivers, download sizes, and lack of tooling on par with native, very few professional developers bother, hence streaming."},{"id":3423357,"author":"Samuel_Michon","month":"2012-01","story":"Windows Phone is failing because the seeds were planted elsewhere","stance":"reference","why":"Trailer showing serious graphics on iOS","quote":"Look at games like Infinity Blade [1] and Rage HD [2], then try telling me that's not serious gaming.","text":"Re: 1. iOS runs the Unreal Engine 3 as well as iD's RAGE engine. Look at games like Infinity Blade [1] and Rage HD [2], then try telling me that's not serious gaming. Re: 2. Yes, iMovie for iOS has been out for 18 months [3]. It's pretty darn good. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"}]},"LQ8Nr3_2724":{"wy":"HN uses it only as a critical reference in political arguments about Common Core, not as something to recommend.","m":[{"id":26362048,"author":"desine","month":"2021-03","story":"Student passes 3 classes in 4 years, ranks near top half of class with 0.13 GPA","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] Often the war on white privilege manifests itself as preventing white success rather than helping PoC. Families that help their children with math homework have been irritated that students often get no credit on math homework if they use the older arithmetic methods. Rather than allowing students to use any valid mathematical principles to learn, children are forced to use a new system that (in my opinion) generates extra work to achieve the correct result. I dated a teacher many years ago, long before the political climate shift, and she constantly ranted about how common core was slowing her students down. At the time I didn’t understand much (and didn’t ask her to elaborate much), but it’s been interesting seeing her rants validated the last couple of years. Common core makes sense when a student struggles with the traditional methods. But forcing all students to use it, and it’s historical background of being driven by racial issues, is a damning sign that it’s intentionally holding high performers (often White or Asian) back."},{"id":22942935,"author":"sergiotapia","month":"2020-04","story":"Why are Soviet math textbooks so hardcore in comparison to US textbooks? (2017)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is common core, and the reason why it was created will shock and enrage you.","text":"This is common core, and the reason why it was created will shock and enrage you. [VIDEO] Straight from the horses mouth."},{"id":13463950,"author":"234dd57d2c8db","month":"2017-01","story":"Track Trump: The First 100 Days","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here he is saying it at a conference","text":"Dr. David Pook, who wrote a large part of Common Core wrote it not because it was a \"better\" education system, but because \"white privilege\" and the evil white man meme. Here he is saying it at a conference: [VIDEO] No thanks, I'll go the private or homeschool route. The public system is bad enough with BS like zero tolerance, no recess, no horseplay, no fun zone learning, adding anti-white features to the learning system is just the cherry on top."}]},"NH0aEp1oDOI":{"wy":"Referenced as the original demo that stunned people when object-removal and image-inpainting algorithms come up.","q":{"x":"This was the video that blew everyone","i":14362074,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14362074,"author":"mikeyouse","month":"2017-05","story":"Google I/O 2017","stance":"reference","why":"Original content-aware fill sneak peek.","quote":"This was the video that blew everyone","text":"Adobe calls it \"Content Aware Fill\" which is likely what you're thinking of. This was the video that blew everyone's mind, but its 7 years old at this point: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2877209,"author":"niels_bom","month":"2011-08","story":"Algorithms to enhance or transform images","stance":"reference","why":"Content-aware fill demo for removing objects.","quote":"reminds me of the Photoshop new feature","text":"The \"remove an object\" image transformation reminds me of the Photoshop new feature \"content-aware fill\" featured here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2572124,"author":"hmottestad","month":"2011-05","story":"How a baseball image got photoshopped by robots","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of content-aware fill removing objects.","quote":"It seems someone decided to remove the banner from the video frame.","text":"Here is how it's done in photoshop: [VIDEO] (2:45) It seems someone decided to remove the banner from the video frame. Have a look at the left guys hat, there is a little \"v\" from where the banner was (in the top red oval). Edited: [LINK] Original: [LINK]"}]},"TBb9O-aW4zI":{"wy":"HN uses it as a punchline reference when a product or trend seems like a parody of artisanal premium goods.","m":[{"id":42470384,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2024-12","story":"Who can claim Aristotle?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"trimming the ink sticks reminded me of nothing so much as !","text":"trimming the ink sticks reminded me of nothing so much as [VIDEO] ! > IRL the substrata (objective-C? PowerPC?) get cleaned out real artists ship, and shipping (like cherry blossoming) is ephemeral? 花の雲 鐘は上野か 浅草か"},{"id":32087928,"author":"odysseus","month":"2022-07","story":"How I clean my glasses","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me just a touch of","text":"I feel like this article lays the groundwork for a high quality artisanal eyeglass cleaning video. Reminds me just a touch of [VIDEO]"},{"id":31724264,"author":"leoff","month":"2022-06","story":"Why championship chess sets are so expensive","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"At first I thought this was a parody like the Artisanal Firewood video","text":"At first I thought this was a parody like the Artisanal Firewood video ( [VIDEO] ). It seems like a lot of this process could and should be automated to improve consistency and quality."},{"id":18644706,"author":"xamuel","month":"2018-12","story":"How Premium Mediocre Conquered Fashion","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of this","text":"I guess I'm a dork, but I'll never understand beer branding. The active ingredient is alcohol, a drug that induces various desirable effects. You wouldn't pay extra for artisanal aspirin or tylenol, so why alcohol? Reminds me of this: [VIDEO]"}]},"UnJ3amzJM94":{"wy":"Cited as the official explanation of Google's scoped storage changes and its intent to limit broad file access.","q":{"x":"This official video is actually a pretty good summary of some the changes made and what the goal is","i":23228198,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":43973616,"author":"Ajedi32","month":"2025-05","story":"Nextcloud cries foul over Google Play Store app rejection","stance":"reference","why":"Google warned about the API change since 2019","quote":"they've definitely been warning about the pending API change since at least 2019","text":"I'm guessing it was back ported to Android 4.4, so it probably hasn't been available quite that long. (Update: Nevermind, looks like it was in the initial 4.4 release: [LINK] ) But they've definitely been warning about the pending API change since at least 2019: [VIDEO] The devs in the comments of that video do have some valid complaints about the added complexity of not being able to use the standard Java filesystem APIs anymore with the new permissions model, but still, it has been 6 years."},{"id":42548380,"author":"daghamm","month":"2024-12","story":"MiceWine – run Windows applications and games on Android smartphones","stance":"reference","why":"Android API reducing need for broad storage permission","quote":"Android has a really nice API for reducing the need for this permission","text":"This is a bit surprising in 2024 (well almost 2025). Android has a really nice API for reducing the need for this permission, and most of it has been around sicnce android 10. [VIDEO] Maybe the author is not aware of these? How about opening an issue?"},{"id":23540948,"author":"anderspitman","month":"2020-06","story":"Syncthing is everything I used to love about computers","stance":"reference","why":"Google wants to minimize apps with all-files access","quote":"GOOG makes it very clear that they want to minimize the number of apps that obtain this permission.","text":"syncthing is an incredible piece of software. It should be noted that: * \"Security\" changes in recent versions of Android seem designed to kill use cases like syncthing. See links below. * There is no iOS version, as near as I can tell because iOS is already where Android is headed. The crux of the problem is syncthing is a single golang binary, but the only way to manage filesystem permissions on iOS (and now Android) is through tightly controlled OS dialogs, and there is no way to do this from golang. There aren't even NDK APIs for it. The closest thing Android is providing to an escape hatch is \"all files access\" [5], for which your app has to go through an approval process in order to be put on the play store. If you watch [2], GOOG makes it very clear that they want to minimize the number of apps that obtain this permission. It's unclear to me whether apps installed through FDroid or side-loaded will be able to use that permission at all, or if the APKs must be signed. I'm all for improved security, but never at the cost of controlling what I am ABLE to do with my computer. It's sad that our operating systems are turning into gatekeepers between developers/users and the hardware they \"own\". [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK] [5]: [LINK]"},{"id":23228198,"author":"anderspitman","month":"2020-05","story":"Termux and Android 10","stance":"reference","why":"Official summary of Android storage changes and their goals","quote":"This official video is actually a pretty good summary of some the changes made and what the goal is","text":"Disclaimer: I'm not really an Android developer. I was considering implementing a service I'm working on for Android and after many hours of research determined it's not a suitable operating system for my purposes. Personally I no longer consider Android a viable computing platform. As a developer you simply don't have the tools available to give your users control over their data and experience. With the changes to storage security the last couple years, they've essentially dumbed it down to the level of a web browser, and in the process pretty much deprecated running native (C, C++, Rust, Golang, etc) applications. I'm not opposed to improving security, but when you can't even run software like syncthing or a file explorer without getting special Play store approval, something is wrong. I've been meaning to write a blog post summarizing what I learned. This official video is actually a pretty good summary of some the changes made and what the goal is: [VIDEO]"}]},"V48M5j-6zdE":{"wy":"Listed among resources in longevity and anti-aging discussions as a key piece of lifespan research.","m":[{"id":11001889,"author":"KasianFranks","month":"2016-01","story":"Ask HN: What should we fund at YC Research?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as precedent in a longevity genomics pitch.","quote":"","text":"AI applied to understanding the hidden relationships linking epigentics and extending human lifespan via algorithmically engineered diet, nutrition and longevity genomics: The precedent: Laura Deming: Making a business out of fighting the ills of aging [LINK] Cynthia Kenyon: Experiments that hint of longer lives (Cynthia now works at Google's Calico Labs) [VIDEO] Now connect this: Nutrigenomics, Epigenetics, and Stress Tolerance [LINK] Now combine with: What is Epigenetics? with Nessa Carey The last 10mins are very important, in particular minute 35:06 [LINK] Statistical modeling of biomedical corpora: mining the Caenorhabditis Genetic Center Bibliography for genes related to life span - Blei DM1, Franks K, Jordan MI, Mian IS. - [LINK] Combining the worlds variants of pattern recogition, machine learning and AI with data associated to epigentic and standard genomic experiments and information, we should be able to discover hidden relationships that will enable advancement in extending human lifespan. Revenue is something we'll need to focus on and that may come in the form of consumer applications that algorithmically engineer their epigenome with data from companies like 23andMe, Navigenics, Ubiome etc. connected to Nutrigenomics including plant compounds, phytochemicals mapped all the way to the consumer through foods connected to apps connected shopping and recipe experiences. Finally connect to CRISPR and Gene Therapy Jennifer Doudna, inventor of CRISPR: We can now edit o"},{"id":9575126,"author":"meeper16","month":"2015-05","story":"Russian Billionaire Dmitry Itskov Plans on Becoming Immortal by 2045","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as part of longevity research landscape.","quote":"","text":"Were almost near the starting line with research and companies like Human Longevity, CalicoLabs(Google,Genentech), GenoPharmix, Buck Institute, SENS Foundation and this: Cynthia Kenyon: Experiments that hint of longer [VIDEO] Epigentics and extending human lifespan: [LINK]"},{"id":9149613,"author":"biomimic","month":"2015-03","story":"Ask HN: How would you solve mortality?","stance":"reference","why":"Kenyon talk on lifespan experiments, cited alongside aging roadblocks.","quote":"","text":"See: [VIDEO] and [LINK] To name some of the top roadblocks: - Cellular garbage collection - Inner-workings of Mitochondria and cell death/apoptosis at the wrong time. - DNA repair genes - Telomerase production/use Analyzing genomic pathways associated to these is key. Understanding why the expression of genes varies or decreases/increases at the wrong time is also key. Being able to use Cogntive computing/biomicry/AI/Machine Learning etc to analyze the hidden connections and relationships between phytochemicals, genes, proteins, pathways and environment is the next frontier. We're working on it and could certainly use additional crowdsourcing approaches."},{"id":9180612,"author":"biomimic","month":"2015-03","story":"Google Ventures' Bill Maris Investing in Idea of Living to 500","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among resources on lifespan extension research.","quote":"","text":"[LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] See also: [VIDEO] and [LINK] To name some of the top roadblocks: - Cellular garbage collection - Inner-workings of Mitochondria and cell death/apoptosis at the wrong time. - DNA repair genes - Telomerase production/use Analyzing genomic pathways associated to these is key. Understanding why the expression of genes varies or decreases/increases at the wrong time is also key. Being able to use Cogntive computing/biomicry/AI/Machine Learning etc to analyze the hidden connections and relationships between phytochemicals, genes, proteins, pathways and environment is the next frontier. We're working on it and could certainly use additional crowdsourcing approaches."}]},"Vk5bxHetL4s":{"wy":"Linked as a readable summary of where warp drive theory stands when threads discuss recent papers.","q":{"x":"PBS Spacetime has a video discussing the recent theoretical advances in warp drives.","i":27817695,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":46063581,"author":"shagie","month":"2025-11","story":"Voyager 1 is about to reach one light-day from Earth","stance":"reference","why":"Theoretically possible warp drives","quote":"and the bit about theoretically possible warp drives (4 years ago)","text":"PBS Space Time has a hoodie for that... (the T-shirts are sold out). [LINK] Why Antimatter Engines Could Launch In Your Lifetime [LINK] (3 weeks ago) ... with that T-shirt. ... and the bit about theoretically possible warp drives (4 years ago) [VIDEO]"},{"id":27817695,"author":"doctoboggan","month":"2021-07","story":"Positive Energy Warp Drive from Hidden Geometric Structures","stance":"reference","why":"Recent theory on solving warp drive's negative energy problem","quote":"PBS Spacetime has a video discussing the recent theoretical advances in warp drives.","text":"PBS Spacetime has a video discussing the recent theoretical advances in warp drives. It does not cover this paper but does discus a paper that also claims to solve the negative energy problem. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26976687,"author":"weare138","month":"2021-04","story":"The U.S. military takes UFOs seriously. Why doesn't Silicon Valley or academia?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Interstellar travel is theoretically possible. If we have advanced far enough to theorize about it, then it's not outside the realm of reason that a hypothetical civilization millennia more advanced than us would be able to develop the technology. The universe is so unfathomably large and the building blocks for life so common there is no way we are the only life in the universe. It just defies logic. In fact I'd bet money we'll find life in our own solar system within our lifetimes. [VIDEO]"}]},"YZFTaEenaHM":{"wy":"Mostly posted as a joke or parody reference in threads about SPACs, layoffs and hype.","m":[{"id":48548926,"author":"godelski","month":"2026-06","story":"I Love the Computer","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> The hard part now is liking the industry around it. This is exactly how I feel. I fell in love with computing for the same reasons I fell in love with physics and engineering. I love making things. I love the puzzles. I love digging down to understand things. And on top of that, it always ends up being useful. And then I can share my work with others and they get utility out of it too?! What an incredible and fulfilling job/hobby! But now, the industry really kills that passion. I don't believe we're solving real problems, but mostly just solving made up problems that get us money. We've become incredibly dismissive of fixing things and using thought terminating cliques like \"I only care that it works\"[0] or \"don't let perfection be the enemy of good enough\"[1]. When finding bugs we end up arguing if it is \"valuable\"[2] rather than if it actually helps people. I can't tell you how many meetings I've been in discussing if we should fix the problem that were a magnitude more time, per person, than it takes to fix the problem. We've become allergic to deep understanding. We abhor expertise[4]! I thought this was supposed to be a community of nerds? I came to the STEM side because my family was all \"business monkeys\" and I didn't want any part of that constant BS. There was a real \"revenge of the nerds\", but the MBAs did strike back. We're incredibly penny-wise and pound-foolish. We love our sayings, but hate understanding. All to keep that velocity up, but forgot that velocity"},{"id":47942869,"author":"godelski","month":"2026-04","story":"Ghostty is leaving GitHub","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The problem is that from the outside it seems like Microsoft no longer cares about the product. So much so that \"the product\" has become \"shareholders\"[0]. We've just been moving into a world where metric hacking is the desired outcome, not an outcome to try to avoid. These companies are only surviving because of their monopoly statuses. Because of momentum. It's a powerful force. It's the reason Twitter still is around. The reason Facebook is still around. But them being around doesn't mean they're good. It doesn't mean they're useful. It doesn't mean it is a good product. It doesn't mean the users like it. It just means people are used to the way things are and they aren't angry enough to leave for something else. But these companies are actively creating friction for users, daring them to leave, gouging them for everything they can. FFS Microsoft is the largest contributor (even more than Valve) to creating \"the year of linux\". Sure, it'll never have M$FT's market share, but it sure is eating into their revenue. We've all lost sight of what made software so powerful in the first place. Why it became so successful and changed the world. We used to ship good products that help people, make their lives better, and make lots of money in the process. Now, I think all that anyone cares about is the last part. Now we're actively being hostile to those that make the systems better. And that system is fucked up and will destroy itself. That's not a good thing, because it does a lot"},{"id":44738818,"author":"gowld","month":"2025-07","story":"The hype is the product","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Pied Piper's product is its stock.","text":"Did the author never watch Silicon Valley? [VIDEO] Richard, listen-- No, you listen to me Jack. You promised me that you would never compromise the product, so do you feel like taking some action and backing me up on this because me and my product feel pretty compromised right now Richard, I don't think you understand what the product is. The product isn't the platform, and the product isn't your algorithm either, and it's not even the software. Do you know what Pied Piper's product is, Richard? Is--is it me? Oh God no no how could it possibly be you? You got fired! Pied Piper's product is its stock."},{"id":36322584,"author":"csomar","month":"2023-06","story":"The Reddit blackout will continue","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Couldn't be more relevant","text":"The product is the stock price, not \"revenue\" or \"users\". If this increase their stock valuation at IPO and destroy the company in a month time, they'll go for it. This is the free market at play, you are welcome. Couldn't be more relevant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34995684,"author":"satvikpendem","month":"2023-03","story":"The SPAC Fad Is Ending in a Pile of Bankruptcies and Fire Sales","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Once again the show Silicon Valley satirizes this extremely well","text":"Once again the show Silicon Valley satirizes this extremely well: [VIDEO] The product of a VC backed company is not whatever it sells; the product is its stock, to be sold in the IPO to make the VCs and founders shit tons of cash."},{"id":35159973,"author":"satvikpendem","month":"2023-03","story":"Meta to Layoff 10k Employees","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The show Silicon Valley has a great parody of this fact","text":"Who knows / cares? Their product is the stock itself that was then sold to the acquirer. The show Silicon Valley has a great parody of this fact: [VIDEO]"}]},"ZO0PXYMVGSU":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a primary source for why Stroustrup created C++, including combining C and Simula and the aim of zero-cost abstractions.","q":{"x":"Bjarne describes his goal for C++, which was abstractions with zero runtime cost","i":33249278,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":36571724,"author":"FrankyHollywood","month":"2023-07","story":"Simula the Forgotten Programming Language","stance":"reference","why":"Full interview about combining C and Simula to create C++.","quote":"Full interview","text":"Stroustrup once told about how he needed the best of both C and Simula, which resulted in creating C++. Full interview: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33249278,"author":"labrador","month":"2022-10","story":"Why Rust?","stance":"reference","why":"Stroustrup describes the goal of abstractions with zero runtime cost.","quote":"Bjarne describes his goal for C++, which was abstractions with zero runtime cost","text":"In \"Oral History of Bjarne Stroustrup\" Bjarne describes his goal for C++, which was abstractions with zero runtime cost : [VIDEO] I understand Rust is fast, but how does it do with complex abstractions?"},{"id":13789886,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2017-03","story":"Rust's language ergonomics initiative","stance":"reference","why":"Stroustrup on why he started C with Classes.","quote":"Around 29:00.","text":"It is, after being forced to use BCPL to finish his PhD and being used to languages like Simula, Bjarne swore he wouldn't be doing that again. Hence why he started to design C with Classes after getting his job at AT&T. [VIDEO] Around 29:00."},{"id":10367087,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2015-10","story":"Is Java more secure than C?","stance":"reference","why":"Stroustrup's compromises with C given his employer.","quote":"","text":"Yeah, I should have let the \"just\" out. There are a whole class of errors caused by C's memory corruption \"feature\" that I can happily live without. C tosses safety away, because its designers were lazy to implement a proper compiler given what the computing world was doing outside AT&T. Bjarne wasn't also quite found of it, but he had to compromise to get adoption, given his employer. [VIDEO]"}]},"bU1QPtOZQZU":{"wy":"HN commenters link it to convey the scale of asteroid impacts, such as the one that killed the dinosaurs, or of ejecta and tsunamis.","q":{"x":"This discovery channel video might help put things in perspective","i":28537215,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":45210004,"author":"tim333","month":"2025-09","story":"Minerals represent potential biosignatures in the search for life on Mars","stance":"reference","why":"Simulation of large impact ejecting material into the sky.","quote":"Not sure it's very accurate but it gives an impression of the sort of thing that may have happened.","text":">the energy required to kick up a rock to a trajectory where it will hit Mars (or vice versa) is orders of magnitude more than the energy required to vaporize all nearby life, so we've got a pretty big problem already Not necessarily. Something like the asteroid that killed the dinosaurs would have created temperatures at the impact point high enough to vapourize everything but probably also chucked a lot of more distant stuff into the sky that didn't get as hot. Here's a Discovery channel 'simulation' of a large impact chucking stuff up [VIDEO] Not sure it's very accurate but it gives an impression of the sort of thing that may have happened."},{"id":37139144,"author":"jacquesm","month":"2023-08","story":"Asteroid ZTm0038 with a >3% impact probability","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Not to spoil your lunch or anything","text":"Not to spoil your lunch or anything: [VIDEO] Of course 500 Km is a big difference with 400 meters but to those near the point of impact it wouldn't matter and the global effects would still be beyond anything in our history. This is one interpretation of what this could look like: [LINK]"},{"id":28537215,"author":"kjaftaedi","month":"2021-09","story":"Modern snakes evolved from few survivors of dino-killing asteroid","stance":"reference","why":"Simulation conveying the scale of a large asteroid impact.","quote":"This discovery channel video might help put things in perspective","text":"Bacteria species, sure, but having full on land animals survive an event like that is worthy of note. This discovery channel video might help put things in perspective: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27886955,"author":"SllX","month":"2021-07","story":"Giant tsunami from dino-killing asteroid impact revealed in fossilized ripples","stance":"reference","why":"Impact simulation, a version with just the impact and music.","quote":"Here’s a version with just the impact","text":"Here’s a version with just the impact and some music to enjoy it with. Used to leave this video running on various Macintoshes at the Apple Store on my way out. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24472687,"author":"thangalin","month":"2020-09","story":"Phosphine detected in the atmosphere of Venus – an indicator of possible life?","stance":"reference","why":"Simulated asteroid impact plume lofting matter off Earth.","quote":"","text":"When a sufficiently large asteroid[0] slams into Earth, the impact plume[1] can hoist matter off the surface. The velocity of said matter may be sufficiently high to escape Earth's orbit, potentially seeding the solar system with life[2]. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":28965140,"author":"jacquesm","month":"2021-10","story":"Nassim Nicholas Taleb Calls Bitcoin a Tulip Bubble Without the Aesthetics","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Absolutely. I'll milk that impact for all it's worth. Too bad I'll be too busy evaporating myself to cash in on it but for a very short while I'll be the richest man on Earth ;) And I'll re-invest my riches into start-ups that aim to deal with very fast climate change. [VIDEO] Applications are open."}]},"cdgQpa1pUUE":{"wy":"Cited as an early milestone of autonomous driving, and later as an example of over-optimistic timelines.","q":{"x":"They've been working with the guy in the video for over 6 years [1]","i":18638898,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18638898,"author":"jonas21","month":"2018-12","story":"After 48 hours we haven't seen any sign people are using Waymo's service","stance":"reference","why":"Waymo's long collaboration with a blind rider","quote":"They've been working with the guy in the video for over 6 years [1]","text":"They've been working with the guy in the video for over 6 years [1], and he's the former CEO of the Santa Clara Valley Blind Center, which has aims for \"a future where awareness, legislation and technology meet at a crossroads to provide individuals who are blind and visually impaired the same opportunities as their sighted peers\" [2]. It sounds like they're taking this seriously. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":5196397,"author":"moe","month":"2013-02","story":"Robot Workers and the Universal Living Wage","stance":"reference","why":"Google driverless car demonstration","quote":"Google's demonstrations of their driverless car are getting increasingly[2] impressive[3].","text":"There are actually quite a few driverless trains already[1] and Google's demonstrations of their driverless car are getting increasingly[2] impressive[3]. The question is really only when the transition to driverless cars will happen - we're long past the if . [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":3916806,"author":"jiggy2011","month":"2012-05","story":"The Pink Collar Future","stance":"reference","why":"Google self-driving car test","quote":"they have already made significant progress on this and have done hundreds of thousands of miles with actual self driving cars.","text":"I disagree, they have already made significant progress on this and have done hundreds of thousands of miles with actual self driving cars. [VIDEO] I think with something like this, if it isn't Google then it will be one of the large car manufacturers first. I just don't see a random company popping out of nowhere and bringing something like this to the market as the costs involved in the production are going to be huge."},{"id":30857260,"author":"londons_explore","month":"2022-03","story":"Waymo begins driverless rides in San Francisco","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This video is just over 10 years old today","text":"This video is just over 10 years old today: [VIDEO] It shows a waymo/google driverless car driving a multi-mile route on public streets with no supervision. And today we're seeing... Pretty much the exact same thing!"},{"id":26060920,"author":"vel0city","month":"2021-02","story":"Ford Makes $29B Commitment to Electric and Self-Driving Cars","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Google showed a self-driving car taking a blind guy through a Taco Bell drive-through. Clearly ready for prime time, probably just a year off.","text":"Google showed a self-driving car taking a blind guy through a Taco Bell drive-through. Clearly ready for prime time, probably just a year off. [VIDEO] That video was released on March 28, 2012. Nine years ago in 48 days. You'd think if it was just a little regulatory red tape a multi-billion dollar corporation or two (or three) would have figured it out in nine years. Maybe its more than just a few rules changes that's keeping truly full self driving carson some very select routes."}]},"dQw4w9WgXcQ":{"wy":"HN mostly uses it as a Rickroll joke or as a stand-in test URL for tools handling YouTube links.","m":[{"id":38538325,"author":"mtmail","month":"2023-12","story":"Does uBlock Origin (uBO) currently bypass YouTube's adblock detection?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of pwnyoutube workaround URL","quote":"Changing a video URL from www.youtube.com to www.pwnyoutube.com redirects there.","text":"My current work-around is [LINK] and then click 'clean viewer'. Changing a video URL from www.youtube.com to www.pwnyoutube.com redirects there. Example [VIDEO]"},{"id":32889010,"author":"chrismorgan","month":"2022-09","story":"YouTubePluginReplacement.cpp: YouTube-specific code in WebKit","stance":"reference","why":"Example embed markup being translated to an iframe","quote":"","text":"I believe this is about allowing this kind of markup (that exists on old web pages that haven’t been updated for a long time) to still work: <embed [VIDEO] … by roughly translating it into <iframe [VIDEO] Probably also the <object> equivalent, maybe only that and not <embed>, I’m not sure. I never had a great deal to do with those two tags. I don’t know why it has to be done this way. I’d have thought that without this extra code it’d roughly fall back to being an iframe on the original URL, and then YouTube could just check if it’s in a frame and behave as an embed instead of the main UI. But there’s probably a reason why they’ve all done it this kind of way."},{"id":28002722,"author":"TomGullen","month":"2021-07","story":"Never Gonna Give You Up by Rick Astley reaches one billion views [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Points to an 8k upscaled version","quote":"There's actually an 8k version out as well although the interpolation is a bit funky in some places","text":"There's actually an 8k version out as well although the interpolation is a bit funky in some places: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15165345,"author":"vortico","month":"2017-09","story":"The End of ProTube 2","stance":"reference","why":"Example URL for mpv playback","quote":"mpv --no-video","text":"Yes, he's at the mercy of YouTube since he's building on the platform in violation of its ToS. But as a consumer, you can use the youtube-dl script ( [LINK] ) along with mpv ( [LINK] ) to play YouTube videos as you wish. Example: mpv [VIDEO] mpv --no-video [VIDEO] mpv <pretty much any video / streaming site in existence> Both packages are available in your nearest package manager, i.e. brew for MacOS, MSYS2 for Windows, etc."},{"id":13341845,"author":"TheAceOfHearts","month":"2017-01","story":"IINA – A modern video player for macOS","stance":"reference","why":"Example URL for playing YouTube via mpv","quote":"For example: mpv","text":"I'm gonna be trying it out, since another commenter mentioned it's powered by mpv. Although, as I have very basic requirements, I'm already perfectly content with starting up mpv from the cli or by using mpv.app. If you try this out and feel like it's not what you're looking for, I'd suggest trying out mpv [0] directly. It integrates youtube-dl [1], so you can easily watch YouTube, as well as from the other hundreds of sources they support, very easily. For example: mpv [VIDEO] The most recent macOS update brought a picture-in-picture feature, where you could keep videos playing on top of everything else in one side of the screen. With mpv you can get a close approximation by using the \"--ontop\" flag. Although admittedly it's not quite as nice as the new built-in feature. And a bit tangential to the whole thing, I found mpv's C API [2] to be very approachable and well documented. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":9858438,"author":"Khao","month":"2015-07","story":"Linkmoji – Turn any link into Emoji","stance":"reference","why":"Used as a test URL that breaks Linkmoji","quote":"I tried linking to the rick roll video (just as a test url) and it breaks","text":"I tried linking to the rick roll video (just as a test url) and it breaks because it seems to lowercase everything. Original url : [VIDEO] Linkmoji url : [LINK] After following the link : [LINK] And then you get a 404 not found"},{"id":32805182,"author":"gpm","month":"2022-09","story":"Diffusion Bee: Stable Diffusion GUI App for M1 Mac","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Stable Diffusion\" is referring to one specific set of models, which were trained on a dataset including some porn. The official implementation has a second model that detects pornography, and replaces outputs including it with a picture of this dude [VIDEO] (Not kidding). Removing that is a really simple a one line change in the official script."},{"id":28575063,"author":"ianhawes","month":"2021-09","story":"NASA Is Launching a New Telescope That Could Offer Some Cosmic Eye Candy","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Sadly this is outdated. On September 16th, 2021 the ESA announced a delay of the launch until mid-2023 to repair pieces of the spacecraft dislodged during transportation to the launch site [0]. More info on the video including damages. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23458072,"author":"growlist","month":"2020-06","story":"Toto’s “Africa” Played on Tesla Coils","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"'The internet is obsessed with two songs and two songs only. But for different reasons. The first is Smash Mouth’s “All Star.”'... 'The world wide web’s other musical obsession though legitimately rules—Toto’s “Africa.”' Surely this is in with a shout? [VIDEO]"},{"id":23088147,"author":"NotABott","month":"2020-05","story":"Humans Not Invited","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Just got in. Greeted as a bot and got a several text messages: 1) tomas say: ya ne botcurl 2) ya tozhe ne bot 3) XSS. Deleted. vk.com/oldlentach 4) ( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡°) 5) ( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡°( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡°) ͡° ͜ʖ ͡°) 6) Message from bots: we will rule the world some day! ~overc9001 (totally a bot) Missed 7th as it disappeared to quickly and got redirected to [VIDEO] What does it all mean?"},{"id":22748388,"author":"solarkraft","month":"2020-04","story":"Alphabet in bid to buy Hacker News","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"A crowd funding campaign has been started to finance one. I'm already a part of it. The introduction video explaining how to join can be found here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22471108,"author":"Balgair","month":"2020-03","story":"Pizza Effect","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yeah, I want to echo this. The recent-ish rise in drop-bear attacks is also a bit of a pizza effect. It seems that the increase can be traced back to a bacteria that irritates the mouth of the drop bear, making them more prone to attacking humans and other large creatures. Recent results state that the bacterial strain was a mutant strain that originated in a Swedish zoo exhibit of drop bears that were on loan from the Australian government. When one of the mating pair was returned to Australia, the bacteria spread like wildfire (no pun intended) to the wild populations and captive ones. More can be found here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19101958,"author":"NicoJuicy","month":"2019-02","story":"Ask HN: Did offering a money-back guarantee help your business?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I never said it was bad, it was an observation with other brands. Ps. I don't trust a lot of research online : [VIDEO]"},{"id":17910845,"author":"wemdyjreichert","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: How to start a tech blog?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"To rephrase, it's not starting the blog itself (that's filling in a template) but coming up with & creating content (that is, the problem facing all content creators). Oh and by the way, this one has much better info: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17512884,"author":"de_watcher","month":"2018-07","story":"Red Flags Signaling That a Rebuild Will Fail","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"You forgot to mention that you should use \"tip\" instead of \"top\" in this idiom. Here is a video with more detail: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13331003,"author":"orderlyoctopus","month":"2017-01","story":"Being Unpredictable Is a Bad Strategy","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Came to say this. A head to head game in the vacuum of a single match, like, say, Chess or Go or Starcraft, is conducive to certain kinds of strategies. You have one opponent and no teammates. Your win is his loss. It's black and white, clearer what you're trying to accomplish. Not all games (or \"games\", i.e. business) are this simple. There can be multiple players. You may have teammates, or you may need to forge alliances. You may then need to break alliances. There can be multiple degrees of victory or defeat, and multiple paths to victory. Being unpredictable is inherently scary. It's effective in direct conflict because your opponent must account for more possible future situations, diluting his ability to attack you directly, or thwart your true attack directly. Exactly what makes this effective in direct conflict, makes it a liability when seeking alliances. People will not want to cooperate with you if they aren't comfortable in their prediction that cooperation will be beneficial, so they'll only do it if the alternative is worse. Being predictable makes it easy for people to work with you. This opens you up to many opportunities for cooperation you'd otherwise miss. When cooperation is essential to victory, this is immensely powerful and puts a hard limit on how unpredictable you want to be if you'd like to win. And heck, in life and business, maybe relationships with people are most valuable trophy anyway, i.e. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8336372,"author":"SEJeff","month":"2014-09","story":"TXT Record XSS","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"From any Linux (or probably OS X) workstation / server, you can run the command \"host -t TXT jaimehankins.co.uk\" ie: $ host -t TXT jamiehankins.co.uk ;; Truncated, retrying in TCP mode. jamiehankins.co.uk descriptive text \"<iframe width='420' height='315' [VIDEO] frameborder='0' allowfullscreen></iframe>\" jamiehankins.co.uk descriptive text \"v=spf1 include:spf.mandrillapp.com ?all\" jamiehankins.co.uk descriptive text \"<script src='//peniscorp.com/topkek.js'></script>\" jamiehankins.co.uk descriptive text \"google-site-verification=nZUP4BagJAjQZO6AImXyzJZBXBf9s1FbDZr8pzNLTCI\""},{"id":8337546,"author":"mike-cardwell","month":"2014-09","story":"TXT Record XSS","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"A while ago I experimented with adding stuff to the version.bind field in bind. Just updated it: mike@glue:~$ dig +short chaos txt version.bind @198.211.125.252 \"<iframe width='420' height='315' [VIDEO] frameborder='0' allowfullscreen></iframe>\" I put this in my named.conf: version \"<iframe width='420' height='315' [VIDEO] frameborder='0' allowfullscreen></iframe>\"; This site is vulnerable: [LINK] Although takes a minute before it kicks in. I did report it to them at the time, but never got a response."},{"id":8188412,"author":"mattl","month":"2014-08","story":"Show HN: Put “GIF” in front of any YouTube URL and hit enter","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is neat but putting it in front of the URL gives me: GIF [VIDEO] Which doesn't work. I am amazed that Google hasn't complained about the gifyoutube.com domain."},{"id":6410410,"author":"adamnemecek","month":"2013-09","story":"Moot: Full House","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"There is this pretty cool research project at Stanford (it's headed by Andrew Ng of the Coursera fame) that uses NLP and machine learning to detect content that the user might prefer not to see. It's still pretty rough around the edges but this demo is pretty cool. [VIDEO]"}]},"eKgPY1adc0A":{"wy":"Posted as a joke when a story involves being tricked or repeated mistakes.","m":[{"id":17161131,"author":"no1youknowz","month":"2018-05","story":"Jeff Bezos announces Amazon is picking up 'The Expanse'","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This is me","text":"This is amazing news. When Syfy cancelled StarGate Universe, I stopped watching their shows. Dark matter came out and I got into that and quelle surprise. It gets cancelled to. Sigh, then started watching Expanse and loving it. Gets cancelled. This is me: [VIDEO] Suffice to say, I'm ditching Syfy for good now. I hope Netflix or Amazon just buys them out at this point. Nothing of value would have been lost. Except for the time viewers put into watching their shows!"},{"id":6011515,"author":"D9u","month":"2013-07","story":"Officially, the Special Collection Service doesn't exist (2012)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"You can fool me once... But ya can't get fooled again...","text":"I no longer believe a word spoken by any government shill, based solely upon the preponderance of the evidence. Ergo, their credibility doesn't exist. What was it that \"Dubya\" mangled when he said it? \"You can fool me once... But ya can't get fooled again...\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":4151605,"author":"chris123","month":"2012-06","story":"Google maps cut prices by 88%","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"What did idiot Bush say?","text":"\"Closing the barn door after the horse is gone\" is the first thing that popped into my mind when I read this headline. The second thing was that they were being \"penny wise and pound foolish\" when they raised prices in the first place (last October, I guess it was). So they got like six months of revenue bump, pissed a lot of people off and created an opportunity for at least one big and viable competitor. Smart. And now they flip flop. The message is that if they think they can bend you over a barrel and have their way, they will. And then, if the competitive landscape changes, giving you a chance to get their dick out of your *ss, they will try to kiss and makeup (until they sense their next opportunity to bend you over). What did idiot Bush say? \"Fool me once, shame on you... Fool me twice...\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":547578,"author":"justinsb","month":"2009-04","story":"Ext Core 3.0 vs jQuery, a comparison","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Why am I reminded of this?","text":"Just wondering why you're confident that the MIT license won't change in the future? They've changed licenses in the past. Why am I reminded of this? [VIDEO]"}]},"eblPwXFb7TE":{"wy":"Cited to settle the question of whether Musk was a Tesla founder and to show how the Roadster pitch won his investment.","q":{"x":"Here the founders describe how their Roadster concept was integral to their pitch to Musk","i":27934591,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":34047028,"author":"jjoonathan","month":"2022-12","story":"Twitter Suspends PG's Account","stance":"reference","why":"Eberhard interview on Tesla's early days","quote":"","text":"> Tesla existed before Musk. So I've watched the Eberhard interview [1] and the Musk counter-interview [2] and I'm still not decided on this. Did Eberhard do a bunch of useful early-day heavy-lifting only to get elbowed out by Musk once things were ticking along and it was time to take credit? Or was he incompetent and un-invested, forcing Musk to reluctantly take back the reins? I'd love to hear an insider opinion, but I'm not going to go digging. I've already spent far too much time today litigating other peoples' bullshit founder drama, lol. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":31157441,"author":"FartyMcFarter","month":"2022-04","story":"Twitter set to accept Musk's $43B offer – sources","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla founders say Musk was not a founder","quote":"They say he was not a founder within the first minute of the video.","text":"> According to elon basically him, jb and martin eberhard founded tesla which was a holding company that acquired AP propulsion (i think it was called) and the basic IP. That's not what the actual founders say though: [VIDEO] They say he was not a founder within the first minute of the video."},{"id":27934591,"author":"dundarious","month":"2021-07","story":"Despite Tuesday’s flight, Jeff Bezos is running out of time to save Blue Origin","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla founders describe how the Roadster concept won Musk's investment","quote":"Here the founders describe how their Roadster concept was integral to their pitch to Musk","text":"Here the founders describe how their Roadster concept was integral to their pitch to Musk to become a Series A investor. [VIDEO]"}]},"f3lUEnMaiAU":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it critically, as an example of Jack Ma speaking poorly or naively about machine learning.","m":[{"id":46772337,"author":"lysace","month":"2026-01","story":"Qwen3-Max-Thinking","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"He was also being widely ridiculed in the west over this interaction with Elon Musk in August 2019","text":"He was also being widely ridiculed in the west over this interaction with Elon Musk in August 2019, back when Elon was still kinda widely popular. [VIDEO] \"I call AI Alibaba Intelligence\", etc. (Yeah, I know, Apple stole that one.) Reddit moment: \"When Elon Musk realised China's richest man is an idiot ( Jack Ma )\" [LINK] I can see the extended loss of face of China (real or perceived) at the time being a factor."},{"id":24911877,"author":"fossuser","month":"2020-10","story":"Jack Ma's Ant Group set for record $34B market debut","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he comes across extremely poorly in that interview.","text":"I'm more skeptical after seeing this: [VIDEO] I would have expected being a party member to just be the default in order for anyone to do anything of substance in China, but he comes across extremely poorly in that interview. So poorly, that I find it more likely that the companies are being propped up/operated by the government than someone like him could successfully build/run them. Maybe I'm indexing too much on that, but watch it for yourself, definitely weird."},{"id":24437509,"author":"renewiltord","month":"2020-09","story":"Nikola: How to Parlay an Ocean of Lies into a Partnership with GM","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"He sounds like some kind of total moron in the panel discussion with Elon Musk.","text":"Have you listened to Jack Ma talk? He sounds like some kind of total moron in the panel discussion with Elon Musk. I know, you're thinking \"It's not his first language. That's what it is\" but go watch it yourself¹. The ideas he expresses seem completely unformed and make it look like he's not operating at a particularly high level. But Alibaba is real. It's a real company doing real things and delivering real value. It's a giant success. Now I'm not saying Nikola will be (maybe those shorts for 2022 are pretty cheap) but people speak like complete idiots and still run successful companies that they start. ¹ [VIDEO] Here's a highlight: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22572155,"author":"spectramax","month":"2020-03","story":"Jack Ma to send 500K coronavirus test kits, 1M face masks to US","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Elon and Jack Ma debates in Shanghai, just listen this guy talk","text":"Jack Ma cannot be trusted. He swings high and low, doesn't know where the ball is. He once hit a Alibaba home run and thinks he is a hotshot. It's hard to trust Jack Ma with undertones of the CCP propaganda in this humanitarian aid. Elon and Jack Ma debates in Shanghai, just listen this guy talk: [VIDEO] Edit: I am not defending the United States. Just look at what our president said this afternoon. Couldn't even string a cohesive sentence together. If Jack Ma is genuine and wants to help - who knows? To me it feels like a huge power move as the OP said - it feels \"correct\" to me. This news is trending big time on Twitter."},{"id":21200838,"author":"missosoup","month":"2019-10","story":"U.S. widens trade blacklist to include some of China’s top AI startups","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Watch Elon Musk try really hard not to cringe at the shit Jack Ma says on the topic of ML.","text":"Alibaba has an edge in machine learning? The CEO of Alibaba is palpably clueless about anything relating to do with machine learning and I haven't seen any evidence that anyone else at the helm of the org is any better. Watch Elon Musk try really hard not to cringe at the shit Jack Ma says on the topic of ML. [VIDEO]"}]},"gtvCnZqZnxc":{"wy":"HN cites it as a practical use case for jet suits in emergency response, though some doubt how usable the suit is.","m":[{"id":29897688,"author":"kragen","month":"2022-01","story":"Monumental Record: the lives of the workers building the Great Pyramid of Giza","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here you can see people flying like birds without being canned in metal tubes","text":"\"Antiquity\" is conventionally considered to end about 1500 years ago, though this really only applies to Europe: [LINK] The reason for this is the very sparse historical record from the European Dark Ages. Scholars in the High Middle Ages like Petrarch could look back on their own recent history, and then a vast gap of mostly unknown, and then the impressive cultures of Antiquity among whose ruins they lived. We know more about the Dark Ages now than we did then but still not that much. Here you can see people flying like birds without being canned in metal tubes: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] It seems likely that we have changed. I suspect Merer's workers, even if they had grown up in modern civilization, would not tolerate being employees, being disarmed, and having their children taken away for education; and probably more of them were dyslexic than in modern populations. But I don't really know. Generally the rule is that domestic animals are stupider, smaller-skulled, more submissive, weaker, more neotenous, less aggressive, fatter, lower in testosterone, less sexually dimorphic, and possessed of smaller teeth than the wild type ( [LINK] ). Modern humans have these traits to a very notable degree compared to, as you allude to, chimpanzees and even Neandertals or Denisovans; but perhaps we have also been substantially domesticated over the last 4500 years, maybe even over the last 2000. Not because of driving cars, but because of having kings. Are we the Eloi? I don't thin"},{"id":27080685,"author":"_Microft","month":"2021-05","story":"Royal Marines tests out ship boarding exercises with jet-suits","stance":"reference","why":"Jet suit used for rapid emergency response","quote":"You might also like it as quick way to move for emergency personell.","text":"The jet suit's performance looks impressive to me everytime I see it again. I wonder how much arm and upper body strength is required to use the device. The engine on the back might certainly help to reduce that though. You might also like it as quick way to move for emergency personell. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27107905,"author":"BiosElement","month":"2021-05","story":"Royal Marines Testing Out Jet Suits [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Jet suit used for saving lives","quote":"","text":"You consider a transportation device \"murder\"? Interesting, seeing as it also can be used to do the opposite: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24795397,"author":"jon-wood","month":"2020-10","story":"'Person in jetpack' spotted flying again near LA airport","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I’m dubious about the actual usefulness given the difficulty in actually controlling the thing","text":"Gravity Industries in the UK have a working jet pack, although flight time is extremely limited, I think in the order 5-10 minutes, and it isn’t designed to fly at altitude. The founder demonstrated using it for rapid casualty assistance in mountains recently, although I’m dubious about the actual usefulness given the difficulty in actually controlling the thing - it’s more like a paramedic would end up smeared across the mountain themselves than actually assist a casualty in anything but the calmest weather. [VIDEO]"}]},"hnOPu0_YWhw":{"wy":"Referenced in threads on busking, art perception and context as the standard example of great work going unnoticed.","m":[{"id":48135493,"author":"growt","month":"2026-05","story":"What happens when you post a real Monet and say it's AI?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There is a similar experiment where a famous violinist plays in a subway station. Nobody really notices or appreciates him and his music. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34603612,"author":"EGreg","month":"2023-01","story":"Instagram’s co-founders are back with Artifact, a kind of TikTok for text","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Someone else told me also that I’m using the wrong fonts and colors. They said once they saw Comic Sans used in a text bubble they instantly closed the website and didn’t look any further. Tomorrow I am supposed to interview Noam Chomsky again, with David Harvey. I wonder how many people have said “Noam speaks in such a slow and boring way, I tuned out immediately.” Guess what… he is famous so people don’t say that :) I enjoy getting these reactions because later on when we finally turn on the hype I will have a collection of how people react to substance rather than superficial things and hype. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11930042,"author":"robertelder","month":"2016-06","story":"My Experience Busking in San Francisco","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Here is a video of Joshua Bell ( [LINK] ) playing a 3.5 million dollar violin in the subway for 45 minutes and making $32: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6359711,"author":"gjmulhol","month":"2013-09","story":"The Science Of Snobbery","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Joshua Bell, one of the greatest violinists of our time, as a busker has only 2 people take any notice, and only one person realize how great he is -- and that is because she recognized him, not his playing. [VIDEO]"}]},"jkhBlmKtEAk":{"wy":"Mentioned as an early example of real-time ray tracing and path tracing demos.","m":[{"id":23181975,"author":"zamalek","month":"2020-05","story":"A first look at Unreal Engine 5","stance":"reference","why":"Prior RTX demo cited against platform-specific claims.","quote":"","text":"The link I shared wasn't UE5, and their other videos specifically indicate that they are running on PC - unbelievably modest PC hardware at that. > it's not a trivial thing to get a demo like this running well on PCs This old tired argument. The current theory for how the meshing is done is something similar to mesh shaders (available on commodity PC hardware since 2018)[1]. This \"PS5 platform specific feature\" running on PC in 2018[2]. As for the lighting, NVDIA have already had this \"platform specific feature\" on PCs for some time now. It's called RTX. In 2018[3] (using DLSS), in 2020[4] (no apparent DLSS usage, but it may have improved). Both next-gen consoles are essentially PCs. Their primary advantage is tightly coupled hardware (e.g. memory latency, the absurdly fast PS5 SSD). While dedicated raytracing silicon on AMD is currently unique to PS5 (AMD claims they can emulate DXRT on Navi), it has been around for more than a year in consumer hands in the form RTX. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO] [4]: [LINK]"},{"id":17677487,"author":"fizixer","month":"2018-08","story":"A Ray-Tracing Pioneer Explains How He Stumbled into Global Illumination","stance":"reference","why":"Example of real-time path tracing demos.","quote":"it's really 'real-time path tracing'. And it's already happening.","text":"Two points: - What you said is true of any rendering method you use. If you have a complex scene, it'll take more time to render one frame. - What I said about 2018 or 2019 is what nVidia's promotion is all about. See the demos linked at the bottom of my comment. They keep calling it 'real-time ray tracing' but it's really 'real-time path tracing'. And it's already happening. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16622210,"author":"make3","month":"2018-03","story":"Announcing Microsoft DirectX Raytracing","stance":"reference","why":"NVIDIA project on ML denoising of ray tracing.","quote":"","text":"Actually, raytracing is a domain with strong potential for AI, as there is a lot of work using ML models to de-noise ray-traced scenes in real time. Edit: Yeah, NVidia actually just announced a big project on that exact subject: [VIDEO]"}]},"rLDgQg6bq7o":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke reply to threads full of dense or impenetrable technical jargon.","m":[{"id":16771844,"author":"sheltron","month":"2018-04","story":"Walt Disney's MultiPlane Camera (1957) [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Wow these are really great thanks for sharing. Heres a couple of others that I like. Differential Steering: [LINK] Turbo Encabulator: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16190043,"author":"vinchuco","month":"2018-01","story":"The De Bruijn-Newman constant is non-negative","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is not a case of the turbo encabulator [1] which you may seemingly enjoy. What he likely meant is that it is well known from the literature. Or there's an easy explanation that supersedes the verbose notation. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15547198,"author":"nn3","month":"2017-10","story":"Remote Maintenance of Molten Salt Reactors [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The beginning narration sounds very similar (down to choice of words and sentences) to the famous Turbo Encabulator text [VIDEO]"}]},"spyJ5yxTfas":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke reference for startups and pitches with lots of presentation and no actual product.","m":[{"id":18106603,"author":"hpcjoe","month":"2018-09","story":"Solid – Reshape the web as we know it","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Who needs a product, when you have a presentation.","text":"This early 2010s sitcom clip [1] comes to mind when reading about this. Many of the same buzzwords were used, in the same way. Notice the lack of substance in the product discussion, and the literal dancing around what it (Jabberwocky) was. Who needs a product, when you have a presentation. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11565291,"author":"mirkules","month":"2016-04","story":"I Have No Idea What This Startup Does and Nobody Will Tell Me","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This reminds me of Better Off Ted's Jabberwocky Project","text":"This reminds me of Better Off Ted's Jabberwocky Project: [VIDEO] Which, of course, is a parody of the late '90s tech boom and some of the useless, fluffy companies that were spawned."},{"id":9682498,"author":"meesterdude","month":"2015-06","story":"Google Ideas","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Maybe it's a jaberwocky.","text":"WTF is this shit? This site makes no sense, groundbreakingly horrible design, and totally unclear mission and purpose. Just 100% BS. Maybe it's a jaberwocky. [VIDEO]"}]},"uUAvXYW5bmI":{"wy":"Cited as an overview of JWST's complex deployment and its many single points of failure.","q":{"x":"The team working on this did an incredible job!","i":29859934,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29859934,"author":"MichaelMoser123","month":"2022-01","story":"James Webb is fully deployed","stance":"reference","why":"Overview of JWST's complex deployment sequence","quote":"The team working on this did an incredible job!","text":"I wonder if there are some aliens looking at this, and wondering if another advanced civilization has put this together (just like we were wondering with ʻOumuamua). To me James Webb looks definitely like alien technology, if it manages to pass 50 deployment stages, depending on 178 deployment mechanisms... The team working on this did an incredible job! [VIDEO]"},{"id":29494289,"author":"topspin","month":"2021-12","story":"The $11B Webb telescope aims to probe the early universe","stance":"reference","why":"NASA production on JWST deployment risks","quote":"JWST has something like 300 SPOFs in the deployment phase.","text":"JWST has something like 300 SPOFs in the deployment phase. Then there are operation issues; the MIRI cryocooler has had a long, troubled development history. Three weeks ago NASA put out a Hollywood production quality disclaimer video[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29318387,"author":"shantara","month":"2021-11","story":"An “incident” with the James Webb Space Telescope has occurred","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"they seem almost proud of having several hundred of single points of failure","text":"After watching an official JWST video [VIDEO] , they seem almost proud of having several hundred of single points of failure and an immensely complex deployment process that takes several weeks and must work perfectly. I don't know what engineer could be proud of coming up with a design like that."}]},"xrzMYL901AQ":{"wy":"Cited as a source for Ellison's comments about Amazon and Oracle, usually in threads about Amazon migrating off Oracle, and often critically.","m":[{"id":23072705,"author":"aserafini","month":"2020-05","story":"Oracle vs. PostgreSQL: First Glance","stance":"reference","why":"Larry Ellison interview with comments about Amazon in cloud databases.","quote":"Perhaps it was retaliation for Larry’s comments about Amazon in this interview","text":"Amazon even made a promotional video when they shut down their last Oracle database [LINK] Perhaps it was retaliation for Larry’s comments about Amazon in this interview [VIDEO]"},{"id":19360099,"author":"tim333","month":"2019-03","story":"Warren Buffett sells his Oracle shares, retains $44B investment in Apple","stance":"reference","why":"Ellison claims customers hate Oracle but can't leave.","quote":"There's an interview with Ellison \"Oracle is destined to beat Amazon at cloud database\" oct 2018","text":"There's an interview with Ellison \"Oracle is destined to beat Amazon at cloud database\" oct 2018 [VIDEO] where he's chatting about some of his customers hating Oracle and wanting to get off, specifically Amazon and SAP. But he says they don't because the Oracle database is better and the other systems don't work properly."},{"id":18513495,"author":"victor106","month":"2018-11","story":"What If Amazon.com Actually Is a Horrible Website?","stance":"reference","why":"Ellison claims Amazon had huge problems moving off Oracle.","quote":"where Larry Ellison talks about Amazon.com still using Oracle DB","text":"Off topic: I watched[1] this recently where Larry Ellison talks about Amazon.com still using Oracle DB and that when they tried to stop using Oracle they had huge problems. Anyone know to what extent Amazon.com still uses Oracle DB? I know they said they would be completely off Oracle by 2020. Is Oracle really that good for mission critical applications that no open source DB can match? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21261259,"author":"jpalomaki","month":"2019-10","story":"Amazon’s Consumer Business Turned Off Final Oracle Database","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Larry Ellison's comments [1] about how Amazon can't run their business on their own databases.","text":"This is kind of reply to Larry Ellison's comments [1] about how Amazon can't run their business on their own databases. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18389852,"author":"asien","month":"2018-11","story":"Re-affirming Long-Term Support for Java in Amazon Linux","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It is and it has always been Oracle and Larry Ellison to dump on others tech to promote his own.","text":"> telling him that Oracle was needed to make sure our data didn't get \"corrupted\" and started blaming random problems on not having Oracle; It is and it has always been Oracle and Larry Ellison to dump on others tech to promote his own.[0] Now you know why enterprise love Oracle so much. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"y8OnoxKotPQ":{"wy":"Posted as a joke or shorthand whenever a thread touches microservices, architecture sprawl, naming, or talking to management.","q":{"x":"Microservices\" youtube sketch which also includes a surreal architecture diagram (and is very funny)","i":33674655,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":40728906,"author":"hn_throwaway_99","month":"2024-06","story":"Ask HN: Why do message queue-based architectures seem less popular now?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There is a reason this video, , is so popular.","text":"I realize you're being sarcastic (I think), but I actually would put microservices in the same boat. There was a huge push to microservices in the mid teens, and a lot of companies came to hugely regret it. There is a reason this video, [VIDEO] , is so popular. And it's not that \"no one is using microservices\", it's just that tons of companies realized they added almost as many complications as they alleviated, and that for many teams they were just way too premature. And a lot of the companies that I've seen have the most success with microservices are also the most pragmatic about them: they use them in some specific, targeted areas (e.g. authn and authz), but otherwise they're content using a well-componentized monolith where they can break off independent services later if there is an explicit reason to do so."},{"id":46235714,"author":"thundergolfer","month":"2025-12","story":"Programmers and software developers lost the plot on naming their tools","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Krazam has excellently parodied this unserious naming indulgence of programmers","text":"My old company Canva, was pretty good about this and I'm bringing that to the culture at my current place. Krazam has excellently parodied this unserious naming indulgence of programmers[1]. \"See, Bingo knows everyone's name-O. So we get the user ID from there.\" Racoon, Wingman, EKS (Entropy Chaos Service), RGS, Barbie Doll, Ringo-2. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45893782,"author":"neilv","month":"2025-11","story":"Collaboration sucks","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Delivering this feature goes against everything I know to be right and true. And I will sooner lay you into this barren earth, than entertain your folly for a moment longer.\" -- Krazam, \"Microservices\", [VIDEO]"},{"id":45557163,"author":"AIorNot","month":"2025-10","story":"How I provide technical clarity to non-technical leaders","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"which I think is very realistic to real life technical conversations with management","text":"good article see also this important technical clarity video -which I think is very realistic to real life technical conversations with management: [VIDEO] I think a lot of engineers would rather try to solve a thorny technical problem or even re-architect a whole system than have to explain anything in words to a senior manager or VP. As a result, those of us who can communicate well in both engineering and management speak tend to rise in the ranks More seriously, good communication is REALLY REALLY hard -communication is not writing a good article, doc or giving a good lecture on the backend. communication is making sure the intended audience understands the situation well enough to make an informed decision on the task in hand. It means getting feedback on their understanding of the situation, trying repeated ways to get your message heard until it does. -What I mean is having empathy for the audience or recipient's point of view and spending time to make sure they understand what you are telling them."},{"id":42370734,"author":"Sohcahtoa82","month":"2024-12","story":"JSON5 – JSON for Humans","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yeah, but OmegaStar still doesn't support ISO timestamps like they said they would a month ago! (Reference: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":41758313,"author":"codethief","month":"2024-10","story":"Distributed transactions in Go: Read before you try","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Watch this classic","text":"> Even if the Netflix microservices architecture is an objectively good idea (which I honestly have no opinion on I have no opinion on that either, but at least this[0] story by ThePrimeagen didn't make it sound all too great. (Watch this classic[1] before for context, unless you already know Wingman, Galactus, etc.) [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40965088,"author":"dwohnitmok","month":"2024-07","story":"We need visual programming. No, not like that","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Big Tech monolith (`bingo`, `papaya`, etc.) is a fantastic callback to this video: [VIDEO] . Although that video is perhaps ironically about microservices."},{"id":40541915,"author":"nozzlegear","month":"2024-06","story":"'Operation Endgame' Hits Malware Delivery Platforms","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> but now I'm an adult that wouldn't call an official war strike \"Operation Beastmaster\" (real name from the Iraq invasion). Why? That name’s dope. If you can’t give cool names to military operations of all things, then you can’t give cool names to anything. And as someone else pointed out, the US military does have a framework for how they name operations. They at least avoid sounding like our names in the software industry: [VIDEO] :P"},{"id":38669173,"author":"resolutebat","month":"2023-12","story":"Sydney man dubbed the 'Annihilator' wins spreadsheet world championship","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Start with Microservices","text":"Congratulations, you are one of today's lucky 10,000 [1] to discover Krazam. Start with Microservices: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":38239245,"author":"adhesive_wombat","month":"2023-11","story":"Horrible edge cases to consider when dealing with music (2022)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Anything in a foreign language seems to be impenetrable to Google Play. It's also got a goldfish brain so when you reject an incorrect guess it'll make the exact same guess next time. Doesn't seem hard with a an entire datacentre of ML servers to go \"hmm, this was wrong 5 seconds ago, maybe it's still wrong now\". But then again this is how I imagine it works, so maybe its just waiting for the new previous query service to be released: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34324623,"author":"tauwauwau","month":"2023-01","story":"Names should be cute, not descriptive","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Please enjoy","text":"I think we are mixing two different scopes in the the discussion here. If you are naming a company or a product that will be offered to clients, name it something unique that'll appear in searches. However, if you're writing a piece of software that's not going to go outside the company, having descriptive name is the way to go. You can even name your products with generic descriptive names, if your company's name is unique enough, then that'll act as a namespace for product with generic names. Think of packages in Java, C#, we don't debate about that we have to use a cute name for a X.java or X.CS, because that problem has already been solved by namespaces. Product name Excel and Office works for Microsoft because that \"Microsoft\" acts as namespace and \"Microsoft Office\" and \"Microsoft Excel\" are unique enough to be fully qualified names. The ntietz entry is talking about using cute names for internal services, which is very bad idea. Please enjoy [VIDEO]"},{"id":34373523,"author":"naiv","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: How would you refactor a big project in 2023?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This pretty much sums up our current situation","text":"To add one new property to an existing entity we need to currently update and deploy 5 services multiplied by review / testing / acceptance / production environments plus the api client and sometimes the messages depending on what kind of field it is. So it takes 3-5 hours instead of 20-30 minutes. This pretty much sums up our current situation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33911782,"author":"pas","month":"2022-12","story":"VSCode remote code execution advisory","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Why would your Linux get messed up? > That sounds horrible^Hy unproductive A friend of mine works for BlackRock, they simply work on remote Windows virtual desktops. He is not complaining about speed/efficiency. A different friend works for ALDI (like Walmart just smaller), he got an issued MacBook (Pro?). When he connected an external USB hard drive the next day (or sooner?) he got an email about it because some automated scanner thing found something suspicious on it. So at these sizes software development is already inefficient. But mostly because of the complexity. (You know ... [VIDEO] )"},{"id":33945606,"author":"rasz","month":"2022-12","story":"NCC Group security audit for Google's One VPN service","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"In video form for those not keeping up","text":">The fundamental anonymity model Google have employed is that the VPN tunnel is authenticated using \"blind tokens\" which are signed by an authorization service \"Zinc.\" The Zinc service validates OAuth credentials passed in from the client app to verify a user's subscription, then signs the blind token created by the client app to authorize the user's session with the actual VPN backend \"Copper.\" Ostensibly, this will separate the user's Google identity from the VPN identity since the VPN service only knows that it got a valid, signed user token, not what user that token belongs to. In video form for those not keeping up: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34137860,"author":"KronisLV","month":"2022-12","story":"General guidance when working as a cloud engineer","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> First off, it's not dangerous. It's just loosely defined. I'm not sure about this. Some people might take it at face value and end up with dozens if not hundreds of services for a domain that doesn't quite need them. Ergo, the project that they work on might have the development velocity plummet, because it has too much accidental complexity and ops overhead. That's how you get humorous parody videos like these (\"Microservices\" by KRAZAM): [VIDEO] As anyone who has ever seen SOLID or DRY taken as gospel, many people out there would probably agree with the claim that some general advice can definitely be dangerous."},{"id":33674655,"author":"naet","month":"2022-11","story":"I quit my programmer job to become a chicken","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Microservices\" youtube sketch which also includes a surreal architecture diagram (and is very funny)","text":"I was just feeling down as I completed a 3 hour code screen where I was supposed to be recording my screen the whole time I worked, but somehow I botched it and don't have any recording to show. Awkward and my own fault... Those chicken diagrams got a legitimate smile and chuckle out of me though. Thanks for the day brightener!! Reminded me a bit of this \"Microservices\" youtube sketch which also includes a surreal architecture diagram (and is very funny): [VIDEO]"},{"id":33398420,"author":"mdaniel","month":"2022-10","story":"How we built the Tinder API Gateway","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] (\"Microservices\" by KRAZAM) But, parody aside, I'm with you: there must be one for every route in their API, and then another 250 for doing schema validation. I was being funny when I wrote that, but in the far left of their diagram is a circle named \"apimeta\""},{"id":32368616,"author":"mLuby","month":"2022-08","story":"GraphQL kinda sucks","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Relevant Krazam","text":"Having worked in big tech and small startups, I think GraphQL is a brilliant way to solve an organizational problem that massive tech companies have. It's that the team maintaining the API is different from the team that needs changes to the API. Due to the scale of the organization the latter doesn't have the access or know-how to easily add fields to that API themselves, so they have to wait for the maintainers to add the work to their roadmap and get back to it in a few quarters. Relevant Krazam: [VIDEO] At a small start-up, if the GET /foo/:fooId/bar/ endpoint is missing a field baz you need, you can usually just add it yourself and move on."},{"id":30209414,"author":"papito","month":"2022-02","story":"Don't make me think, or why I switched to Rails from JavaScript SPAs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think this is more a revolt against unnecessary complexity, which I think is a discussion to be had. I am not solving drastically more difficult problems than I did twenty years ago, yet the amount of complexity, maintenance, and overhead is staggering. Why? Oh, because \"that's how they do it at the FAANG\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":29609654,"author":"miles","month":"2021-12","story":"Wingman for Haskell","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Wingman is cool and all, but OmegaStar is really holding things up - still waiting on ISO timestamp support: [VIDEO] [LINK] Shout out to the Galactus team!"}]},"ybrQvs4x0Ps":{"wy":"The standard citation whenever null safety and language design come up.","q":{"x":"Tony Hoare's famous talk about the invention of null references which he calls his billion dollar mistake","i":22150257,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":44862599,"author":"Ygg2","month":"2025-08","story":"Partially Matching Zig Enums","stance":"reference","why":"Hoare says avoiding null wastes people's time.","quote":"","text":"> Sure. With infinite energy, anything’s possible - we can prevent all bugs. The problem is, we don’t have infinite energy. You don't need infinite energy but it is a significant undertaking. The UB seem to obey a power law in regards to their lifetime i.e. every X years number of UB caused problems halves. > I can only recall one serious concurrency bug I’ve ever had (a data race) which took some debugging time to track down, but it wasn’t in Go. YMMV. That's what is nasty about UB in data races. It's not trivial to find and it's a pain in the neck to reproduce. So even taking you at your word, you not having issues with data races isn't the same as \"I wrote code free of data races\". > As for the “billion dollar mistake”, I understand the argument in the context of C or C++, but not in the context of Go. Even without a chance of UB. You're adding an implicit `Type | null` to every piece that uses nullable code without handling the null case. Each time you forget to do it, you cause either an UB or a null pointer error. And the place it manifests is different from where it's generated. Furthermore, listening to Tony Hoare's talk ( [VIDEO] ), he mentions that trying to avoid the null, in the context of a language like Java or C#, that permits them is also causing people to waste time working around them. > The middle network layer will report the error to the user, I fix it, and move on, a $0 mistake. Sure, but that's not a $0 mistake. It's {time to fix * hourly rate}. Even if"},{"id":44865128,"author":"lossolo","month":"2025-08","story":"Partially Matching Zig Enums","stance":"reference","why":"Cited by other commenter regarding workarounds for null.","quote":"","text":"> Even without a chance of UB. You're adding an implicit `Type | null` to every piece that uses nullable code without handling the null case. Each time you forget to do it, you cause either an UB or a null pointer error. And the place it manifests is different from where it's generated. > Furthermore, listening to Tony Hoare's talk ( [VIDEO] ), he mentions that trying to avoid the null, in the context of a language like Java or C#, that permits them is also causing people to waste time working around them. It's an insignificant amount of time in my experience, which makes this irrelevant in practice. I’ve never had to \"work around them\", this is either a theoretical argument or just sloppy programming. > Sure, but that's not a $0 mistake. It's {time to fix * hourly rate}. Even if you're doing for OSS, you could have been spending that time doing something else. Oh, come on, you’re smart enough to understand that a few minutes every few months may not be exactly $0, but it’s such an insignificant value that it can be treated as $0. > Sure. But trading \"ease of use\" for \"preventing errors\" is not something I want in any language I use. Well, you can choose your trade offs, and you can let others choose theirs. So I guess you use Ada for everything? or maybe you use Coq to prove everything? Of course you don’t, you’re also making trade offs, just different ones. > Null/nil are about as good concepts today as silently skipping errors. You exaggerate again. I do not agree with tha"},{"id":32537994,"author":"benreesman","month":"2022-08","story":"C++ Concurrency Model on x86 for Dummies (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Hoare's billion-dollar mistake on null.","quote":"C.A.R. Hoare calls `null` his \"billion-dollar mistake","text":"There's a constant tension between bit-packing and rich semantics older than me! As you likely know C.A.R. Hoare calls `null` his \"billion-dollar mistake\": [VIDEO] . I linked the Cliff Click talk because it's the best example that I know about of someone using some semi-formal rigor around valid states and the transitions between them in the context of raw atomic operations to get a quite clearly correct result with a legitimate performance imperative: he had a machine with 768 cores, customers who bought that machine to have 768 threads jump on a hash table all at once and wanted ~~700x more throughput having paid for the machine. But even Cliff Click made sure that both his conceptual states and their physical representations were going A -> B -> C w.r.t. a given conceptual \"register\". I'm not trying to knock the serious concurrency pros who need atomic and/or relaxed operations in some super hot path: there are unambiguous use cases for this stuff. I've legitimately needed these things once or twice myself. Lock-free is a bad default. Even the elite concurrency pros work very hard to get something that's both robust and still a win after the constant factors. I'm saying \"proceed with high caution, and on the basis of measurement\", not \"never ever do this\"."},{"id":22150257,"author":"unlinked_dll","month":"2020-01","story":"A Reply to “Let’s stop copying C”","stance":"reference","why":"Hoare's talk on inventing null references.","quote":"Tony Hoare's famous talk about the invention of null references which he calls his billion dollar mistake","text":"I believe that's a reference to Tony Hoare's famous talk about the invention of null references which he calls his billion dollar mistake [VIDEO]"}]}});
